window.EXAM_QUESTIONS = [{"id": 2, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "An 18-month-old girl is brought for a routine visit. Her parents report that she walks well, uses a spoon with some spilling, and can say approximately 15 single words. On formal testing today, the child stacks two blocks and points to one body part when asked. She does not yet combine words into short phrases. There is no significant past medical history and preventive care has been up to date. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Refer immediately to early intervention for expressive language delay", "B": "Order brain magnetic resonance imaging to evaluate for structural delay", "C": "Refer for formal audiology testing to rule out congenital hearing loss", "D": "Reassure the family that this represents normal development for her age"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reassure the family that this represents normal development for her age): Combining words into short phrases is a 2-year milestone, not an 18-month one, so this child's language is age-appropriate and reassurance with continued routine surveillance is correct. Early intervention referral, imaging, and audiology testing are not indicated for development that falls within the expected range for age."}, {"id": 3, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 6-year-old boy is noted by his teacher to have difficulty keeping up with classmates in reading and appears to squint frequently at the whiteboard. His parents report he was walking and talking on time as a toddler and has no chronic medical conditions. Prior developmental milestones have been met on time and there is no other significant past medical history. Growth parameters are appropriate for age, and the remainder of the examination is unremarkable. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in his care?", "options": {"A": "Refer for genetic testing given possible global developmental delay", "B": "Refer for a comprehensive vision screening examination", "C": "Begin a trial of stimulant medication for suspected attention deficit disorder", "D": "Reassure the family that squinting is a normal childhood behavior"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Refer for a comprehensive vision screening examination): School-age children who squint at distant objects and struggle academically most commonly have an uncorrected refractive error, so vision screening is the appropriate first step before pursuing more invasive workups. Genetic testing is not indicated because his early milestones were normal. Stimulant medication should not be started without a formal attention deficit evaluation, and this presentation is more consistent with a vision problem. Reassurance would delay a correctable cause of poor school performance."}, {"id": 6, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 9-month-old infant is brought for a well visit. The mother reports the infant recently began pulling to stand at furniture and can transfer a toy from one hand to the other. On examination the infant uses a raking motion rather than fingers to pick up a small piece of cereal. There is no significant past medical history and preventive care has been up to date. Growth parameters are appropriate for age, and the remainder of the examination is unremarkable. Which of the following best characterizes this finding?", "options": {"A": "This finding indicates a significant fine motor delay requiring referral", "B": "A mature pincer grasp should already be present by 6 months of age", "C": "This raking pattern suggests an underlying neuromuscular disorder", "D": "The pincer grasp typically matures slightly later, around 9 to 12 months"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The pincer grasp typically matures slightly later, around 9 to 12 months): A mature pincer grasp, using the thumb and index finger, typically emerges near the end of the first year, often between 9 and 12 months, so a raking grasp at 9 months can still be within the normal maturing range and does not by itself indicate delay."}, {"id": 7, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "An 80-year-old woman is evaluated for a routine annual wellness visit. Her family reports no falls, and she independently manages her medications and finances. On examination she completes the Timed Up and Go test in 9 seconds and scores normally on a brief cognitive screen. There is no other significant past medical history, and no daily medications are taken. No acute concerns beyond those described above prompted this visit. Which of the following statements about normal aging best applies to this patient?", "options": {"A": "Any decline in reaction time at this age should prompt an urgent neurologic workup, as one consideration among several raised by this scenario.", "B": "A Timed Up and Go time under 10 seconds suggests significant fall risk", "C": "Decreased renal clearance and reduced lean body mass are expected findings of normal aging", "D": "Normal aging should not be associated with any decrease in muscle mass"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Decreased renal clearance and reduced lean body mass are expected findings of normal aging): Normal, non-pathologic aging is associated with predictable physiologic changes including decreased lean body mass, reduced renal clearance, presbyopia, presbycusis, and modestly slower reaction time, all of which should be distinguished from disease. A Timed Up and Go under 10 seconds is actually reassuring, not concerning, for fall risk."}, {"id": 8, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 5-day-old term newborn is brought to the emergency department by his parents because he appears yellow. He was breastfeeding well and voiding and stooling normally until this morning. On examination he is jaundiced to the mid-abdomen but otherwise vigorous and afebrile. His birth weight was 3.4 kilograms, and his current weight is 3.05 kilograms. Follow-up with preventive care has generally been consistent in the past. There is no other significant past medical history, and no daily medications are taken. Which of the following statements best applies to this weight change?", "options": {"A": "A weight loss of about 10% from birth weight can be within the expected range in the first week", "B": "This degree of weight loss indicates significant dehydration requiring hospitalization, as a general clinical consideration in similar cases.", "C": "Term newborns should regain their birth weight by one month of age", "D": "Weight loss of any amount in the first week is abnormal and requires formula supplementation"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A weight loss of about 10% from birth weight can be within the expected range in the first week): Healthy newborns commonly lose up to about 10% of their birth weight in the first week of life and are expected to regain it by about 2 weeks of age, so a loss of roughly 10% in this vigorous, well-hydrated infant is within the expected range rather than a sign of dehydration. Hospitalization is not automatically required for weight loss at this level in an otherwise well infant."}, {"id": 9, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A father asks his pediatrician about safe sleep practices for his 2-month-old daughter to reduce her risk of sudden infant death syndrome. There is no significant past medical history and preventive care has been up to date. Growth parameters are appropriate for age, and the remainder of the examination is unremarkable. Follow-up with preventive care has generally been consistent in the past. Which of the following recommendations is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Place the infant on her back on a firm mattress with no loose bedding or soft objects", "B": "Place the infant on her stomach to prevent choking on regurgitated milk, as a general clinical consideration in similar cases.", "C": "Allow the infant to sleep in the parents' bed for closer nighttime monitoring", "D": "Use a soft pillow and blanket to keep the infant warm and comfortable"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Place the infant on her back on a firm mattress with no loose bedding or soft objects): Supine positioning on a firm, flat surface free of soft bedding, pillows, or loose blankets is the cornerstone of safe sleep counseling and is associated with the lowest risk of sudden infant death syndrome."}, {"id": 10, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A mother asks when it is safe to introduce honey into her 10-month-old son's diet. She has heard conflicting advice from friends. Prior developmental milestones have been met on time and there is no other significant past medical history. Growth parameters are appropriate for age, and the remainder of the examination is unremarkable. Follow-up with preventive care has generally been consistent in the past. Which of the following is the most accurate response?", "options": {"A": "Honey is safe to introduce at any age once solid foods have started", "B": "Honey should be avoided until after age 5 because of dental caries risk, as one factor considered during routine evaluation.", "C": "Honey should be avoided until after the first birthday because of the risk of infant botulism", "D": "Honey poses a choking hazard but no other specific risk in infancy"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Honey should be avoided until after the first birthday because of the risk of infant botulism): Honey can contain Clostridium botulinum spores that an infant's immature gastrointestinal tract cannot yet clear, risking infant botulism, so honey should be withheld until after the first birthday."}, {"id": 11, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A pediatrician recommends that a family keep their 14-month-old son rear-facing in his car seat. The parents ask why this is still necessary since he can now walk independently. Growth parameters are appropriate for age, and the remainder of the examination is unremarkable. No acute concerns beyond those described above prompted this visit. Prior developmental milestones have been met on time and there is no other significant past medical history. Which of the following best explains the rationale for this recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Forward-facing seats are not approved for use until age 4 by federal regulation", "B": "Rear-facing seating better protects a young child's head, neck, and spine in a crash", "C": "Rear-facing seating is recommended only until the infant begins crawling", "D": "Motor development milestones determine when a forward-facing seat becomes appropriate"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Rear-facing seating better protects a young child's head, neck, and spine in a crash): Current guidance recommends keeping children rear-facing until at least age 2, or until they exceed the seat's rear-facing height or weight limit, because this position better distributes crash forces away from the relatively large head and immature cervical spine of a young child."}, {"id": 13, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 6-month-old girl is brought for a well visit. Examination reveals that her Moro reflex, present since birth, is no longer elicitable, and she now sits briefly without support and babbles with repetitive syllables. Growth parameters are appropriate for age, and the remainder of the examination is unremarkable. Prior developmental milestones have been met on time and there is no other significant past medical history. This visit is being conducted as part of routine, guideline-based preventive care. Which of the following statements is most accurate regarding these findings?", "options": {"A": "Persistence of the Moro reflex beyond birth is itself generally pathologic, as sometimes noted in comparable clinical scenarios.", "B": "The Moro reflex should still be strongly present at 6 months of age", "C": "Loss of the Moro reflex at this age suggests upper motor neuron injury", "D": "Resolution of the Moro reflex by this age reflects normal maturation of the central nervous system"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Resolution of the Moro reflex by this age reflects normal maturation of the central nervous system): Primitive reflexes such as the Moro reflex are expected to disappear by around 4 to 6 months as cortical inhibition matures, so its absence at 6 months, alongside age-appropriate sitting and babbling, reflects normal development."}, {"id": 16, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 16-year-old girl presents for a routine adolescent well visit. Her mother remains in the room throughout the visit. The physician wants to ask about sexual activity, substance use, and mood in a way that ensures honest answers. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. There is no other significant past medical history, and no daily medications are taken. No acute concerns beyond those described above prompted this visit. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Proceed with the full interview with the mother present to build trust, as a general clinical consideration in similar cases.", "B": "Ask the mother to step out so the adolescent can be interviewed confidentially", "C": "Defer sensitive questions largely until the patient turns 18", "D": "Ask the mother directly whether her daughter is sexually active"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ask the mother to step out so the adolescent can be interviewed confidentially): Confidential time alone with an adolescent patient, without a parent present, substantially increases the likelihood of honest disclosure about sensitive topics such as sexual activity, substance use, and mental health, and is a standard component of adolescent preventive visits."}, {"id": 17, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 55-year-old woman comes for a routine physical and screens positive on a validated depression questionnaire administered at check-in. She denies any current thoughts of self-harm but reports low mood and poor sleep for the past 2 months. Follow-up with preventive care has generally been consistent in the past. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. Vital signs are stable, and the rest of the examination is unremarkable aside from the findings above. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Immediately admit the patient to an inpatient psychiatric unit, as one aspect of the overall clinical picture here.", "B": "Perform a more detailed clinical assessment to confirm the diagnosis and evaluate severity", "C": "Disregard the screening result since she denies suicidal ideation", "D": "Start a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor without further evaluation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Perform a more detailed clinical assessment to confirm the diagnosis and evaluate severity): A positive depression screen should prompt a more thorough clinical evaluation of symptoms, duration, functional impact, and safety before deciding on management, which may then include counseling, medication, or referral as appropriate."}, {"id": 19, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 38-year-old man with a family history of early myocardial infarction in his father asks when he should begin lipid screening. He has no personal history of cardiovascular disease and does not smoke. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. This visit is being conducted as part of routine, guideline-based preventive care. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. Which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Defer all lipid screening until age 45 without fully considering family history", "B": "Lipid screening is unnecessary until symptoms of coronary disease develop", "C": "Begin lipid screening now rather than waiting until the usual average-risk starting age", "D": "Begin lipid screening only after he turns 65 years old"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Begin lipid screening now rather than waiting until the usual average-risk starting age): A strong family history of early cardiovascular disease is a recognized indication to begin lipid screening earlier than the usual average-risk starting age of around 40 to 45, since it identifies elevated risk before symptoms occur."}, {"id": 25, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 45-year-old man with a long history of alcohol use disorder is brought to the emergency department confused after several days of poor oral intake. On examination he has nystagmus, a wide-based unsteady gait, and is disoriented to time and place. There is no other significant past medical history, and no daily medications are taken. Follow-up with preventive care has generally been consistent in the past. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate step in his management?", "options": {"A": "Administer intravenous glucose alone as the first priority", "B": "Obtain magnetic resonance imaging before any treatment is given", "C": "Administer thiamine before giving any intravenous glucose", "D": "Begin empiric antibiotics for suspected central nervous system infection"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Administer thiamine before giving any intravenous glucose): This presentation of confusion, nystagmus, and ataxia is classic for Wernicke encephalopathy from thiamine deficiency, and thiamine must be given before or together with glucose because glucose administered alone can precipitate or worsen the encephalopathy by accelerating depletion of remaining thiamine stores."}, {"id": 26, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 62-year-old man with alcohol use disorder who was previously treated for an acute confusional episode now has a persistent memory disorder. He fabricates detailed but inaccurate stories about recent events and cannot form new memories, though his other cognitive functions are relatively preserved. Follow-up with preventive care has generally been consistent in the past. There is no other significant past medical history, and no daily medications are taken. Vital signs are stable, and the rest of the examination is unremarkable aside from the findings above. Which of the following best describes this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Wernicke encephalopathy in its acute, reversible phase", "B": "Korsakoff syndrome with confabulation and anterograde amnesia", "C": "Normal age-related cognitive decline unrelated to nutrition, as sometimes noted in comparable clinical scenarios.", "D": "Hepatic encephalopathy from portal-systemic shunting"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Korsakoff syndrome with confabulation and anterograde amnesia): Korsakoff syndrome is the often-irreversible chronic sequela of untreated or inadequately treated Wernicke encephalopathy, characterized by anterograde amnesia and confabulation with relative preservation of other cognitive domains."}, {"id": 27, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 50-year-old man who follows a diet based almost entirely on corn without other significant sources of protein develops a photosensitive rash on his neck and forearms, chronic diarrhea, and progressive confusion. Vital signs are stable, and the rest of the examination is unremarkable aside from the findings above. This visit is being conducted as part of routine, guideline-based preventive care. There is no other significant past medical history, and no daily medications are taken. Which of the following deficiencies is most likely responsible for this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Vitamin B12 deficiency", "B": "Folate (vitamin B9) deficiency", "C": "Vitamin C deficiency", "D": "Niacin (vitamin B3) deficiency"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Niacin (vitamin B3) deficiency): This presentation of dermatitis in sun-exposed areas, diarrhea, and dementia is the classic triad of pellagra from niacin deficiency, which is associated with corn-based diets because corn niacin is poorly bioavailable and low in tryptophan. Vitamin B12 and folate deficiency cause megaloblastic anemia and, for B12, neurologic findings distinct from this triad."}, {"id": 28, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 30-year-old woman who follows a strict vegan diet without any supplementation presents with fatigue and numbness and tingling in her feet. Laboratory testing reveals macrocytic anemia with elevated methylmalonic acid and elevated homocysteine. Vital signs are stable, and the rest of the examination is unremarkable aside from the findings above. No acute concerns beyond those described above prompted this visit. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Vitamin B12 deficiency", "B": "Folate deficiency", "C": "Iron deficiency", "D": "Vitamin C deficiency"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Vitamin B12 deficiency): Elevated methylmalonic acid together with elevated homocysteine is specific for vitamin B12 deficiency, since methylmalonic acid metabolism depends on B12 as a cofactor while homocysteine metabolism depends on both B12 and folate, and B12 deficiency also explains the peripheral neuropathy from subacute combined degeneration."}, {"id": 30, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 6-year-old boy adopted from a region with limited access to fortified foods presents with bowing of both legs, delayed closure of his fontanelle, and beading noted along his rib margins on examination. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. Follow-up with preventive care has generally been consistent in the past. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. Which of the following is the most likely underlying cause?", "options": {"A": "Vitamin D deficiency", "B": "Vitamin A deficiency", "C": "Vitamin C deficiency", "D": "Vitamin K deficiency"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Vitamin D deficiency): Bowed legs, delayed fontanelle closure, and a rachitic rosary at the costochondral junctions are classic features of rickets from vitamin D deficiency, which impairs bone mineralization in a growing child."}, {"id": 34, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 55-year-old man began taking a very high dose over-the-counter vitamin supplement several months ago in hopes of improving his eyesight. He now presents with headache, blurred vision, dry skin, and diffuse hair thinning. Liver enzymes are mildly elevated. Vital signs are stable, and the rest of the examination is unremarkable aside from the findings above. No acute concerns beyond those described above prompted this visit. There is no other significant past medical history, and no daily medications are taken. Which of the following vitamins is most likely responsible for this toxicity?", "options": {"A": "Vitamin C", "B": "Vitamin A", "C": "Vitamin B12", "D": "Folate"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Vitamin A): Chronic vitamin A toxicity causes headache, blurred vision from pseudotumor cerebri, dry skin, alopecia, and hepatotoxicity, matching this presentation in a patient who began high-dose supplementation."}, {"id": 36, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 3-year-old girl from a region with limited access to fortified foods and green leafy vegetables presents with difficulty seeing at dusk. Examination of her eyes reveals dry, foamy patches on the conjunctiva. Follow-up with preventive care has generally been consistent in the past. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. There is no other significant past medical history, and no daily medications are taken. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Vitamin B12 deficiency causing optic neuropathy", "B": "Congenital cataracts unrelated to nutrition, as one consideration among several raised by this scenario.", "C": "Vitamin D deficiency causing ocular calcification", "D": "Vitamin A deficiency causing xerophthalmia with Bitot spots"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Vitamin A deficiency causing xerophthalmia with Bitot spots): Night blindness together with dry conjunctiva and Bitot spots is a classic and early presentation of vitamin A deficiency, which can progress to corneal ulceration and permanent blindness if untreated, and remains an important cause of preventable childhood blindness in resource-limited settings."}, {"id": 37, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 27-year-old woman with iron deficiency anemia reports craving and chewing on ice several times a day for the past few months, which she finds embarrassing. Follow-up with preventive care has generally been consistent in the past. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. Which of the following terms best describes this behavior?", "options": {"A": "Bulimia", "B": "Pica", "C": "Rumination", "D": "Orthorexia"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Pica): Pica refers to persistent craving and consumption of non-nutritive substances such as ice, clay, or starch, and is a well-recognized association with iron deficiency, sometimes resolving once the deficiency is corrected."}, {"id": 38, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 62-year-old man with a history of carcinoid tumor presents with a photosensitive rash, chronic diarrhea, and cognitive slowing. His tumor is known to secrete large amounts of serotonin. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. Vital signs are stable, and the rest of the examination is unremarkable aside from the findings above. This visit is being conducted as part of routine, guideline-based preventive care. Which of the following best explains this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Excess tryptophan diversion to serotonin synthesis leading to niacin deficiency", "B": "Direct toxic injury to the skin from circulating serotonin", "C": "An unrelated vitamin B12 deficiency from small bowel involvement, as one detail relevant to this differential.", "D": "Zinc deficiency from chronic diarrhea and malabsorption alone"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Excess tryptophan diversion to serotonin synthesis leading to niacin deficiency): In carcinoid syndrome, the tumor diverts large amounts of dietary tryptophan toward serotonin production, leaving insufficient tryptophan available for niacin synthesis, which can produce pellagra with its characteristic photosensitive dermatitis, diarrhea, and dementia."}, {"id": 40, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 48-year-old man who underwent gastric bypass surgery three years ago and has not consistently taken his recommended vitamin supplements presents with fatigue, glossitis, and a positive Romberg sign along with decreased vibration sense in his feet. There is no other significant past medical history, and no daily medications are taken. Vital signs are stable, and the rest of the examination is unremarkable aside from the findings above. No acute concerns beyond those described above prompted this visit. Which of the following is the most likely deficiency?", "options": {"A": "Folate deficiency", "B": "Iron deficiency", "C": "Vitamin B12 deficiency", "D": "Vitamin B6 deficiency"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Vitamin B12 deficiency): Loss of vibration and position sense with a positive Romberg sign points to dorsal column involvement, which along with glossitis and fatigue is characteristic of vitamin B12 deficiency causing subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord, a well-known long-term risk after gastric bypass surgery without adequate supplementation."}, {"id": 41, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 20-year-old man with Hartnup disease, a hereditary disorder impairing intestinal and renal absorption of certain amino acids including tryptophan, presents with a scaly, sun-exposed rash and mild diarrhea. No acute concerns beyond those described above prompted this visit. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. Which of the following vitamins is most likely deficient in this condition?", "options": {"A": "Vitamin B12", "B": "Folate, as sometimes discussed in similar clinical situations.", "C": "Vitamin C", "D": "Niacin (vitamin B3)"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Niacin (vitamin B3)): Hartnup disease impairs absorption of tryptophan, an amino acid precursor for endogenous niacin synthesis, so affected patients can develop pellagra-like symptoms including a photosensitive rash and diarrhea similar to dietary niacin deficiency."}, {"id": 42, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 55-year-old woman with long-standing alcohol use disorder and poor nutritional intake presents with bilateral lower extremity edema, dyspnea, and a widened pulse pressure. Echocardiography reveals a high-output state with a dilated heart. Vital signs are stable, and the rest of the examination is unremarkable aside from the findings above. No acute concerns beyond those described above prompted this visit. There is no other significant past medical history, and no daily medications are taken. Which of the following is the most likely underlying cause?", "options": {"A": "Dry beriberi from thiamine deficiency", "B": "Wernicke encephalopathy, as one detail relevant to this differential.", "C": "Wet beriberi from thiamine deficiency", "D": "Pellagra from niacin deficiency"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Wet beriberi from thiamine deficiency): Wet beriberi refers to the cardiovascular manifestation of thiamine deficiency, producing high-output heart failure with peripheral edema and a wide pulse pressure, distinct from dry beriberi, which affects peripheral nerves rather than the heart."}, {"id": 43, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 9-year-old boy with a genetic defect in intestinal zinc transport presents with a well-demarcated, erythematous rash around his mouth and on his hands and feet, along with hair loss and chronic diarrhea. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. This visit is being conducted as part of routine, guideline-based preventive care. Vital signs are stable, and the rest of the examination is unremarkable aside from the findings above. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Kwashiorkor", "B": "Acrodermatitis enteropathica", "C": "Pellagra", "D": "Scurvy"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Acrodermatitis enteropathica): Acrodermatitis enteropathica is a hereditary defect in intestinal zinc absorption that produces a characteristic perioral and acral dermatitis along with alopecia and diarrhea, closely mirroring the clinical picture of acquired zinc deficiency."}, {"id": 44, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 68-year-old woman with chronic iron deficiency anemia is noted on examination to have spoon-shaped fingernails. No acute concerns beyond those described above prompted this visit. Vital signs are stable, and the rest of the examination is unremarkable aside from the findings above. There is no other significant past medical history, and no daily medications are taken. Which of the following terms describes this nail finding?", "options": {"A": "Koilonychia", "B": "Onycholysis", "C": "Beau lines", "D": "Clubbing"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Koilonychia): Koilonychia refers to concave, spoon-shaped nails and is a recognized finding in chronic iron deficiency anemia."}, {"id": 45, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 32-year-old man returns from an extended stay in a region where rice is the dominant dietary staple with little dietary diversity. He now reports difficulty climbing stairs due to leg weakness along with numbness in his feet, but no cardiac symptoms. Follow-up with preventive care has generally been consistent in the past. There is no other significant past medical history, and no daily medications are taken. Vital signs are stable, and the rest of the examination is unremarkable aside from the findings above. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Wet beriberi from thiamine deficiency", "B": "Dry beriberi from thiamine deficiency", "C": "Guillain-Barre syndrome, as sometimes noted in comparable clinical scenarios.", "D": "Diabetic peripheral neuropathy"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Dry beriberi from thiamine deficiency): Dry beriberi refers to the peripheral neuropathy form of thiamine deficiency, producing symmetric weakness and sensory loss without the cardiac findings seen in wet beriberi, and is classically associated with diets heavily dependent on polished rice, which is low in thiamine."}, {"id": 46, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "Which of the following is the most likely underlying cause of this infant's presentation?", "options": {"A": "Iron deficiency from prolonged exclusive breastfeeding, as a general clinical consideration in similar cases.", "B": "Congenital hypothyroidism unrelated to maternal diet", "C": "Vitamin B12 deficiency from inadequate maternal intake reflected in breast milk", "D": "Vitamin K deficiency from inadequate newborn prophylaxis"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Vitamin B12 deficiency from inadequate maternal intake reflected in breast milk): An infant exclusively breastfed by a mother with an unsupplemented vegan diet is at high risk for vitamin B12 deficiency, since maternal B12 stores directly determine breast milk B12 content, and the resulting deficiency in the infant produces macrocytic anemia along with hypotonia, lethargy, and developmental regression.", "case_id": "C2", "case_text": "A 5-month-old male infant is brought to clinic because he has become increasingly lethargic and has stopped reaching new motor milestones over the past 6 weeks. He is exclusively breastfed. His mother, who is otherwise healthy, has followed a strict vegan diet for the past 4 years without any vitamin supplementation. On examination the infant is hypotonic, has poor eye contact, and is pale. A complete blood count reveals macrocytic anemia.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 47, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "Which additional laboratory finding would most strongly support this diagnosis over an isolated folate deficiency?", "options": {"A": "Elevated homocysteine with a normal methylmalonic acid level, as a general clinical consideration in similar cases.", "B": "Low serum ferritin with a normal mean corpuscular volume", "C": "A markedly elevated reticulocyte count", "D": "Elevated methylmalonic acid in addition to elevated homocysteine"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Elevated methylmalonic acid in addition to elevated homocysteine): Both vitamin B12 and folate deficiency raise homocysteine, but only vitamin B12 deficiency also raises methylmalonic acid, since methylmalonic acid metabolism specifically requires B12 as a cofactor, making an elevated methylmalonic acid level the key distinguishing laboratory finding.", "case_id": "C2", "case_text": "A 5-month-old male infant is brought to clinic because he has become increasingly lethargic and has stopped reaching new motor milestones over the past 6 weeks. He is exclusively breastfed. His mother, who is otherwise healthy, has followed a strict vegan diet for the past 4 years without any vitamin supplementation. On examination the infant is hypotonic, has poor eye contact, and is pale. A complete blood count reveals macrocytic anemia.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 48, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "If untreated, which of the following neurologic complications is this infant most at risk for as he grows older?", "options": {"A": "Subacute combined degeneration affecting dorsal columns and corticospinal tracts", "B": "Peripheral neuropathy limited to autonomic fibers only", "C": "Cerebellar ataxia from Purkinje cell loss alone", "D": "Anterior horn cell degeneration mimicking motor neuron disease, as sometimes noted in comparable clinical scenarios."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Subacute combined degeneration affecting dorsal columns and corticospinal tracts): Vitamin B12 deficiency, if prolonged, can progress to subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord, affecting both the dorsal columns, causing loss of vibration and position sense, and the lateral corticospinal tracts, causing spasticity, a combination unique to B12 deficiency among the common nutrient deficiencies.", "case_id": "C2", "case_text": "A 5-month-old male infant is brought to clinic because he has become increasingly lethargic and has stopped reaching new motor milestones over the past 6 weeks. He is exclusively breastfed. His mother, who is otherwise healthy, has followed a strict vegan diet for the past 4 years without any vitamin supplementation. On examination the infant is hypotonic, has poor eye contact, and is pale. A complete blood count reveals macrocytic anemia.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 49, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "Which of the following is the most appropriate long-term recommendation for this family to prevent recurrence of this problem in future children?", "options": {"A": "The mother should discontinue breastfeeding largely for all future children", "B": "The infant should be started on an adult multivitamin rather than an infant formulation", "C": "No supplementation is needed once this episode has been treated", "D": "The mother should take a vitamin B12 supplement while breastfeeding any future infants"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The mother should take a vitamin B12 supplement while breastfeeding any future infants): Because the underlying problem is inadequate maternal B12 intake reflected in breast milk, the most direct and appropriate long-term solution is for the mother to take B12 supplementation while breastfeeding, which corrects the deficiency at its source while preserving the benefits of breastfeeding.", "case_id": "C2", "case_text": "A 5-month-old male infant is brought to clinic because he has become increasingly lethargic and has stopped reaching new motor milestones over the past 6 weeks. He is exclusively breastfed. His mother, who is otherwise healthy, has followed a strict vegan diet for the past 4 years without any vitamin supplementation. On examination the infant is hypotonic, has poor eye contact, and is pale. A complete blood count reveals macrocytic anemia.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 51, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 16-year-old girl is brought to clinic by her parents, who are concerned she has lost a significant amount of weight over the past 6 months. Her body mass index is now well below the expected range for her age, she has not had a menstrual period in 4 months, and she expresses intense fear of gaining weight despite her low weight. Follow-up with preventive care has generally been consistent in the past. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Bulimia nervosa", "B": "Binge eating disorder", "C": "Anorexia nervosa", "D": "Major depressive disorder alone."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Anorexia nervosa): Significantly low body weight, intense fear of weight gain, and secondary amenorrhea in an adolescent describe the classic presentation of anorexia nervosa."}, {"id": 52, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 22-year-old woman with a normal body mass index presents for evaluation of dental enamel erosion and calluses on the dorsal surface of her right hand. Laboratory studies show a metabolic alkalosis with hypokalemia. She admits to inducing vomiting after most meals. There is no other significant past medical history, and no daily medications are taken. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Bulimia nervosa", "B": "Anorexia nervosa, restricting subtype.", "C": "Binge eating disorder", "D": "Rumination disorder"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Bulimia nervosa): Recurrent self-induced vomiting at a normal body weight, with dental erosion, knuckle calluses (Russell sign), and hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis, is characteristic of bulimia nervosa."}, {"id": 53, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 55-year-old man is hospitalized for severe malnutrition after several weeks of minimal oral intake related to untreated depression. The medical team plans to begin nutritional support. There is no other significant past medical history, and no daily medications are taken. Vital signs are stable, and the rest of the examination is unremarkable aside from the findings above. This visit is being conducted as part of routine, guideline-based preventive care. Which of the following is the most important consideration to prevent a life-threatening complication during the first days of feeding?", "options": {"A": "Start feeding slowly while proactively monitoring and repleting phosphate, potassium, and magnesium", "B": "Begin feeding at full caloric goals immediately to reverse deficits quickly", "C": "Withhold all electrolyte supplementation until levels are confirmed abnormal", "D": "Restrict all fluids during the first week without fully considering feeding rate"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Start feeding slowly while proactively monitoring and repleting phosphate, potassium, and magnesium): In a severely malnourished patient, reintroducing nutrition triggers an insulin surge that drives phosphate, potassium, and magnesium into cells, and starting feeding slowly while proactively monitoring and repleting these electrolytes is the standard approach to prevent refeeding syndrome, which can cause fatal arrhythmias and respiratory failure."}, {"id": 54, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 45-year-old woman hospitalized for a severe pneumonia has a low serum albumin level. Her physician is concerned this reflects poor nutritional status. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. This visit is being conducted as part of routine, guideline-based preventive care. Vital signs are stable, and the rest of the examination is unremarkable aside from the findings above. Which of the following statements about serum albumin in this setting is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "A low albumin level is generally a reliable, specific marker of malnutrition", "B": "Albumin levels change within hours and reflect only very recent intake", "C": "Albumin is also a negative acute-phase reactant, so it can fall with inflammation independent of nutrition", "D": "Albumin is unaffected by systemic inflammation or acute illness, as one consideration among several raised by this scenario."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Albumin is also a negative acute-phase reactant, so it can fall with inflammation independent of nutrition): Albumin has a long half-life and its level is also influenced by systemic inflammation, since it behaves as a negative acute-phase reactant and falls during acute illness independent of true nutritional status, which limits its specificity as an isolated nutrition marker in an acutely ill patient."}, {"id": 55, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 19-year-old college student with binge eating episodes several times per week, without any compensatory vomiting, laxative use, or excessive exercise, has a body mass index of 29. No acute concerns beyond those described above prompted this visit. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Bulimia nervosa", "B": "Anorexia nervosa, binge-purge subtype", "C": "Night eating syndrome only", "D": "Binge eating disorder"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Binge eating disorder): Recurrent binge eating episodes without regular compensatory behaviors such as purging, laxative misuse, or excessive exercise define binge eating disorder, which is now recognized as the most common eating disorder overall and is often associated with overweight or obesity."}, {"id": 56, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 17-year-old girl with anorexia nervosa and a body mass index of 15 is admitted for inpatient nutritional rehabilitation. On hospital day 2 of refeeding, she develops new weakness and an irregular heart rhythm. Laboratory studies reveal a phosphate level of 1.0 milligrams per deciliter. No acute concerns beyond those described above prompted this visit. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. Which of the following is the most likely explanation for this new finding?", "options": {"A": "Acute kidney injury causing phosphate retention", "B": "Refeeding syndrome causing an intracellular shift of phosphate", "C": "Hyperparathyroidism causing increased phosphate excretion", "D": "A normal, expected response to improved nutritional intake"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Refeeding syndrome causing an intracellular shift of phosphate): In a severely malnourished patient, reintroducing calories stimulates insulin release, which drives phosphate, along with potassium and magnesium, into cells, producing profound hypophosphatemia that can cause muscle weakness and cardiac arrhythmia, the hallmark of refeeding syndrome."}, {"id": 57, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 24-year-old woman with a body mass index of 17 and a 2-year history of amenorrhea undergoes a bone density scan, which reveals significantly reduced bone mineral density for her age. There is no other significant past medical history, and no daily medications are taken. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. No acute concerns beyond those described above prompted this visit. Which of the following best explains this finding?", "options": {"A": "Chronic estrogen deficiency from prolonged low body weight and amenorrhea", "B": "A primary parathyroid disorder unrelated to her weight history", "C": "Excessive dietary calcium intake causing paradoxical bone loss", "D": "Normal bone density variation unrelated to her menstrual history"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Chronic estrogen deficiency from prolonged low body weight and amenorrhea): Prolonged low body weight in conditions such as anorexia nervosa suppresses hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal function, producing amenorrhea and chronic estrogen deficiency, which in turn impairs bone formation and accelerates bone loss, explaining the low bone density seen here."}, {"id": 58, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 30-year-old man with a body mass index of 42 and type 2 diabetes has not achieved adequate weight loss despite 18 months of structured lifestyle counseling and a trial of pharmacotherapy. There is no other significant past medical history, and no daily medications are taken. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. This visit is being conducted as part of routine, guideline-based preventive care. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Continue the same lifestyle counseling indefinitely without other changes", "B": "Refer for evaluation for bariatric surgery", "C": "Recommend an extended water-only fasting regimen", "D": "Discontinue all diabetes medications to promote weight loss"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Refer for evaluation for bariatric surgery): Bariatric surgery is appropriate for patients with a body mass index of 40 or above, or a body mass index of 35 or above with a significant obesity-related comorbidity such as type 2 diabetes (some newer guidance lowers this comorbidity threshold to a body mass index of 30 to 35). This patient's body mass index of 42 already meets the standard threshold on its own, particularly after an inadequate response to lifestyle and pharmacologic measures, making surgical referral the appropriate next step here."}, {"id": 59, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 26-year-old woman with bulimia nervosa is being evaluated. Her physician wants to identify a physical examination clue specific to repeated self-induced vomiting. Follow-up with preventive care has generally been consistent in the past. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. Which of the following findings is most specific to this behavior?", "options": {"A": "Generalized hair thinning affecting the whole scalp, as one consideration among several raised by this scenario.", "B": "Bilateral parotid gland swelling that resolves and recurs", "C": "Calluses on the dorsal surface of the fingers used to induce vomiting", "D": "Fine lanugo hair over the trunk and face"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Calluses on the dorsal surface of the fingers used to induce vomiting): Repeated use of the fingers to induce vomiting produces characteristic calluses or scarring on the dorsal knuckles, known as Russell sign, which is relatively specific to this behavior."}, {"id": 62, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Bulimia nervosa", "B": "Major depressive disorder with weight loss.", "C": "Hyperthyroidism", "D": "Anorexia nervosa"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Anorexia nervosa): A significantly low body weight, an intense fear of being fat despite objective thinness, amenorrhea, bradycardia, hypotension, and lanugo hair together form the classic presentation of anorexia nervosa.", "case_id": "C3", "case_text": "A 15-year-old girl is brought to clinic by her mother, who is concerned about a 15 kilogram weight loss over the past 8 months. The patient states she feels 'fat' despite her mother's protests that she is very thin. She has not had a menstrual period in 5 months. Vital signs show a heart rate of 48 beats per minute and blood pressure of 88/56 mmHg. Examination reveals fine, soft hair over her back and arms, and her body mass index is calculated at 15.5.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 63, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "Which of the following best explains her bradycardia and hypotension?", "options": {"A": "Physiologic adaptation to chronic starvation with reduced metabolic demand", "B": "An underlying primary cardiac conduction system disease", "C": "Excessive caffeine intake reported earlier in the history, as one consideration among several raised by this scenario.", "D": "Acute myocarditis from a recent viral illness"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Physiologic adaptation to chronic starvation with reduced metabolic demand): Chronic starvation lowers metabolic rate and cardiac workload as an adaptive response, producing bradycardia and hypotension that are common and expected findings in significantly underweight patients with anorexia nervosa, and that generally improve with weight restoration.", "case_id": "C3", "case_text": "A 15-year-old girl is brought to clinic by her mother, who is concerned about a 15 kilogram weight loss over the past 8 months. The patient states she feels 'fat' despite her mother's protests that she is very thin. She has not had a menstrual period in 5 months. Vital signs show a heart rate of 48 beats per minute and blood pressure of 88/56 mmHg. Examination reveals fine, soft hair over her back and arms, and her body mass index is calculated at 15.5.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 64, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "She is admitted for inpatient nutritional rehabilitation. Which of the following complications is most important to anticipate and prevent during the first several days of refeeding?", "options": {"A": "Acute pancreatitis from excessive fat intake", "B": "Refeeding-associated hypercalcemia, as one consideration among several raised by this scenario.", "C": "Refeeding syndrome with hypophosphatemia, hypokalemia, and hypomagnesemia", "D": "Iron overload from oral iron supplementation"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Refeeding syndrome with hypophosphatemia, hypokalemia, and hypomagnesemia): Severely malnourished patients like this one are at high risk for refeeding syndrome when nutrition is reintroduced, since an insulin surge drives phosphate, potassium, and magnesium into cells, producing electrolyte derangements that can cause cardiac arrhythmia and respiratory failure if not anticipated and managed with slow refeeding and proactive electrolyte repletion.", "case_id": "C3", "case_text": "A 15-year-old girl is brought to clinic by her mother, who is concerned about a 15 kilogram weight loss over the past 8 months. The patient states she feels 'fat' despite her mother's protests that she is very thin. She has not had a menstrual period in 5 months. Vital signs show a heart rate of 48 beats per minute and blood pressure of 88/56 mmHg. Examination reveals fine, soft hair over her back and arms, and her body mass index is calculated at 15.5.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 65, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "Her bone density scan reveals osteopenia. Which of the following best explains this finding in the context of her overall presentation?", "options": {"A": "A coincidental, unrelated primary bone disease, in the context of this specific presentation.", "B": "Excess dietary calcium intake impairing bone remodeling", "C": "Vitamin D toxicity from excessive supplementation", "D": "Chronic hypoestrogenism from prolonged amenorrhea and low body weight"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Chronic hypoestrogenism from prolonged amenorrhea and low body weight): Prolonged low body weight suppresses the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis, leading to amenorrhea and a chronic hypoestrogenic state that impairs bone formation and increases bone resorption, explaining the osteopenia commonly seen in anorexia nervosa.", "case_id": "C3", "case_text": "A 15-year-old girl is brought to clinic by her mother, who is concerned about a 15 kilogram weight loss over the past 8 months. The patient states she feels 'fat' despite her mother's protests that she is very thin. She has not had a menstrual period in 5 months. Vital signs show a heart rate of 48 beats per minute and blood pressure of 88/56 mmHg. Examination reveals fine, soft hair over her back and arms, and her body mass index is calculated at 15.5.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 66, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 45-year-old woman with no personal or family history of breast cancer asks her physician when she should begin routine mammography screening. This visit is being conducted as part of routine, guideline-based preventive care. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. Vital signs are stable, and the rest of the examination is unremarkable aside from the findings above. Which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Delay mammography screening until age 60 given her average risk", "B": "Mammography is not recommended for any average-risk woman", "C": "Begin mammography screening now and continue every 1 to 2 years", "D": "Begin screening only if she develops a palpable breast mass"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Begin mammography screening now and continue every 1 to 2 years): For average-risk women, mammography screening is generally recommended starting somewhere in the range of age 40 to 50 and continuing every 1 to 2 years through at least the mid-70s, so beginning now at age 45 is appropriate and consistent with recommended timing."}, {"id": 68, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 50-year-old man with no personal or family history of colorectal cancer and no symptoms asks about colorectal cancer screening options. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. This visit is being conducted as part of routine, guideline-based preventive care. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. Which of the following is an appropriate screening strategy for him?", "options": {"A": "Colonoscopy every 10 years or annual fecal immunochemical testing", "B": "No screening is needed until age 60 given his average risk", "C": "A single lifetime flexible sigmoidoscopy is fully sufficient", "D": "Screening should only be pursued if he develops rectal bleeding"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Colonoscopy every 10 years or annual fecal immunochemical testing): Average-risk adults should begin colorectal cancer screening at age 45, so this 50-year-old man is already within the recommended window, and accepted strategies include colonoscopy every 10 years or annual fecal immunochemical testing, among other validated options."}, {"id": 69, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 62-year-old man who has smoked one pack of cigarettes daily for the past 35 years and continues to smoke presents for a routine visit. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. This visit is being conducted as part of routine, guideline-based preventive care. Which of the following is the most appropriate cancer screening recommendation specific to his smoking history?", "options": {"A": "Annual low-dose computed tomography of the chest", "B": "Annual chest radiography instead of computed tomography", "C": "No lung cancer screening is recommended until age 70", "D": "Sputum cytology performed every 6 months"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Annual low-dose computed tomography of the chest): Adults age 50 to 80 with a significant smoking history, such as this patient's 35 pack-year history, who currently smoke or quit within the past 15 years, are recommended to undergo annual low-dose computed tomography of the chest for lung cancer screening, which has been shown to reduce lung cancer mortality in this population."}, {"id": 70, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 66-year-old woman with no prior bone density testing asks her physician whether she needs a screening test for osteoporosis. She has no additional risk factors beyond her age and sex. Follow-up with preventive care has generally been consistent in the past. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. Which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Screening is unnecessary unless she has already sustained a fracture", "B": "Dual-energy x-ray absorptiometry screening is recommended starting at age 65", "C": "Screening should be delayed until she reaches age 75", "D": "A plain radiograph of the spine is the preferred screening test"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Dual-energy x-ray absorptiometry screening is recommended starting at age 65): Routine dual-energy x-ray absorptiometry screening for osteoporosis is recommended for all women starting at age 65 regardless of additional risk factors, since bone loss accelerates after menopause and early detection allows for preventive treatment before a fragility fracture occurs."}, {"id": 71, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 68-year-old man who quit smoking 10 years ago after a 40 pack-year history has never had an abdominal aortic aneurysm screening ultrasound. There is no other significant past medical history, and no daily medications are taken. Follow-up with preventive care has generally been consistent in the past. Vital signs are stable, and the rest of the examination is unremarkable aside from the findings above. Which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation?", "options": {"A": "No screening is indicated since he quit smoking over a decade ago", "B": "Annual abdominal ultrasound screening for the rest of his life", "C": "A one-time screening abdominal ultrasound for abdominal aortic aneurysm", "D": "Screening should be deferred until he develops abdominal pain"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A one-time screening abdominal ultrasound for abdominal aortic aneurysm): Men age 65 to 75 who have ever smoked are recommended to undergo a one-time screening ultrasound for abdominal aortic aneurysm regardless of how long ago they quit, since prior smoking exposure remains a major risk factor for aneurysm development."}, {"id": 72, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 30-year-old woman with a confirmed BRCA1 mutation and a strong family history of early-onset breast cancer asks about her screening plan. Vital signs are stable, and the rest of the examination is unremarkable aside from the findings above. This visit is being conducted as part of routine, guideline-based preventive care. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. Which of the following statements about her situation is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Her screening plan should be identical to that of an average-risk woman", "B": "She should wait until age 50 to begin any breast cancer screening", "C": "Genetic mutation carriers do not benefit from earlier or more frequent imaging, as one factor considered during routine evaluation.", "D": "She should begin more intensive breast cancer screening earlier than average-risk women"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (She should begin more intensive breast cancer screening earlier than average-risk women): Women with a confirmed BRCA mutation and a strong family history of early-onset breast cancer are at substantially elevated risk and are recommended to begin more intensive screening, often including earlier initiation and supplemental imaging such as breast magnetic resonance imaging, rather than following the average-risk schedule."}, {"id": 73, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 40-year-old man with a father who had a myocardial infarction at age 45 asks about lipid screening. He has no personal cardiovascular history and does not smoke. Follow-up with preventive care has generally been consistent in the past. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. Vital signs are stable, and the rest of the examination is unremarkable aside from the findings above. Which of the following statements best applies?", "options": {"A": "Lipid screening should rarely begin before age 65 without fully considering family history, as one possibility to consider in this context.", "B": "Family history of early cardiovascular disease has no bearing on screening timing", "C": "His family history supports beginning lipid screening now rather than waiting for a later average-risk starting age", "D": "He should wait until he develops chest pain before any lipid testing"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (His family history supports beginning lipid screening now rather than waiting for a later average-risk starting age): A father with a myocardial infarction at an unusually young age is a recognized risk factor that supports earlier lipid screening than the general average-risk starting age, allowing for earlier identification and management of dyslipidemia to reduce his own cardiovascular risk."}, {"id": 75, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 70-year-old woman with a personal history of a first-degree relative diagnosed with colorectal cancer at age 42 is now overdue for screening after moving to a new city. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. No acute concerns beyond those described above prompted this visit. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. Which of the following statements about her situation is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Her family history is not relevant since colorectal cancer screening is standardized for everyone", "B": "She should not be screened in this setting given her current age", "C": "Only genetic testing, rather than colonoscopy, is appropriate for her situation", "D": "She should have begun screening earlier than the average-risk starting age given her family history"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (She should have begun screening earlier than the average-risk starting age given her family history): A first-degree relative diagnosed with colorectal cancer at a young age is an established indication to begin screening earlier than the standard average-risk age and often to use shorter surveillance intervals, reflecting her elevated personal risk."}, {"id": 76, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 45-year-old woman has just undergone a hysterectomy with removal of the cervix for benign uterine fibroids. She has no history of abnormal cervical cytology. This visit is being conducted as part of routine, guideline-based preventive care. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. Vital signs are stable, and the rest of the examination is unremarkable aside from the findings above. Which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation regarding future cervical cancer screening?", "options": {"A": "She should continue cervical cytology screening every 3 years indefinitely", "B": "Cervical cancer screening is no longer needed since the cervix has been removed for benign disease", "C": "She requires more frequent screening than before her surgery, as one aspect of the overall clinical picture here.", "D": "She should switch exclusively to HPV testing performed monthly"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cervical cancer screening is no longer needed since the cervix has been removed for benign disease): When the cervix is removed as part of a hysterectomy performed for benign disease, and there is no history of high-grade cervical dysplasia, routine cervical cancer screening is no longer necessary since there is no cervix remaining to screen."}, {"id": 77, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 52-year-old woman without significant risk factors is deciding between cervical cancer screening strategies. Follow-up with preventive care has generally been consistent in the past. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. Which of the following options is an appropriate strategy for her age group?", "options": {"A": "Cytology alone every 10 years", "B": "High-risk HPV testing alone every 5 years", "C": "HPV testing alone every 6 months", "D": "No further screening is needed after age 50"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (High-risk HPV testing alone every 5 years): For women age 30 to 65, accepted strategies include high-risk HPV testing alone every 5 years, cytology alone every 3 years, or co-testing every 5 years, so HPV testing alone at a 5-year interval is an appropriate option in this age group."}, {"id": 78, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 63-year-old woman has had consistently normal cervical cancer screening results throughout her adult life, with her most recent co-testing at age 62 showing normal cytology and a negative HPV test. Follow-up with preventive care has generally been consistent in the past. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. Which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation?", "options": {"A": "She must continue cervical cancer screening indefinitely without fully considering age, as one possibility to consider in this context.", "B": "She should switch to annual screening starting immediately, as a general clinical consideration in similar cases.", "C": "She may reasonably stop routine cervical cancer screening once she reaches age 65 with adequate prior negative results", "D": "She requires colposcopy now despite her normal results, in the broader context of this presentation."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (She may reasonably stop routine cervical cancer screening once she reaches age 65 with adequate prior negative results): For women with a history of adequate prior screening and no history of high-grade dysplasia, screening can reasonably be discontinued around age 65, so this patient with consistently normal results approaching that age fits the criteria to stop routine screening once she reaches that threshold."}, {"id": 80, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 38-year-old woman with a body mass index of 24 and no personal history of diabetes or prediabetes asks whether she needs diabetes screening now. She has no family history of diabetes. This visit is being conducted as part of routine, guideline-based preventive care. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. Vital signs are stable, and the rest of the examination is unremarkable aside from the findings above. Which of the following statements is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "All adults without fully considering age or risk factors should be screened annually starting in their twenties", "B": "She should be screened only if she becomes pregnant, in the broader context of this presentation.", "C": "A normal body mass index eliminates any future need for diabetes screening", "D": "At 38, she has reached the age around which routine screening reasonably begins per ADA guidance, even without additional risk factors"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (At 38, she has reached the age around which routine screening reasonably begins per ADA guidance, even without additional risk factors): The ADA recommends routine diabetes screening in average-risk adults starting around age 35 regardless of weight, while USPSTF restricts routine screening below that age to adults who are overweight or obese or who have other risk factors. This patient is 38, normal weight, and has no additional risk factors, so she has already reached the age at which routine screening is reasonable to begin under ADA guidance, rather than needing to have started screening years earlier as a young adult."}, {"id": 82, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 58-year-old woman with early menopause at age 40 and a history of long-term glucocorticoid use for rheumatoid arthritis asks when she should have bone density testing. There is no other significant past medical history, and no daily medications are taken. No acute concerns beyond those described above prompted this visit. Vital signs are stable, and the rest of the examination is unremarkable aside from the findings above. Which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation?", "options": {"A": "She should be screened earlier than the routine age 65 starting point given her risk factors", "B": "She should wait until age 65 like an average-risk woman, as one consideration among several raised by this scenario.", "C": "Bone density testing is not useful in patients on glucocorticoids", "D": "She should have testing only if she sustains a fracture first"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (She should be screened earlier than the routine age 65 starting point given her risk factors): Early menopause and chronic glucocorticoid use are both established risk factors for accelerated bone loss, and postmenopausal women under 65 with such risk factors are recommended to undergo earlier bone density screening rather than waiting for the routine age of 65."}, {"id": 83, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 41-year-old man with a strong family history of premature coronary artery disease undergoes cardiovascular risk assessment. No acute concerns beyond those described above prompted this visit. There is no other significant past medical history, and no daily medications are taken. Vital signs are stable, and the rest of the examination is unremarkable aside from the findings above. Which of the following best reflects appropriate use of his atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease risk score?", "options": {"A": "It is used exclusively to diagnose acute coronary syndrome, as one factor considered during routine evaluation.", "B": "It replaces the need for blood pressure measurement largely", "C": "It is calculated only after a myocardial infarction has occurred", "D": "It helps guide the decision of whether to initiate statin therapy for primary prevention"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It helps guide the decision of whether to initiate statin therapy for primary prevention): Calculating a patient's estimated atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease risk score is a standard part of primary prevention counseling, helping determine whether the potential benefit of starting statin therapy outweighs its risks for a given patient before any cardiovascular event has occurred."}, {"id": 84, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 36-year-old woman with polycystic ovary syndrome and a family history of type 2 diabetes asks whether her risk factors change any of her screening recommendations compared with an average-risk woman her age. This visit is being conducted as part of routine, guideline-based preventive care. There is no other significant past medical history, and no daily medications are taken. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. Which of the following is the most accurate response?", "options": {"A": "Her risk factors have no effect on any screening recommendations, as one consideration among several raised by this scenario.", "B": "Her risk factors support earlier diabetes screening than would be recommended for an average-risk woman her age", "C": "She should be screened only for cervical cancer and nothing else", "D": "Polycystic ovary syndrome eliminates her need for future diabetes screening"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Her risk factors support earlier diabetes screening than would be recommended for an average-risk woman her age): Polycystic ovary syndrome and a family history of type 2 diabetes are both recognized risk factors that support earlier initiation of diabetes screening compared with an average-risk woman of the same age without these factors."}, {"id": 86, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A mother brings her 2-month-old daughter for a well visit and vaccination. The infant was born at term with no complications. Follow-up with preventive care has generally been consistent in the past. There is no significant past medical history and preventive care has been up to date. Growth parameters are appropriate for age, and the remainder of the examination is unremarkable. Which of the following vaccines is appropriately given as part of the routine 2-month visit?", "options": {"A": "Measles, mumps, and rubella vaccine", "B": "Diphtheria, tetanus, and acellular pertussis vaccine", "C": "Varicella vaccine, as one consideration among several raised by this scenario.", "D": "Human papillomavirus vaccine"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Diphtheria, tetanus, and acellular pertussis vaccine): The DTaP series routinely begins at 2 months of age as part of the standard childhood immunization schedule, along with several other vaccines given at this visit."}, {"id": 88, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 6-month-old infant has not yet started the rotavirus vaccine series because of a delayed immigration to the United States. Her pediatrician reviews the vaccine's age restrictions. There is no significant past medical history and preventive care has been up to date. Growth parameters are appropriate for age, and the remainder of the examination is unremarkable. This visit is being conducted as part of routine, guideline-based preventive care. Which of the following best explains why rotavirus vaccination has a strict maximum age for initiation?", "options": {"A": "The vaccine loses all effectiveness after 6 months of age", "B": "Rotavirus infection itself becomes very difficult to prevent after infancy, as sometimes noted in comparable clinical scenarios.", "C": "An association between the vaccine and intussusception when given outside the recommended age window", "D": "The vaccine is only available in limited quantities each year"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (An association between the vaccine and intussusception when given outside the recommended age window): The rotavirus vaccine series has strict age limits for starting and completing doses because of an observed association with intussusception when the vaccine is given outside its recommended age window, making the risk-benefit balance less favorable if started too late."}, {"id": 89, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 12-year-old girl is being seen for a routine visit, and her mother asks about starting the human papillomavirus vaccine series. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. No acute concerns beyond those described above prompted this visit. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. Which of the following statements about this vaccine is most accurate for this patient?", "options": {"A": "The vaccine cannot be started until age 15 at the earliest", "B": "This vaccine is only recommended for adolescent boys, not girls", "C": "The vaccine provides protection only against a single HPV strain, as one aspect of the overall clinical picture here.", "D": "Starting before age 15 allows for a shorter 2-dose vaccine series"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Starting before age 15 allows for a shorter 2-dose vaccine series): When the human papillomavirus vaccine series is started before age 15, only 2 doses are needed to complete the series, whereas starting at 15 or older, or in immunocompromised patients, requires a 3-dose series, so this 12-year-old is in the age range that allows for the shorter regimen."}, {"id": 90, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 68-year-old woman without any qualifying chronic conditions asks her physician about vaccines recommended specifically because of her age. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. This visit is being conducted as part of routine, guideline-based preventive care. Which of the following vaccines should be recommended based on her age alone?", "options": {"A": "Rotavirus vaccine", "B": "Hepatitis A vaccine, unless she has another specific indication", "C": "Recombinant zoster (shingles) vaccine", "D": "Human papillomavirus vaccine"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Recombinant zoster (shingles) vaccine): The recombinant zoster vaccine is recommended starting at age 50, so this patient at 68 without any additional qualifying conditions should be offered it based on her age alone, along with the pneumococcal vaccine series once she reaches 65."}, {"id": 92, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 45-year-old man received his last tetanus-containing vaccine 9 years ago and now presents with a deep, contaminated wound from a rusty nail. His wound care is otherwise appropriate. No acute concerns beyond those described above prompted this visit. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. Vital signs are stable, and the rest of the examination is unremarkable aside from the findings above. Which of the following is the most appropriate vaccine-related step?", "options": {"A": "No booster is needed since it has been less than 10 years", "B": "Administer a Td or Tdap booster now given the wound type and time since last dose", "C": "Administer a full primary tetanus vaccine series from the beginning, as one detail relevant to this differential.", "D": "Administer only a rabies vaccine given the animal-associated wound source"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Administer a Td or Tdap booster now given the wound type and time since last dose): For a contaminated or high-risk wound, a booster is recommended if it has been 5 or more years since the last tetanus-containing vaccine, rather than waiting for the standard 10-year interval used for clean, minor wounds, making a booster now appropriate given both the wound type and the 9-year interval."}, {"id": 94, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 40-year-old man is planning to travel to a country where yellow fever vaccination is required for entry. He has a severe egg allergy with a history of anaphylaxis. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. No acute concerns beyond those described above prompted this visit. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Refer him for specialized allergy evaluation before considering yellow fever vaccination", "B": "Administer the yellow fever vaccine without any additional precautions, as one detail relevant to this differential.", "C": "Tell him that his allergy has no relevance to this specific vaccine", "D": "Cancel his travel plans since no alternative approach exists"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Refer him for specialized allergy evaluation before considering yellow fever vaccination): Because some yellow fever vaccine formulations are produced using egg-based methods, a severe egg allergy with a history of anaphylaxis requires careful evaluation, often with allergy specialist input and possible desensitization protocols, before proceeding with vaccination, rather than either administering it without precaution or assuming no risk exists."}, {"id": 95, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 55-year-old woman with chronic kidney disease asks her physician which pneumococcal vaccination approach is most appropriate for her given her medical condition. There is no other significant past medical history, and no daily medications are taken. No acute concerns beyond those described above prompted this visit. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. Which of the following statements is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Pneumococcal vaccination is typically only given starting at age 65 without fully considering medical conditions", "B": "Chronic kidney disease has no bearing on pneumococcal vaccine timing, as sometimes noted in comparable clinical scenarios.", "C": "Her chronic kidney disease qualifies her for pneumococcal vaccination before the routine age of 65", "D": "She should avoid pneumococcal vaccination largely given her kidney disease"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Her chronic kidney disease qualifies her for pneumococcal vaccination before the routine age of 65): Certain chronic medical conditions, including chronic kidney disease, qualify younger adults for pneumococcal vaccination before the routine age 65 recommendation that applies to the general population, reflecting their elevated risk for invasive pneumococcal disease."}, {"id": 97, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 60-year-old man asks whether he should receive an annual influenza vaccine given his age and his well-controlled hypertension. This visit is being conducted as part of routine, guideline-based preventive care. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. Which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Influenza vaccination is unnecessary until age 65, as one factor considered during routine evaluation.", "B": "Influenza vaccination is contraindicated in patients with hypertension, in the context of this specific presentation.", "C": "He should receive an annual influenza vaccine as recommended for essentially all adults without contraindication", "D": "He only needs the vaccine once every 5 years rather than annually"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (He should receive an annual influenza vaccine as recommended for essentially all adults without contraindication): Annual influenza vaccination is recommended for essentially everyone 6 months of age and older without a specific contraindication, regardless of age group or the presence of hypertension, since influenza strains change yearly and immunity wanes over time."}, {"id": 98, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 25-year-old healthcare worker who never completed her childhood hepatitis B vaccine series asks about starting it now as an adult. Follow-up with preventive care has generally been consistent in the past. There is no other significant past medical history, and no daily medications are taken. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. Which of the following statements is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "The hepatitis B vaccine can only be given during infancy, as one consideration among several raised by this scenario.", "B": "The hepatitis B vaccine series can be safely completed later in life if missed in childhood", "C": "Adults who missed this series in infancy are for the foreseeable future ineligible", "D": "The vaccine is contraindicated in healthcare workers due to occupational exposure risk"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The hepatitis B vaccine series can be safely completed later in life if missed in childhood): Vaccines missed in childhood, including the hepatitis B series, can generally be given later in life through an appropriate catch-up schedule, and this is particularly important for healthcare workers given their occupational exposure risk to bloodborne pathogens."}, {"id": 105, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 52-year-old man who smokes cigarettes daily wants to quit and asks whether counseling alone or medication alone would be more effective. No acute concerns beyond those described above prompted this visit. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. There is no other significant past medical history, and no daily medications are taken. Which of the following statements is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Counseling alone is generally sufficient and medication adds no benefit", "B": "Combining behavioral counseling with pharmacotherapy is more effective than either approach used alone", "C": "Medication alone is generally superior and counseling adds no benefit", "D": "Neither counseling nor medication has been shown to improve quit rates"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Combining behavioral counseling with pharmacotherapy is more effective than either approach used alone): Evidence consistently shows that combining behavioral counseling with pharmacotherapy, such as varenicline, bupropion, or nicotine replacement therapy, roughly doubles quit rates compared with either approach used alone, making combined treatment the most effective evidence-based strategy."}, {"id": 106, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 34-year-old woman is being screened for depression at a routine visit using a 2-question tool asking about little interest or pleasure in doing things and feeling down, depressed, or hopeless over the past 2 weeks. She answers yes to both. No acute concerns beyond those described above prompted this visit. There is no other significant past medical history, and no daily medications are taken. Vital signs are stable, and the rest of the examination is unremarkable aside from the findings above. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Immediately start an antidepressant medication without further evaluation", "B": "Disregard the result since this is only a brief screening tool", "C": "Refer directly to inpatient psychiatric hospitalization", "D": "Administer a more detailed depression assessment such as the PHQ-9"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Administer a more detailed depression assessment such as the PHQ-9): A positive result on the brief PHQ-2 screening tool should prompt a more detailed follow-up assessment, such as the PHQ-9, to better characterize symptom severity, duration, and any safety concerns before deciding on a specific management plan."}, {"id": 108, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 27-year-old man reports drinking 4 to 5 alcoholic beverages most nights of the week and says he has tried unsuccessfully to cut back twice in the past year. He denies any current desire for treatment. No acute concerns beyond those described above prompted this visit. Vital signs are stable, and the rest of the examination is unremarkable aside from the findings above. There is no other significant past medical history, and no daily medications are taken. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Refuse any further care until he agrees to stop drinking substantially", "B": "Document the finding without any further discussion or follow-up plan", "C": "Use a nonjudgmental, motivational approach to discuss his drinking and options for support", "D": "Report him to law enforcement given his level of alcohol consumption"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Use a nonjudgmental, motivational approach to discuss his drinking and options for support): A nonjudgmental, motivational approach that explores the patient's own perspective, provides information, and offers a range of support options respects patient autonomy while still addressing a clear pattern of unhealthy alcohol use and prior unsuccessful attempts to cut back."}, {"id": 109, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 60-year-old man with long-standing hypertension and a sedentary lifestyle asks his physician what single lifestyle change would most broadly reduce his cardiovascular and overall mortality risk. There is no other significant past medical history, and no daily medications are taken. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. No acute concerns beyond those described above prompted this visit. Which of the following is the most evidence-supported recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Avoiding all dietary fat entirely", "B": "Daily high-dose vitamin E supplementation", "C": "Eliminating all carbohydrates from his diet", "D": "Regular moderate-intensity physical activity"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Regular moderate-intensity physical activity): Regular moderate-intensity physical activity has strong, consistent evidence for reducing cardiovascular events and all-cause mortality, making it one of the most broadly beneficial lifestyle interventions available for a sedentary patient with hypertension."}, {"id": 110, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 22-year-old college student is being screened for substance use at a routine visit. She reports occasional marijuana use with friends on weekends but denies any other drug use. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. This visit is being conducted as part of routine, guideline-based preventive care. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Immediately report her to school administration for disciplinary action, as one possibility to consider in this context.", "B": "Further explore the frequency, context, and any functional impact of her use in a nonjudgmental manner", "C": "Refuse to provide any further care until she stops all use largely", "D": "Document the finding and take no further action of any kind"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Further explore the frequency, context, and any functional impact of her use in a nonjudgmental manner): A disclosed pattern of substance use should prompt further, nonjudgmental exploration of frequency, context, associated risks, and any functional impact, which helps determine whether this represents low-risk experimentation or a pattern warranting more active intervention or referral."}, {"id": 111, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 58-year-old man with obesity and prediabetes asks his physician for a single most impactful dietary change he can make. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. Follow-up with preventive care has generally been consistent in the past. There is no other significant past medical history, and no daily medications are taken. Which of the following is the most broadly supported recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Reduce intake of refined carbohydrates and added sugars while increasing whole foods such as vegetables, fruits, and whole grains", "B": "Eliminate all forms of dietary protein to reduce metabolic strain, in the context of this specific presentation.", "C": "Adopt a diet based exclusively on a single food group, in the broader context of this presentation.", "D": "Avoid all forms of physical activity to conserve caloric intake"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reduce intake of refined carbohydrates and added sugars while increasing whole foods such as vegetables, fruits, and whole grains): Reducing refined carbohydrates and added sugars while emphasizing whole, minimally processed foods such as vegetables, fruits, and whole grains is a broadly supported dietary approach for reducing cardiometabolic risk in a patient with obesity and prediabetes."}, {"id": 112, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 40-year-old woman asks her physician about the health effects of moderate alcohol consumption, since she has read conflicting information online. Vital signs are stable, and the rest of the examination is unremarkable aside from the findings above. No acute concerns beyond those described above prompted this visit. There is no other significant past medical history, and no daily medications are taken. Which of the following statements best reflects current understanding?", "options": {"A": "Moderate alcohol consumption has been definitively proven to prevent all cardiovascular disease", "B": "Alcohol consumption at any amount is largely without any health risk, as one consideration among several raised by this scenario.", "C": "Even moderate alcohol consumption carries some health risks, and counseling should reflect an individualized risk discussion", "D": "There is no role for physician counseling regarding alcohol intake in any patient"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Even moderate alcohol consumption carries some health risks, and counseling should reflect an individualized risk discussion): Current evidence increasingly recognizes that even moderate alcohol consumption carries some level of health risk rather than being risk-free, so counseling should be individualized to a patient's overall health, personal risk factors, and preferences rather than presenting alcohol as either protective or entirely harmless."}, {"id": 113, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "Which of the following screening tools would have been most appropriate to identify his risk for an alcohol use disorder at an earlier visit?", "options": {"A": "The Mini-Cog", "B": "The CAGE questionnaire", "C": "The PHQ-9", "D": "The Timed Up and Go test"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The CAGE questionnaire): The CAGE questionnaire, which asks about cutting down, annoyance at criticism, guilt, and morning eye-opener drinking, directly corresponds to this patient's history and would have been an appropriate brief tool to identify his risk for alcohol use disorder before this acute presentation.", "case_id": "C5", "case_text": "A 52-year-old man with a 20-year history of heavy daily alcohol use is brought to the emergency department by his roommate, who reports the patient has been eating very little for the past week and has become increasingly confused. On examination he is disoriented to place and date, has horizontal nystagmus, and walks with a wide-based, unsteady gait. His roommate reports the patient often drinks in the morning to avoid feeling shaky and irritable.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 114, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate step in his management?", "options": {"A": "Administer intravenous glucose first, followed by thiamine afterward", "B": "Order brain imaging before initiating any treatment", "C": "Administer thiamine before or together with any intravenous glucose", "D": "Begin empiric antibiotic therapy for suspected meningitis"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Administer thiamine before or together with any intravenous glucose): His presentation of confusion, nystagmus, and ataxia is classic for Wernicke encephalopathy, and thiamine should be given before or together with glucose because glucose alone can worsen the encephalopathy by further depleting already low thiamine stores.", "case_id": "C5", "case_text": "A 52-year-old man with a 20-year history of heavy daily alcohol use is brought to the emergency department by his roommate, who reports the patient has been eating very little for the past week and has become increasingly confused. On examination he is disoriented to place and date, has horizontal nystagmus, and walks with a wide-based, unsteady gait. His roommate reports the patient often drinks in the morning to avoid feeling shaky and irritable.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 115, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "Despite appropriate treatment of his acute presentation, he is later found to have a persistent memory disorder characterized by an inability to form new memories and fabricated but confidently stated accounts of recent events. Which of the following terms best describes this chronic finding?", "options": {"A": "Wernicke encephalopathy", "B": "Delirium tremens", "C": "Alcoholic hallucinosis", "D": "Korsakoff syndrome"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Korsakoff syndrome): Korsakoff syndrome is the chronic, often irreversible amnestic disorder that can follow inadequately treated Wernicke encephalopathy, characterized by anterograde amnesia and confabulation with relative preservation of other cognitive functions.", "case_id": "C5", "case_text": "A 52-year-old man with a 20-year history of heavy daily alcohol use is brought to the emergency department by his roommate, who reports the patient has been eating very little for the past week and has become increasingly confused. On examination he is disoriented to place and date, has horizontal nystagmus, and walks with a wide-based, unsteady gait. His roommate reports the patient often drinks in the morning to avoid feeling shaky and irritable.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 116, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "His roommate asks what could have been done to prevent this outcome. Which of the following statements is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Earlier recognition of his alcohol use disorder and thiamine supplementation during any hospitalization or clinic visit could have reduced this risk", "B": "Nothing could have been done since this outcome is largely unpredictable, as one consideration among several raised by this scenario.", "C": "Only complete abstinence from alcohol for at least 10 years could have prevented this", "D": "This outcome is unrelated to nutritional status and cannot be prevented through supplementation, in the context of this specific presentation."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Earlier recognition of his alcohol use disorder and thiamine supplementation during any hospitalization or clinic visit could have reduced this risk): Earlier identification of his alcohol use disorder through routine screening, along with proactive thiamine supplementation, particularly during any hospitalization or clinical encounter for a patient with heavy alcohol use, are recognized strategies to reduce the risk of progressing to Wernicke encephalopathy and subsequent Korsakoff syndrome.", "case_id": "C5", "case_text": "A 52-year-old man with a 20-year history of heavy daily alcohol use is brought to the emergency department by his roommate, who reports the patient has been eating very little for the past week and has become increasingly confused. On examination he is disoriented to place and date, has horizontal nystagmus, and walks with a wide-based, unsteady gait. His roommate reports the patient often drinks in the morning to avoid feeling shaky and irritable.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 117, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 75-year-old woman who lives alone reports two falls in the past 6 months, neither resulting in injury. She takes a benzodiazepine for sleep, a diuretic, and an alpha-blocker for benign prostatic hyperplasia symptoms that were prescribed years ago in error. This visit is being conducted as part of routine, guideline-based preventive care. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. There is no other significant past medical history, and no daily medications are taken. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Reassure her that occasional falls without injury require no further evaluation, as one consideration among several raised by this scenario.", "B": "Recommend she use a wheelchair for the foreseeable future to eliminate all future fall risk", "C": "Refer her for hip replacement surgery as a preventive measure", "D": "Perform a comprehensive medication review to identify and address contributors to fall risk"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Perform a comprehensive medication review to identify and address contributors to fall risk): Polypharmacy, particularly with sedating or blood pressure lowering medications such as a benzodiazepine, diuretic, and alpha-blocker, is a major modifiable contributor to fall risk in older adults, making a comprehensive medication review an essential next step, especially when a medication appears to have been continued in error."}, {"id": 118, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 79-year-old man completes a Timed Up and Go test in 15 seconds during his annual wellness visit. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. Follow-up with preventive care has generally been consistent in the past. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. Which of the following is the most appropriate interpretation and next step?", "options": {"A": "This result is well within the normal range and requires no further action", "B": "This result suggests increased fall risk and should prompt further gait and balance evaluation", "C": "This result reflects normal aging and should be disregarded, as a general clinical consideration in similar cases.", "D": "This result indicates a need for immediate hip fracture surgery"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (This result suggests increased fall risk and should prompt further gait and balance evaluation): A Timed Up and Go time greater than about 12 seconds is associated with an increased risk of falls and should prompt further assessment of gait, balance, strength, and home safety, along with review of contributing medications."}, {"id": 119, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 70-year-old woman with a history of falls asks her physician about strategies to improve her home safety. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. No acute concerns beyond those described above prompted this visit. Which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation?", "options": {"A": "Install thick carpeting throughout to cushion any future falls", "B": "Recommend she avoid using her stairs largely for the rest of her life, as sometimes noted in comparable clinical scenarios.", "C": "Remove loose rugs and improve lighting throughout her home, particularly on stairways", "D": "Advise her to keep her home dimly lit to reduce glare"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Remove loose rugs and improve lighting throughout her home, particularly on stairways): Removing tripping hazards such as loose rugs and improving lighting, especially on stairways, are well-established, evidence-based components of home safety modification to reduce fall risk in older adults."}, {"id": 120, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 76-year-old man is noted to have unexplained bruising on both upper arms in a pattern suggestive of being grabbed, along with weight loss and poor hygiene despite living with his adult son who manages his finances. Follow-up with preventive care has generally been consistent in the past. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. Which of the following findings would most strongly suggest neglect specifically, rather than physical or financial abuse?", "options": {"A": "Evidence that his basic hygiene and nutritional needs are not being met despite adequate resources being available", "B": "The presence of finger-shaped bruising on his upper arms, as one aspect of the overall clinical picture here.", "C": "Unexplained withdrawals from his bank account by his son, in the broader context of this presentation.", "D": "A verbal threat made by his son in front of clinic staff"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Evidence that his basic hygiene and nutritional needs are not being met despite adequate resources being available): Neglect refers specifically to a caregiver's failure to provide necessary care, such as adequate nutrition and hygiene, despite having the resources or responsibility to do so, which is distinct from the direct physical force implied by bruising or the misuse of funds implied by unexplained withdrawals."}, {"id": 124, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 55-year-old woman with a small bowel obstruction that has not resolved after several days is being managed by the surgical team, who anticipate she will need bowel rest for an extended period. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. No acute concerns beyond those described above prompted this visit. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. Which of the following is the most appropriate nutritional strategy during this time?", "options": {"A": "Begin enteral tube feeding directly into her stomach", "B": "Advance her to a regular oral diet as tolerated", "C": "Provide only oral electrolyte solutions with no additional nutrition", "D": "Initiate parenteral nutrition given her nonfunctional gastrointestinal tract"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Initiate parenteral nutrition given her nonfunctional gastrointestinal tract): A bowel obstruction that has not resolved represents a nonfunctional gastrointestinal tract, making enteral feeding unsafe, so parenteral nutrition delivered intravenously is the appropriate strategy to meet her nutritional needs during this period of anticipated prolonged bowel rest."}, {"id": 129, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 40-year-old woman with short bowel syndrome following extensive small bowel resection requires long-term nutritional support at home. There is no other significant past medical history, and no daily medications are taken. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. Follow-up with preventive care has generally been consistent in the past. Which of the following statements about her likely nutritional management is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "She will likely require long-term home parenteral nutrition given her significantly reduced absorptive capacity", "B": "She should be transitioned immediately to a normal oral diet without any additional support, as sometimes discussed in similar clinical situations.", "C": "Enteral tube feeding alone will fully meet her nutritional needs without fully considering bowel length", "D": "No nutritional intervention is needed once her surgical wounds have healed"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (She will likely require long-term home parenteral nutrition given her significantly reduced absorptive capacity): Extensive small bowel resection resulting in short bowel syndrome often leaves insufficient absorptive surface area to meet nutritional needs through oral or enteral intake alone, making long-term parenteral nutrition a likely necessary component of her care, sometimes combined with some oral or enteral intake depending on her remaining bowel length and adaptation."}, {"id": 133, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 47-year-old man with well-controlled hypertension and no other medical conditions is due for his periodic preventive visit. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. Follow-up with preventive care has generally been consistent in the past. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. Which of the following best reflects an appropriate overall approach to this visit?", "options": {"A": "Address blood pressure control, appropriate age-based screening, and lifestyle counseling together", "B": "Focus exclusively on his blood pressure medication and address nothing else, as one factor considered during routine evaluation.", "C": "Order every available laboratory and imaging test without fully considering indication", "D": "Skip all preventive counseling since he has no acute complaints today"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Address blood pressure control, appropriate age-based screening, and lifestyle counseling together): A well-rounded preventive visit integrates chronic disease management, such as blood pressure control, with appropriate age- and risk-based screening recommendations and lifestyle counseling on topics such as diet, exercise, and substance use, rather than narrowly focusing on a single issue."}, {"id": 135, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 30-year-old man asks his physician about the difference between screening and diagnostic testing, using colorectal cancer as an example. There is no other significant past medical history, and no daily medications are taken. No acute concerns beyond those described above prompted this visit. Vital signs are within normal limits, and the remainder of the physical examination is unremarkable. Which of the following statements best describes screening in this context?", "options": {"A": "Screening is only performed after a patient develops symptoms such as rectal bleeding", "B": "Screening is performed in asymptomatic average- or elevated-risk individuals to detect disease before symptoms develop", "C": "Screening and diagnostic testing use identical criteria for choosing which test to perform, as one factor considered during routine evaluation.", "D": "Screening is reserved exclusively for patients with a confirmed genetic mutation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Screening is performed in asymptomatic average- or elevated-risk individuals to detect disease before symptoms develop): Screening tests, such as colonoscopy or fecal immunochemical testing for colorectal cancer, are applied to asymptomatic individuals at average or elevated risk with the goal of detecting disease at an earlier, more treatable stage, in contrast to diagnostic testing performed to evaluate a specific symptom or finding."}, {"id": 139, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 66-year-old man with a 45 pack-year smoking history who quit smoking 8 years ago asks whether he still needs lung cancer screening given how long ago he quit. Vital signs are stable, and the rest of the examination is unremarkable aside from the findings above. No acute concerns beyond those described above prompted this visit. There is no other significant past medical history, no tobacco use, and only occasional alcohol use. Which of the following statements is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "He is no longer eligible for screening since he has not smoked in several years", "B": "Lung cancer screening eligibility depends only on current age and not on smoking history, as one aspect of the overall clinical picture here.", "C": "He remains eligible for screening since he quit within the past 15 years and falls within the recommended age range", "D": "He should have stopped screening once he reached age 65, as one possibility to consider in this context."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (He remains eligible for screening since he quit within the past 15 years and falls within the recommended age range): Lung cancer screening recommendations apply to adults age 50 to 80 with a significant smoking history who currently smoke or who quit within the past 15 years, so this patient, who quit 8 years ago and remains within the recommended age range, is still eligible for screening."}, {"id": 140, "domain": "nutrition_and_preventive_care", "question": "A 36-year-old woman presents for a routine preventive visit and asks her physician to explain the body mass index categories so she understands where her own result falls. She is 165 centimeters tall and weighs 71 kilograms, giving a calculated body mass index of 26. She reports no chronic medical conditions, takes no daily medications, does not smoke, and exercises several times a week. Vital signs today are within normal limits, and the remainder of her physical examination is unremarkable aside from her weight. Which of the following ranges correctly matches the overweight category into which her body mass index falls?", "options": {"A": "A body mass index of 25 to 29.9", "B": "A body mass index of 18.5 to 24.9", "C": "A body mass index of 30 to 34.9", "D": "A body mass index below 18.5"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A body mass index of 25 to 29.9): A body mass index of 25 to 29.9 defines the overweight category, placing this patient's value of 26 within that range."}, {"id": 143, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A 30-year-old woman with well-controlled schizophrenia on a stable antipsychotic regimen presents for a scheduled hysteroscopy to evaluate abnormal uterine bleeding. She is calm and coherent and clearly explains the reason for the procedure, its risks, and the alternative of continued observation. Her sister, who accompanies her, insists the physician should speak only with her because she believes her sister cannot make good decisions due to her psychiatric diagnosis. Which of the following is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Assess the patient's capacity directly and proceed with her consent if she demonstrates capacity", "B": "Delay the procedure until a psychiatrist formally declares the patient legally competent", "C": "Ask the sister to sign as a co-decision-maker alongside the patient", "D": "Obtain consent from the sister because schizophrenia impairs decision-making capacity"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Assess the patient's capacity directly and proceed with her consent if she demonstrates capacity): a psychiatric diagnosis does not automatically remove decision-making capacity. Capacity must be assessed based on the patient's actual ability to understand, appreciate, reason, and communicate a choice about this particular procedure, all of which she demonstrates. Competence is a separate legal status determined by a court, not by a family member's opinion."}, {"id": 144, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A 22-year-old woman arrives unconscious after a motor vehicle collision with signs of an expanding abdominal hematoma and hypotension. No family members are present, and she carries no documentation naming a health care proxy. The trauma surgeon recommends an emergent exploratory laparotomy. Her vital signs show a blood pressure of 78/50 mmHg and a heart rate of 132 beats per minute, and point-of-care ultrasound confirms free intraperitoneal fluid consistent with active hemorrhage. Which of the following best describes the legal basis for proceeding with surgery without obtaining consent?", "options": {"A": "Substituted judgment exercised by the surgical team", "B": "Implied consent under the emergency exception", "C": "Waiver of the right to informed consent", "D": "Therapeutic privilege permitting nondisclosure"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Implied consent under the emergency exception): when a patient cannot communicate, no surrogate is immediately available, and delay would cause serious harm, the law presumes a reasonable person would consent to necessary lifesaving treatment. Therapeutic privilege and waiver both apply to a patient who can communicate but is having information withheld or declined, and substituted judgment describes how a surrogate should decide, neither of which fits this unrepresented, unconscious patient."}, {"id": 145, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A 16-year-old girl presents to a clinic requesting a prescription for oral contraceptives. She states she does not want her parents to know she is sexually active. She understands the risks and benefits of the medication and has no medical contraindications. Her parents have full legal custody and there is no history of conflict with the clinic. She reports using condoms inconsistently with her current partner and has already discussed the decision with a trusted school counselor, who encouraged her to seek medical care directly. Which of the following is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Contact the parents before prescribing given their legal custody", "B": "Decline to prescribe until the patient turns eighteen", "C": "Require a parent to attend before continuing the prescription", "D": "Prescribe the contraceptive without notifying the parents"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Prescribe the contraceptive without notifying the parents): most states allow minors to independently consent to contraceptive services, and disclosing this information to parents without the adolescent's permission would violate confidentiality protections that specifically apply to reproductive health care for minors. Legal custody over general decisions does not override this recognized exception, and requiring parental involvement would likely discourage the adolescent from seeking necessary preventive care at all."}, {"id": 146, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A 55-year-old man with early Alzheimer disease is brought to clinic by his adult daughter for a routine visit. He is able to describe his memory difficulties, name his medications, and explain why he takes each one, and he consistently states he wants to continue his current blood pressure medication. His daughter asks the physician to instead discuss all future decisions with her because she worries her father will eventually be unable to manage his own care. Which of the following is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Refer the patient for immediate legal guardianship proceedings", "B": "Explain that decisions will continue to involve the patient directly as long as he retains capacity for each decision", "C": "Agree to make all future decisions through the daughter starting now", "D": "Ask the daughter to obtain durable power of attorney before the visit can continue in the situation described, rather than the alternative approaches listed"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Explain that decisions will continue to involve the patient directly as long as he retains capacity for each decision): a diagnosis of early dementia does not itself remove capacity, and this patient currently demonstrates the ability to understand, appreciate, and communicate his preferences about his own care. Capacity should be reassessed over time as the disease progresses, but it is not appropriate to bypass a currently capacitated patient out of anticipated future decline."}, {"id": 148, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "Neurosurgery recommends an emergent decompressive craniotomy. Which of the following individuals has the appropriate legal authority to provide surrogate consent for this patient?", "options": {"A": "A judge must appoint a guardian before any surgery can proceed, even in a genuine life-threatening surgical emergency requiring immediate decompression", "B": "The attending emergency physician, since the situation is time-sensitive and no family members are immediately available to weigh in on the decision", "C": "Either adult child, since both are equally ranked default surrogates in the absence of a spouse", "D": "The hospital ethics committee, since the children disagree and neither one is willing to defer to the other regarding this decision"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Either adult child, since both are equally ranked default surrogates in the absence of a spouse): with no living spouse, no advance directive, and no guardian, adult children occupy the next tier of the default surrogate hierarchy and are equally ranked with one another. Disagreement between two equally ranked surrogates should first be addressed through discussion rather than automatically escalating to an ethics committee or the courts, which is a slower process poorly suited to a time-sensitive surgical emergency.", "case_id": "SOC-C1", "case_text": "A 72-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department after a fall at home and is found to have a subdural hematoma with a rapidly declining level of consciousness. She is intubated for airway protection shortly after arrival and can no longer participate in decision-making. She has no documented advance directive and no court-appointed guardian. Her husband died three years ago. She is survived by two adult children, a son and a daughter, who both arrive at the hospital and disagree about how aggressively to proceed with neurosurgical intervention.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 149, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "After further discussion, the son recalls that his mother once told him she \"never wants to be kept alive by machines if there's no real hope of getting better,\" while the daughter states she does not remember any such conversation and believes surgery should proceed regardless of the prognosis. Which decision-making standard should guide the surrogates in this situation?", "options": {"A": "Majority vote among all family members present", "B": "Best interest standard, since the mother's own wishes are not clearly documented", "C": "Substituted judgment, based on what the patient herself would have wanted", "D": "Whatever standard minimizes the hospital's legal liability"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Substituted judgment, based on what the patient herself would have wanted): when a patient's own previously expressed wishes are known, even informally, surrogates are ethically obligated to apply substituted judgment rather than substitute their own preferences. The son's recollection of a specific statement about not wanting to be sustained by machines without real hope of recovery is directly relevant evidence of the patient's values and should be weighed carefully.", "case_id": "SOC-C1", "case_text": "A 72-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department after a fall at home and is found to have a subdural hematoma with a rapidly declining level of consciousness. She is intubated for airway protection shortly after arrival and can no longer participate in decision-making. She has no documented advance directive and no court-appointed guardian. Her husband died three years ago. She is survived by two adult children, a son and a daughter, who both arrive at the hospital and disagree about how aggressively to proceed with neurosurgical intervention.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 150, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A repeat head CT shows a large hemorrhage with early signs of herniation and a very poor expected functional recovery even with surgery. The son, citing his mother's prior statement, asks that surgery be withheld and that care be redirected toward comfort measures. The daughter continues to insist on proceeding with surgery. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Proceed with surgery immediately because one surrogate has requested it", "B": "Convene the hospital ethics committee to help the family reach consensus", "C": "Ask the hospital risk management department to make the final decision", "D": "Withhold surgery immediately because one surrogate has declined it"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Convene the hospital ethics committee to help the family reach consensus): when equally ranked surrogates disagree and the medical facts and the patient's own values need to be weighed together, ethics committee consultation is the appropriate mechanism rather than unilaterally acting on only one surrogate's preference. The committee can help clarify the prognosis and mediate the disagreement rather than the medical team choosing a side.", "case_id": "SOC-C1", "case_text": "A 72-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department after a fall at home and is found to have a subdural hematoma with a rapidly declining level of consciousness. She is intubated for airway protection shortly after arrival and can no longer participate in decision-making. She has no documented advance directive and no court-appointed guardian. Her husband died three years ago. She is survived by two adult children, a son and a daughter, who both arrive at the hospital and disagree about how aggressively to proceed with neurosurgical intervention.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 151, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "The ethics committee meets with both children and helps them agree to comfort-focused care rather than surgery, consistent with the mother's previously expressed wishes. Two days later, the patient dies peacefully with her family present. Which of the following best describes the ethical basis for withholding the craniotomy in this case?", "options": {"A": "It represented appropriate application of the patient's own previously expressed wishes to withhold a non-beneficial intervention", "B": "It was equivalent to euthanasia because the family made the final decision", "C": "It was a form of physician-assisted death because a life-sustaining intervention was withheld as presented in this vignette, rather than the alternative approaches listed", "D": "It was inappropriate because surrogates cannot decline a potentially life-saving surgery"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It represented appropriate application of the patient's own previously expressed wishes to withhold a non-beneficial intervention): withholding an intervention that would not meaningfully change a very poor prognosis, based on the patient's own previously stated values, is standard, accepted end-of-life practice. It differs fundamentally from physician-assisted death or euthanasia, since no lethal medication was given and the underlying hemorrhage caused death, not any action by the physicians.", "case_id": "SOC-C1", "case_text": "A 72-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department after a fall at home and is found to have a subdural hematoma with a rapidly declining level of consciousness. She is intubated for airway protection shortly after arrival and can no longer participate in decision-making. She has no documented advance directive and no court-appointed guardian. Her husband died three years ago. She is survived by two adult children, a son and a daughter, who both arrive at the hospital and disagree about how aggressively to proceed with neurosurgical intervention.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 153, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A physician is treating a 60-year-old man for hypertension and diabetes. The man's employer calls the office and asks whether the patient has been attending his scheduled appointments, explaining that the company is considering him for a promotion that requires frequent travel. The office manager, who took the call, is uncertain how to respond and asks the physician for guidance before returning the employer's call later that afternoon. Which of the following is the most appropriate response by the office?", "options": {"A": "Provide general information since the employer is not asking about specific diagnoses", "B": "Confirm only that the patient has been attending appointments, without discussing his diagnoses", "C": "Ask the patient's spouse for permission to speak with the employer", "D": "Decline to provide any information without the patient's written authorization"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Decline to provide any information without the patient's written authorization): under HIPAA, protected health information, including whether a patient has attended appointments, cannot be disclosed to an employer without the patient's specific authorization. The employer's stated reason for asking does not change this requirement, and a spouse cannot authorize release of another adult's medical information."}, {"id": 154, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A 29-year-old man is diagnosed with active pulmonary tuberculosis confirmed by sputum culture. He asks his physician not to report the diagnosis because he is worried about losing his job if his employer finds out. His chest radiograph shows a cavitary lesion in the right upper lobe, and he has already missed several days of work due to a persistent cough and night sweats. Which of the following is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Honor the patient's request for confidentiality since HIPAA protects all diagnoses equally from any external disclosure", "B": "Report the diagnosis only if the patient later develops symptoms severe enough to require hospitalization", "C": "Report the case to the local public health department regardless of the patient's request", "D": "Ask the patient's employer directly whether reporting is necessary before contacting any public health authority"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report the case to the local public health department regardless of the patient's request): active tuberculosis is a reportable communicable disease in every state because of its risk to public health, and this legal reporting duty is a recognized exception to ordinary confidentiality protections. The patient's preference not to have the diagnosis reported does not override a mandatory public health reporting requirement, though public health reporting is itself subject to strict confidentiality."}, {"id": 156, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A physician learns that a 50-year-old patient with poorly controlled seizures, who continues to drive despite being advised to stop, was recently in a minor car accident that he attributes to a seizure while driving. The state where the physician practices has a mandatory reporting law for drivers with uncontrolled seizure disorders. He reports that his seizures have become more frequent over the past several months despite adjustments to his antiepileptic medication regimen, and he admits he has continued driving to work daily. Which of the following is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Discuss the risk with the patient and rely on him to stop driving voluntarily", "B": "Report the patient to the state department of motor vehicles as required by law", "C": "Report the patient only if he has a second accident", "D": "Take no action since driving restrictions are a matter for law enforcement, not medicine"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report the patient to the state department of motor vehicles as required by law): counseling a patient to stop driving voluntarily is a reasonable first conversation, but in a jurisdiction with a mandatory reporting statute for uncontrolled seizure disorders, the physician's legal duty once aware of a reportable condition is to file the required report, not simply rely on voluntary compliance or wait for a second accident."}, {"id": 157, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A clinic implements a new electronic health record system. A staff member with no clinical role in a particular patient's care looks up that patient's chart out of personal curiosity after recognizing the patient's name in the waiting room, with no treatment, payment, or operational reason to access the record. The access log later shows the chart was opened for several minutes and multiple sections, including recent laboratory results and a mental health note, were viewed during that time. Which of the following best describes this action?", "options": {"A": "A permitted disclosure as long as no information was later shared with anyone else, given the circumstances described here", "B": "A HIPAA violation because the access was not for treatment, payment, or health care operations", "C": "An acceptable use of the record since the staff member works at the same clinic", "D": "Permitted under HIPAA's treatment, payment, and operations exception"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A HIPAA violation because the access was not for treatment, payment, or health care operations): simply working at the same institution does not authorize access to a patient's record. HIPAA permits access only for legitimate purposes such as direct treatment, billing, or operational functions like quality review. Accessing a record purely out of personal curiosity is an impermissible disclosure and a violation even if the information is never shared further."}, {"id": 159, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A physician is asked by a state licensing board investigator to release the complete medical record of a patient who has filed a formal complaint alleging that the physician provided negligent care. The physician is uncertain whether releasing the record without the patient's separate authorization is permitted. The complaint alleges a delay in diagnosis that the patient believes contributed to a worsened outcome, and the investigator explains that the board is conducting a formal disciplinary review under its statutory authority. Which of the following is true?", "options": {"A": "Release is permitted only if the physician personally agrees with the substance of the allegations being investigated by the board.", "B": "Release requires the patient's separate written authorization in every circumstance, even when a state licensing board is formally investigating.", "C": "Release is prohibited unless a subpoena has first been issued by a court with proper jurisdiction over the matter.", "D": "Release to a state licensing board conducting an official investigation is generally permitted without additional patient authorization"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Release to a state licensing board conducting an official investigation is generally permitted without additional patient authorization): HIPAA specifically permits disclosures to health oversight agencies, including state licensing boards, conducting investigations related to the health care system, without requiring separate patient authorization. This falls within one of HIPAA's recognized exceptions for oversight and legal compliance activities."}, {"id": 160, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A 16-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department by his mother after an argument at home. During a private portion of the interview he discloses that he has been drinking alcohol most weekends with friends but denies any thoughts of self-harm and has no medical complications from the alcohol use. His mother, waiting outside, asks the physician directly what was discussed. Which of the following is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Decline to disclose the specifics of the substance use conversation without the patient's permission", "B": "Tell the mother only that her son is fine, without further explanation", "C": "Tell the mother everything that was discussed because she is his legal guardian", "D": "Ask the son to leave the room so the mother can be told the full details privately"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Decline to disclose the specifics of the substance use conversation without the patient's permission): most states allow adolescents to consent to confidential substance use counseling, and this confidentiality generally extends to disclosures made in that context. Because there is no immediate safety concern such as suicidality or a medical emergency requiring disclosure, the physician should protect the confidentiality of this conversation rather than sharing it with the parent without the adolescent's agreement."}, {"id": 162, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A 26-year-old woman with intellectual disability who lives semi-independently discloses to her primary care physician that a paid caregiver has been taking money from her without permission. She asks the physician not to tell anyone because she is afraid the caregiver will retaliate. She describes several specific incidents over the past few months in which small amounts of cash disappeared from her wallet shortly after the caregiver's visits, and she appears anxious while describing these events. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Report suspected abuse of a vulnerable adult to protective services despite her request", "B": "Respect her request for confidentiality since she is a competent adult in this particular scenario", "C": "Confront the caregiver directly without involving any outside agency", "D": "Take no action unless the patient reports a physical injury"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report suspected abuse of a vulnerable adult to protective services despite her request): individuals with intellectual disabilities are considered a vulnerable population, and financial exploitation by a caregiver falls under mandatory reporting requirements for abuse or exploitation of a vulnerable adult in most states. This duty exists precisely because vulnerable individuals may fear retaliation and be reluctant to report abuse themselves."}, {"id": 165, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A physician's office receives a request from a life insurance company for a copy of a patient's medical records as part of underwriting for a new policy application. The request includes a signed authorization form that the patient completed as part of the insurance application. The application also asks the physician to confirm whether the patient has any history of hospitalization for a chronic medical condition within the past five years. Which of the following is true regarding this disclosure?", "options": {"A": "The physician must independently verify the necessity of every requested record before release", "B": "The disclosure is prohibited because insurance underwriting is not considered a permitted purpose", "C": "The disclosure requires a court order in addition to patient authorization", "D": "The disclosure is permitted because the patient provided a signed authorization"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The disclosure is permitted because the patient provided a signed authorization): disclosures for purposes outside treatment, payment, and health care operations, such as sharing records with a life insurance company for underwriting, generally require a specific, signed patient authorization, which is exactly what was provided. Because a valid authorization exists, the office may release the requested records consistent with its scope."}, {"id": 167, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A researcher wants to contact former patients of a clinic to invite them to participate in a new clinical trial, using contact information obtained from their medical records, without a prior treatment relationship with those specific patients. The trial is evaluating a new maintenance medication for a chronic condition several of these former patients were previously treated for at the clinic, and enrollment is currently below the target needed for the study. Which of the following is required before this outreach can occur?", "options": {"A": "Informal verbal permission from the clinic's office manager, without any written documentation of that conversation on file", "B": "No special authorization is needed since the researcher works within the same health system", "C": "Either a signed patient authorization or institutional review board approval of a waiver of authorization", "D": "A general notice simply posted somewhere in the clinic waiting room area is generally considered sufficient"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Either a signed patient authorization or institutional review board approval of a waiver of authorization): using protected health information to recruit patients for research generally requires the patient's specific authorization, unless an institutional review board has reviewed and approved a waiver under specific regulatory criteria, such as minimal risk research that could not practicably be conducted otherwise. Working within the same health system does not substitute for either pathway."}, {"id": 168, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A hospital wants to publish a case report describing an unusual clinical presentation for an academic journal. The report includes rare details about the patient's occupation, hometown, and a distinctive physical finding that, combined together, could make the patient identifiable to colleagues even without using the patient's actual name. The occupation described is highly unusual in the region where the hospital is located, and the physical finding involves a rare congenital anomaly that few clinicians in the area have ever encountered. Which of the following is the most appropriate action before publication?", "options": {"A": "Replace the patient's name with a pseudonym and publish without further changes", "B": "Obtain specific patient consent for publication or further modify the details to prevent identification", "C": "Publish the case report as written since the patient's name is not used", "D": "Publish only in a journal with restricted subscriber access"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Obtain specific patient consent for publication or further modify the details to prevent identification): removing a patient's name is not sufficient to protect confidentiality if enough distinctive combined details remain that colleagues, family, or the patient's own community could reasonably identify the individual. The appropriate step is either obtaining informed consent for publication or further altering identifying details while preserving the clinically relevant information."}, {"id": 169, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A physician is asked by a patient's adult daughter, who is a nurse at the same hospital where her mother is admitted, to view her mother's chart so she can help keep an eye on things, even though she is not part of the assigned care team. She explains that she is not asking to change any orders or document anything in the chart, only to periodically check laboratory results and vital sign trends out of personal concern. Which of the following is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Grant access only during off-duty hours, since that would seem to reduce any conflict with her regular clinical duties", "B": "Allow access but ask her only to keep whatever she reads to herself and not share it with others", "C": "Allow access since she is both a licensed nurse and an immediate family member of the patient", "D": "Decline unless she is formally added to the care team or given specific authorization by the patient"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Decline unless she is formally added to the care team or given specific authorization by the patient): being a licensed nurse and a family member does not by itself authorize access to a patient's record outside an assigned clinical role. Access should be limited to individuals with a genuine treatment, payment, or operations purpose, or to those the patient has specifically authorized, regardless of the requester's own credentials or family relationship."}, {"id": 170, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A physician learns that a colleague has been accessing the electronic medical records of several well-known patients admitted to the hospital who are not under the colleague's care, apparently out of curiosity. The colleague explains, when asked informally, that curiosity got the better of them after seeing a familiar name appear on a hospital census list circulated among staff. Which of the following is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Confront the colleague informally and consider the matter resolved if they agree to stop", "B": "Report the behavior through the appropriate institutional compliance or privacy channel", "C": "Wait to see if the colleague repeats the behavior before taking action", "D": "Ignore the behavior since no harm to the patients has been demonstrated"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Report the behavior through the appropriate institutional compliance or privacy channel): unauthorized access to patient records is a HIPAA violation and a professionalism concern that should be reported through the hospital's formal privacy or compliance process, which can properly investigate and address the pattern. An informal conversation or simply waiting does not address the underlying breach or prevent recurrence for other patients."}, {"id": 171, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A 33-year-old man with a documented history of intimate partner violence toward his current girlfriend, who is a competent adult with no children in the home, presents alone for a laceration on his hand. He denies wanting police involvement or a shelter referral when the physician raises the topic gently, and there is no evidence of a weapon injury to the girlfriend. State law does not mandate reporting of intimate partner violence between competent adults in this situation. Which of the following is the most appropriate approach to the girlfriend's situation, discussed hypothetically with the patient present?", "options": {"A": "Report the relationship to police without first discussing it with the patient or considering his stated wishes", "B": "Take no action at all, since domestic violence is generally considered a private family matter best left alone", "C": "Screen for immediate danger, offer resources, and respect the couple's autonomy if there is no mandatory reporting trigger", "D": "Insist that the girlfriend come in personally for a mandatory safety evaluation regardless of her own preference"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Screen for immediate danger, offer resources, and respect the couple's autonomy if there is no mandatory reporting trigger): most states do not mandate reporting of intimate partner violence involving a competent adult victim in the absence of specific triggers such as a weapon injury, which preserves the adult victim's autonomy over her own safety decisions. The appropriate approach is to screen for immediate danger, document findings, and offer resources such as a hotline, rather than overriding autonomy or ignoring the issue entirely."}, {"id": 172, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A physician is treating a 9-year-old boy for recurrent unexplained fractures. The child's stepfather explains each injury as the result of rough play, but the pattern and healing stages of the fractures are inconsistent with the stated mechanisms. The stepfather states the boy is generally clumsy and prone to accidents, but the child appears withdrawn during the interview and avoids eye contact when asked about how the injuries occurred. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Report suspected child abuse to child protective services", "B": "Refer the family to counseling without any formal report", "C": "Ask the child directly and rely solely on his answer", "D": "Discuss the concern with the stepfather and monitor closely"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Report suspected child abuse to child protective services): reporting suspected child abuse is a mandatory legal duty for physicians in every state, triggered by reasonable suspicion based on clinical findings inconsistent with the stated history, not by obtaining a confession or proof. Relying on a child's direct denial, which is common even in genuine abuse due to fear, or simply monitoring informally, would fail this legal and protective obligation."}, {"id": 173, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A physician is treating a patient for major depressive disorder. The patient's employer contacts the office requesting a note confirming the patient is \"medically cleared\" to return to work, and asks the physician to also share the specific diagnosis and treatment plan so the employer can \"better support\" the employee. The patient has only authorized release of a basic return-to-work clearance. Which of the following is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Ask the patient's spouse whether additional details can be shared", "B": "Share the full diagnosis and treatment plan since the employer is trying to help in this specific clinical situation", "C": "Provide only the return-to-work clearance the patient has specifically authorized", "D": "Decline to provide any documentation to avoid the issue"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Provide only the return-to-work clearance the patient has specifically authorized): disclosure of protected health information to an employer must be limited to what the patient has specifically authorized, following the minimum necessary standard. Even a well-intentioned request from an employer for additional diagnostic detail does not expand what may be disclosed beyond the scope of the patient's own authorization."}, {"id": 174, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A 27-year-old woman with a seizure disorder that has been well controlled on medication for two years without a breakthrough seizure asks her physician whether she is legally required to disclose her diagnosis to the department of motor vehicles at her next license renewal. She has never had a seizure while driving and has maintained a valid driver's license throughout her diagnosis, but she recently moved to a different state and wants to understand her obligations there. Which of the following statements about mandatory reporting of seizure disorders is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Reporting requirements and seizure-free intervals required for driving vary significantly by state", "B": "Only patients who have caused an accident must be reported", "C": "No state has any reporting requirement related to seizure disorders and driving", "D": "All states require physicians to report every patient diagnosed with a seizure disorder under the clinical circumstances described"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reporting requirements and seizure-free intervals required for driving vary significantly by state): some states require physician reporting of any seizure disorder diagnosis directly to the department of motor vehicles, while others rely on patient self-reporting with a specified seizure-free interval, commonly around six months to one year, before driving may resume. Because this varies by jurisdiction, physicians should know their own state's specific requirement rather than assuming a single national rule."}, {"id": 175, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "Which of the following is the most appropriate approach to the patient's disclosure of sexual activity?", "options": {"A": "Refuse to discuss contraception until the mother is informed", "B": "Maintain confidentiality about the sexual activity disclosure, consistent with adolescent reproductive health confidentiality protections", "C": "Tell the mother immediately since she brought her daughter in for care", "D": "Report the relationship to child protective services solely because of the age difference, based on the details presented in this case, rather than the alternative approaches listed"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Maintain confidentiality about the sexual activity disclosure, consistent with adolescent reproductive health confidentiality protections): most states protect an adolescent's ability to receive confidential reproductive health counseling and contraceptive services without automatic parental notification. A two-year age difference between two adolescents in a consensual relationship does not, by itself, trigger a mandatory report, and disclosing the relationship to the mother without the patient's consent would undermine the confidentiality this visit depends on.", "case_id": "SOC-C2", "case_text": "A 15-year-old girl is brought to a pediatrician's office by her mother for a routine sports physical. During the private portion of the visit, without her mother present, the patient discloses that she has been sexually active with her 17-year-old boyfriend for the past three months, that they inconsistently use condoms, and that she has not told her mother because she is afraid of getting in trouble. She also mentions that her boyfriend sometimes grabs her arm hard enough to leave marks when he is angry, though she says she is not afraid of him and does not want anyone to intervene.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 176, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "The physician offers screening for sexually transmitted infections and counseling on contraceptive options. Which of the following statements about confidentiality for this counseling is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Contraceptive counseling requires parental consent in this situation", "B": "Confidentiality protections for contraceptive counseling apply only to patients over age 18", "C": "Confidentiality applies only if the patient is already sexually active with more than one partner", "D": "Contraceptive counseling for a minor can generally be provided confidentially in most states"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Contraceptive counseling for a minor can generally be provided confidentially in most states): the majority of states recognize a minor's ability to independently consent to contraceptive services and to receive that counseling confidentially, one of the well-established exceptions to requiring parental consent for a minor's medical care. This protection does not depend on the number of partners the patient has had or wait until age 18.", "case_id": "SOC-C2", "case_text": "A 15-year-old girl is brought to a pediatrician's office by her mother for a routine sports physical. During the private portion of the visit, without her mother present, the patient discloses that she has been sexually active with her 17-year-old boyfriend for the past three months, that they inconsistently use condoms, and that she has not told her mother because she is afraid of getting in trouble. She also mentions that her boyfriend sometimes grabs her arm hard enough to leave marks when he is angry, though she says she is not afraid of him and does not want anyone to intervene.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 177, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "Regarding the disclosure that her boyfriend sometimes grabs her arm hard enough to leave marks, which of the following is the most appropriate next step, assuming the state has no mandatory reporting requirement for intimate partner violence between adolescents in an otherwise consensual relationship?", "options": {"A": "Take no further action since she stated she is not afraid of him, even though this approach would leave any underlying pattern of controlling behavior completely unaddressed", "B": "Tell her mother immediately since the patient is a minor, without first exploring the disclosure further with the patient herself or considering her preferences", "C": "Screen further for the pattern and severity of the behavior, assess safety, and offer resources while respecting her stated wishes if no imminent danger is identified", "D": "Report the boyfriend to police immediately without discussing it with her first in the situation described, rather than the alternative approaches listed"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Screen further for the pattern and severity of the behavior, assess safety, and offer resources while respecting her stated wishes if no imminent danger is identified): even for a minor, a single vague description of grabbing without other signs of imminent danger requires careful further assessment rather than an automatic report or automatic parental disclosure. Taking no action at all, however, would fail the physician's duty to at least screen further and offer resources appropriate to a potentially unsafe relationship.", "case_id": "SOC-C2", "case_text": "A 15-year-old girl is brought to a pediatrician's office by her mother for a routine sports physical. During the private portion of the visit, without her mother present, the patient discloses that she has been sexually active with her 17-year-old boyfriend for the past three months, that they inconsistently use condoms, and that she has not told her mother because she is afraid of getting in trouble. She also mentions that her boyfriend sometimes grabs her arm hard enough to leave marks when he is angry, though she says she is not afraid of him and does not want anyone to intervene.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 178, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "On further questioning, the patient discloses that the marks have become more frequent, that her boyfriend recently threatened to \"make her regret it\" if she ever left him, and that she is now afraid to end the relationship. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Tell her mother every detail of what was disclosed without seeking the patient's permission or involving her in that decision", "B": "Delay any intervention or safety planning until the patient's next regularly scheduled follow-up visit several weeks later", "C": "Take no action since she is still a minor without full legal autonomy as presented in this vignette, rather than the alternative approaches listed", "D": "Provide a more thorough safety assessment, safety planning, and referral to appropriate resources given the escalation and expressed fear"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Provide a more thorough safety assessment, safety planning, and referral to appropriate resources given the escalation and expressed fear): the disclosure of escalating physical aggression, a threat, and expressed fear represents a meaningful change from the initial vague description and warrants a more thorough safety assessment and connection to resources such as a domestic violence advocate, done collaboratively with the patient rather than unilaterally informing her mother without her knowledge or simply waiting for a future visit.", "case_id": "SOC-C2", "case_text": "A 15-year-old girl is brought to a pediatrician's office by her mother for a routine sports physical. During the private portion of the visit, without her mother present, the patient discloses that she has been sexually active with her 17-year-old boyfriend for the past three months, that they inconsistently use condoms, and that she has not told her mother because she is afraid of getting in trouble. She also mentions that her boyfriend sometimes grabs her arm hard enough to leave marks when he is angry, though she says she is not afraid of him and does not want anyone to intervene.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 182, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A 55-year-old woman is diagnosed with amyotrophic lateral sclerosis. While she still has full decision-making capacity, she completes a durable power of attorney for health care naming her husband as her agent, and a detailed living will specifying she does not want a feeding tube or tracheostomy-dependent ventilation once she can no longer communicate meaningfully. Two years later, she has lost the ability to communicate and requires artificial nutrition to survive. Her husband, overwhelmed with grief, asks the team to place a feeding tube anyway. Which of the following is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Explain that the agent's role is to represent the patient's own previously expressed wishes, which specifically declined this intervention, and offer support in this decision", "B": "Ask the hospital ethics committee to overrule the husband's request immediately, without first attempting any further conversation with him.", "C": "Refuse to discuss the matter with him any further, since the wording of the living will is already clearly unambiguous.", "D": "Place the feeding tube as the husband requests, since as her named health care agent he now holds full authority over this decision."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Explain that the agent's role is to represent the patient's own previously expressed wishes, which specifically declined this intervention, and offer support in this decision): a health care agent's authority is meant to carry out the patient's own known wishes, not to substitute the agent's personal preferences, especially when the patient has left clear, specific, written instructions on exactly this scenario. The appropriate response is a compassionate conversation reorienting the husband to his role and offering emotional support, rather than either blindly following his request or dismissing him."}, {"id": 183, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A 90-year-old man with severe dementia, no advance directive, and no available family is admitted from a nursing facility in cardiac arrest. Emergency medical services began resuscitation en route. On arrival, no surrogate can be reached and no DNR order exists in any available record. Emergency medical services report that he had a witnessed collapse approximately ten minutes before their arrival and that bystander cardiopulmonary resuscitation was started promptly at the scene. Which of the following is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Stop resuscitation immediately given his age and dementia diagnosis", "B": "Continue resuscitation according to standard advanced cardiac life support protocols in the absence of a documented DNR order", "C": "Wait for a surrogate to be located before continuing resuscitation", "D": "Contact the hospital ethics committee before continuing any further intervention under the facts described here, rather than the alternative approaches listed"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Continue resuscitation according to standard advanced cardiac life support protocols in the absence of a documented DNR order): in the absence of any documented advance directive or DNR order, the default presumption is in favor of attempting resuscitation, regardless of the patient's age or a dementia diagnosis, since neither age nor dementia alone determines a patient's wishes about resuscitation. Age and comorbidity can inform prognosis after resuscitation begins, but they do not justify withholding care absent a documented directive."}, {"id": 185, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A hospital team caring for a patient in a persistent vegetative state after a severe anoxic brain injury believes that continued mechanical ventilation and artificial nutrition are not providing any meaningful benefit given the patient's prognosis, but the patient's surrogate insists that everything possible be done indefinitely. Repeated family meetings and second opinions have not resolved the disagreement. The patient has been in this state for over a year following the injury, with no meaningful improvement on repeated neurologic examinations and imaging studies during that time. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Transfer the patient to a different hospital immediately without further discussion or any attempt at mediation", "B": "Unilaterally withdraw the ventilator since the team has determined the treatment is futile", "C": "Obtain a court order to withdraw treatment immediately without first attempting further discussion", "D": "Continue current treatment while involving the hospital ethics committee to help mediate the disagreement"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Continue current treatment while involving the hospital ethics committee to help mediate the disagreement): when physicians and a surrogate disagree about whether an intervention is futile and initial conversations and second opinions have not resolved the conflict, the appropriate next step is ethics committee involvement to help mediate, clarify the medical facts, and explore the underlying values driving the disagreement, rather than the team unilaterally withdrawing treatment against the surrogate's wishes."}, {"id": 186, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A 67-year-old man is admitted with a large ischemic stroke and is ventilator dependent with a very poor prognosis for meaningful neurologic recovery. His wife, serving as his health care agent, decides after extensive discussion with the team to withdraw mechanical ventilation and focus on comfort care, consistent with values he expressed to her in prior conversations about not wanting to live dependent on machines. He dies comfortably several hours after the ventilator is withdrawn. Which of the following best characterizes this withdrawal of the ventilator?", "options": {"A": "An appropriate withdrawal of a non-beneficial intervention consistent with the patient's expressed values", "B": "An act of euthanasia because the physician's actions directly led to his death in this specific clinical situation", "C": "A form of physician-assisted death since a medical intervention was stopped", "D": "An illegal act since court approval was not obtained"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (An appropriate withdrawal of a non-beneficial intervention consistent with the patient's expressed values): withdrawing a treatment that is not achieving a meaningful benefit, based on a surrogate's substituted judgment about the patient's own previously expressed wishes, is standard and legally accepted end-of-life practice. It is fundamentally different from euthanasia or physician-assisted death because the underlying stroke, not any lethal act, causes death, and no court approval is required for this type of decision."}, {"id": 187, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A 70-year-old woman with metastatic breast cancer and a life expectancy of a few weeks is enrolled in hospice care at home. Her son, who lives with her, calls the hospice nurse in distress because his mother is having increasing pain despite escalating doses of morphine, and asks whether increasing the dose further could hasten her death. Which of the following is the most accurate response regarding the ethical principle at play?", "options": {"A": "Increasing the morphine dose in this context constitutes euthanasia, since any dose adjustment that could hasten death is ethically equivalent to intentionally ending life", "B": "Increasing the morphine dose to relieve pain is ethically appropriate under the principle of double effect, even if it might incidentally shorten life", "C": "The dose can only be increased with a new court order, given her terminal prognosis and the legal risks of opioid titration in hospice care", "D": "Increasing the morphine dose to relieve pain is not permissible if it could shorten her life, since preserving remaining time should generally take priority in end of life care"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Increasing the morphine dose to relieve pain is ethically appropriate under the principle of double effect, even if it might incidentally shorten life): the principle of double effect holds that an action with a good intended effect, here, relieving severe pain, is ethically permissible even if it carries a foreseen but unintended risk of a harmful secondary effect, such as respiratory depression, as long as the intent is symptom control rather than causing death. This is fundamentally different from euthanasia, where causing death is the direct intent."}, {"id": 188, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A patient with a documented POLST, or physician orders for life-sustaining treatment, form specifying \"do not intubate\" is brought to the emergency department in respiratory distress from an asthma exacerbation that is expected to be fully reversible with noninvasive treatment and possibly brief intubation if it worsens. The emergency team notes that his oxygen saturation is currently 88 percent on high-flow supplemental oxygen despite aggressive bronchodilator therapy, and his mental status remains alert and appropriate. Which of the following is the most appropriate interpretation of the POLST form in this specific scenario?", "options": {"A": "POLST forms carry no binding legal authority whatsoever and may simply be disregarded by the emergency team any time the clinical picture differs even slightly from what was originally documented", "B": "The POLST form only applies in the outpatient setting and does not apply in the emergency department under the clinical circumstances described, rather than the alternative approaches listed", "C": "The POLST form should be interpreted in the context of the patient's goals, and the team should clarify with the patient or surrogate whether it was intended to apply to a reversible acute illness like this one", "D": "The POLST form categorically prohibits any airway intervention of any kind no matter what the underlying cause of the patient's respiratory decline may turn out to be"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The POLST form should be interpreted in the context of the patient's goals, and the team should clarify with the patient or surrogate whether it was intended to apply to a reversible acute illness like this one): a POLST form reflects a patient's goals of care, which were often documented with a particular clinical trajectory in mind, such as progressive decline from a chronic illness. When a patient develops an acutely reversible condition that may not match the scenario originally envisioned, the appropriate step is to clarify intent with the patient or surrogate rather than either rigidly applying or dismissing the document."}, {"id": 189, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A 58-year-old man with capacity is diagnosed with locally advanced laryngectomy-requiring laryngeal cancer. He understands that without surgery his cancer will likely progress and eventually cause airway obstruction, and that with surgery he has a good chance of cure but will permanently lose his natural voice. After extensive discussion, he declines the surgery, stating that preserving his voice matters more to him than extending his life. Which of the following is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Respect his informed decision and discuss alternative approaches to his goals of care", "B": "Refer him for an involuntary psychiatric hold to assess for depression before accepting his decision", "C": "Proceed with surgery anyway since it offers the best chance of survival", "D": "Ask his family to override his decision"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Respect his informed decision and discuss alternative approaches to his goals of care): a patient with decision-making capacity may decline even a potentially curative treatment for reasons that reflect his own values, such as prioritizing voice preservation over survival. Family members cannot override a capacitated patient's informed refusal, and an involuntary psychiatric hold is not an appropriate response to a well-reasoned, values-based treatment refusal absent evidence of an underlying psychiatric condition impairing his judgment."}, {"id": 191, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A patient's advance directive names his wife as his health care agent but was completed before their divorce, which occurred four years ago. He is now unmarried and has not updated the document. He loses capacity after a stroke, and his adult son from a prior relationship asks who has decision-making authority. The son explains that his father has been managing well independently since the stroke rehabilitation began, but now requires someone to help make decisions about ongoing medical care during this acute event. Which of the following is generally true regarding an advance directive naming a former spouse?", "options": {"A": "The document becomes void upon divorce, leaving no valid advance directive in place under most state laws", "B": "Only a court can determine whether the former spouse's authority continues, since state law is often considered ambiguous on this point", "C": "The former spouse retains decision-making authority despite the divorce, since her original designation is assumed to remain legally valid", "D": "Most states automatically revoke a former spouse's designation as health care agent upon divorce unless the patient has specifically reaffirmed it"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Most states automatically revoke a former spouse's designation as health care agent upon divorce unless the patient has specifically reaffirmed it): many states have statutes that automatically revoke a former spouse's authority as a designated health care agent once a divorce is finalized, on the theory that the patient would generally not want an ex-spouse retaining this role. This does not usually void the entire document, since a properly executed advance directive can still have valid provisions, such as a living will, but decision-making authority would typically default to the next-ranked surrogate."}, {"id": 192, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A patient in a persistent vegetative state has an advance directive stating that she would not want to be kept alive by artificial means \"if there is no hope of recovery,\" but does not further define what she considers artificial means. Her family disagrees about whether this includes artificial nutrition through a feeding tube, which is currently the only intervention keeping her alive, since she is otherwise breathing without ventilator support. Which of the following is the most appropriate approach to resolving this disagreement?", "options": {"A": "Automatically discontinue the feeding tube since any artificial intervention is understood to be prohibited by the document, regardless of the family's own interpretation of her wishes", "B": "Automatically continue the feeding tube since it was not specifically named in the document, even though the family strongly believes she would not have wanted it", "C": "Explore what the patient likely meant through further discussion with family and, if needed, ethics consultation, since the directive's language is ambiguous", "D": "Ask a judge to interpret the exact meaning of the document before any decision is made, rather than first attempting to resolve the disagreement through family discussion"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Explore what the patient likely meant through further discussion with family and, if needed, ethics consultation, since the directive's language is ambiguous): general or vague language in an advance directive requires interpretation in light of the patient's broader values and any other statements she made, which is best accomplished through further family discussion and ethics consultation rather than either extreme of automatically continuing or automatically discontinuing the intervention, or defaulting immediately to court involvement for what is fundamentally a clinical and family conversation."}, {"id": 193, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A 40-year-old man with capacity is admitted for a severe traumatic injury requiring an urgent blood transfusion to survive. He is a Jehovah's Witness and states clearly and repeatedly that he refuses blood products under any circumstance, understanding that he may die without the transfusion. He has no dependents and has discussed this decision with his religious community in the past. Which of the following is the most appropriate action?", "options": {"A": "Honor his informed refusal of blood products", "B": "Transfuse him only if he loses consciousness", "C": "Administer the transfusion despite his stated wishes because his life is at risk", "D": "Obtain an emergency court order to compel the transfusion"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Honor his informed refusal of blood products): a capacitated adult has the right to refuse any medical intervention, including a lifesaving blood transfusion, based on sincerely held religious beliefs, even when death may result. This right does not disappear if the patient later loses consciousness, since his prior clear, informed refusal continues to apply, and a court order compelling treatment over a competent adult's informed refusal is not appropriate."}, {"id": 194, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A 76-year-old woman with advanced heart failure who has previously declined a left ventricular assist device is admitted for worsening symptoms. During this admission, she tells her cardiologist she has changed her mind and now wants to be evaluated for the device after all, having discussed it further with her family. She has discussed the decision with her cardiologist, her family, and a palliative care team over the past several visits, and repeat testing confirms she remains an appropriate candidate for the device. Which of the following is the most appropriate response to this change in her stated wishes?", "options": {"A": "Proceed with the evaluation only if her family also signs off, even though her own informed decision should already be legally sufficient", "B": "Refuse to reconsider since she previously declined the device in writing, treating that earlier decision as permanently binding regardless of her current wishes", "C": "Require a court hearing to validate her change of mind, even though she currently retains full decision-making capacity to make this choice herself", "D": "Reassess her current wishes and proceed with evaluation for the device if she has capacity and desires it now"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reassess her current wishes and proceed with evaluation for the device if she has capacity and desires it now): patient preferences about treatment can change over time, and a patient with capacity retains the right to revise a previous decision at any point. A prior written refusal does not permanently bind a patient who later changes her mind while still possessing capacity, and family consent is not an additional legal requirement for a capacitated adult's own decision."}, {"id": 197, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A physician is caring for a patient with a new diagnosis of a rapidly progressive neurodegenerative disease. The patient asks the physician to help her complete an advance directive while she still has full capacity, specifically naming her sister as her health care agent and describing which interventions she would and would not want as her disease progresses. Which of the following best describes the primary purpose of this document?", "options": {"A": "To transfer all future medical decision-making immediately to her sister, even while the patient still has capacity, given the circumstances described here, rather than the alternative approaches listed", "B": "To limit the physician's legal liability no matter what happens to the patient's condition in the years that follow", "C": "To replace the need for any future conversations between the physician and the patient once the document has been signed and filed", "D": "To allow the patient's own wishes to guide care and to designate a trusted decision-maker for the time when she can no longer participate directly in decisions"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (To allow the patient's own wishes to guide care and to designate a trusted decision-maker for the time when she can no longer participate directly in decisions): an advance directive completed while a patient has capacity serves to document her own values and preferences and to name a trusted surrogate for the future, but it does not transfer authority away from the patient while she still retains capacity to participate in her own care decisions, and it does not eliminate the need for ongoing conversation as her disease progresses."}, {"id": 198, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A 52-year-old man with metastatic colon cancer and a prognosis of several months is admitted for a small bowel obstruction. Surgery could relieve the obstruction but carries significant perioperative risk given his overall condition, while nonoperative management with a nasogastric tube might resolve the obstruction more slowly with less risk but a lower chance of full symptom relief. After a detailed discussion of both options and his goals, he chooses the nonoperative approach because he prioritizes avoiding a difficult recovery over a faster resolution. Which of the following best characterizes this decision-making process?", "options": {"A": "An invalid decision because it does not follow the option with the highest likelihood of symptom relief", "B": "Therapeutic privilege, since risk information was withheld", "C": "Shared decision-making, incorporating the patient's own values alongside the medical facts", "D": "Paternalistic decision-making imposed by the medical team"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Shared decision-making, incorporating the patient's own values alongside the medical facts): this scenario reflects the modern standard of shared decision-making, in which the physician presents the relevant clinical facts, risks, and benefits of each option, and the patient applies his own values and goals to choose among reasonable alternatives. There is no requirement that a patient choose the option with the statistically higher chance of a particular outcome, and no information was withheld here, so therapeutic privilege does not apply."}, {"id": 199, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A 3-year-old child with a severe traumatic brain injury is expected to remain in a persistent vegetative state if she survives. Her parents, who share legal custody, disagree about whether to continue mechanical ventilation, with the father wanting to continue indefinitely and the mother wanting to transition to comfort care. The hospital's pediatric ethics consultation service has already met with both parents separately once, and each parent continues to hold firmly to their original position after that initial conversation. Which of the following is the most appropriate approach for the medical team?", "options": {"A": "Facilitate ongoing discussion between both parents, supported by palliative care or ethics consultation, since both hold equal legal authority", "B": "Ask the hospital's legal department to select which parent's decision to follow", "C": "Automatically follow the father's wishes since he spoke first", "D": "Automatically follow the mother's wishes since mothers are generally given priority as legal decision-makers for young children, consistent with the details given"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Facilitate ongoing discussion between both parents, supported by palliative care or ethics consultation, since both hold equal legal authority): parents who share legal custody generally have equal decision-making authority for their minor child, and neither parent's wishes automatically take precedence over the other's based on birth order of statements or gender. When parents disagree, the appropriate approach is facilitated discussion, often supported by ethics or palliative care consultation, rather than the hospital's legal department unilaterally selecting a side."}, {"id": 200, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A 66-year-old man with a written DNR and do-not-intubate order is undergoing an elective outpatient endoscopy under moderate sedation for a routine screening colonoscopy. The gastroenterologist wonders whether the existing DNR and DNI order should be automatically suspended for the duration of the procedure, given the small possibility of a sedation-related respiratory event that could be reversed with brief intervention. The gastroenterologist notes that the patient has tolerated sedation without complication during two prior similar procedures, though a brief, self-limited oxygen desaturation occurred once during the most recent one. Which of the following is the most appropriate approach?", "options": {"A": "Suspend the DNR and DNI order for the duration of the procedure without discussing it with the patient beforehand", "B": "Discuss with the patient beforehand whether he wants the order suspended for the specific, time-limited context of the procedure", "C": "Cancel the procedure, since sedation is not compatible with a DNR order in place", "D": "Continue the DNR and DNI order as written, without discussing the procedure-specific context with the patient"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Discuss with the patient beforehand whether he wants the order suspended for the specific, time-limited context of the procedure): many institutions require an explicit conversation before an elective procedure with sedation to clarify whether a patient wants their DNR or DNI order to remain in effect during a brief, reversible, procedure-related event, since this is a different context from the original goals behind the order. Neither automatically suspending nor automatically continuing the order without this conversation respects the patient's actual wishes, and sedation itself is not incompatible with a DNR order."}, {"id": 203, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A 58-year-old man with a new diagnosis of glioblastoma asks his oncologist directly, \"How long do I have?\" His prognosis is measured in months, and there is significant uncertainty in the exact timeline. He is accompanied by his wife and adult son, both of whom are present in the room when he asks the question directly of his oncologist during the visit. Which of the following is the most appropriate way to respond?", "options": {"A": "Direct him to research the statistics himself rather than discussing it directly in this specific clinical situation, rather than the alternative approaches listed", "B": "Refuse to give any estimate at all, since prognosis is inherently uncertain and precise timelines can rarely be predicted accurately", "C": "Give an artificially optimistic estimate to preserve his hope, even though this risks undermining trust if his condition later worsens as expected", "D": "Provide an honest range based on typical outcomes, acknowledging uncertainty, in a manner tailored to how much detail he wants"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Provide an honest range based on typical outcomes, acknowledging uncertainty, in a manner tailored to how much detail he wants): patients generally have a right to honest information about their prognosis when they ask for it, delivered compassionately and calibrated to how much detail the individual patient wants. Refusing to answer, giving a falsely reassuring answer, or deflecting the question to outside research all fail to respect his request for honest information from his own physician."}, {"id": 204, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A patient with capacity who has just been told she has a terminal diagnosis states that she does not want any further information about prognosis or treatment options and asks that all future discussions be directed to her husband instead. She explains that she trusts her husband completely to make good decisions on her behalf and states clearly that she has thought carefully about this request before making it. Which of the following best describes this situation?", "options": {"A": "Evidence that the patient currently lacks the mental capacity needed to make her own informed medical decisions at this time", "B": "A valid exercise of the patient's autonomy known as waiver, in which she is choosing to delegate information and decision-making to another person", "C": "A request the physician must refuse in order to comply with informed consent requirements under the clinical circumstances described, rather than the alternative approaches listed", "D": "An entirely invalid request, since under informed consent principles only the patient herself is permitted to receive any medical information about her own diagnosis"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A valid exercise of the patient's autonomy known as waiver, in which she is choosing to delegate information and decision-making to another person): a patient with capacity has the right to waive her own right to receive detailed medical information and to ask that information and decisions instead be directed to a chosen person, such as a spouse. This is an affirmative exercise of autonomy rather than evidence of impaired capacity, and honoring it is fully consistent with the underlying purpose of informed consent, which is to respect the patient's own choices about her care."}, {"id": 210, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "After a series of medication errors involving look-alike vial labels in the intensive care unit, a hospital's patient safety team convenes a structured multidisciplinary review to identify every contributing factor, including label design, storage location, staffing patterns during the errors, and handoff communication, rather than stopping once the label design is identified as the most obvious cause. Investigators interview staff who were present during each incident and review medication administration records spanning the preceding three months across the affected unit. Which of the following best describes this process?", "options": {"A": "A Plan-Do-Study-Act cycle", "B": "A root cause analysis", "C": "An informed consent discussion", "D": "A malpractice deposition"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A root cause analysis): a root cause analysis is a structured, retrospective, multidisciplinary process used after a serious event or pattern of events to identify all of the underlying systems factors that contributed to the error, continuing past the most obvious proximate cause to ask why repeatedly. A PDSA cycle, by contrast, is a forward-looking tool for testing a specific proposed change on a small scale, not a retrospective investigation of what already went wrong."}, {"id": 211, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A quality improvement team wants to reduce catheter-associated urinary tract infections on a hospital unit. They design a small pilot in which a new catheter-care checklist is used by nurses on just one unit for two weeks, then analyze the infection rate compared to the prior two weeks, and use the results to decide whether to expand the checklist hospital-wide. Prior to the pilot, infection rates on this unit had been persistently above the hospital's internal target for several consecutive months despite general infection prevention education. Which of the following best describes this approach?", "options": {"A": "A Plan-Do-Study-Act cycle", "B": "A double-blind trial", "C": "A root-cause analysis", "D": "A case report"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A Plan-Do-Study-Act cycle): this scenario describes the classic structure of a PDSA cycle, in which a team plans a specific, testable change, implements it on a small scale, studies the results against the original goal, and then decides whether to adopt, adapt, or abandon the change before a larger rollout. This iterative, small-scale testing approach is fundamentally different from a large randomized trial or a retrospective investigation of a single past event."}, {"id": 212, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A surgeon performs a procedure on the correct patient but at the wrong vertebral level due to a preoperative imaging labeling error that was not caught during the surgical time-out. The patient experiences no permanent harm after a second corrective procedure but requires an additional hospitalization. The hospital's quality team is separately reviewing the preoperative imaging and marking process used across the surgical department to determine whether other similar labeling errors have occurred recently. Which of the following is the most appropriate approach to disclosing this event to the patient?", "options": {"A": "Avoid disclosure to reduce the hospital's legal liability exposure", "B": "Disclose only the fact that a second procedure was needed, without further detail", "C": "Disclose promptly and honestly what happened, acknowledge the error, apologize, and describe steps being taken to prevent recurrence", "D": "Wait until the patient specifically asks what happened before providing any explanation given the specific facts of this case, rather than the alternative approaches listed"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Disclose promptly and honestly what happened, acknowledge the error, apologize, and describe steps being taken to prevent recurrence): current professionalism standards require full and prompt disclosure of medical errors to patients regardless of whether they cause lasting harm, including an honest explanation, an apology, and a description of preventive steps. Waiting for the patient to ask, disclosing only a partial account, or withholding disclosure to limit liability all fail this ethical obligation, and current evidence suggests transparent disclosure is more likely to reduce than increase litigation risk."}, {"id": 216, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A hospital implements barcode medication administration technology, requiring nurses to scan both the patient's wristband and the medication before administration, as one layer of defense against medication errors, alongside existing safeguards such as pharmacist verification and computerized order entry. This approach, in which multiple imperfect but independent layers of defense work together so that an error is unlikely to pass through every layer at once, is best represented by which of the following models?", "options": {"A": "The substituted judgment standard for surrogate decisions", "B": "The Swiss cheese model of error", "C": "The principle of double effect in ethics", "D": "The best interest standard for incapacitated patients"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The Swiss cheese model of error): the Swiss cheese model illustrates how multiple layers of imperfect defense, such as barcode scanning, pharmacist verification, and computerized order entry, each contain small gaps, and a serious error typically requires several of these gaps to align at once for harm to reach the patient. Adding an additional independent layer of defense, like barcode scanning, reduces the likelihood that any single gap will allow an error through undetected."}, {"id": 219, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A hospital wants to improve hand hygiene compliance among staff. Rather than implementing a single large mandatory training session for the entire hospital at once, the quality improvement team first tests a new hand hygiene reminder sign and observation feedback system on one unit for one month, reviews the compliance data, and adjusts the intervention based on staff feedback before considering hospital-wide rollout. Which of the following best explains the advantage of this small-scale testing approach compared to an immediate hospital-wide rollout?", "options": {"A": "It is a requirement under federal law for any hospital undertaking a quality improvement initiative of this kind.", "B": "It guarantees that hand hygiene compliance will improve hospital-wide once the reminder signs and feedback system are eventually rolled out.", "C": "It eliminates the need for any further measurement of compliance once the intervention is implemented across the entire hospital.", "D": "It allows the team to identify and fix problems with the intervention on a small scale before investing resources in a larger rollout"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It allows the team to identify and fix problems with the intervention on a small scale before investing resources in a larger rollout): the core advantage of a small-scale PDSA test is that unforeseen problems, unintended consequences, or the need for adjustments can be identified and corrected while the stakes and resource investment are still low, before deciding whether to expand the intervention. This iterative approach is not a legal requirement, and no quality intervention can guarantee success without ongoing monitoring even after a wider rollout."}, {"id": 221, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A hospital reviews its data on retained surgical items after operations and finds that most cases occurred during complex, emergent procedures where the standard instrument count process was interrupted or rushed. As a result, the hospital revises its protocol to require a specific hard stop before wound closure in every case, regardless of urgency, with an independent second count performed by a different team member. Which of the following best describes this type of intervention?", "options": {"A": "A punitive measure targeting the surgical team involved in prior incidents in the situation described, rather than the alternative approaches listed", "B": "An informed consent requirement rather than a safety intervention", "C": "A systems-level process change designed to add a redundant safeguard against a recurring type of error", "D": "A change made solely to satisfy a specific patient's individual request"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A systems-level process change designed to add a redundant safeguard against a recurring type of error): identifying that interrupted or rushed counting processes contributed to prior events, and responding by building in an additional, independent verification step that applies to every case regardless of urgency, exemplifies a systems-based safety intervention intended to close a recurring gap, rather than a punitive response directed at the individuals involved in past incidents."}, {"id": 222, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A hospital's leadership wants to build a culture in which staff feel comfortable reporting their own errors and near misses without excessive fear of punishment, while still holding individuals accountable for reckless or intentionally unsafe behavior. Leadership has also invested in additional training resources and improved reporting software as part of this broader initiative to change how the organization responds to reported events. Which of the following best describes this approach?", "options": {"A": "A zero-tolerance culture in which reported errors generally lead to some disciplinary consequence for the individual involved", "B": "A just culture, which distinguishes human error and at-risk behavior from reckless conduct", "C": "A culture in which individuals are rarely held accountable for actions that contributed to patient harm", "D": "A culture that focuses mainly on legal risk management rather than on improving patient safety"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A just culture, which distinguishes human error and at-risk behavior from reckless conduct): a just culture framework recognizes that most errors result from ordinary human fallibility or system-encouraged shortcuts rather than intentional wrongdoing, and it responds to these situations by focusing on learning and system improvement, while still holding individuals accountable when their actions represent genuinely reckless or intentional misconduct. This balance is what encourages honest error reporting while preserving meaningful accountability."}, {"id": 223, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "After a sentinel event involving a patient suicide on an inpatient psychiatric unit, the hospital is required to conduct a formal, thorough analysis and submit a report to the relevant accrediting body. The unit had implemented a new suicide risk assessment tool six months prior to the event, though staff report inconsistent use of the tool during busy shifts. Which of the following best describes the primary purpose of this required analysis?", "options": {"A": "To determine whether the hospital should be sued by the patient's family", "B": "To satisfy a purely administrative requirement with no expected impact on future practice given the specific facts of this case, rather than the alternative approaches listed", "C": "To assign individual blame to the staff members on duty at the time of the event", "D": "To identify the underlying systemic contributing factors and implement changes to reduce the likelihood of a similar future event"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (To identify the underlying systemic contributing factors and implement changes to reduce the likelihood of a similar future event): a sentinel event, defined as an unexpected occurrence involving serious harm or death not primarily related to the natural course of the patient's illness, requires a thorough root cause analysis focused on identifying the systemic factors that contributed to the event and implementing corrective action, rather than being primarily an exercise in individual blame or litigation preparation."}, {"id": 224, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A resident physician makes a documentation error in a discharge summary, listing an incorrect follow-up appointment date that differs from what the patient was actually told verbally at discharge. The error is discovered a week later when the patient calls confused about the date. The patient, an elderly man living alone, had already missed one scheduled appointment earlier in the week before this discrepancy was discovered during the follow-up call. Which of the following is the most appropriate way to address this error at the individual patient level?", "options": {"A": "Contact the patient promptly to clarify the correct appointment date and correct the documentation error", "B": "Blame the discharge planning staff for the error without further verification", "C": "Ignore the discrepancy since the patient will presumably call to clarify if confused, given the circumstances described here", "D": "Wait until the patient's next scheduled visit to address the discrepancy"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Contact the patient promptly to clarify the correct appointment date and correct the documentation error): once a documentation error affecting a patient's care, such as an incorrect follow-up date, is identified, the appropriate response is to promptly contact the patient to clarify the correct information and to correct the underlying documentation, minimizing any risk of a missed or delayed follow-up visit, rather than waiting passively or assigning blame without first verifying what actually happened."}, {"id": 225, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A quality improvement team is evaluating whether a new sepsis screening protocol implemented in the emergency department has reduced the time to first antibiotic administration for patients with septic shock. They compare data from the six months before implementation to the six months after implementation. Emergency department leadership had already identified delayed antibiotic administration as a recurring concern during a prior internal audit conducted six months before this protocol was introduced. Which of the following best describes this type of evaluation?", "options": {"A": "A legal audit conducted for malpractice defense purposes", "B": "A root cause analysis of a single adverse event", "C": "An informed consent process for emergency department patients, consistent with the details given", "D": "An outcome measurement used to evaluate the effect of a systems-level process change"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (An outcome measurement used to evaluate the effect of a systems-level process change): comparing a specific measurable outcome, here time to antibiotic administration, before and after implementing a new protocol is a standard quality improvement method for evaluating whether a systems-level change achieved its intended effect. This is distinct from a root cause analysis, which investigates a specific past event, and has no direct connection to consent processes or malpractice defense."}, {"id": 227, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A hospital's leadership is deciding how to respond after an internal review finds that a nurse who was working a mandated double shift due to a staffing shortage made a medication administration error that reached the patient and caused temporary harm. The hospital had been experiencing a persistent nursing shortage on this unit for several months prior to the incident, requiring frequent use of mandatory overtime shifts. Which of the following most reflects a systems-based, just culture response to this specific finding?", "options": {"A": "Terminate the nurse immediately for the error", "B": "Address the underlying staffing shortage and fatigue-related risk factors in addition to reviewing the specific error", "C": "Take no action since the error was ultimately the nurse's individual responsibility", "D": "Require the nurse to work exclusively short shifts as a punitive measure without addressing the staffing shortage under the facts described here"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Address the underlying staffing shortage and fatigue-related risk factors in addition to reviewing the specific error): a just culture, systems-based response recognizes that a mandated double shift and resulting fatigue are significant systemic contributing factors to this error, and appropriately addressing them, alongside any individual education needed, is more likely to prevent recurrence than terminating the nurse, ignoring the systemic issue entirely, or imposing a punitive schedule change that does not fix the underlying staffing problem."}, {"id": 228, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A hospital wants to reduce readmissions for patients with heart failure. A multidisciplinary team including physicians, nurses, pharmacists, and social workers is assembled to review discharge processes, medication reconciliation practices, and access to outpatient follow-up, rather than assigning the task to a single department alone. The project's initial planning phase revealed that prior single-department efforts to reduce readmissions on this unit had shown only modest and inconsistent improvement over the past two years. Which of the following best explains the rationale for this multidisciplinary approach to a quality improvement project?", "options": {"A": "Multidisciplinary teams are required only when a sentinel event has occurred", "B": "This approach is used exclusively for research studies, not routine quality improvement in this particular scenario, rather than the alternative approaches listed", "C": "Complex, multifactorial problems like readmissions usually require input and coordinated change across multiple contributing processes and disciplines", "D": "A single department is generally sufficient to address any quality improvement problem"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Complex, multifactorial problems like readmissions usually require input and coordinated change across multiple contributing processes and disciplines): heart failure readmissions typically result from the interaction of many contributing factors, including discharge education, medication reconciliation, and outpatient access, each of which is best addressed by the relevant discipline working together. A single department alone often cannot capture or effectively address every contributing factor to a problem this multifactorial."}, {"id": 229, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A hospital implements a policy requiring that any adverse event resulting in serious patient harm be reported internally within twenty-four hours, and that a preliminary review begin within seventy-two hours, in order to capture accurate details before memories fade and to identify any urgent, ongoing safety hazard needing immediate correction. The policy was developed after a prior serious event took several weeks to be formally reviewed, during which time a similar contributing hazard persisted unaddressed on another unit. Which of the following best describes the primary purpose of this reporting timeline requirement?", "options": {"A": "To provide a basis for immediate disciplinary action against involved staff", "B": "To satisfy an insurance billing requirement unrelated to patient safety", "C": "To ensure a rapid, accurate investigation and allow prompt identification of any ongoing hazard requiring immediate action", "D": "To delay disclosure to the patient until the investigation is fully complete in this specific clinical situation, rather than the alternative approaches listed"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (To ensure a rapid, accurate investigation and allow prompt identification of any ongoing hazard requiring immediate action): a prompt internal reporting and review timeline is intended to capture accurate, timely information while memories and documentation are fresh, and, importantly, to identify quickly whether an ongoing systemic hazard needs urgent correction to prevent additional patients from being harmed. Disclosure to the patient about what happened should occur promptly and is not appropriately delayed until an entire internal investigation concludes."}, {"id": 234, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A 67-year-old man is reviewing his Medicare options. He wants coverage for his upcoming outpatient cardiology visits and durable medical equipment, distinct from his recent inpatient hospital stay, which was covered under a different part of Medicare. He has no history of hospitalization in the past year and takes two prescription medications for hypertension and hyperlipidemia that require ongoing monitoring at his outpatient visits. Which part of Medicare covers his outpatient physician visits and durable medical equipment?", "options": {"A": "Medicare Part C", "B": "Medicare Part B", "C": "Medicare Part D", "D": "Medicare Part A"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Medicare Part B): Medicare Part B covers outpatient physician services, durable medical equipment, and preventive services, and requires payment of a monthly premium. Part A covers inpatient hospital stays, skilled nursing facility care, and hospice, Part C is Medicare Advantage, an alternative private-plan structure for the overall benefit, and Part D covers outpatient prescription drugs."}, {"id": 236, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A 58-year-old woman with a low income and two young children qualifies for a state-administered program that covers her family's medical care, including well-child visits and prenatal care for a subsequent pregnancy. The specific services covered and the income eligibility threshold differ somewhat from a similar family living in a neighboring state. The pediatric visits are scheduled through a local clinic that participates in her state's program, and her prenatal care will be coordinated through the same clinic once she confirms her pregnancy. Which government program does this best describe?", "options": {"A": "A PPO plan", "B": "An HMO plan", "C": "Medicaid", "D": "Medicare"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Medicaid): Medicaid is a joint federal and state program providing coverage for eligible low-income individuals and families, and because states administer their own programs within federal guidelines, both the specific covered benefits and the income eligibility thresholds vary meaningfully from state to state. Medicare, in contrast, primarily serves patients 65 and older or those with specific disabilities or end-stage renal disease, with more uniform national eligibility rules."}, {"id": 237, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A patient enrolled in a health insurance plan is told she must select a primary care physician who will coordinate her care and provide referrals before she can see most specialists, and that care received outside her plan's network will generally not be covered except in an emergency. She previously required a referral and preauthorization from her primary care physician before seeing any specialist under a different plan she had the year before switching to this one. Which type of health plan does this describe?", "options": {"A": "Traditional fee-for-service Medicare with no supplemental plan", "B": "A health maintenance organization", "C": "A preferred provider organization", "D": "A high-deductible plan with no network restrictions"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A health maintenance organization): an HMO typically requires enrollees to select a primary care physician who coordinates care and provides referrals to specialists, and generally does not cover out-of-network care except in emergencies. A PPO, by contrast, offers more flexibility, allowing patients to see specialists without a referral and to receive at least partial coverage for out-of-network care, usually in exchange for a higher premium."}, {"id": 238, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A patient enrolled in a health insurance plan is able to see a dermatologist directly without first obtaining a referral from a primary care physician, and her plan provides partial coverage even when she chooses to see a specialist who is outside the plan's preferred network, though her out-of-pocket cost is somewhat higher in that case. She recently switched to this plan specifically because her previous plan required a referral before she could see any specialist for a chronic skin condition she has struggled to manage. Which type of health plan does this describe?", "options": {"A": "A preferred provider organization", "B": "A health maintenance organization", "C": "A capitated Medicaid managed care plan exclusively", "D": "Traditional fee-for-service Medicare Part A alone"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A preferred provider organization): a PPO allows enrollees to see specialists directly without a referral and provides at least partial coverage for out-of-network care, generally at a higher cost-sharing level, in exchange for a higher monthly premium than a more restrictive plan. This flexibility contrasts with an HMO, which generally requires a primary care referral and does not cover out-of-network care except in an emergency."}, {"id": 241, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A hospital system implements a payment arrangement with several insurers in which a portion of its total reimbursement is withheld or bonused based on measured performance on specific quality metrics, such as hospital readmission rates and blood pressure control rates across its patient population, rather than being based purely on the number of services billed. The hospital's leadership team reviews this quality data quarterly alongside its overall financial performance to identify units that may benefit from additional targeted quality improvement support. Which of the following best describes this payment model?", "options": {"A": "Pay-for-performance or value-based payment", "B": "Fee-for-service reimbursement", "C": "An indemnity insurance model", "D": "Straight capitation with no quality component"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Pay-for-performance or value-based payment): tying a portion of reimbursement to measured quality outcomes, such as readmission rates or blood pressure control across a population, rather than to the volume of services delivered, is the defining feature of pay-for-performance or value-based payment models. This is distinct from pure fee-for-service, which pays for each service regardless of outcome, and from pure capitation, which pays a fixed per-patient amount without an explicit quality-based adjustment."}, {"id": 243, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A pharmaceutical sales representative offers a physician an all-expenses-paid trip to a resort conference to learn about a new medication the representative's company manufactures, in exchange for the physician agreeing to prescribe the medication more frequently to eligible patients. The conference itself includes several hours of legitimate accredited educational content alongside recreational activities, and attendance is fully covered including airfare, lodging, and meals. Which of the following is the most appropriate response?", "options": {"A": "Accept the offer as long as the medication is genuinely effective for some patients", "B": "Decline the offer, since it represents an inappropriate conflict of interest tying a personal benefit to a specific prescribing commitment", "C": "Accept the offer but disclose it only if a patient specifically asks", "D": "Accept the offer since continuing medical education is appropriate no matter the funding source given the specific facts of this case, rather than the alternative approaches listed"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Decline the offer, since it represents an inappropriate conflict of interest tying a personal benefit to a specific prescribing commitment): accepting a substantial personal benefit explicitly conditioned on a commitment to prescribe a specific product creates a direct conflict of interest that can improperly influence clinical judgment, independent of whether the medication happens to be effective for some patients. This differs from accepting a small, non-conditioned token of appreciation or attending genuinely independent continuing education."}, {"id": 244, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A medical student notices that a senior resident on her team appears to be diverting leftover opioid medication intended for patients and appears impaired during several recent shifts, including slurred speech and unsteady gait. She has also noticed that controlled substance counts on the unit have been off on two occasions during his recent shifts, though no formal discrepancy report was filed at the time. Which of the following is the most appropriate action for the medical student to take?", "options": {"A": "Confront the resident directly and privately, then take no further action no matter the response", "B": "Wait to see if the behavior continues before deciding whether to say anything", "C": "Report the concern to the appropriate supervising attending or institutional channel promptly", "D": "Say nothing since reporting a senior resident could jeopardize her own evaluation"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Report the concern to the appropriate supervising attending or institutional channel promptly): signs of possible substance impairment and medication diversion by a colleague represent a serious patient safety concern and a professionalism obligation to report through appropriate channels, regardless of any discomfort about hierarchy or fear of professional consequences. Waiting, addressing it only informally, or remaining silent all risk continued harm to patients under that colleague's care."}, {"id": 246, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A researcher conducting a clinical trial owns a meaningful amount of stock in the pharmaceutical company that manufactures the experimental drug being studied in the trial. The trial is a multi-site study evaluating the drug's effectiveness compared to the current standard treatment, and the researcher is also one of the principal investigators listed on the study protocol. Which of the following is the most appropriate way to address this situation?", "options": {"A": "The financial interest should be disclosed to the institutional review board and appropriately managed, since disclosure and oversight, not automatic exclusion, is the usual remedy", "B": "The researcher should generally be excluded from serving as an investigator on this study given the scale of the potential financial benefit involved", "C": "The financial interest only needs to be disclosed if the drug is later found to be effective, since a negative outcome would carry no risk of biased reporting", "D": "No disclosure is needed since owning stock does not itself prove the researcher will act unethically or alter the study's reported results"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The financial interest should be disclosed to the institutional review board and appropriately managed, since disclosure and oversight, not automatic exclusion, is the usual remedy): a conflict of interest exists because of the mere potential for the financial interest to bias professional judgment, regardless of whether bias is ever proven to have occurred. The standard approach is transparent disclosure to the appropriate oversight body, such as the institutional review board, along with appropriate management strategies, rather than an automatic, blanket exclusion from participating in the research at all."}, {"id": 247, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A patient enrolling in a clinical trial for a new chemotherapy agent is told during the informed consent process that participation is voluntary, that she may withdraw at any time without any effect on her ongoing standard clinical care, and is informed of risks specific to the experimental agent beyond those of standard treatment. Which of the following best describes the purpose of specifically disclosing these particular elements during research informed consent?", "options": {"A": "These elements are considered optional additions that go beyond what federal regulations actually require for standard research consent documents", "B": "These elements protect the autonomy of a research participant, who may otherwise feel obligated to continue participating in a study they believe is required for their care", "C": "These elements are relevant only for studies involving an experimental surgical procedure rather than a medication trial like this one", "D": "These elements are required only for research funded directly by a pharmaceutical company rather than an academic institution"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (These elements protect the autonomy of a research participant, who may otherwise feel obligated to continue participating in a study they believe is required for their care): explicitly disclosing that participation is voluntary, that withdrawal will not affect ongoing standard care, and any research-specific risks helps prevent a patient from feeling coerced into continuing participation, particularly one who might otherwise believe that declining or withdrawing from the study would jeopardize her regular clinical treatment. These protections apply broadly to research consent generally, not only to surgical studies or industry-funded trials."}, {"id": 248, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A physician who also serves as a paid consultant for a medical device company is asked to write a continuing medical education presentation reviewing treatment options for a condition in which the company's device is one option among several. The presentation is intended for a regional continuing medical education audience of primary care physicians who are deciding among several treatment options for their own patients. Which of the following is the most appropriate way to handle this situation?", "options": {"A": "Present only favorable information about the device to the audience without disclosing the consulting relationship or its potential influence", "B": "Ask the company to review and approve the presentation content before it is given to the audience", "C": "Decline to mention the device at all during the presentation to avoid any appearance of bias toward it", "D": "Disclose the consulting relationship prominently to the audience and present a balanced review of all reasonable treatment options"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Disclose the consulting relationship prominently to the audience and present a balanced review of all reasonable treatment options): the appropriate way to manage this conflict of interest is transparent disclosure of the relationship to the audience, combined with an educational presentation that fairly represents the full range of reasonable treatment options rather than emphasizing only the sponsoring company's device. Allowing the company to control or approve the content, or omitting disclosure, would compromise the educational integrity of the presentation."}, {"id": 249, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A 45-year-old man with a new diagnosis of hypertension is invited by his own treating physician to enroll in a clinical trial being conducted by that same physician, comparing two different classes of antihypertensive medication. The trial is currently enrolling additional participants at a slower rate than originally projected, and the physician's research coordinator has suggested his own patient panel as a potential source of eligible participants. Which of the following is an important additional consideration in obtaining informed consent in this specific situation, beyond the standard elements of research consent?", "options": {"A": "Only the standard elements of research consent would typically be relevant here, since most patients are thought to make enrollment decisions independently of their relationship with a treating physician.", "B": "The physician should specifically address the potential for the patient to feel pressured to participate given the existing treatment relationship, and should clarify that his standard care will not be affected by his decision", "C": "The study would be unlikely to proceed under most typical ethical review processes when the treating physician also serves as the study's own researcher recruiting his patients.", "D": "The patient's family should also be asked to provide their own separate written consent, in addition to the consent already given directly by the patient himself."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The physician should specifically address the potential for the patient to feel pressured to participate given the existing treatment relationship, and should clarify that his standard care will not be affected by his decision): when a treating physician is also the researcher recruiting his own patient, there is a heightened risk that the patient may feel obligated to participate to please his physician or fear his regular care will suffer if he declines. This dual role does not automatically prohibit the study, but it does require the consent process to explicitly address this pressure and reaffirm that standard care is unaffected by the patient's research decision."}, {"id": 250, "domain": "social_sciences_ethics_law_and_patient_safety", "question": "A 62-year-old man is recovering from a hospitalization for community-acquired pneumonia. Before discharge, the hospital's care coordination team schedules a follow-up appointment with his primary care physician, reconciles his medication list to remove a duplicate prescription created during the admission, and provides him with written instructions in plain language describing warning signs that should prompt him to seek care again. Which of the following best describes the overarching purpose of this coordinated discharge process?", "options": {"A": "To improve care transitions and reduce the risk of a preventable readmission or adverse event after discharge", "B": "To shift most of the legal responsibility for the patient's ongoing care toward the primary care physician", "C": "To satisfy an insurance billing requirement that carries little direct clinical relevance for this patient", "D": "To reduce the hospital's total length of stay with less emphasis on patient safety considerations"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (To improve care transitions and reduce the risk of a preventable readmission or adverse event after discharge): coordinated discharge processes, including medication reconciliation, scheduled follow-up, and clear patient-facing instructions, are core systems-based practice interventions designed to improve the transition of care from hospital to outpatient settings and reduce the risk of a preventable readmission or a post-discharge adverse event, rather than being driven primarily by length-of-stay metrics or billing requirements alone."}, {"id": 255, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 58-year-old woman with stage 4 chronic kidney disease from long-standing poorly controlled hypertension presents for a routine nephrology visit. She reports several months of fatigue and mild exertional dyspnea that has gradually worsened, though she denies chest pain, melena, or obvious blood loss of any kind. Her blood pressure today is 148/88 mmHg. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 9.2 g/dL with a normal mean corpuscular volume, normal serum iron, ferritin, and total iron binding capacity, and an estimated GFR of 22 mL/min/1.73m2. Which of the following best explains her anemia?", "options": {"A": "Iron deficiency from occult chronic gastrointestinal blood loss that has not yet been identified", "B": "Reduced renal production of erythropoietin as her functioning kidney mass has declined", "C": "Folate deficiency related to poor oral intake over the preceding several months", "D": "Ongoing hemolysis driven by uremic platelet and red cell membrane dysfunction"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reduced renal production of erythropoietin as her functioning kidney mass has declined): As functioning renal mass declines in chronic kidney disease, erythropoietin production falls, producing a normocytic, normochromic anemia with normal iron studies, exactly as seen here. This is treated with an erythropoiesis-stimulating agent once adequate iron stores are confirmed, since ESAs are ineffective when iron is deficient."}, {"id": 256, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 71-year-old man with a new diagnosis of small cell lung cancer is admitted with confusion. He is found to have a serum sodium of 118 mEq/L. He appears euvolemic on exam, with no peripheral edema, no jugular venous distension, and no orthostatic blood pressure changes when moved from lying to standing. Serum osmolality is 244 mOsm/kg, urine osmolality is 450 mOsm/kg, and urine sodium is 60 mEq/L. Thyroid stimulating hormone and morning cortisol are both within normal limits. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Hypovolemic hyponatremia resulting from unrecognized excessive diuretic use continuing at home", "B": "Hypervolemic hyponatremia caused by an undiagnosed underlying dilated cardiomyopathy", "C": "Primary polydipsia resulting from persistent excessive free water intake alone", "D": "Syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion related to his malignancy"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion related to his malignancy): A euvolemic patient with hyponatremia, inappropriately concentrated urine relative to a low serum osmolality, and an elevated urine sodium, in the setting of small cell lung cancer, is classic for SIADH. Small cell lung cancer is a well-known ectopic source of antidiuretic hormone production. Primary polydipsia would instead show a dilute urine osmolality, not a concentrated one, which argues against that diagnosis here."}, {"id": 257, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 45-year-old man with end-stage renal disease on a three-times-weekly hemodialysis schedule misses two consecutive dialysis sessions after a family emergency out of town and now presents with generalized weakness and mild shortness of breath. His potassium returns at 7.4 mEq/L, and an ECG shows peaked T waves in multiple leads without QRS widening or a sine wave pattern. Intravenous calcium gluconate is given promptly at the bedside for cardiac membrane stabilization. Which of the following should be administered next to rapidly shift potassium into cells while urgent dialysis is arranged?", "options": {"A": "Regular insulin given together with concurrent dextrose to prevent hypoglycemia", "B": "Oral sodium polystyrene sulfonate given alone as the sole next intervention, a possibility sometimes raised early in the workup", "C": "A thiazide diuretic added to his existing medication regimen", "D": "Intravenous normal saline infused at a slow, maintenance rate only"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Regular insulin given together with concurrent dextrose to prevent hypoglycemia): After membrane stabilization with calcium, the next step is to shift potassium intracellularly quickly, which is accomplished with intravenous insulin, given with dextrose to prevent hypoglycemia, and can be supplemented with a nebulized beta agonist. Sodium polystyrene sulfonate removes potassium from the body but acts too slowly for this emergency, and dialysis remains the definitive treatment when a patient cannot be managed with medical therapy alone."}, {"id": 259, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 34-year-old Black man with a history of intravenous heroin use presents with two weeks of progressive bilateral lower extremity edema and new frothy urine that he first noticed while urinating at work. He denies fever, rash, or joint pain. A spot urine protein to creatinine ratio is 6.2, serum albumin is 2.1 g/dL, and serum creatinine is mildly elevated at 1.4 mg/dL. HIV testing is negative. Renal biopsy shows segmental sclerosis affecting some but not all glomeruli, with diffuse podocyte foot process effacement seen on electron microscopy. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Minimal change disease presenting atypically for his age", "B": "Membranous nephropathy related to an occult solid malignancy", "C": "Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis associated with his heroin use", "D": "Post-infectious glomerulonephritis following an unrecognized skin infection"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis associated with his heroin use): FSGS is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in Black adults and is strongly associated with heroin use and HIV infection, though this patient's HIV test is negative. The biopsy finding of segmental sclerosis in only some glomeruli, with foot process effacement, is characteristic. Minimal change disease shows a normal light microscopy appearance, and membranous nephropathy shows diffuse basement membrane thickening rather than segmental sclerosis."}, {"id": 264, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 40-year-old man presents to the emergency department with sudden severe left flank pain that began abruptly two hours ago while he was at the gym, radiating down toward his groin, along with nausea, one episode of vomiting, and grossly visible blood in his urine. Noncontrast CT of the abdomen and pelvis shows a 6 mm stone lodged at the ureterovesical junction with mild upstream hydronephrosis. He mentions that he eats a large amount of red meat daily and admits he rarely drinks more than one glass of water per day. Which stone type is most likely responsible for his presentation?", "options": {"A": "Struvite, related to a chronic urease-producing urinary infection, a pattern also encountered in related clinical contexts", "B": "Cystine, related to an inherited amino acid transport defect", "C": "Calcium oxalate, related to low fluid intake and a high animal protein diet", "D": "Uric acid, related to an underlying purine metabolism disorder"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Calcium oxalate, related to low fluid intake and a high animal protein diet): Calcium oxalate stones are the most common type of kidney stone overall and are strongly promoted by chronically low fluid intake and diets high in oxalate or animal protein, matching this patient's history exactly. Struvite stones require a urease-producing infection, cystine stones suggest an inherited transport defect, and uric acid stones are linked to gout and appear radiolucent on plain film, none of which fit this presentation as well."}, {"id": 265, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 55-year-old woman with a long history of recurrent urinary tract infections, most recently growing Proteus mirabilis on culture, is found incidentally on a CT scan obtained for unrelated abdominal pain to have a large branching stone filling most of the renal pelvis and extending into several calyces on the right side. She reports chronic mild flank discomfort but no acute severe pain today. Urine pH on dipstick is notably alkaline at 7.8. Which of the following stone types is most likely responsible for this branching calculus?", "options": {"A": "Uric acid, which typically forms in persistently acidic urine", "B": "Struvite (magnesium ammonium phosphate), which forms in alkaline urine from urease-producing bacteria", "C": "Calcium oxalate, the most common stone type in the general population", "D": "Cystine, which reflects an inherited renal tubular transport defect"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Struvite (magnesium ammonium phosphate), which forms in alkaline urine from urease-producing bacteria): Struvite stones form in alkaline urine generated by urease-producing organisms such as Proteus mirabilis, which splits urea into ammonia and raises urine pH. They can grow rapidly into large staghorn calculi filling the collecting system, exactly as described here, and typically require surgical removal along with treatment of the underlying chronic infection."}, {"id": 268, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "An 80-year-old woman who lives in a nursing home and has mild baseline cognitive impairment undergoes a routine urinalysis as part of a broader workup for a fall, which shows bacteria and a moderate number of white blood cells. When directly questioned, she denies dysuria, urgency, fever, flank pain, or any change in her usual baseline mental status, and the nursing staff confirms she has been acting entirely like herself. Which of the following is the most appropriate management of her urinalysis finding?", "options": {"A": "No antibiotic treatment is indicated based on these findings alone", "B": "Immediate empiric treatment with a fluoroquinolone antibiotic", "C": "Immediate treatment with intravenous ceftriaxone given her age, a mechanism described in some patients with similar findings", "D": "Referral for cystoscopy to further evaluate the bladder"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (No antibiotic treatment is indicated based on these findings alone): Asymptomatic bacteriuria in an elderly, nonpregnant patient without a planned urologic procedure should not be treated, since treatment does not improve outcomes and only increases the risk of antibiotic resistance and adverse drug effects. Treatment of asymptomatic bacteriuria is reserved specifically for pregnancy and prior to invasive urologic procedures."}, {"id": 270, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 66-year-old woman describes a sudden, overwhelming urge to urinate that comes on with little warning, followed by involuntary leakage before she can reach the bathroom, occurring several times daily and increasingly at night as well. She specifically denies any leakage associated with coughing, laughing, or physical exertion. She has already tried a structured bladder training program with scheduled voiding for six weeks with only partial improvement. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in her treatment?", "options": {"A": "Continue pelvic floor muscle exercises alone without adding any additional pharmacologic therapy at this time", "B": "Start an antimuscarinic agent such as oxybutynin or a beta-3 agonist", "C": "Begin intermittent self-catheterization on a scheduled basis to manage her ongoing urinary symptoms", "D": "Proceed directly to a midurethral sling procedure without first trying additional medical therapy"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Start an antimuscarinic agent such as oxybutynin or a beta-3 agonist): Sudden urgency with leakage before reaching the toilet describes urgency incontinence from an overactive detrusor muscle. After behavioral measures such as bladder training have provided only partial benefit, as in this patient, first-line pharmacologic treatment is an antimuscarinic agent or a beta-3 agonist such as mirabegron, not a sling procedure, which instead treats stress incontinence."}, {"id": 272, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 71-year-old man returns for a routine visit and is found on digital rectal exam to have an asymmetric, hard nodule on the posterior aspect of his prostate that was not present on his exam two years earlier. His PSA has risen steadily from 2.1 to 6.4 ng/mL over that same period despite no urinary symptoms and no recent instrumentation or infection. She denies any recent travel, sick contacts, or exposure to new medications or supplements. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in his evaluation?", "options": {"A": "Reassure him, since some degree of PSA elevation is expected with normal aging", "B": "Start finasteride empirically and recheck his PSA level in six months", "C": "Begin a course of empiric antibiotics for presumed chronic prostatitis", "D": "Refer for prostate biopsy to evaluate for underlying malignancy"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Refer for prostate biopsy to evaluate for underlying malignancy): A hard, asymmetric nodule combined with a rising PSA over time is concerning for prostate cancer rather than benign hyperplasia, which typically produces smooth, symmetric enlargement instead. Biopsy is needed to confirm the diagnosis and obtain a Gleason score, which guides both prognosis and treatment decisions going forward."}, {"id": 273, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 15-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department by his mother for sudden onset of severe left testicular pain that began three hours ago while he was at soccer practice, associated with nausea and one episode of vomiting on the drive in. He has never had a similar episode before. On exam his left testicle appears high-riding with an abnormal horizontal lie compared to the right side, and the cremasteric reflex is absent on the left. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in his management?", "options": {"A": "Obtain a scrotal ultrasound with Doppler before taking any further action", "B": "Arrange emergent urologic consultation for surgical exploration and detorsion", "C": "Provide reassurance and arrange outpatient urology follow-up within one week", "D": "Begin empiric antibiotics for presumed acute epididymitis"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Arrange emergent urologic consultation for surgical exploration and detorsion): This presentation, sudden severe pain, a high-riding testicle with abnormal transverse lie, and an absent cremasteric reflex, is classic for testicular torsion, a surgical emergency. When clinical suspicion is this high, surgical exploration should not be delayed for imaging, since testicular salvage depends heavily on detorsion within about six hours of symptom onset."}, {"id": 274, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 24-year-old sexually active man with two new partners in the last month presents with two days of gradually worsening left scrotal pain and swelling accompanied by dysuria. He notes that elevating the scrotum with his hand while lying down provides some relief of the pain. On exam the cremasteric reflex is intact bilaterally, and a small amount of urethral discharge is visible at the meatus. Her physical exam is otherwise unremarkable aside from the findings already described above. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Epididymitis, most likely caused by a sexually transmitted organism", "B": "Testicular torsion requiring emergent surgical exploration", "C": "Testicular malignancy presenting with secondary inflammation, a mechanism described in some patients with similar findings", "D": "A simple hydrocele that has become acutely symptomatic"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Epididymitis, most likely caused by a sexually transmitted organism): Gradual onset of scrotal pain, relief with scrotal elevation (a positive Prehn sign), an intact cremasteric reflex, and urethral discharge in a sexually active young man with multiple recent partners point to epididymitis, commonly caused by Chlamydia trachomatis or Neisseria gonorrhoeae, which requires antibiotics covering both organisms."}, {"id": 275, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis given this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Pelvic inflammatory disease", "B": "Ectopic pregnancy", "C": "Ovarian torsion", "D": "Acute appendicitis"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Pelvic inflammatory disease): Fever, lower abdominal pain, and cervical motion tenderness with bilateral adnexal tenderness in a sexually active young woman with multiple recent partners is the classic clinical picture of pelvic inflammatory disease, an ascending infection of the upper genital tract usually caused by Chlamydia trachomatis or Neisseria gonorrhoeae. A negative pregnancy test helps exclude ectopic pregnancy as the cause of her pain.", "case_id": "case_PID1", "case_text": "A 23-year-old sexually active woman presents with three days of lower abdominal pain, fever, and a new, foul-smelling vaginal discharge that started around the same time as the pain. She reports having had two new sexual partners over the past two months and admits she uses condoms inconsistently. On exam she has a temperature of 38.3C, diffuse lower abdominal tenderness without rebound or guarding, and marked cervical motion tenderness with bilateral adnexal tenderness on bimanual exam. A urine pregnancy test performed in the department is negative, and she is tolerating oral intake without difficulty.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 276, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "Which of the following organisms is most commonly implicated as the primary trigger of this condition?", "options": {"A": "Escherichia coli, the most common cause of simple cystitis", "B": "Chlamydia trachomatis, a leading sexually transmitted cause", "C": "Candida albicans, a common cause of vulvovaginal irritation", "D": "Herpes simplex virus, typically causing painful genital ulcers"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Chlamydia trachomatis, a leading sexually transmitted cause): Chlamydia trachomatis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae are the two organisms most commonly responsible for pelvic inflammatory disease, though anaerobic and other vaginal flora often contribute as polymicrobial co-pathogens once the ascending infection is established, which is why empiric treatment covers multiple organism classes at once.", "case_id": "case_PID1", "case_text": "A 23-year-old sexually active woman presents with three days of lower abdominal pain, fever, and a new, foul-smelling vaginal discharge that started around the same time as the pain. She reports having had two new sexual partners over the past two months and admits she uses condoms inconsistently. On exam she has a temperature of 38.3C, diffuse lower abdominal tenderness without rebound or guarding, and marked cervical motion tenderness with bilateral adnexal tenderness on bimanual exam. A urine pregnancy test performed in the department is negative, and she is tolerating oral intake without difficulty.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 277, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "Given her stable vital signs, ability to tolerate oral intake, and the absence of a tubo-ovarian abscess on pelvic ultrasound, which of the following is the most appropriate initial management approach for this patient?", "options": {"A": "Observation alone with a repeat pelvic exam scheduled in one week", "B": "Outpatient antifungal therapy directed at vulvovaginal candidiasis, a finding that can appear in several overlapping conditions", "C": "Immediate diagnostic laparoscopy before starting any antibiotics", "D": "Outpatient antibiotic therapy covering gonorrhea, chlamydia, and anaerobic organisms"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Outpatient antibiotic therapy covering gonorrhea, chlamydia, and anaerobic organisms): A hemodynamically stable patient with pelvic inflammatory disease who can tolerate oral intake and does not have a tubo-ovarian abscess or other complication can be treated as an outpatient with a regimen that covers gonorrhea, chlamydia, and anaerobic organisms, given the polymicrobial nature of most of these infections.", "case_id": "case_PID1", "case_text": "A 23-year-old sexually active woman presents with three days of lower abdominal pain, fever, and a new, foul-smelling vaginal discharge that started around the same time as the pain. She reports having had two new sexual partners over the past two months and admits she uses condoms inconsistently. On exam she has a temperature of 38.3C, diffuse lower abdominal tenderness without rebound or guarding, and marked cervical motion tenderness with bilateral adnexal tenderness on bimanual exam. A urine pregnancy test performed in the department is negative, and she is tolerating oral intake without difficulty.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 278, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "If this episode of pelvic inflammatory disease is left untreated or recurs multiple times over the following years, which of the following long-term complications is she at the greatest risk of developing?", "options": {"A": "Ovarian cancer developing gradually from years of chronic pelvic inflammation", "B": "Uterine fibroids arising secondary to recurrent untreated pelvic infection over time", "C": "Tubal scarring leading to infertility and an increased ectopic pregnancy risk", "D": "Cervical cancer related directly to the underlying untreated bacterial infection itself"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Tubal scarring leading to infertility and an increased ectopic pregnancy risk): Untreated or repeated episodes of pelvic inflammatory disease cause scarring and adhesions in the fallopian tubes, which can lead to tubal factor infertility and a substantially increased risk of future ectopic pregnancy, making prompt recognition and treatment of each episode important.", "case_id": "case_PID1", "case_text": "A 23-year-old sexually active woman presents with three days of lower abdominal pain, fever, and a new, foul-smelling vaginal discharge that started around the same time as the pain. She reports having had two new sexual partners over the past two months and admits she uses condoms inconsistently. On exam she has a temperature of 38.3C, diffuse lower abdominal tenderness without rebound or guarding, and marked cervical motion tenderness with bilateral adnexal tenderness on bimanual exam. A urine pregnancy test performed in the department is negative, and she is tolerating oral intake without difficulty.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 279, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 58-year-old man with a history of coronary artery disease and stable angina, currently maintained on isosorbide mononitrate for symptom control, asks his cardiologist about treatment for new erectile dysfunction that has developed over the past year. He specifically mentions that he saw an advertisement for sildenafil and would like to try it. Which of the following is the most important consideration before this medication could safely be prescribed for him?", "options": {"A": "Phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors are generally ineffective in men with underlying vascular disease, an option often considered in the initial differential diagnosis", "B": "No specific precaution or dose adjustment is needed given his cardiac history", "C": "The medication should only be taken together with food for it to work properly", "D": "It is contraindicated because combining it with his nitrate can cause severe, life-threatening hypotension"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (It is contraindicated because combining it with his nitrate can cause severe, life-threatening hypotension): Phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors and nitrates both increase nitric oxide-mediated vasodilation, and combining them can cause profound, refractory hypotension. This combination is an absolute contraindication, and patients on regular nitrate therapy for angina should not be prescribed a PDE-5 inhibitor under any circumstances."}, {"id": 280, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 24-year-old woman presents for evaluation of irregular menstrual cycles that have occurred every 45 to 90 days since menarche, along with progressively worsening facial hair growth along her jaw and persistent acne despite over-the-counter treatments. Pelvic ultrasound shows multiple small peripheral follicles in both ovaries. Total testosterone is mildly elevated on repeat testing, and thyroid function tests and prolactin levels are both within normal limits. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Premature ovarian insufficiency related to accelerated follicular loss", "B": "Congenital adrenal hyperplasia presenting later in life than usual", "C": "Polycystic ovary syndrome meeting standard diagnostic criteria", "D": "Hypothalamic amenorrhea related to excessive exercise or low body weight"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Polycystic ovary syndrome meeting standard diagnostic criteria): This patient meets the Rotterdam criteria with oligo-ovulation (irregular cycles), clinical hyperandrogenism (hirsutism and acne) confirmed biochemically, and polycystic-appearing ovaries on ultrasound, after other causes such as thyroid disease and hyperprolactinemia have been excluded, making polycystic ovary syndrome the most likely diagnosis overall."}, {"id": 281, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 29-year-old woman reports several years of pelvic pain that reliably begins two to three days before the onset of her period and resolves shortly after menses ends, along with pain during intercourse and painful bowel movements that cluster around the same time each month. She has tried over-the-counter ibuprofen with only partial relief. Pelvic exam is notable for focal tenderness in the posterior fornix and along the uterosacral ligaments. She has no other significant past medical history and takes no regular home medications. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Endometriosis, given the cyclic timing and associated dyspareunia and dyschezia", "B": "Uterine fibroids causing bulk-related cyclic discomfort", "C": "Pelvic inflammatory disease from an unrecognized infection, a possibility sometimes raised early in the workup", "D": "Ovarian torsion occurring intermittently each cycle"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Endometriosis, given the cyclic timing and associated dyspareunia and dyschezia): Cyclic pelvic pain that begins before menses, along with dyspareunia and dyschezia, is the classic triad of endometriosis, caused by endometrial-like tissue implanted outside the uterus. Laparoscopy with visualization remains the definitive diagnostic test, although empiric medical treatment, as she has already partially tried, is often reasonably started before pursuing surgery."}, {"id": 282, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 44-year-old woman reports that her periods have become progressively heavier and more painful over the past two years, to the point that she now misses work on the first two days of each cycle. Bimanual exam reveals a diffusely enlarged, globular, tender uterus without any discrete palpable masses. Pelvic ultrasound shows a thickened, heterogeneous myometrium without a distinct fibroid identified anywhere in the uterine wall. He has no other significant past medical history and takes no regular home medications. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Uterine leiomyoma presenting atypically without a discrete mass", "B": "Adenomyosis, given the diffuse uterine enlargement and tenderness", "C": "Endometrial carcinoma presenting with heavy cyclic bleeding", "D": "A cervical polyp causing intermenstrual spotting"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Adenomyosis, given the diffuse uterine enlargement and tenderness): A diffusely enlarged, tender, globular uterus with progressively heavy and painful periods, without a discrete mass, is classic for adenomyosis, which is the presence of endometrial glands and stroma within the myometrium. This differs from leiomyoma, which typically produces a discrete, well-defined mass rather than diffuse enlargement of the whole uterus."}, {"id": 284, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 26-year-old sexually active woman presents with a few days of migratory pain moving between several joints, swelling and pain over her right wrist tendons consistent with tenosynovitis, and several small pustular skin lesions scattered across the backs of her hands. She specifically denies any dysuria, vaginal discharge, or other genitourinary symptoms during this same period. She denies any recent travel, sick contacts, or exposure to new medications or supplements. He denies any recent travel, sick contacts, or exposure to new medications or supplements. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis explaining her combination of findings?", "options": {"A": "Reactive arthritis following an unrecognized gastrointestinal infection", "B": "Septic arthritis caused by Staphylococcus aureus bacteremia", "C": "Secondary syphilis presenting with a disseminated skin eruption", "D": "Disseminated gonococcal infection despite the absence of local symptoms"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Disseminated gonococcal infection despite the absence of local symptoms): The triad of migratory polyarthralgia, tenosynovitis, and pustular or vesiculopustular skin lesions in a sexually active patient is characteristic of disseminated gonococcal infection, which can occur even without prominent genitourinary symptoms and requires blood and often synovial fluid cultures along with targeted antibiotic therapy."}, {"id": 285, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 30-year-old woman presents with a week of frothy, malodorous, yellow-green vaginal discharge along with vulvar itching and mild discomfort during intercourse. She reports one new sexual partner over the past month and inconsistent condom use. Speculum exam reveals a cervix studded with small punctate hemorrhages, sometimes described as giving a strawberry appearance. He denies any recent travel, sick contacts, or exposure to new medications or supplements. Her physical exam is otherwise unremarkable aside from the findings already described above. Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment for both her and her sexual partner?", "options": {"A": "Fluconazole taken as a single oral dose", "B": "Topical clindamycin cream applied vaginally, an option often considered in the initial differential diagnosis", "C": "Oral metronidazole given to the patient and her partner", "D": "Doxycycline taken twice daily for one week"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Oral metronidazole given to the patient and her partner): A frothy, malodorous discharge with a strawberry cervix is classic for Trichomonas vaginalis infection. Because it is a sexually transmitted infection, both the patient and her sexual partner require treatment, typically with oral metronidazole, to prevent reinfection after her own symptoms resolve."}, {"id": 290, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 58-year-old woman notices a new, firm, irregular, immobile mass in her right breast while performing a breast self-exam, with overlying skin dimpling visible when she raises her arm. There is no associated fever, tenderness, or recent trauma to the area, and she has no personal history of prior breast disease. The remainder of her review of systems is unremarkable, and she has no known drug allergies. There is no family history of a similar condition, and this is her first presentation of this kind. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in her evaluation?", "options": {"A": "Provide reassurance and arrange a follow-up exam in six months, a possibility that should still be kept in mind during evaluation", "B": "Obtain mammography and ultrasound followed by core needle biopsy", "C": "Start a trial of oral antibiotics for presumed mastitis", "D": "Recommend warm compresses and continued observation only"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Obtain mammography and ultrasound followed by core needle biopsy): A firm, irregular, immobile mass with skin dimpling in a woman over 40 is concerning for malignancy and cannot be safely observed. Prompt imaging followed by tissue diagnosis with core needle biopsy is required, since clinical exam alone cannot reliably distinguish benign from malignant disease in this setting."}, {"id": 292, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 62-year-old woman presents with several months of a persistent eczematous, crusting, itchy rash involving her right nipple and areola that has failed to improve despite two separate trials of topical corticosteroid cream prescribed by her dermatologist. She denies a palpable breast mass on self-exam, and her most recent mammogram one year ago was reported as normal. Which of the following is the most likely underlying diagnosis explaining this persistent nipple change?", "options": {"A": "Simple contact dermatitis from a new laundry detergent or soap, a mechanism described in some patients with similar findings", "B": "Paget disease of the breast, often associated with an underlying ductal carcinoma", "C": "Uncomplicated bacterial mastitis of the nipple and areola", "D": "Fibrocystic breast changes involving the nipple region"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Paget disease of the breast, often associated with an underlying ductal carcinoma): A persistent eczematous, crusting change of the nipple and areola that fails to respond to topical treatment is characteristic of Paget disease of the breast, which is frequently associated with an underlying ductal carcinoma even when no mass is palpable, and warrants biopsy of the nipple itself rather than continued topical therapy."}, {"id": 294, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 30-year-old man is found down at home after what witnesses describe as a prolonged tonic-clonic seizure, and he was unable to move his legs when paramedics arrived, though sensation and movement gradually returned over the following hours in the emergency department. His creatine kinase returns at 45,000 U/L and his creatinine has risen to 2.4 mg/dL from a normal baseline. A urine dipstick is strongly positive for blood, yet microscopic examination of the urine sediment shows only rare intact red blood cells. Which of the following best explains this apparent urinalysis discrepancy?", "options": {"A": "Concurrent glomerulonephritis causing selective loss of intact red cells", "B": "An unrelated urinary tract infection affecting the dipstick result", "C": "Myoglobin in the urine causing a positive heme dipstick without true hematuria", "D": "A laboratory processing error requiring no further clinical action"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Myoglobin in the urine causing a positive heme dipstick without true hematuria): In rhabdomyolysis, released myoglobin is filtered by the kidney and reacts with the dipstick's heme-based test, producing a strongly positive result for blood even though few actual red blood cells are seen on microscopy. Myoglobin is directly nephrotoxic to the renal tubules and is a well-recognized cause of acute tubular necrosis in this setting."}, {"id": 295, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 66-year-old man with diabetes and a baseline creatinine of 1.5 mg/dL undergoes a coronary angiogram with iodinated contrast for evaluation of exertional chest pain. He received standard pre-procedure hydration. Two days after the procedure his creatinine rises to 2.3 mg/dL, and it then gradually trends back toward his baseline value over the following week without any specific intervention beyond continued intravenous fluids. A basic metabolic panel and complete blood count obtained at this visit are otherwise within normal limits. Which of the following best describes the expected clinical course of this condition?", "options": {"A": "A progressive, irreversible decline in kidney function with no expected recovery", "B": "A transient rise in creatinine peaking within a few days, typically followed by recovery", "C": "Immediate anuria requiring emergent dialysis in nearly all affected patients", "D": "No meaningful change in creatinine is expected after this type of contrast exposure, a finding that can appear in several overlapping conditions"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A transient rise in creatinine peaking within a few days, typically followed by recovery): Contrast-induced nephropathy classically causes a rise in creatinine peaking around 48 to 72 hours after exposure, followed by gradual recovery toward baseline over the following one to two weeks in most patients, particularly those without severe underlying kidney disease. Risk is higher in patients with diabetes and pre-existing chronic kidney disease, both present in this patient."}, {"id": 296, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 55-year-old man started omeprazole two weeks ago for reflux symptoms and now presents with fever, a diffuse maculopapular rash across his trunk, and a rising creatinine noted on routine labs drawn by his primary care physician. He has taken no other new medications and has no history of kidney disease. Urinalysis shows white blood cells, white cell casts, and eosinophils on Hansel stain. She denies any recent travel, sick contacts, or exposure to new medications or supplements. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Acute tubular necrosis from an unrecognized ischemic insult", "B": "Post-infectious glomerulonephritis following a recent viral illness", "C": "Acute interstitial nephritis triggered by the proton pump inhibitor", "D": "Nephrolithiasis causing an obstructive rise in creatinine"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Acute interstitial nephritis triggered by the proton pump inhibitor): Fever, rash, eosinophiluria, and an acute rise in creatinine following initiation of a new drug, particularly a proton pump inhibitor, beta-lactam, sulfa drug, or NSAID, is the classic presentation of drug-induced acute interstitial nephritis, and the offending medication should be identified and stopped promptly."}, {"id": 297, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 60-year-old woman with stage 5 chronic kidney disease maintained on hemodialysis three times weekly has persistent anemia with a hemoglobin around 8.5 g/dL despite documented adequate iron stores, with a ferritin of 350 ng/mL and transferrin saturation of 28 percent on recent labs. He denies any recent travel, sick contacts, or exposure to new medications or supplements. Her physical exam is otherwise unremarkable aside from the findings already described above. Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment to address the underlying mechanism driving her anemia?", "options": {"A": "Folic acid supplementation added to her dialysis regimen", "B": "Additional intravenous iron despite her already adequate iron stores", "C": "Vitamin B12 supplementation given intramuscularly", "D": "An erythropoiesis-stimulating agent to replace her deficient hormone"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (An erythropoiesis-stimulating agent to replace her deficient hormone): Anemia of chronic kidney disease is caused primarily by reduced renal production of erythropoietin. Once iron stores are confirmed adequate, as in this patient, an erythropoiesis-stimulating agent directly addresses the underlying mechanism by replacing the deficient hormone signal needed for normal red blood cell production."}, {"id": 298, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 52-year-old man with stage 4 chronic kidney disease from longstanding diabetic nephropathy is found on routine labs to have an elevated parathyroid hormone level, a low-normal corrected serum calcium, and a high serum phosphate. He has no symptoms currently but his nephrologist explains that this pattern will eventually contribute to bone disease if left unaddressed. Her physical exam is otherwise unremarkable aside from the findings already described above. His physical exam is otherwise unremarkable aside from the findings already described above. Which of the following best explains the underlying pathophysiology of his secondary hyperparathyroidism?", "options": {"A": "Reduced renal activation of vitamin D and phosphate retention lower serum calcium and stimulate the parathyroid glands", "B": "Excessive dietary calcium intake is directly suppressing his parathyroid hormone, a mechanism described in some patients with similar findings", "C": "Autoimmune destruction of his parathyroid glands is underway, gradually impairing hormone secretion over time", "D": "Overproduction of calcitonin by his thyroid gland is responsible for the abnormal laboratory pattern seen here"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Reduced renal activation of vitamin D and phosphate retention lower serum calcium and stimulate the parathyroid glands): Failing kidneys cannot convert vitamin D to its active form and cannot excrete phosphate normally, both of which lower ionized calcium and directly stimulate parathyroid hormone secretion, producing the secondary hyperparathyroidism and eventual renal osteodystrophy seen in advanced chronic kidney disease."}, {"id": 300, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "Which of the following best describes his volume status based on these exam findings?", "options": {"A": "Hypovolemic, based on his reported fatigue and confusion", "B": "Euvolemic, since his blood pressure remains within normal range", "C": "Indeterminate without further specialized testing", "D": "Hypervolemic, based on his edema, ascites, and weight gain"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hypervolemic, based on his edema, ascites, and weight gain): Jugular venous distension, peripheral edema, shifting dullness consistent with ascites, and a documented weight gain all indicate volume overload, so this patient's hyponatremia is hypervolemic, driven by his underlying heart failure, which activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system and antidiuretic hormone release despite an overall excess of total body sodium and water.", "case_id": "case_HypoNa1", "case_text": "A 76-year-old man with a long history of heart failure with reduced ejection fraction presents with two weeks of worsening fatigue and new mild confusion noticed by his wife. She reports increasing bilateral leg swelling and abdominal distension over that same period, along with a ten-pound weight gain despite eating less than usual. On exam he has jugular venous distension estimated at 12 cm of water, bilateral lower extremity pitting edema to the knees, shifting dullness on abdominal percussion, and a displaced point of maximal cardiac impulse. Serum sodium returns at 126 mEq/L, and his weight today is notably higher than his documented weight at his last clinic visit one month ago.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 301, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management of his hyponatremia given this volume status?", "options": {"A": "Aggressive infusion of isotonic normal saline", "B": "Rapid correction toward a normal sodium using hypertonic saline over just a few hours, a finding that can appear in several overlapping conditions", "C": "Free water restriction combined with treatment of his underlying heart failure, including diuresis", "D": "No specific treatment is needed since his sodium is only mildly reduced"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Free water restriction combined with treatment of his underlying heart failure, including diuresis): Hypervolemic hyponatremia from heart failure reflects a dilutional process from excess free water relative to total body sodium, so management focuses on free water restriction and treating the underlying cause with diuresis, not administering additional isotonic or hypertonic saline, which would worsen his existing volume overload.", "case_id": "case_HypoNa1", "case_text": "A 76-year-old man with a long history of heart failure with reduced ejection fraction presents with two weeks of worsening fatigue and new mild confusion noticed by his wife. She reports increasing bilateral leg swelling and abdominal distension over that same period, along with a ten-pound weight gain despite eating less than usual. On exam he has jugular venous distension estimated at 12 cm of water, bilateral lower extremity pitting edema to the knees, shifting dullness on abdominal percussion, and a displaced point of maximal cardiac impulse. Serum sodium returns at 126 mEq/L, and his weight today is notably higher than his documented weight at his last clinic visit one month ago.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 302, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "If his sodium were instead corrected too rapidly over the first day of admission, which of the following complications would be of greatest concern given how long this hyponatremia has likely been present?", "options": {"A": "Cerebral edema from rapid fluid shifts into the brain", "B": "Osmotic demyelination syndrome from an overly rapid sodium rise", "C": "Acute tubular necrosis from the correction itself", "D": "Hypokalemic paralysis unrelated to his sodium correction, a possibility that should still be kept in mind during evaluation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Osmotic demyelination syndrome from an overly rapid sodium rise): Rapid correction of chronic hyponatremia causes a sudden osmotic shift that can damage myelin in the pons and other brain regions, producing osmotic demyelination syndrome, which can cause severe and sometimes permanent neurologic injury. This is why chronic hyponatremia correction is generally limited to about 8 mEq/L in any 24-hour period.", "case_id": "case_HypoNa1", "case_text": "A 76-year-old man with a long history of heart failure with reduced ejection fraction presents with two weeks of worsening fatigue and new mild confusion noticed by his wife. She reports increasing bilateral leg swelling and abdominal distension over that same period, along with a ten-pound weight gain despite eating less than usual. On exam he has jugular venous distension estimated at 12 cm of water, bilateral lower extremity pitting edema to the knees, shifting dullness on abdominal percussion, and a displaced point of maximal cardiac impulse. Serum sodium returns at 126 mEq/L, and his weight today is notably higher than his documented weight at his last clinic visit one month ago.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 303, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "Which of the following laboratory findings would be most expected in this patient given his hypervolemic hyponatremia from decompensated heart failure?", "options": {"A": "A dilute urine osmolality that measures well below 100 mOsm per kilogram of water.", "B": "A markedly and inappropriately elevated serum sodium concentration found on laboratory testing.", "C": "A urine sodium that is often low due to avid renal sodium reabsorption", "D": "A urine osmolality that is inappropriately low relative to his serum osmolality"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A urine sodium that is often low due to avid renal sodium reabsorption): In hypervolemic hyponatremia from heart failure, reduced effective circulating volume triggers avid renal sodium and water reabsorption through activation of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system and antidiuretic hormone, so urine sodium is often low and urine osmolality is inappropriately concentrated relative to the low serum osmolality, rather than dilute.", "case_id": "case_HypoNa1", "case_text": "A 76-year-old man with a long history of heart failure with reduced ejection fraction presents with two weeks of worsening fatigue and new mild confusion noticed by his wife. She reports increasing bilateral leg swelling and abdominal distension over that same period, along with a ten-pound weight gain despite eating less than usual. On exam he has jugular venous distension estimated at 12 cm of water, bilateral lower extremity pitting edema to the knees, shifting dullness on abdominal percussion, and a displaced point of maximal cardiac impulse. Serum sodium returns at 126 mEq/L, and his weight today is notably higher than his documented weight at his last clinic visit one month ago.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 304, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 50-year-old woman on chronic furosemide therapy for long-standing hypertension has a potassium of 2.9 mEq/L on routine labs, despite already having received two full doses of intravenous potassium chloride in the emergency department earlier that same day. A repeat basic metabolic panel drawn afterward shows her magnesium level is 1.2 mg/dL, which is low. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in her management?", "options": {"A": "Administer magnesium repletion in addition to continued potassium repletion", "B": "Increase her home furosemide dose to address the underlying issue", "C": "Give intravenous calcium gluconate for cardiac protection", "D": "Withhold all further electrolyte repletion and repeat labs in 24 hours"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Administer magnesium repletion in addition to continued potassium repletion): Hypomagnesemia impairs renal potassium conservation and makes hypokalemia refractory to potassium repletion alone. Correcting magnesium alongside continued potassium replacement is necessary to successfully normalize the potassium level whenever both abnormalities are present together like this."}, {"id": 305, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 45-year-old woman presents with several days of severe, repeated vomiting from an acute viral gastroenteritis and reports feeling generally weak and lightheaded when standing. She has been unable to keep down any oral rehydration fluids at home. Laboratory studies show sodium 138 mEq/L, potassium 3.0 mEq/L, chloride 88 mEq/L, and bicarbonate 34 mEq/L. She has no other significant past medical history and takes no regular home medications. He has no other significant past medical history and takes no regular home medications. Which of the following best describes her acid-base disturbance?", "options": {"A": "An anion gap metabolic acidosis from her illness", "B": "Metabolic alkalosis from loss of gastric acid and chloride through vomiting", "C": "Respiratory alkalosis from anxiety-driven hyperventilation, a possibility sometimes raised early in the workup", "D": "A normal anion gap metabolic acidosis from diarrhea"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Metabolic alkalosis from loss of gastric acid and chloride through vomiting): Vomiting causes loss of hydrochloric acid from the stomach, which raises serum bicarbonate and lowers chloride, producing a hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis. The associated volume depletion and hypokalemia, from renal potassium wasting as the kidney tries to conserve sodium, both help perpetuate the alkalosis until her volume deficit is corrected."}, {"id": 307, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 20-year-old woman who ingested a large quantity of aspirin tablets several hours earlier during a suicide attempt presents with ringing in her ears, rapid deep breathing, and nausea. Arterial blood gas obtained on arrival shows pH 7.44, PaCO2 22 mmHg, and bicarbonate 15 mEq/L. A basic metabolic panel and complete blood count obtained at this visit are otherwise within normal limits. She denies any recent travel, sick contacts, or exposure to new medications or supplements. Which of the following best describes this acid-base pattern given her history of salicylate ingestion?", "options": {"A": "A simple respiratory alkalosis occurring alone with no metabolic component present", "B": "A simple metabolic acidosis occurring alone with no respiratory component present", "C": "A simple respiratory acidosis from central nervous system depression alone", "D": "A mixed respiratory alkalosis and elevated anion gap metabolic acidosis"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A mixed respiratory alkalosis and elevated anion gap metabolic acidosis): Salicylate toxicity classically causes early direct stimulation of the respiratory center, producing a respiratory alkalosis, together with an elevated anion gap metabolic acidosis from accumulating organic acids, giving a near-normal pH despite two opposing primary disturbances, exactly as seen in this patient's blood gas result."}, {"id": 308, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A patient admitted with diabetic ketoacidosis has repeat labs drawn after several hours of treatment showing sodium 140 mEq/L, chloride 96 mEq/L, and bicarbonate 8 mEq/L. The treating team notices that her bicarbonate has not risen as expected despite falling ketones and wants to check for another process. She denies any recent travel, sick contacts, or exposure to new medications or supplements. He denies any recent travel, sick contacts, or exposure to new medications or supplements. Which of the following calculations should be performed next to look for a second, hidden acid-base process?", "options": {"A": "Recheck the serum glucose level alone", "B": "Recalculate her fractional excretion of sodium", "C": "Calculate the delta ratio, comparing the rise in anion gap to the fall in bicarbonate", "D": "Simply repeat the arterial blood gas again in 24 hours with no calculation, a possibility that should still be kept in mind during evaluation"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Calculate the delta ratio, comparing the rise in anion gap to the fall in bicarbonate): The delta ratio compares how much the anion gap has increased above normal to how much bicarbonate has fallen below normal. A ratio far from the expected 1 to 2 range suggests a second, hidden acid-base process, such as a concurrent normal anion gap acidosis from large-volume saline resuscitation, a common twist tested on harder acid-base questions."}, {"id": 309, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 4-year-old boy is brought to the pediatrician by his parents for sudden facial and periorbital swelling that developed over just two days, which they initially attributed to allergies before it worsened. Urinalysis shows heavy proteinuria without any hematuria. Serum albumin is markedly low and total cholesterol is elevated. Renal biopsy with light microscopy appears entirely normal, but electron microscopy shows diffuse podocyte foot process effacement throughout the sampled tissue. He denies any recent travel, sick contacts, or exposure to new medications or supplements. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Minimal change disease, the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in young children", "B": "Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis presenting at an unusually young age, an option often considered in the initial differential diagnosis", "C": "Membranous nephropathy occurring as a primary process in childhood", "D": "IgA nephropathy presenting with prominent edema rather than hematuria"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Minimal change disease, the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in young children): Sudden-onset nephrotic syndrome in a young child with a normal-appearing glomerulus on light microscopy and podocyte foot process effacement on electron microscopy is classic for minimal change disease, which usually responds well to a course of corticosteroid therapy."}, {"id": 310, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 60-year-old man with a recently diagnosed solid tumor of the lung develops new lower extremity edema and is found on urinalysis to have heavy proteinuria that prompts renal biopsy. The biopsy shows diffuse thickening of the glomerular basement membrane with subepithelial deposits seen on electron microscopy, and serum testing for anti-phospholipase A2 receptor antibodies returns negative, consistent with a secondary process related to his malignancy. Her physical exam is otherwise unremarkable aside from the findings already described above. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Minimal change disease occurring secondary to his malignancy", "B": "Membranous nephropathy occurring secondary to his underlying solid tumor", "C": "Post-infectious glomerulonephritis unrelated to his cancer diagnosis", "D": "Diabetic nephropathy despite the absence of any diabetes history"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Membranous nephropathy occurring secondary to his underlying solid tumor): Diffuse basement membrane thickening with subepithelial deposits is characteristic of membranous nephropathy, which can occur as a primary autoimmune disease, often with positive anti-phospholipase A2 receptor antibodies, or secondary to conditions such as solid tumors, hepatitis B, or certain autoimmune diseases, as is more likely here given his negative antibody result and recent cancer diagnosis."}, {"id": 312, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 58-year-old man with known membranous nephropathy, previously stable on supportive management alone, develops sudden left flank pain overnight along with new gross hematuria the next morning and a creatinine that has worsened compared to his last recorded value from clinic two weeks earlier. Vital signs are otherwise stable at this visit, and review of systems is negative for any other acute complaints. The remainder of his review of systems is unremarkable, and he has no known drug allergies. Which of the following complications should be suspected first given his underlying diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Acute pyelonephritis from an ascending urinary infection", "B": "Testicular torsion presenting with referred flank discomfort", "C": "A new kidney stone obstructing the left ureter", "D": "Renal vein thrombosis related to his nephrotic syndrome"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Renal vein thrombosis related to his nephrotic syndrome): Patients with nephrotic syndrome, particularly membranous nephropathy, are at increased risk of venous thromboembolism because of urinary loss of antithrombin III and other natural anticoagulant proteins. Sudden flank pain, hematuria, and worsening renal function in this setting should prompt evaluation for renal vein thrombosis with dedicated imaging."}, {"id": 315, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 60-year-old woman with a long-standing indwelling urinary catheter placed for neurogenic bladder and a history of multiple recurrent urinary tract infections over several years develops new left flank discomfort. Imaging obtained for this complaint reveals a large staghorn calculus filling most of the left renal collecting system. Prior urine cultures on file have grown Proteus mirabilis on more than one occasion. There is no family history of a similar condition, and this is her first presentation of this kind. Which of the following organisms is most directly responsible for the formation of this particular stone?", "options": {"A": "Escherichia coli, the most common cause of simple cystitis", "B": "Enterococcus faecalis, a common catheter-associated organism", "C": "Candida albicans, a fungal organism seen in catheterized patients, an option often considered in the initial differential diagnosis", "D": "Proteus mirabilis, a urease-producing organism promoting stone formation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Proteus mirabilis, a urease-producing organism promoting stone formation): Proteus mirabilis and other urease-producing organisms split urea into ammonia, alkalinizing the urine and promoting precipitation of struvite (magnesium ammonium phosphate) crystals, which can grow into large staghorn calculi filling the collecting system, exactly as described in this patient with chronic catheter-associated infection."}, {"id": 316, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 26-year-old woman who is 18 weeks pregnant has a routine screening urinalysis performed at a prenatal visit that shows bacteria on microscopy, subsequently confirmed by a positive urine culture, despite the fact that she reports no dysuria, urgency, frequency, or flank pain of any kind and feels entirely well. She has no other significant past medical history and takes no regular home medications. Which of the following is the most appropriate management of this finding?", "options": {"A": "Treat with an appropriate antibiotic even though she remains asymptomatic", "B": "No treatment is needed since she has no urinary symptoms to suggest active infection", "C": "Simply repeat the urinalysis in one month with no treatment given now, a mechanism described in some patients with similar findings", "D": "Refer her for diagnostic cystoscopy to further evaluate her bladder"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Treat with an appropriate antibiotic even though she remains asymptomatic): Asymptomatic bacteriuria in pregnancy is one of the specific situations where treatment is indicated even without symptoms, because untreated bacteriuria in pregnant patients carries a significant risk of progressing to pyelonephritis and is associated with preterm labor and low birth weight."}, {"id": 317, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 70-year-old man with long-standing, poorly controlled type 2 diabetes complicated by peripheral and autonomic neuropathy reports frequent, near-constant dribbling of urine throughout the day without ever feeling a clear, sudden urge beforehand. A bladder scan performed in clinic shows a post-void residual volume of 480 mL immediately after he attempts to fully empty his bladder. She has no other significant past medical history and takes no regular home medications. He has no other significant past medical history and takes no regular home medications. Which of the following is the most likely mechanism underlying his incontinence?", "options": {"A": "Urethral hypermobility related to scarring from a prior prostate surgery procedure", "B": "An underactive detrusor muscle from diabetic autonomic neuropathy causing overflow incontinence", "C": "Overactive detrusor contractions triggering sudden urinary leakage before he reaches the bathroom", "D": "Cognitive impairment preventing him from reaching the toilet in time"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (An underactive detrusor muscle from diabetic autonomic neuropathy causing overflow incontinence): A large post-void residual with constant dribbling rather than a sudden urge suggests overflow incontinence from an underactive, poorly contractile bladder, commonly caused by diabetic autonomic neuropathy affecting the bladder's motor innervation, or alternatively by bladder outlet obstruction."}, {"id": 318, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "An 85-year-old woman with advanced dementia and severe osteoarthritis of both knees that severely limits her ability to walk quickly frequently has urinary accidents, particularly when she cannot reach the bathroom in time from her recliner. Urodynamic testing and a measured post-void residual volume are both entirely normal when performed by her urologist. He has no other significant past medical history and takes no regular home medications. A basic metabolic panel and complete blood count obtained at this visit are otherwise within normal limits. Which of the following best describes the type of incontinence she is experiencing?", "options": {"A": "Stress incontinence related to prior pelvic floor weakening, a pattern also encountered in related clinical contexts", "B": "Overflow incontinence from an underactive bladder muscle", "C": "Functional incontinence related to her mobility and cognitive limitations", "D": "Urgency incontinence from an overactive detrusor muscle"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Functional incontinence related to her mobility and cognitive limitations): When the urinary tract itself functions normally but the patient cannot reach the toilet in time because of a physical or cognitive limitation, such as advanced dementia or severe mobility impairment from arthritis, this is classified as functional incontinence, and management focuses on improving access and assistance rather than bladder-directed therapy."}, {"id": 319, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 25-year-old man notices a soft, entirely painless scrotal mass on the left side that he describes, when asked, as feeling like a bag of worms on palpation. He mentions that the mass appears to decrease noticeably in size whenever he lies down flat to rest, and it becomes more prominent again when he stands for long periods at his job. A basic metabolic panel and complete blood count obtained at this visit are otherwise within normal limits. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "A hydrocele from fluid accumulation around the testicle", "B": "A varicocele from dilation of the pampiniform venous plexus", "C": "Testicular torsion presenting atypically without acute pain, a finding that can appear in several overlapping conditions", "D": "An inguinal hernia extending down into the scrotum"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A varicocele from dilation of the pampiniform venous plexus): A soft, painless scrotal mass described as a bag of worms that decreases in size with recumbency is the classic description of a varicocele, caused by dilation of the pampiniform venous plexus, and it is far more common on the left side because of how the left gonadal vein drains into the left renal vein."}, {"id": 320, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 32-year-old man presents with painless scrotal swelling that has developed gradually over the past several weeks and does not fluctuate significantly with position or time of day. On exam the mass transilluminates brightly when a penlight is held against the scrotum in a dark room, and the testicle itself can still be palpated separately as a distinct, normal structure within the fluid collection. She denies any recent travel, sick contacts, or exposure to new medications or supplements. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "A varicocele arising from dilated and tortuous scrotal veins", "B": "An underlying testicular tumor causing this secondary scrotal swelling", "C": "A hydrocele, a fluid collection within the tunica vaginalis", "D": "Acute epididymitis resulting from an ascending bacterial infection"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A hydrocele, a fluid collection within the tunica vaginalis): A painless, fluid-filled scrotal swelling that transilluminates brightly is characteristic of a hydrocele, a collection of fluid within the tunica vaginalis surrounding the testicle. It is generally benign, though a new hydrocele appearing in an adult should still prompt an ultrasound to exclude an underlying testicular mass as the cause."}, {"id": 322, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 27-year-old man notices a firm, painless nodule on his left testicle while showering that has grown slightly over the past three weeks. It does not transilluminate when tested with a penlight, and scrotal ultrasound confirms a solid intratesticular mass measuring 1.8 cm without any cystic components. Her physical exam is otherwise unremarkable aside from the findings already described above. His physical exam is otherwise unremarkable aside from the findings already described above. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in his management?", "options": {"A": "Perform a needle biopsy of the testicle through the scrotal skin", "B": "Recommend observation with a repeat ultrasound in six months", "C": "Begin a course of antibiotics for presumed orchitis", "D": "Send tumor markers and proceed to radical inguinal orchiectomy"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Send tumor markers and proceed to radical inguinal orchiectomy): A firm, painless, solid intratesticular mass in a young man is presumed to be testicular cancer until proven otherwise. Tumor markers such as alpha-fetoprotein, beta-hCG, and lactate dehydrogenase are checked, and definitive diagnosis and treatment is radical inguinal orchiectomy rather than a transscrotal biopsy, which risks seeding the scrotal tissues with tumor cells."}, {"id": 323, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 52-year-old man with no previously known cardiac history presents to his primary care physician reporting new erectile dysfunction that has developed gradually over the past six months, without any associated chest pain, dyspnea, or exertional symptoms that he has noticed. He otherwise feels well and continues to exercise regularly, though somewhat less than he used to. His physical exam is otherwise unremarkable aside from the findings already described above. Which of the following is the most appropriate consideration for his physician to raise during this visit?", "options": {"A": "Erectile dysfunction in this age group is often initially considered to be psychogenic, and no further workup is typically pursued", "B": "No further evaluation is generally needed if he denies any associated chest pain during exertion", "C": "New erectile dysfunction may be an early marker of underlying cardiovascular disease warranting risk assessment", "D": "Erectile dysfunction of this type is often considered unrelated to overall vascular health in patients without other symptoms"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (New erectile dysfunction may be an early marker of underlying cardiovascular disease warranting risk assessment): Because the small penile arteries develop atherosclerotic narrowing before larger coronary arteries typically do, new erectile dysfunction in a middle-aged man can be an early warning sign of systemic vascular disease, and evaluation of his cardiovascular risk factors is an important part of a thorough workup at this visit."}, {"id": 325, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 34-year-old woman presents with several months of heavy, irregular vaginal bleeding that has begun to affect her daily activities. Her pregnancy test is negative and a pelvic ultrasound shows a normal-sized uterus without any polyps, fibroids, or other structural abnormality identified. Coagulation studies including platelet count and PT/PTT are normal, and thyroid function testing is also within normal limits. Which of the following categories from the PALM-COEIN classification most likely explains her bleeding?", "options": {"A": "Polyp, given her age and bleeding pattern", "B": "Leiomyoma, despite the normal ultrasound findings", "C": "Coagulopathy, despite her normal coagulation studies, a finding that can appear in several overlapping conditions", "D": "Ovulatory dysfunction, once other causes have been excluded"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ovulatory dysfunction, once other causes have been excluded): Once pregnancy, structural causes such as polyps and fibroids, and coagulopathy have all been reasonably excluded as in this patient, ovulatory dysfunction becomes the most likely explanation for abnormal uterine bleeding, reflecting irregular or absent ovulation that disrupts the normal cyclic hormonal pattern controlling endometrial shedding."}, {"id": 327, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 33-year-old woman with several years of cyclic pelvic pain, dyspareunia, and painful bowel movements around the time of her period has already failed adequate trials of both NSAIDs and combined oral contraceptives prescribed sequentially over the past year. Her gynecologist wants definitive confirmation of the underlying diagnosis before proceeding to consider further surgical management options. There is no family history of a similar condition, and this is her first presentation of this kind. Which of the following is the gold standard diagnostic test for her condition?", "options": {"A": "Laparoscopy with direct visualization and biopsy of suspected lesions", "B": "Pelvic MRI performed alone without any additional testing", "C": "A serum CA-125 level checked alone as a screening test", "D": "Transvaginal ultrasound performed alone without further evaluation, an option often considered in the initial differential diagnosis"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Laparoscopy with direct visualization and biopsy of suspected lesions): Although endometriosis is frequently treated empirically based on clinical presentation, laparoscopy with direct visualization of endometrial implants, ideally confirmed with biopsy, remains the definitive diagnostic gold standard, particularly when a patient has already failed empiric medical therapy and further surgical management is being seriously considered."}, {"id": 328, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 22-year-old sexually active woman presents with several days of mild vaginal discharge and occasional spotting after intercourse that she initially attributed to her menstrual cycle timing. Speculum exam reveals a friable cervix with visible mucopurulent discharge coming from the cervical os. Nucleic acid amplification testing sent from the cervix returns positive. There is no family history of a similar condition, and this is his first presentation of this kind. She has no other significant past medical history and takes no regular home medications. Which of the following organisms is most likely responsible for her presentation?", "options": {"A": "Trichomonas vaginalis, typically causing a frothy discharge", "B": "Candida albicans, typically causing thick, curdy discharge", "C": "Chlamydia trachomatis, a common cause of mucopurulent cervicitis", "D": "Gardnerella vaginalis, associated with bacterial vaginosis"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Chlamydia trachomatis, a common cause of mucopurulent cervicitis): Mucopurulent cervical discharge with a friable cervix and postcoital spotting in a sexually active young woman is a classic presentation of chlamydial cervicitis, which is frequently asymptomatic but, if left untreated, can ascend to cause pelvic inflammatory disease and subsequent infertility."}, {"id": 330, "domain": "renal_urinary_and_reproductive_systems", "question": "A 38-year-old woman reports bilateral breast tenderness and a sense of generalized lumpiness that reliably worsens in the week before her period begins and then noticeably improves once menstrual flow starts, a pattern she has noticed consistently for several years. Breast exam during the symptomatic phase reveals diffuse, mobile, somewhat cord-like nodularity in both breasts without any single dominant mass identified anywhere. He has no other significant past medical history and takes no regular home medications. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Invasive ductal carcinoma presenting bilaterally", "B": "Fibrocystic breast changes related to normal hormonal fluctuation", "C": "A fibroadenoma presenting with bilateral symptoms", "D": "Paget disease of the breast affecting both sides"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Fibrocystic breast changes related to normal hormonal fluctuation): Bilateral, cyclic breast tenderness and diffuse nodularity that fluctuates with the menstrual cycle, without a discrete dominant mass, is characteristic of fibrocystic breast changes, a common benign condition related to normal hormonal fluctuation across the menstrual cycle."}, {"id": 331, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "A 58-year-old man with a history of hyperlipidemia, a 30-pack-year smoking history, and a sedentary desk job reports 3 months of substernal chest tightness that occurs after climbing two flights of stairs at work or carrying groceries up a hill, and resolves within 2 to 3 minutes of stopping to rest. The discomfort has been identical in character and intensity on every occasion and has not become more frequent, more severe, or provoked by lesser exertion. He denies chest pain at rest, denies nocturnal symptoms, and has never required an emergency department visit. Vital signs are blood pressure 138/86 mmHg and heart rate 78/min. Cardiac exam is unremarkable, and a resting 12-lead ECG obtained in clinic is normal. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in evaluation?", "options": {"A": "Exercise stress ECG, since he can exercise and the baseline ECG is interpretable", "B": "Serial troponin measurements every 6 hours for the next 24 hours", "C": "Immediate cardiac catheterization without any noninvasive testing first", "D": "Reassurance with no further testing and follow-up in 6 months"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Exercise stress ECG, since he can exercise and the baseline ECG is interpretable): His presentation is classic for stable angina: predictable, exertional, reproducible chest pain relieved by rest, with a normal resting ECG and no rest symptoms to suggest an acute coronary syndrome. When a patient can exercise and the baseline ECG is interpretable, an exercise stress ECG is the appropriate first noninvasive test to evaluate for inducible ischemia before considering catheterization or serial biomarkers, which are reserved for suspected acute coronary syndrome."}, {"id": 332, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "A 61-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes and hypertension is brought in by ambulance after 45 minutes of crushing chest pain that radiates to her jaw and is associated with diaphoresis and nausea. On arrival her blood pressure is 96/60 mmHg, heart rate is 52/min, and she appears pale and diaphoretic. A 12-lead ECG is obtained promptly and shows 3 mm ST elevation in leads II, III, and aVF with reciprocal ST depression in leads I and aVL. Lung fields are clear on auscultation and there is no jugular venous distension noted at this point. Which coronary artery is most likely occluded, and what hemodynamic complication should be anticipated as her care proceeds?", "options": {"A": "Left main coronary artery occlusion, with risk of immediate free wall rupture on presentation", "B": "Left anterior descending artery occlusion, with risk of complete heart block developing within minutes", "C": "Right coronary artery occlusion, with risk of right ventricular infarction and preload-dependent hypotension", "D": "Left circumflex artery occlusion, with risk of papillary muscle rupture within the first hour"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Right coronary artery occlusion, with risk of right ventricular infarction and preload-dependent hypotension): ST elevation in the inferior leads II, III, and aVF reflects occlusion of the right coronary artery in most patients, which also supplies the AV node (explaining her relative bradycardia here) and, frequently, the right ventricle. Inferior STEMI carries a real risk of right ventricular infarction, which makes the patient preload dependent, so nitrates and morphine can precipitate profound hypotension in this setting and should be used cautiously if at all."}, {"id": 333, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "A 55-year-old man presents to a small rural emergency department 10 minutes after the onset of severe substernal chest pain. His ECG shows 3 mm ST elevation across the anterior precordial leads, and he is diagnosed with an anterior STEMI. The nearest percutaneous coronary intervention capable hospital is 100 minutes away by ground ambulance transport, and no interventional cardiology service is available locally. He is otherwise hemodynamically stable, with a blood pressure of 128/80 mmHg and no signs of active bleeding, recent stroke, or other contraindication to fibrinolytic therapy on careful review of his history. Which management strategy is most appropriate at this point?", "options": {"A": "Start only oral antiplatelet therapy and arrange outpatient stress testing in one week", "B": "Observe for 6 hours to see whether the ST elevation resolves before deciding on a reperfusion strategy", "C": "Give fibrinolytic therapy now if no contraindication exists, since transfer would exceed the acceptable PCI time window", "D": "Transfer him to the interventional hospital for primary PCI instead, given its typically better outcomes compared with fibrinolysis"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Give fibrinolytic therapy now if no contraindication exists, since transfer would exceed the acceptable PCI time window): Primary PCI is preferred only if it can be performed within 120 minutes of first medical contact when transfer is required. When that window cannot realistically be met, as in this rural setting, fibrinolytic therapy should be given within 30 minutes of arrival provided there is no contraindication, because time to reperfusion is the dominant factor limiting eventual infarct size and long-term outcome."}, {"id": 335, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "A 70-year-old man is recovering on the cardiac care unit two days after undergoing primary PCI for an anterior STEMI. His postprocedure course had been unremarkable, with a stable ejection fraction on echocardiogram and no arrhythmias noted on telemetry until this morning, when he develops new palpitations and mild fatigue. He is awake, alert, and oriented, denies chest pain or dyspnea, and his blood pressure is 118/76 mmHg with an oxygen saturation of 97 percent on room air. Telemetry reveals an irregular rhythm, and a rhythm strip is obtained for closer review. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": {"A": "Start a beta blocker or diltiazem for rate control and calculate a stroke risk score", "B": "Proceed with synchronized cardioversion now, since restoring sinus rhythm promptly is the preferred initial approach in new atrial fibrillation.", "C": "Give intravenous epinephrine specifically to increase his ventricular rate", "D": "Defer any intervention for now, since his stable vital signs suggest this new rhythm is unlikely to need immediate attention."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Start a beta blocker or diltiazem for rate control and calculate a stroke risk score): The rhythm strip shows an irregularly irregular rhythm without discrete P waves, consistent with new atrial fibrillation, a common complication after myocardial infarction. Because he is hemodynamically stable, the priority is rate control with a beta blocker or a non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker, along with formal assessment of his stroke risk using the CHA2DS2-VASc score to decide on anticoagulation, rather than emergency cardioversion.", "image": "question_images/q_335.png"}, {"id": 336, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "A 64-year-old woman with a history of hypertension and hyperlipidemia presents to the emergency department with 2 hours of dull chest pressure that began while she was resting at home watching television, without any preceding exertion. She describes mild associated shortness of breath but denies radiation of the pain, nausea, or diaphoresis. Vital signs are stable. An ECG shows no ST elevation but does show 1 mm ST depression in the lateral leads compared with a prior tracing. Initial troponin drawn on arrival is within normal limits, but a repeat troponin obtained 3 hours later has risen to a mildly elevated value above the laboratory's reference cutoff. Which diagnosis best fits this overall pattern?", "options": {"A": "Stable angina, given no new exertion", "B": "ST elevation myocardial infarction, per her ECG", "C": "Unstable angina, given her normal troponin", "D": "Non-ST elevation myocardial infarction (NSTEMI)"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Non-ST elevation myocardial infarction (NSTEMI)): Unstable angina and NSTEMI both present with rest pain and may show ST depression or T wave inversion without ST elevation, but they are distinguished by biomarkers: unstable angina has a persistently normal troponin on serial testing, while NSTEMI has a rising, elevated troponin, exactly as seen on this patient's repeat measurement several hours after presentation."}, {"id": 337, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "Four days after being treated for an inferior myocardial infarction, a 66-year-old man who had otherwise been recovering well on the cardiac unit suddenly develops acute dyspnea while sitting up in bed. On exam he is tachypneic and diaphoretic, and auscultation reveals a new loud holosystolic murmur at the cardiac apex that radiates toward the axilla, which was not present on his admission exam. Portable chest x-ray obtained shortly afterward shows bilateral pulmonary edema, and his oxygen saturation has dropped despite supplemental oxygen. Which complication is most likely responsible for this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Post-infarction pericarditis, also known as Dressler syndrome", "B": "Papillary muscle rupture causing acute severe mitral regurgitation", "C": "Ventricular septal rupture causing a new left-to-right intracardiac shunt", "D": "Free wall rupture causing acute cardiac tamponade"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Papillary muscle rupture causing acute severe mitral regurgitation): A new holosystolic murmur radiating to the axilla with sudden flash pulmonary edema several days after infarction is classic for papillary muscle rupture causing acute, severe mitral regurgitation. Because the left atrium has had no time to accommodate the abrupt new volume load, pulmonary edema develops rapidly, and this complication is considered a surgical emergency requiring urgent evaluation."}, {"id": 338, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "Three days after suffering a large transmural anterior myocardial infarction, a 72-year-old woman who had been recovering uneventfully suddenly becomes acutely hypotensive, with a blood pressure that drops from 130/80 mmHg to 78/50 mmHg over a matter of minutes. Bedside exam reveals new jugular venous distension and muffled, distant heart sounds on auscultation. A bedside echocardiogram is obtained emergently and demonstrates a large, new pericardial effusion with evidence of right ventricular diastolic collapse. Which complication of her myocardial infarction has most likely occurred?", "options": {"A": "Post-infarction pericarditis, also known as Dressler syndrome", "B": "Papillary muscle rupture with acute mitral regurgitation", "C": "Free wall rupture with cardiac tamponade", "D": "Right ventricular infarction without any associated rupture"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Free wall rupture with cardiac tamponade): Free wall rupture typically occurs 3 to 5 days after a transmural infarct and presents with rapid hemodynamic collapse from cardiac tamponade, exactly as seen here with sudden hypotension, jugular venous distension, muffled heart sounds, and echocardiographic evidence of tamponade physiology. This is a surgical emergency requiring immediate pericardiocentesis and definitive repair."}, {"id": 339, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "Six weeks after being discharged following an uncomplicated myocardial infarction, a 59-year-old man returns to clinic reporting several days of pleuritic chest pain that is sharp, worsens with deep inspiration and when lying flat, and improves noticeably when he sits forward. He also describes a low-grade fever and mild malaise over the same period, but denies any new exertional symptoms similar to his original infarction. An ECG obtained in clinic shows diffuse ST elevation across multiple lead territories along with PR segment depression, a pattern distinctly different from his admission ECG. Which condition is most likely responsible?", "options": {"A": "Recurrent myocardial infarction in a new territory", "B": "Acute papillary muscle rupture, presenting late", "C": "Ventricular septal rupture, presenting weeks later", "D": "Dressler syndrome (post-infarction pericarditis)"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Dressler syndrome (post-infarction pericarditis)): Dressler syndrome is an immune-mediated pericarditis that occurs weeks after myocardial infarction, presenting with pleuritic, positional chest pain relieved by sitting forward, low-grade fever, and diffuse ST elevation with PR depression on ECG, a pattern clearly distinct from the localized ST elevation seen with a new, acute infarction in a specific coronary territory."}, {"id": 340, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "The ECG shows 2 mm ST elevation in leads II, III, and aVF with reciprocal changes anteriorly. Which is the most appropriate immediate management at this point?", "options": {"A": "Discharge home with outpatient stress testing given his currently stable vital signs", "B": "Immediate initiation of a beta blocker drip alone as the sole reperfusion strategy", "C": "Aspirin, and transfer for primary PCI if it can be performed within 120 minutes", "D": "Observation with serial troponin measurements over the next 12 hours before deciding on any reperfusion strategy"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Aspirin, and transfer for primary PCI if it can be achieved within 120 minutes): ST elevation in leads II, III, and aVF indicates an inferior STEMI, usually from right coronary artery occlusion. Aspirin should be given immediately and reperfusion pursued as fast as possible, with primary PCI preferred if achievable within the guideline-recommended time window from first medical contact.", "case_id": "case_a_1", "case_text": "A 66-year-old man with a history of hypertension, type 2 diabetes, and hyperlipidemia presents with 30 minutes of crushing substernal chest pain radiating to his left arm, associated with diaphoresis and nausea. He reports the pain began suddenly while he was watching television and has been constant since onset, rated 9 out of 10 in severity. Vital signs on arrival are blood pressure 142/88 mmHg, heart rate 96/min, respiratory rate 18/min, and oxygen saturation 97 percent on room air. He appears uncomfortable and diaphoretic but is alert and able to answer questions clearly. A 12-lead ECG is obtained within minutes of arrival.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 341, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "The same patient undergoes successful primary PCI of the right coronary artery with a drug eluting stent placed. Two hours later, while still in the coronary care unit on telemetry, he becomes acutely hypotensive with a blood pressure dropping to 78/50 mmHg. He remains alert and oriented, denies any chest pain or dyspnea, and lung auscultation reveals clear breath sounds bilaterally with no crackles. His jugular venous pressure appears mildly elevated on repeat exam. Which of the following is the most likely explanation and appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "New cardiac tamponade from a procedural complication; proceed directly to emergency pericardiocentesis without further evaluation", "B": "Anaphylaxis to the contrast agent used during catheterization; give epinephrine and stop all intravenous fluids", "C": "Right ventricular involvement causing preload-dependent hypotension; give intravenous fluids and avoid nitrates", "D": "Cardiogenic shock from left ventricular pump failure; start a nitroglycerin infusion immediately"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Right ventricular involvement causing preload-dependent hypotension; give intravenous fluids and avoid nitrates): Inferior STEMI from right coronary artery occlusion frequently involves the right ventricle, since the right coronary artery typically supplies it. Right ventricular infarction produces a preload-dependent state: hypotension with clear lung fields and elevated jugular venous pressure suggests this mechanism rather than pump failure or tamponade, both of which would be expected to show pulmonary edema or other distinguishing signs. Intravenous fluids are given to support preload, while agents that reduce preload, such as nitrates, are avoided because they can precipitate severe hypotension in this setting.", "case_id": "case_a_1", "case_text": "A 66-year-old man with a history of hypertension, type 2 diabetes, and hyperlipidemia presents with 30 minutes of crushing substernal chest pain radiating to his left arm, associated with diaphoresis and nausea. He reports the pain began suddenly while he was watching television and has been constant since onset, rated 9 out of 10 in severity. Vital signs on arrival are blood pressure 142/88 mmHg, heart rate 96/min, respiratory rate 18/min, and oxygen saturation 97 percent on room air. He appears uncomfortable and diaphoretic but is alert and able to answer questions clearly. A 12-lead ECG is obtained within minutes of arrival.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 342, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "The patient responds well to intravenous fluids, his blood pressure normalizes, and he continues to recover uneventfully over the following two days. He is now preparing for discharge on hospital day 4, with a repeat echocardiogram showing preserved ejection fraction and no significant valvular abnormality. Which combination of medications should be included in his discharge regimen, given his history of diabetes and hypertension in addition to his recent stent placement?", "options": {"A": "Dual antiplatelet therapy, a high-intensity statin, a beta blocker, and an ACE inhibitor", "B": "A low-intensity statin and a calcium channel blocker, with beta blockers deliberately avoided after PCI", "C": "Aspirin monotherapy, since dual antiplatelet therapy is generally used for patients treated with fibrinolysis rather than primary PCI", "D": "Warfarin in place of antiplatelet therapy, used for long-term secondary prevention after stent placement"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Dual antiplatelet therapy, a high-intensity statin, a beta blocker, and an ACE inhibitor): Standard discharge therapy after an acute coronary syndrome treated with stent placement includes dual antiplatelet therapy (aspirin plus a P2Y12 inhibitor) for at least 12 months, a high-intensity statin, a beta blocker, and an ACE inhibitor or ARB, particularly important here given his diabetes and hypertension, all of which meaningfully reduce his long-term cardiovascular risk.", "case_id": "case_a_1", "case_text": "A 66-year-old man with a history of hypertension, type 2 diabetes, and hyperlipidemia presents with 30 minutes of crushing substernal chest pain radiating to his left arm, associated with diaphoresis and nausea. He reports the pain began suddenly while he was watching television and has been constant since onset, rated 9 out of 10 in severity. Vital signs on arrival are blood pressure 142/88 mmHg, heart rate 96/min, respiratory rate 18/min, and oxygen saturation 97 percent on room air. He appears uncomfortable and diaphoretic but is alert and able to answer questions clearly. A 12-lead ECG is obtained within minutes of arrival.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 344, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "Five days after suffering an anterior myocardial infarction, a 68-year-old man who had seemed to be recovering well develops progressively worsening dyspnea over several hours. On exam, a new harsh holosystolic murmur is best heard at the left lower sternal border, distinctly different from his admission exam, and he now has bibasilar crackles and mild hypotension. Right heart catheterization is performed urgently and demonstrates a significant step-up in oxygen saturation measured between the right atrium and the right ventricle. Which mechanical complication of his infarction is present?", "options": {"A": "Papillary muscle rupture", "B": "New onset atrial fibrillation", "C": "Ventricular septal rupture", "D": "Free wall rupture"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Ventricular septal rupture): A step-up in oxygen saturation between the right atrium and the right ventricle indicates a left-to-right shunt occurring at the level of the ventricles, which occurs with ventricular septal rupture after infarction. This produces a new harsh murmur at the left sternal border, as described here, and is a mechanical complication requiring urgent surgical evaluation and repair."}, {"id": 345, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "An 82-year-old man with a history of hypertension is brought to the emergency department by his daughter after she found him unusually lethargic and slow to respond this morning. On arrival he is drowsy but arousable, oriented only to person, and his vital signs show a heart rate of 38/min, blood pressure of 82/54 mmHg, and oxygen saturation of 95 percent on room air. His skin is cool to the touch and his capillary refill is mildly delayed. A rhythm strip is obtained as part of his initial evaluation. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate treatment?", "options": {"A": "Perform immediate synchronized cardioversion", "B": "Start a beta blocker", "C": "Begin an amiodarone infusion", "D": "Give atropine"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Give atropine): This patient has a symptomatic, unstable bradycardia, evidenced by his altered mentation, hypotension, and signs of poor perfusion. Atropine is the first-line pharmacologic treatment; if it fails to improve heart rate and perfusion, transcutaneous pacing or an epinephrine or dopamine infusion is the next step while preparing for definitive pacing. A beta blocker would worsen his bradycardia further, and cardioversion treats tachyarrhythmias rather than bradyarrhythmias like this one.", "image": "question_images/q_345.png"}, {"id": 347, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "A 28-year-old woman with no known cardiac disease presents to clinic describing recurrent episodes over the past several months of a sudden-onset, regular, rapid heartbeat that starts and stops abruptly, sometimes lasting 10 to 20 minutes at a time. During a witnessed episode in the office, her monitor shows a narrow-complex tachycardia at 190/min, and she remains fully alert with a blood pressure of 108/70 mmHg throughout. Vagal maneuvers, including a supervised Valsalva maneuver, fail to terminate the rhythm. Which medication should be given next?", "options": {"A": "Adenosine", "B": "Amiodarone", "C": "Intravenous furosemide", "D": "Digoxin"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Adenosine): This presentation is classic for supraventricular tachycardia from atrioventricular nodal reentry. In a stable patient like this one, vagal maneuvers are tried first, and if unsuccessful, adenosine is the next step because it transiently blocks conduction through the AV node and can terminate the reentrant circuit responsible for the arrhythmia."}, {"id": 348, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "A 72-year-old woman with a history of hypertension, type 2 diabetes, and prior transient ischemic attack is newly diagnosed with atrial fibrillation after presenting with palpitations. Her CHA2DS2-VASc score, calculated based on her age, sex, and comorbidities, comes out to 6. She is otherwise hemodynamically stable, has no history of a mechanical heart valve, and echocardiogram shows no significant mitral stenosis. Which is the most appropriate long-term strategy for stroke prevention in her case?", "options": {"A": "Anticoagulation with a direct oral anticoagulant such as apixaban", "B": "Anticoagulation only if she eventually undergoes cardioversion, with no therapy needed otherwise", "C": "No anticoagulation needed, since aspirin alone is considered adequate at this level of stroke risk", "D": "Anticoagulation with warfarin only, since direct oral anticoagulants are typically avoided in atrial fibrillation"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Anticoagulation with a direct oral anticoagulant such as apixaban): Her CHA2DS2-VASc score is 6 (1 for hypertension, 1 for age 65-74, 1 for diabetes, 2 for prior TIA, 1 for female sex), indicating a markedly elevated stroke risk that warrants long-term anticoagulation. In the absence of a mechanical valve or moderate to severe mitral stenosis, a direct oral anticoagulant is preferred over warfarin for most patients with nonvalvular atrial fibrillation like her."}, {"id": 349, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "A 58-year-old woman presents with palpitations and is found to be in atrial fibrillation on ECG, though she is unsure exactly when her symptoms began and may have had intermittent palpitations for several weeks beforehand. She is currently hemodynamically stable, with a blood pressure of 132/84 mmHg, and is not currently on any anticoagulation. Her cardiologist is considering elective electrical cardioversion to restore normal sinus rhythm given her ongoing symptoms. Which is the most appropriate next step before proceeding with cardioversion?", "options": {"A": "Give a single dose of aspirin immediately before the cardioversion procedure to help lower embolic risk", "B": "Delay cardioversion for 6 months, since her ongoing symptoms are unlikely to change the anticoagulation timeline much", "C": "Proceed directly to cardioversion today since she is currently hemodynamically stable and reports no chest pain", "D": "Transesophageal echocardiogram to exclude a left atrial thrombus, or at least 3 weeks of anticoagulation first"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Transesophageal echocardiogram to exclude a left atrial thrombus, or at least 3 weeks of anticoagulation first): When the duration of atrial fibrillation is unknown or exceeds 48 hours, as is the case here, cardioversion carries a real risk of dislodging an atrial thrombus and causing an embolic stroke. Either at least 3 weeks of therapeutic anticoagulation before cardioversion, or a transesophageal echocardiogram to specifically exclude a left atrial thrombus, is required first."}, {"id": 350, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "A 45-year-old man with well-controlled hypertension undergoes a routine ECG as part of an annual physical examination and has no cardiac symptoms whatsoever, denying any dizziness, syncope, or palpitations. The ECG shows progressive PR interval prolongation over three consecutive beats followed by a single dropped QRS complex, after which the pattern resets and repeats. His vital signs and the remainder of his physical exam are entirely unremarkable. Which is the most appropriate management at this point?", "options": {"A": "Refer promptly for permanent pacemaker placement, since this rhythm pattern is generally thought to warrant device therapy even without symptoms.", "B": "Arrange for emergency transcutaneous pacing to be initiated right away in the office, given the abnormal rhythm noted on this tracing.", "C": "Observation, since this pattern is usually benign and does not require pacing", "D": "Start high-dose atropine and plan to continue it for an extended period to help slow further progression of this rhythm."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Observation, since this pattern is usually benign and does not require pacing): This describes second degree AV block, Mobitz type I (Wenckebach), which is usually benign, often due to increased vagal tone, and typically does not require pacing unless the patient becomes symptomatic. This is in clear contrast to Mobitz type II, which carries a real risk of progressing abruptly to complete heart block and usually warrants pacemaker placement even without symptoms."}, {"id": 351, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "A 19-year-old competitive swimmer has a witnessed episode of sudden loss of consciousness during a race, with rapid spontaneous recovery within seconds and no confusion afterward. An ECG obtained afterward shows a corrected QT interval of 510 milliseconds, notably prolonged compared with the normal reference range. On further questioning, her family history is notable for a younger sibling who died suddenly and unexpectedly at age 16 during a swim meet, with no clear explanation identified at the time. Which arrhythmia is this patient at greatest risk for going forward?", "options": {"A": "Atrial fibrillation", "B": "Torsades de pointes", "C": "First degree AV block", "D": "Sinus bradycardia"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Torsades de pointes): A markedly prolonged corrected QT interval, especially in the setting of a family history of sudden death at a young age, strongly suggests congenital long QT syndrome, which predisposes affected individuals to torsades de pointes, a polymorphic ventricular tachycardia that can degenerate into ventricular fibrillation and cause sudden cardiac death, particularly during exertion or strong emotional stimuli."}, {"id": 354, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "A 68-year-old man with a history of hypertension and a prior myocardial infarction 5 years ago presents with progressive dyspnea on exertion over the past 2 months, along with orthopnea requiring him to sleep propped up on three pillows and new bilateral leg swelling by the end of each day. On exam, he has an audible S3 gallop, elevated jugular venous pressure, and bibasilar crackles on lung auscultation. His BNP is significantly elevated above the laboratory reference range. Which test is most useful at this point to establish his underlying ejection fraction and guide further pharmacologic therapy?", "options": {"A": "Exercise stress ECG", "B": "Transthoracic echocardiogram", "C": "Serum BNP alone, without imaging", "D": "Right heart catheterization first"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Transthoracic echocardiogram): While his elevated BNP supports a diagnosis of heart failure, echocardiography is the key test needed to establish the ejection fraction, which determines whether he has HFrEF or HFpEF, a distinction that directly and substantially changes the pharmacologic approach to his ongoing management."}, {"id": 355, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "Which is the most appropriate initial management for his current presentation?", "options": {"A": "Dobutamine infusion", "B": "Intravenous loop diuretic", "C": "SGLT2 inhibitor initiation", "D": "Intravenous fluid resuscitation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Intravenous loop diuretic): This is acute decompensated heart failure triggered by dietary sodium indiscretion and a missed diuretic dose, presenting as volume overloaded ('wet') but well perfused ('warm'). Intravenous loop diuretics are the appropriate initial treatment for this congested, well-perfused presentation. Intravenous fluids would clearly worsen his existing congestion, and inotropes are reserved instead for poorly perfused, low-output states, which he does not currently have.", "case_id": "case_b_1", "case_text": "A 71-year-old man with a well-documented history of HFrEF, with an ejection fraction of 25 percent on his most recent echocardiogram, presents to the emergency department with 3 days of worsening dyspnea, new orthopnea, and a 4 kg weight gain that he first noticed after attending a family gathering over the weekend. He admits to eating unusually salty foods at that gathering and forgetting one dose of his home diuretic. Exam reveals jugular venous distension, bibasilar crackles on lung auscultation, and 2+ pitting edema in both lower extremities. His blood pressure is 138/84 mmHg, heart rate is 92/min, and his skin remains warm and well perfused to the touch throughout the exam.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 356, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "Using the bedside clinical classification of heart failure presentations by congestion and perfusion status, a framework commonly used to guide initial therapy in acute decompensated heart failure, how should this same patient be categorized based on his exam findings above, and what does that classification imply about the therapy he most likely needs right now?", "options": {"A": "Cold and dry, implying he actually needs additional fluid resuscitation", "B": "Warm and dry, implying no further intervention of any kind is needed", "C": "Warm and wet, implying diuresis alone is likely sufficient without the need for inotropic support", "D": "Cold and wet, implying he needs an inotrope added in addition to ongoing diuresis"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Warm and wet, implying diuresis alone is likely sufficient without the need for inotropic support): He is congested (wet, reflecting his volume overload) but remains well perfused (warm, given his normal blood pressure and lack of any signs of poor perfusion), so diuresis alone is generally sufficient in his case. A 'cold' classification, indicating poor perfusion instead, would prompt consideration of inotropic support, which is not needed here.", "case_id": "case_b_1", "case_text": "A 71-year-old man with a well-documented history of HFrEF, with an ejection fraction of 25 percent on his most recent echocardiogram, presents to the emergency department with 3 days of worsening dyspnea, new orthopnea, and a 4 kg weight gain that he first noticed after attending a family gathering over the weekend. He admits to eating unusually salty foods at that gathering and forgetting one dose of his home diuretic. Exam reveals jugular venous distension, bibasilar crackles on lung auscultation, and 2+ pitting edema in both lower extremities. His blood pressure is 138/84 mmHg, heart rate is 92/min, and his skin remains warm and well perfused to the touch throughout the exam.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 357, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "After successful diuresis over the following two days and clear clinical improvement in his symptoms, the same patient is preparing for discharge back home. Review of his prior outpatient medication list shows he was previously maintained on an ACE inhibitor and a beta blocker only, with no mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist or SGLT2 inhibitor ever having been started, and his admission labs show adequate renal function with a normal potassium level. Which is the most appropriate approach to his further outpatient pharmacologic therapy going forward?", "options": {"A": "Consider stopping his beta blocker at this visit, since his heart failure symptoms have already improved with diuresis", "B": "Add a mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist and an SGLT2 inhibitor to complete guideline directed medical therapy", "C": "Replace his ACE inhibitor entirely with a non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker instead", "D": "Continue his current medications without changes, since he appears to have improved adequately with diuretics this admission"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Add a mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist and an SGLT2 inhibitor to complete guideline directed medical therapy): Complete guideline directed medical therapy for HFrEF includes an ACE inhibitor or ARB (or ARNI), a beta blocker, a mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist, and an SGLT2 inhibitor. He is already on two of these four pillars, so the mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist and SGLT2 inhibitor should now be added, given his adequate renal function and potassium, to further reduce his future mortality and hospitalization risk.", "case_id": "case_b_1", "case_text": "A 71-year-old man with a well-documented history of HFrEF, with an ejection fraction of 25 percent on his most recent echocardiogram, presents to the emergency department with 3 days of worsening dyspnea, new orthopnea, and a 4 kg weight gain that he first noticed after attending a family gathering over the weekend. He admits to eating unusually salty foods at that gathering and forgetting one dose of his home diuretic. Exam reveals jugular venous distension, bibasilar crackles on lung auscultation, and 2+ pitting edema in both lower extremities. His blood pressure is 138/84 mmHg, heart rate is 92/min, and his skin remains warm and well perfused to the touch throughout the exam.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 358, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "A 58-year-old man with HFrEF and a measured ejection fraction of 30 percent has been on guideline directed therapy for several months, including an ACE inhibitor, a beta blocker, and spironolactone, with his most recent basic metabolic panel showing well-controlled potassium and stable renal function. He does not have diabetes and has never required hospitalization for decompensated heart failure. His cardiologist is reviewing his medication regimen at a routine follow-up visit to identify any additional therapy that could further reduce his long-term mortality risk. Which additional medication class is most appropriate to add?", "options": {"A": "A dihydropyridine CCB", "B": "A short-acting nitrate", "C": "A non-dihydropyridine CCB", "D": "An SGLT2 inhibitor"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (An SGLT2 inhibitor): SGLT2 inhibitors have demonstrated a clear mortality benefit in HFrEF independent of diabetes status and are now considered part of complete guideline directed medical therapy alongside an ACE inhibitor or ARB (or ARNI), a beta blocker, and a mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist. Non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers are generally avoided in HFrEF because of their negative inotropic effect on an already weakened ventricle."}, {"id": 360, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "A 66-year-old man with a prior myocardial infarction and known ischemic cardiomyopathy with a reduced ejection fraction presents to the emergency department after developing sudden palpitations while at home. He is alert and able to converse normally, his blood pressure is 106/70 mmHg, and he denies any chest pain, though he does report mild lightheadedness. A rhythm strip is obtained as part of his workup, showing a wide-complex, regular tachycardia at a rate of approximately 160/min. Which is the most appropriate management given his current clinical stability?", "options": {"A": "Give an antiarrhythmic such as amiodarone, or perform elective synchronized cardioversion", "B": "Perform unsynchronized defibrillation immediately, since he currently has a palpable pulse", "C": "Reassure him and arrange discharge, since his blood pressure remains within a normal range", "D": "Give adenosine, since it is considered safe and effective for any wide-complex tachycardia"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Give an antiarrhythmic such as amiodarone, or perform elective synchronized cardioversion): This is monomorphic ventricular tachycardia, which in a patient with known structural heart disease like his ischemic cardiomyopathy should be assumed to be ventricular in origin until proven otherwise. Because he remains hemodynamically stable, treatment options include antiarrhythmic drug therapy (amiodarone, procainamide, or sotalol) or elective synchronized cardioversion; adenosine is not appropriate for a wide-complex tachycardia of likely ventricular origin, and defibrillation is unsynchronized and reserved specifically for pulseless rhythms, not this stable, perfusing rhythm.", "image": "question_images/q_360.png"}, {"id": 361, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "A 74-year-old woman with long-standing hypertension and obesity presents with several weeks of exertional dyspnea and progressive bilateral leg edema. She denies any history of myocardial infarction. Echocardiogram reveals an ejection fraction of 60 percent with clear echocardiographic evidence of diastolic dysfunction and left atrial enlargement, but no significant valvular disease or regional wall motion abnormality. Her cardiologist is discussing her overall long-term management plan given this diagnosis. Which best describes the appropriate overall approach to her heart failure management?", "options": {"A": "Diuretics for congestion, an SGLT2 inhibitor, and aggressive treatment of her hypertension and obesity", "B": "No pharmacologic therapy, since medications tend to offer only modest and inconsistent benefit in this type of heart failure", "C": "A mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist as the primary agent, with diuretics playing a comparatively minor supporting role in her case", "D": "The same mortality-reducing regimen typically used in HFrEF, since most heart failure phenotypes tend to respond similarly to therapy"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Diuretics for congestion, an SGLT2 inhibitor, and aggressive treatment of her hypertension and obesity): In HFpEF, the ACE inhibitor, beta blocker, and mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist regimen proven effective in HFrEF has not shown the same clear mortality benefit. Management instead centers on diuretics for volume control, SGLT2 inhibitors, which do have demonstrated benefit specifically in HFpEF, and aggressive treatment of underlying contributors such as her hypertension and obesity."}, {"id": 362, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "A 61-year-old man with HFrEF is started on spironolactone in addition to his existing regimen of an ACE inhibitor and a beta blocker, after his cardiologist determines he meets criteria for a mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist based on his ejection fraction and symptom burden. Before starting the new medication, baseline labs are drawn, and a follow-up visit is scheduled shortly after initiation to reassess. Which laboratory parameters should be monitored particularly closely after starting this medication?", "options": {"A": "Serum sodium and liver function tests", "B": "Serum potassium and renal function", "C": "Serum calcium and thyroid function tests", "D": "Complete blood count and coagulation studies"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Serum potassium and renal function): Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists such as spironolactone can cause clinically significant hyperkalemia and worsening renal function, particularly when combined with an ACE inhibitor or ARB as in his case, so potassium and renal function should be checked before starting therapy and monitored closely at follow-up thereafter."}, {"id": 363, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "During a routine hospital admission for volume overload, a 70-year-old woman with known decompensated heart failure is examined by the admitting team. On cardiac auscultation, a low-pitched extra heart sound is heard just after S2, best appreciated with the bell of the stethoscope at the apex with the patient in the left lateral decubitus position. The team documents this finding along with her elevated jugular venous pressure and bibasilar crackles as part of her overall clinical picture. Which finding does this represent, and what does it reflect physiologically?", "options": {"A": "An opening snap, which specifically reflects underlying mitral stenosis", "B": "A physiologic split S2, which is considered a normal finding in essentially all adults", "C": "An S4 gallop, reflecting atrial contraction against a stiff ventricle in a patient without heart failure", "D": "An S3 gallop, reflecting rapid filling of a volume-overloaded, poorly compliant ventricle"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (An S3 gallop, reflecting rapid filling of a volume-overloaded, poorly compliant ventricle): An S3 gallop occurs early in diastole and reflects rapid ventricular filling into an already volume-overloaded, poorly compliant ventricle, making it a classic and fairly specific finding in decompensated systolic heart failure, distinct from an S4, which occurs late in diastole during atrial contraction against a stiff, noncompliant ventricle."}, {"id": 364, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "A 63-year-old man with HFrEF taking an ACE inhibitor for the past several years continues to report persistent exertional symptoms despite otherwise appropriate titration of his medications. His cardiologist recommends transitioning him to sacubitril-valsartan, an angiotensin receptor neprilysin inhibitor (ARNI), given the additional mortality benefit this class has shown over ACE inhibitors alone in patients like him. Which specific precaution is necessary when carrying out this medication transition?", "options": {"A": "He should pause his antihypertensive therapy for about one week before starting the new medication", "B": "His ACE inhibitor dose should be increased to its maximum immediately before switching medications", "C": "A washout period of at least 36 hours off the ACE inhibitor before starting the ARNI, to reduce angioedema risk", "D": "No washout period is generally needed, since the two drug classes can reasonably be started together"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A washout period of at least 36 hours off the ACE inhibitor before starting the ARNI, to reduce angioedema risk): Combining an ACE inhibitor with an ARNI significantly increases the risk of life-threatening angioedema because both medication classes increase circulating bradykinin levels. A washout period of at least 36 hours is therefore required between stopping the ACE inhibitor and starting sacubitril-valsartan to meaningfully reduce this risk."}, {"id": 366, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "Three days after suffering an inferior myocardial infarction, a 74-year-old woman who had seemed to be recovering as expected suddenly develops severe dyspnea while sitting in her hospital bed. On urgent exam, a new holosystolic murmur is heard at the cardiac apex radiating toward the axilla, and a portable chest x-ray obtained shortly afterward reveals bilateral pulmonary edema that was not present on her admission films. Which is the most likely underlying mechanism for this acute deterioration?", "options": {"A": "New onset mitral stenosis resulting from acute rheumatic fever", "B": "Aortic stenosis that has progressed acutely over the course of just 3 days", "C": "Chronic mitral regurgitation that has been present for years and is only now decompensating", "D": "Acute severe mitral regurgitation from papillary muscle rupture or ischemic dysfunction"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Acute severe mitral regurgitation from papillary muscle rupture or ischemic dysfunction): Acute severe mitral regurgitation after myocardial infarction, whether from frank papillary muscle rupture or severe ischemic papillary muscle dysfunction, causes sudden flash pulmonary edema because the left atrium has not had time to dilate and accommodate the new regurgitant volume, quite unlike chronic mitral regurgitation, which is typically well tolerated for years before symptoms develop."}, {"id": 370, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "Which is the most appropriate next step in her overall stroke risk assessment at this point?", "options": {"A": "Assume no anticoagulation is needed simply because she has no personal history of prior stroke", "B": "Plan to address anticoagulation only after cardioversion has been performed, since her risk score could be calculated at that later visit.", "C": "Calculate her CHA2DS2-VASc score to guide the anticoagulation decision", "D": "Begin warfarin therapy right away, reasoning that her risk profile is probably high enough to justify treatment before any formal scoring."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Calculate her CHA2DS2-VASc score to guide the anticoagulation decision): The rhythm strip shows an irregularly irregular rhythm without discrete P waves, consistent with new atrial fibrillation. Because she is currently hemodynamically stable, the next step is to calculate her CHA2DS2-VASc score, accounting for her age, sex, and hypertension, to determine whether anticoagulation is indicated, rather than assuming her risk based on stroke history alone.", "image": "question_images/q_370.png", "case_id": "case_c_1", "case_text": "A 75-year-old woman with a history of hypertension presents to the emergency department with 2 days of palpitations and mild fatigue that have gradually worsened. She denies any chest pain, syncope, or focal neurologic symptoms. Vital signs on arrival are blood pressure 128/78 mmHg, heart rate 96/min and notably irregular on palpation, respiratory rate 16/min, and oxygen saturation 98 percent on room air. She appears comfortable and is able to provide a clear history without difficulty. A rhythm strip is obtained as part of her initial workup.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 371, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "Four hours after her initial presentation, while awaiting further workup, the same patient suddenly becomes confused and difficult to arouse appropriately, and her blood pressure is rechecked and found to have dropped to 76/48 mmHg. A repeat rhythm strip is obtained and again shows the same irregularly irregular pattern with no discrete P waves seen anywhere in the tracing. Which is the most appropriate immediate management at this point in her care?", "options": {"A": "Begin therapeutic warfarin first", "B": "Synchronized cardioversion", "C": "Continue rate control and reassess later", "D": "Give adenosine for chemical cardioversion"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Synchronized cardioversion): She has now become hemodynamically unstable, with new confusion and significant hypotension directly attributable to her arrhythmia. Any unstable tachyarrhythmia should be treated with immediate synchronized cardioversion, regardless of her current anticoagulation status, since further delay risks progression to cardiovascular collapse. Adenosine, notably, is not an effective treatment for atrial fibrillation.", "image": "question_images/q_371.png", "case_id": "case_c_1", "case_text": "A 75-year-old woman with a history of hypertension presents to the emergency department with 2 days of palpitations and mild fatigue that have gradually worsened. She denies any chest pain, syncope, or focal neurologic symptoms. Vital signs on arrival are blood pressure 128/78 mmHg, heart rate 96/min and notably irregular on palpation, respiratory rate 16/min, and oxygen saturation 98 percent on room air. She appears comfortable and is able to provide a clear history without difficulty. A rhythm strip is obtained as part of her initial workup.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 372, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "Following successful electrical cardioversion, the same patient's repeat rhythm strip now shows a regular rhythm with a discrete P wave clearly preceding every QRS complex, at a rate of 78/min. Given that her atrial fibrillation was of entirely unknown duration before the cardioversion was performed, which anticoagulation plan is most appropriate for her going forward from this point?", "options": {"A": "Restart anticoagulation only if and when she becomes symptomatic again in the future", "B": "Conclude that no anticoagulation was ever necessary since she is now in normal sinus rhythm", "C": "Stop all anticoagulation immediately now that normal sinus rhythm has been successfully restored", "D": "Continue anticoagulation for at least 4 weeks after cardioversion, then reassess based on her CHA2DS2-VASc score"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Continue anticoagulation for at least 4 weeks after cardioversion, then reassess based on her CHA2DS2-VASc score): Even after successful cardioversion restores normal sinus rhythm, atrial mechanical function can lag meaningfully behind the restored electrical rhythm, and there remains a real risk of thrombus formation and subsequent embolization for several weeks afterward. Anticoagulation should therefore be continued for at least 4 weeks post-cardioversion, with the decision on further long-term therapy then guided by her overall CHA2DS2-VASc score.", "image": "question_images/q_372.png", "case_id": "case_c_1", "case_text": "A 75-year-old woman with a history of hypertension presents to the emergency department with 2 days of palpitations and mild fatigue that have gradually worsened. She denies any chest pain, syncope, or focal neurologic symptoms. Vital signs on arrival are blood pressure 128/78 mmHg, heart rate 96/min and notably irregular on palpation, respiratory rate 16/min, and oxygen saturation 98 percent on room air. She appears comfortable and is able to provide a clear history without difficulty. A rhythm strip is obtained as part of her initial workup.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 375, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "A 58-year-old man with no cardiac symptoms undergoes a routine ECG as part of a preoperative evaluation before an elective orthopedic procedure. The tracing shows a constant PR interval throughout, but with intermittently dropped QRS complexes that occur without any predictable pattern of warning. He denies any history of dizziness, near-syncope, or syncope, and his surgical team is asking cardiology to clarify whether he can safely proceed with surgery as scheduled. A rhythm strip is obtained for closer review.", "options": {"A": "Observation, since he currently has no symptoms of dizziness or syncope", "B": "Pacemaker placement, given the risk of progression to complete heart block", "C": "Atropine used as definitive, long-term therapy for his condition", "D": "A beta blocker added specifically to further slow his conduction"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Pacemaker placement, given the risk of progression to complete heart block): This rhythm strip shows second degree AV block, Mobitz type II, characterized by a constant PR interval with intermittently and unpredictably dropped beats, exactly as described here. Unlike Mobitz type I, this pattern carries a substantial risk of abrupt progression to complete heart block, so pacemaker placement is generally recommended even in asymptomatic patients like him, ideally before proceeding with elective surgery.", "image": "question_images/q_375.png"}, {"id": 376, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "A 50-year-old man with no significant past medical history presents for a routine physical exam. His blood pressure is measured using proper cuff technique after 5 minutes of quiet sitting and reads 134/82 mmHg, and this elevated reading is confirmed on a second separate office visit two weeks later using the same careful technique. He reports no symptoms, takes no medications, and his physical exam and basic laboratory studies are otherwise unremarkable. Which is the most appropriate initial management for him?", "options": {"A": "Lifestyle modification, including the DASH diet, sodium reduction, and exercise", "B": "Immediate initiation of two antihypertensive medications simultaneously", "C": "No intervention needed, since his blood pressure is below the threshold that defines hypertension", "D": "Immediate initiation of a beta blocker specifically as first-line therapy"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Lifestyle modification, including the DASH diet, sodium reduction, and exercise): A blood pressure of 130/80 mmHg or higher, confirmed on repeated proper measurement as it was here, meets the threshold for a diagnosis of hypertension. In a patient without another compelling indication for immediate pharmacotherapy, lifestyle modification, including the DASH diet, sodium reduction, weight loss, exercise, and moderation of alcohol intake, is the appropriate first step."}, {"id": 377, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "A 55-year-old man with a 10-year history of type 2 diabetes is found on routine screening to have a urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio consistent with microalbuminuria, a new finding for him. His blood pressure today is measured at 148/92 mmHg on two occasions during the same visit, and he has never previously been treated with any antihypertensive medication. Which class of antihypertensive medication is most appropriate to start as first-line therapy in his case?", "options": {"A": "A beta blocker as first-line therapy", "B": "A thiazide diuretic used alone", "C": "An ACE inhibitor or ARB", "D": "A direct renin inhibitor alone"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (An ACE inhibitor or ARB): In patients with diabetes and evidence of early kidney involvement such as his new microalbuminuria, an ACE inhibitor or ARB is preferred as first-line therapy because of its added benefit in reducing intraglomerular pressure and slowing progression of diabetic kidney disease, well beyond its blood pressure lowering effect alone."}, {"id": 378, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "A 48-year-old Black man presents to establish care and is found to have newly diagnosed hypertension, with repeated readings around 152/94 mmHg. He has no history of diabetes, no proteinuria on urinalysis, and no symptoms or exam findings suggestive of heart failure. His clinician is selecting an appropriate first antihypertensive agent for him. Which class of medication is generally most effective as initial monotherapy in a patient like him?", "options": {"A": "A beta blocker used first-line for his hypertension", "B": "An ACE inhibitor, often preferred as a first agent here", "C": "An alpha blocker used first-line for his hypertension", "D": "A thiazide diuretic or calcium channel blocker"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A thiazide diuretic or calcium channel blocker): In Black patients without a specific compelling comorbid indication, such as diabetic nephropathy or heart failure, which he does not have, thiazide diuretics and calcium channel blockers tend to produce a greater initial blood pressure response than ACE inhibitors or ARBs used alone, and are generally preferred as first-line monotherapy in this population."}, {"id": 379, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "A 40-year-old woman with resistant hypertension, despite adherence to three different antihypertensive medications from different classes at adequate doses, is referred to a hypertension specialist for further workup. Laboratory studies obtained at this visit show a potassium level of 3.0 mEq/L along with a mild metabolic alkalosis on her basic metabolic panel. She denies diuretic use outside of her prescribed regimen. Which secondary cause of hypertension is most likely responsible for her presentation?", "options": {"A": "Pheochromocytoma", "B": "Obstructive sleep apnea", "C": "Primary hyperaldosteronism", "D": "Renal artery stenosis"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Primary hyperaldosteronism): Resistant hypertension accompanied by unexplained hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis, as seen in her case, is a classic clue for primary hyperaldosteronism, in which excess aldosterone drives renal potassium wasting and hydrogen ion loss, and this finding should prompt measurement of a plasma aldosterone-to-renin ratio for further evaluation."}, {"id": 380, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "A 27-year-old woman with no significant past medical history and no family history of hypertension is found on a routine visit to have severe, newly diagnosed hypertension along with an abdominal bruit heard clearly on auscultation just above her umbilicus. Given these findings, renal artery imaging is obtained and demonstrates a characteristic 'string of beads' appearance along the mid to distal renal artery. Which is the most likely underlying cause of her hypertension?", "options": {"A": "Atherosclerotic renal artery stenosis", "B": "Pheochromocytoma", "C": "Fibromuscular dysplasia", "D": "Primary hyperaldosteronism"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Fibromuscular dysplasia): Fibromuscular dysplasia is the classic cause of renal artery stenosis in young women like her, producing the characteristic 'string of beads' appearance on angiographic imaging, in clear contrast to atherosclerotic renal artery stenosis, which typically affects older patients who have significant vascular risk factors such as long-standing smoking or diabetes."}, {"id": 381, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "A 38-year-old man presents to clinic describing recurrent episodes, occurring roughly once every couple of weeks, of severe headache, palpitations, and profuse diaphoresis, each episode lasting about 20 minutes before resolving on its own. Between episodes he feels entirely well. His blood pressure is noted to be markedly elevated during a witnessed episode in clinic, though it returns to a normal range afterward. Which diagnosis should be actively pursued in his workup?", "options": {"A": "Pheochromocytoma", "B": "Coarctation", "C": "Hyperaldosteronism", "D": "Sleep apnea"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Pheochromocytoma): Episodic headache, palpitations, and diaphoresis accompanied by paroxysmal severe hypertension, exactly as described in his case, is the classic triad-plus-pattern for pheochromocytoma, a catecholamine-secreting adrenal tumor, and should prompt measurement of plasma or urine metanephrines as the next diagnostic step."}, {"id": 382, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "A 60-year-old man is found to have a blood pressure of 210/124 mmHg on a routine screening visit at a community health fair. He reports feeling entirely well, denies headache, chest pain, dyspnea, or visual changes, and a focused physical exam and basic point-of-care labs performed on site show no evidence of acute end-organ damage. Which is the most appropriate management for him at this time?", "options": {"A": "Rapid reduction of his blood pressure to a normal range within one hour using intravenous agents", "B": "Gradual reduction of blood pressure over 24 to 48 hours with oral medication, without hospitalization", "C": "Immediate intravenous antihypertensive therapy administered in an intensive care unit", "D": "No treatment needed right now, since he currently reports no symptoms at this visit"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Gradual reduction of blood pressure over 24 to 48 hours with oral medication, without hospitalization): This is hypertensive urgency: severely elevated blood pressure without any evidence of acute end-organ damage. It is managed with gradual blood pressure reduction over 24 to 48 hours using oral medication, typically without the need for hospitalization, in clear contrast to hypertensive emergency, which requires intravenous therapy administered in a monitored setting."}, {"id": 383, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "A 62-year-old man with poorly controlled hypertension presents with sudden, severe tearing chest pain that began abruptly while at rest. His blood pressure on arrival is 198/112 mmHg and his heart rate is 104/min. CT angiography confirms an acute aortic dissection. His care team is preparing to initiate medical therapy to control his blood pressure and heart rate as quickly and safely as possible. Which medication should be given first?", "options": {"A": "An intravenous beta blocker, such as esmolol or labetalol", "B": "A loop diuretic given specifically to reduce his intravascular volume", "C": "An oral calcium channel blocker given as his initial therapy", "D": "A direct arterial vasodilator such as hydralazine given alone as the first agent"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (An intravenous beta blocker, such as esmolol or labetalol): In aortic dissection, blood pressure and heart rate should be lowered rapidly with an intravenous beta blocker first, in order to reduce the shear stress, or dP/dt, on the aortic wall, before adding a vasodilator if further blood pressure reduction is still needed. Starting with a pure vasodilator alone can cause reflex tachycardia, which would increase shear stress and could actively worsen his dissection."}, {"id": 385, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "A 74-year-old man with a known but previously untreated abdominal aortic aneurysm, followed only loosely over the years without regular imaging, presents to the emergency department with sudden severe abdominal and back pain that began about 30 minutes ago while gardening. On arrival his blood pressure is 84/50 mmHg and he appears pale and diaphoretic. Abdominal exam reveals a tender, pulsatile mass just above the umbilicus. Which diagnosis should be assumed until proven otherwise?", "options": {"A": "A perforated peptic ulcer", "B": "Acute pancreatitis", "C": "Renal colic from a ureteral stone", "D": "Ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm): The triad of sudden severe abdominal or back pain, hypotension, and a pulsatile abdominal mass, exactly as seen in his presentation, is classic for ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm, a surgical emergency requiring immediate resuscitation and emergent repair without delay for extensive further workup."}, {"id": 387, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "CT angiography is obtained in a hemodynamically stable 58-year-old man with acute chest pain and confirms a dissection that clearly involves the ascending aorta, extending just proximal to the origin of the brachiocephalic artery. His vital signs remain stable throughout the imaging study and initial evaluation, without any signs of tamponade, stroke, or malperfusion identified so far. Which is the most appropriate definitive management for him?", "options": {"A": "Endovascular stenting alone", "B": "Medical management alone", "C": "Outpatient follow-up imaging", "D": "Emergency surgical repair"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Emergency surgical repair): A dissection involving the ascending aorta, classified as Stanford type A, is a surgical emergency because of the risk of rupture into the pericardium causing tamponade, or involvement of the coronary arteries or the aortic valve itself. Emergency surgical repair is indicated regardless of the patient's current hemodynamic stability, since the risk of sudden catastrophic complications remains high without repair."}, {"id": 388, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "A 70-year-old man with suspected acute aortic dissection is found to be profoundly hypotensive and confused shortly after arrival, and the treating team determines he is too hemodynamically unstable to be safely transported to the CT scanner located in a separate part of the hospital. Bedside stabilization measures are being initiated while further imaging is urgently arranged. Which imaging modality is most appropriate to perform at his bedside in this situation?", "options": {"A": "Transesophageal echocardiography", "B": "A ventilation-perfusion scan", "C": "An exercise stress echocardiogram", "D": "Abdominal ultrasound"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Transesophageal echocardiography): Transesophageal echocardiography can be performed directly at the bedside and provides excellent visualization of the aorta, making it the preferred imaging modality in a hemodynamically unstable patient with suspected aortic dissection, like him, who cannot safely be transported elsewhere for CT angiography."}, {"id": 394, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "A 68-year-old man with known peripheral artery disease, previously managed with exercise therapy and medical therapy alone, returns to clinic reporting new pain in his foot that occurs even at rest and disrupts his sleep, along with a small, non-healing ulcer that has developed on his great toe over the past month. Which term best describes this new presentation, and how does its management differ from his prior, simple claudication?", "options": {"A": "Venous stasis disease, which is best managed with compression stockings", "B": "Diabetic peripheral neuropathy, which requires only glycemic control going forward", "C": "Intermittent claudication, which should continue to be managed the same way as his prior symptoms", "D": "Critical limb ischemia, which requires urgent vascular evaluation for revascularization rather than exercise therapy alone"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Critical limb ischemia, which requires urgent vascular evaluation for revascularization rather than exercise therapy alone): Rest pain and ulceration in a patient with established peripheral artery disease define critical limb ischemia, a considerably more severe stage than claudication that directly threatens limb viability and requires urgent evaluation for revascularization, rather than continuing the conservative, exercise-based approach that was appropriate for his prior claudication alone."}, {"id": 396, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "A 22-year-old woman is standing in a hot, crowded room at a wedding reception when she begins to feel nauseated and notices things becoming gray at the edges of her vision, followed by loss of consciousness for approximately 15 seconds, witnessed by a friend standing next to her. She recovers spontaneously and completely within a minute, with no confusion afterward. She has no significant cardiac history, takes no medications, and a subsequent ECG performed in the emergency department is entirely normal. Which type of syncope is most likely responsible for this episode?", "options": {"A": "Cardiac syncope from arrhythmia", "B": "Vasovagal (neurocardiogenic) syncope", "C": "Orthostatic syncope from volume depletion", "D": "Syncope from inherited channelopathy"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Vasovagal (neurocardiogenic) syncope): A clear situational trigger, here prolonged standing in a hot, crowded environment, combined with a classic prodrome of nausea, warmth, and visual graying, followed by rapid and complete recovery without confusion, is characteristic of vasovagal syncope, the most common and generally benign cause of syncope in young, otherwise healthy patients like her."}, {"id": 403, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "A 55-year-old woman with a known history of metastatic breast cancer presents to the emergency department with progressive dyspnea over the past week. On exam she is found to be hypotensive with a blood pressure of 84/58 mmHg, has notably distended jugular veins, and her heart sounds are described as distant and muffled on careful auscultation. Her care team also measures a pulsus paradoxus of 15 mmHg on repeat blood pressure checks during inspiration and expiration. Which diagnosis is most likely, and what physiologic finding directly supports it?", "options": {"A": "Tension pneumothorax, a condition which does not typically cause pulsus paradoxus", "B": "Constrictive pericarditis, supported instead by a normal pulsus paradoxus measurement", "C": "Acute myocardial infarction, which is classically supported by a significant pulsus paradoxus", "D": "Cardiac tamponade, supported by an exaggerated inspiratory drop in systolic blood pressure (pulsus paradoxus)"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Cardiac tamponade, supported by an exaggerated inspiratory drop in systolic blood pressure (pulsus paradoxus)): Beck triad, consisting of hypotension, jugular venous distension, and muffled heart sounds, together with pulsus paradoxus, an exaggerated inspiratory drop in systolic blood pressure of more than 10 mmHg as measured here, strongly supports cardiac tamponade, a complication that can occur with a malignant pericardial effusion compressing the heart in a patient with known metastatic cancer."}, {"id": 404, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "A 62-year-old man with a remote history of tuberculosis treated many years ago presents with several months of progressive dyspnea and lower extremity swelling. On exam, his jugular venous pressure is elevated with a notably prominent y descent, and a distinctive pericardial knock is heard shortly after S2 on careful auscultation. Echocardiogram reveals a thickened, calcified pericardium surrounding the heart. Which diagnosis is most consistent with these findings, and how is it best distinguished from restrictive cardiomyopathy, a condition with a similar overall clinical presentation?", "options": {"A": "Restrictive cardiomyopathy, distinguished from constrictive pericarditis by a normal pericardium along with abnormal myocardial tissue characteristics", "B": "Constrictive pericarditis, distinguished from restrictive cardiomyopathy by pericardial thickening or calcification on imaging", "C": "Dilated cardiomyopathy, distinguished instead by a markedly reduced ejection fraction on echocardiogram", "D": "Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, distinguished instead by asymmetric septal hypertrophy on imaging"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Constrictive pericarditis, distinguished from restrictive cardiomyopathy by pericardial thickening or calcification on imaging): Constrictive pericarditis, often a late consequence of conditions such as his prior tuberculosis, produces a thickened or calcified pericardium clearly visible on imaging, along with a prominent y descent in the jugular venous waveform and a pericardial knock, all of which help distinguish it from restrictive cardiomyopathy, a condition that instead involves the myocardium itself rather than the pericardium."}, {"id": 405, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "A 55-year-old man with no history of established cardiovascular disease is seen for a routine preventive care visit. Using a validated cardiovascular risk calculator that incorporates his age, blood pressure, cholesterol panel, and smoking status, his estimated 10-year atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease risk comes out to 12 percent. He does not have diabetes and his LDL cholesterol is moderately elevated but not dramatically so. Which is the most appropriate approach to statin therapy in his case?", "options": {"A": "Initiate a statin for primary prevention, given his intermediate to high calculated risk", "B": "Statin therapy is generally reserved until his LDL climbs well above 250 mg/dL, largely independent of his calculated risk", "C": "Defer statin therapy for now, since he has no established cardiovascular disease at this time", "D": "Statin therapy is typically reserved for patients who also carry a diagnosis of diabetes"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Initiate a statin for primary prevention, given his intermediate to high calculated risk): Long-term cardiovascular risk calculators, which incorporate age, sex, blood pressure, cholesterol values, diabetes status, and smoking history, are used specifically to guide statin therapy in primary prevention. A calculated risk in the intermediate to high range, as seen in his case, supports starting a statin even in an asymptomatic patient who has no established cardiovascular disease."}, {"id": 406, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "An 80-year-old woman reports recurrent episodes of dizziness and near-syncope over the past several months, occurring unpredictably throughout the day. Ambulatory rhythm monitoring placed for two weeks reveals alternating periods of significant sinus bradycardia interspersed with episodes of paroxysmal atrial tachyarrhythmia, a pattern her cardiologist explains to her carefully. She remains significantly symptomatic despite conservative measures. Which is the most appropriate long-term management for her at this point?", "options": {"A": "Isolated antiarrhythmic drug therapy used alone, without any consideration of pacing", "B": "Chronic atropine therapy used as definitive, long-term treatment for her condition", "C": "Observation only, since sinus node dysfunction of this type rarely requires any intervention", "D": "Permanent pacemaker placement, often combined with rate-controlling medication for the tachyarrhythmia episodes"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Permanent pacemaker placement, often combined with rate-controlling medication for the tachyarrhythmia episodes): This describes sick sinus syndrome, also known as tachycardia-bradycardia syndrome, in which alternating bradyarrhythmias and tachyarrhythmias make medical therapy alone genuinely difficult, since rate-controlling drugs given for the tachycardia component would predictably worsen her underlying bradycardia. A permanent pacemaker allows the safe subsequent use of rate-controlling medication specifically for her tachyarrhythmic episodes."}, {"id": 407, "domain": "cardiovascular_system", "question": "A 66-year-old man with HFrEF has persistent lower extremity edema and worsening dyspnea despite escalating doses of his oral furosemide over the past two weeks, with his outpatient care team noting only a modest diuretic response to each dose increase. His renal function has remained relatively stable throughout, and his adherence to his prescribed medication regimen has been confirmed. Which strategy is most appropriate to help overcome his apparent diuretic resistance?", "options": {"A": "Increase his free water intake, since improving his renal perfusion is believed to enhance diuretic delivery to the kidney", "B": "Switch to or add intravenous loop diuretic therapy, or add a second diuretic acting at a different nephron segment", "C": "Switch him to a mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist, using it in place of his loop diuretic for ongoing management", "D": "Discontinue his diuretics for now and rely mainly on fluid and sodium restriction to manage his volume status"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Switch to or add intravenous loop diuretic therapy, or add a second diuretic acting at a different nephron segment): Diuretic resistance in decompensated heart failure can result from reduced oral bioavailability due to gut wall edema, as well as reduced renal blood flow limiting drug delivery to its site of action. Switching to intravenous loop diuretic dosing, or adding a second agent acting at a different part of the nephron, such as sequential nephron blockade with a thiazide-type diuretic, can help overcome this resistance and achieve more adequate decongestion."}, {"id": 411, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A 68-year-old woman presents to the emergency department after slipping on ice and landing on her outstretched right hand. She reports immediate pain and swelling of the wrist. On examination there is dorsal displacement and angulation of the distal radial fragment, producing a classic silver-fork deformity, along with point tenderness over the distal radius. Radiographs confirm a fracture of the distal radius with dorsal displacement of the distal fragment. Her vital signs are notable for a heart rate of 88 and blood pressure of 138/82, and she has no other injuries on secondary survey. She takes alendronate for osteoporosis diagnosed two years ago. Which of the following best describes this injury?", "options": {"A": "Colles fracture, from a fall onto an extended, outstretched hand", "B": "Smith fracture, from a fall onto a flexed wrist, an approach sometimes used in similar clinical situations", "C": "Scaphoid fracture, from axial loading of the wrist", "D": "Galeazzi fracture, from a direct blow to the forearm"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Colles fracture, from a fall onto an extended, outstretched hand): This patient's mechanism, a fall onto an outstretched hand, combined with dorsal displacement of the distal radial fragment producing a silver-fork deformity, is the classic description of a Colles fracture. It is the most common wrist fracture in older adults with osteoporotic bone. A Smith fracture instead results from a fall onto a flexed wrist and produces volar displacement, the mirror image of this presentation."}, {"id": 413, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A 22-year-old man falls onto an outstretched hand during a basketball game and presents with pain localized to the radial side of the wrist. On examination there is tenderness within the anatomic snuffbox, but initial wrist radiographs are read as normal with no visible fracture line. His vital signs are normal and he has full range of motion of the fingers with intact capillary refill. He is otherwise healthy and takes no medications. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": {"A": "Reassure the patient that the radiographs are normal and discharge without follow-up", "B": "Place the wrist in a de Quervain tenosynovitis splint and refer to physical therapy", "C": "Proceed immediately to open reduction and internal fixation, which some clinicians consider in patients with comparable presentations", "D": "Immobilize in a thumb spica splint and repeat radiographs or obtain further imaging in 10 to 14 days"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Immobilize in a thumb spica splint and repeat radiographs or obtain further imaging in 10 to 14 days): Snuffbox tenderness after a fall on an outstretched hand should raise suspicion for a scaphoid fracture even when initial radiographs are normal, since these fractures are frequently occult on first imaging. The correct approach is thumb spica immobilization with repeat radiographs or advanced imaging in ten to fourteen days, because missing this fracture risks avascular necrosis given the scaphoid's retrograde blood supply."}, {"id": 414, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A radiology resident asks why fractures through the proximal pole of the scaphoid carry a substantially higher risk of avascular necrosis than fractures through the distal pole. This distinction has direct implications for how aggressively a nondisplaced scaphoid fracture is immobilized and monitored during healing. Which of the following best explains this difference? This distinction has direct implications for how aggressively a nondisplaced scaphoid fracture is immobilized and monitored during healing, since a fracture through the waist behaves differently from one confined to the well-vascularized distal pole.", "options": {"A": "The scaphoid's blood supply enters distally and travels retrograde, so proximal fractures more often disrupt perfusion to the proximal fragment", "B": "The proximal pole has thicker, denser cortical bone that tends to heal more slowly than the distal pole after a fracture", "C": "The proximal pole is more frequently missed on initial radiographs, delaying treatment and worsening outcomes", "D": "The proximal pole bears greater mechanical load during grip strength, causing repeated microtrauma to healing bone"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The scaphoid's blood supply enters distally and travels retrograde, so proximal fractures more often disrupt perfusion to the proximal fragment): The scaphoid receives its dominant blood supply through vessels that enter at the distal pole and travel retrograde toward the proximal pole. A fracture through the waist or proximal pole can therefore cut off perfusion to the proximal fragment, explaining the elevated risk of avascular necrosis compared with distal pole fractures, which usually retain adequate blood flow."}, {"id": 415, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "An 82-year-old woman with known osteoporosis falls at home and is brought to the emergency department unable to bear weight. Examination shows a shortened, externally rotated right leg. Radiographs reveal a displaced fracture through the femoral neck. Laboratory studies including a complete blood count and basic metabolic panel are unremarkable, and she has no other acute injuries identified on trauma evaluation. She lives independently at home prior to this fall. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": {"A": "Skeletal traction with delayed weight-bearing over several weeks, which remains a consideration in some analogous clinical presentations", "B": "Urgent surgical fixation, given the risk of avascular necrosis from disrupted retrograde blood flow to the femoral head", "C": "Nonoperative management with early mobilization and analgesia alone", "D": "A course of NSAIDs and outpatient physical therapy, an option that is sometimes discussed for patients with similar findings"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Urgent surgical fixation, given the risk of avascular necrosis from disrupted retrograde blood flow to the femoral head): Displaced femoral neck fractures disrupt the retrograde blood supply carried mainly by the medial femoral circumflex artery along the femoral neck, placing the femoral head at real risk of avascular necrosis. Urgent surgical fixation, often with hemiarthroplasty or total hip arthroplasty for a displaced fracture in an older patient, is required both to address this vascular risk and to allow early mobilization, which sharply reduces mortality from prolonged immobility in this population."}, {"id": 416, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A 79-year-old man with well-controlled type 2 diabetes and hypertension sustains a low-energy fall at home and is found to have a fracture through the intertrochanteric region of the femur. He is otherwise hemodynamically stable, and comparison with displaced femoral neck fractures is being discussed on rounds as part of his preoperative planning. Compared with a displaced femoral neck fracture, which of the following best explains why this fracture carries a lower risk of avascular necrosis of the femoral head?", "options": {"A": "Intertrochanteric fractures are typically nondisplaced and heal without any surgical intervention, a step that some clinicians take in comparable situations", "B": "Weight-bearing forces at this level are reduced compared with the femoral neck, a choice occasionally made for patients presenting in a similar fashion", "C": "The intertrochanteric region receives blood supply from multiple collateral vessels, unlike the femoral neck, which depends heavily on a single retrograde source", "D": "The intertrochanteric bone is more vascular due to its higher proportion of cancellous bone alone"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The intertrochanteric region receives blood supply from multiple collateral vessels, unlike the femoral neck, which depends heavily on a single retrograde source): The femoral neck depends heavily on retrograde flow through vessels traveling along its surface, so a fracture there can readily disrupt perfusion to the femoral head. The intertrochanteric region, in contrast, is supplied by a richer network of collateral vessels, so avascular necrosis is far less common there even though prompt surgical fixation is still required to allow early mobilization."}, {"id": 419, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A 24-year-old man punches a wall during an argument and presents with pain and swelling over the ulnar side of his hand. Examination shows tenderness and a slight depression over the fifth metacarpal with mild angulation, and radiographs confirm a fracture through the neck of the fifth metacarpal. He has full range of motion of the remaining digits, and his vital signs are unremarkable aside from mild tachycardia from pain. Which of the following is the most appropriate management? He has no history of prior hand fractures and works as an office administrator, and he is asking whether any surgical intervention will be required for this specific fracture pattern.", "options": {"A": "Closed reduction with ulnar gutter splinting, since some angulation at this joint is well tolerated", "B": "Open reduction and internal fixation, typically reserved for fractures with more significant angulation than seen here", "C": "Amputation of the affected digit, an approach sometimes considered for severely comminuted or unsalvageable injuries", "D": "Return to full activity without immobilization, relying on the joint's inherent stability during healing"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Closed reduction with ulnar gutter splinting, since some angulation at this joint is well tolerated): This presentation, a fracture through the neck of the fifth metacarpal after a closed-fist punch, is the classic boxer's fracture. Because the fifth metacarpometacarpal joint tolerates a meaningful degree of angulation without significant functional loss, most boxer's fractures are managed with closed reduction and splinting rather than surgery, which is reserved for fractures with excessive angulation, rotation, or intra-articular extension."}, {"id": 421, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A 27-year-old woman sustains an inversion injury to her ankle while hiking. She has mild swelling over the anterior talofibular ligament but no bony tenderness at either malleolus or at the base of the fifth metatarsal, and she is able to walk four steps unassisted both at the scene and in the clinic. Her vital signs are normal, and there is no bruising or deformity, only mild soft tissue swelling over the lateral ankle. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?", "options": {"A": "Obtain ankle radiographs before allowing any weight-bearing, a step that some clinicians take in comparable situations", "B": "Manage with rest, ice, compression, elevation, and early functional rehabilitation without radiographs", "C": "Order an MRI of the ankle to assess ligamentous integrity before treatment", "D": "Refer immediately for orthopedic surgical consultation, a choice occasionally made for patients presenting in a similar fashion"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Manage with rest, ice, compression, elevation, and early functional rehabilitation without radiographs): This patient does not meet Ottawa Ankle Rule criteria for imaging, since she has no bony tenderness at the malleoli or fifth metatarsal base and can bear weight for four steps both immediately after injury and on examination. The great majority of ankle sprains without these red flags are managed conservatively with rest, ice, compression, elevation, and early functional rehabilitation, without the need for radiographs."}, {"id": 422, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A 19-year-old man sustains a closed tibial shaft fracture in a motorcycle accident and is placed in a long-leg cast. Six hours later he reports severe leg pain that is markedly worse with passive dorsiflexion of the toes, out of proportion to what would be expected from the fracture alone. Distal pulses remain palpable and capillary refill is normal. His vital signs are a heart rate of 102 and blood pressure of 128/78, and the leg is tensely swollen with intact sensation to light touch over the dorsum of the foot. Which of the following is the most appropriate interpretation of these findings?", "options": {"A": "Preserved pulses and capillary refill effectively exclude compartment syndrome at this stage, an approach sometimes used in similar clinical situations", "B": "This presentation is expected after any tibial fracture and requires no further action, which is sometimes seen in patients with a similar history", "C": "These findings, pain out of proportion to injury and pain with passive stretch, are early signs of compartment syndrome and should not be dismissed because pulses are still present", "D": "Pain with passive stretch indicates a co-existing peroneal nerve injury that is unrelated to compartment pressure and does not require any further vascular workup"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (These findings, pain out of proportion to injury and pain with passive stretch, are early signs of compartment syndrome and should not be dismissed because pulses are still present): Pain out of proportion to the injury and pain with passive stretch of the muscles in the affected compartment are the earliest and most reliable signs of compartment syndrome. Pulses and capillary refill often remain normal until late in the process, since compartment pressure compromises capillary perfusion long before it is high enough to occlude a major artery, so normal pulses must never be used to exclude the diagnosis."}, {"id": 423, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A patient develops compartment syndrome of the leg following a tibial fracture, confirmed by pain out of proportion to injury, pain with passive stretch, and a measured compartment pressure within 25 mmHg of diastolic blood pressure. His compartment pressure was measured using a needle manometer at bedside after the clinical findings raised concern, given the risk of missing a reversible ischemic process. Which of the following is the definitive treatment? The orthopedic surgery team has been urgently notified and is on their way to evaluate him for definitive management of this diagnosis.", "options": {"A": "Elevation of the limb and application of ice packs", "B": "A trial of intravenous mannitol to reduce edema", "C": "Observation with serial compartment pressure measurements", "D": "Emergent fasciotomy to decompress the affected compartments"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Emergent fasciotomy to decompress the affected compartments): Once compartment syndrome is diagnosed, whether by clinical findings alone or supported by compartment pressure measurement showing a pressure within 30 mmHg of diastolic pressure, emergent fasciotomy is the definitive treatment. Any circumferential cast or constrictive dressing should also be released immediately while arrangements for surgery are made, since delay converts a reversible ischemic injury into permanent muscle and nerve damage."}, {"id": 425, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A child sustains a supracondylar humerus fracture that is treated with delayed reduction after a period of unrecognized vascular compromise. Months later he is noted to have fixed flexion contractures of the fingers and wrist with claw-like posturing of the hand. He now requires ongoing occupational therapy for functional limitation of the hand, and his family asks whether this outcome could have been prevented. Which of the following best describes this outcome? His family asks whether this outcome could have been prevented with earlier recognition of the underlying process affecting his forearm following the original injury.", "options": {"A": "Erb palsy from brachial plexus traction injury, which some clinicians consider in patients with comparable presentations", "B": "Complex regional pain syndrome, a distinct pain condition thought to be unrelated to the original fracture in this case.", "C": "A variant of normal healing that would be unlikely to relate meaningfully to the original injury he sustained.", "D": "Volkmann contracture, resulting from unrecognized or inadequately treated compartment syndrome of the forearm"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Volkmann contracture, resulting from unrecognized or inadequately treated compartment syndrome of the forearm): Volkmann contracture describes the permanent flexion contracture of the forearm and hand that results when compartment syndrome of the forearm is not recognized and treated promptly. Ischemic muscle and nerve tissue is replaced by fibrous scar tissue, producing the fixed, claw-like posturing described here. It is a preventable outcome when compartment syndrome is diagnosed early and treated with emergent fasciotomy."}, {"id": 426, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A 19-year-old collegiate soccer player plants her foot and pivots suddenly during a game, feeling and hearing a pop in her knee followed by immediate swelling. On examination the knee has a large effusion, and anterior tibial translation is noted relative to the femur when the knee is flexed to 20 to 30 degrees and gently pulled forward. Her vital signs are normal, and she is unable to continue playing due to instability and pain when attempting to bear weight and pivot on the leg. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Medial collateral ligament sprain", "B": "Patellar tendon rupture", "C": "Posterior cruciate ligament tear", "D": "Anterior cruciate ligament tear"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Anterior cruciate ligament tear): A noncontact pivoting injury with an audible pop, rapid effusion, and increased anterior tibial translation on a positive Lachman test is the classic presentation of an anterior cruciate ligament tear. The rapid hemarthrosis reflects bleeding from the torn, richly vascularized ligament, and MRI is typically used to confirm the diagnosis and evaluate for concomitant meniscal injury."}, {"id": 430, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A 64-year-old woman reports several years of gradually worsening pain and bony enlargement affecting the distal joints of several fingers on both hands, worse with activity and improved with rest, with brief morning stiffness lasting only a few minutes. She has no fever, redness, or warmth of the affected joints, and no systemic symptoms such as fatigue or weight loss. She undergoes radiography of both hands as part of her evaluation, and her physician reviews the resulting images before discussing the diagnosis with her. Which of the following is the most likely underlying diagnosis based on this radiographic pattern and her clinical history?", "options": {"A": "Joint space narrowing, subchondral sclerosis, and osteophyte formation at the distal interphalangeal joints", "B": "Symmetric marginal erosions with periarticular osteopenia at the metacarpophalangeal joints", "C": "Diffuse osteopenia with a normal joint space throughout, a choice occasionally made for patients presenting in a similar fashion", "D": "Punched-out lytic lesions with overhanging bony edges at the first metatarsophalangeal joint"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Joint space narrowing, subchondral sclerosis, and osteophyte formation at the distal interphalangeal joints): This radiographic pattern, joint space narrowing, subchondral sclerosis, and osteophyte formation concentrated at the distal interphalangeal joints, is characteristic of osteoarthritis, which corresponds clinically to the bony enlargement felt as Heberden nodes. This is distinct from the marginal erosions and periarticular osteopenia of rheumatoid arthritis or the punched-out erosions with overhanging edges seen in chronic tophaceous gout.", "image": "question_images/q_430.png"}, {"id": 433, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A patient with suspected rheumatoid arthritis has both rheumatoid factor and anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide antibody testing sent as part of her initial rheumatologic evaluation. She has symmetric swelling of the metacarpophalangeal joints of both hands with morning stiffness lasting over an hour that improves with activity, and radiographs show early periarticular osteopenia without frank erosions at this stage of disease. Her rheumatologist reviews both results together before deciding how confidently to proceed with treatment. Which of the following statements about these two tests is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Rheumatoid factor is more specific for rheumatoid arthritis than anti-CCP antibody", "B": "Neither test carries meaningful diagnostic value in rheumatoid arthritis", "C": "A positive rheumatoid factor with a negative anti-CCP antibody makes rheumatoid arthritis unlikely", "D": "Anti-CCP antibody is more specific for rheumatoid arthritis than rheumatoid factor"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Anti-CCP antibody is more specific for rheumatoid arthritis than rheumatoid factor): Both rheumatoid factor and anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide antibody support the diagnosis of rheumatoid arthritis, but anti-CCP antibody is more specific, since rheumatoid factor can be positive in a variety of other conditions and even in healthy individuals. Neither test is required for diagnosis, and clinical presentation combined with imaging findings remains central to making the diagnosis."}, {"id": 434, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A 39-year-old woman is newly diagnosed with rheumatoid arthritis based on symmetric small joint synovitis and a positive anti-CCP antibody. She has no contraindications to standard first-line therapy, including normal liver function tests and no current desire for pregnancy, and her disease activity score indicates moderate to severe involvement. Which of the following represents the most appropriate initial pharmacologic approach? Her disease activity score indicates moderate to severe involvement requiring prompt treatment, and she asks her rheumatologist what medication should be started first.", "options": {"A": "Early initiation of a disease-modifying antirheumatic drug, most often methotrexate, to prevent irreversible joint damage", "B": "Long-term high-dose NSAIDs alone, reserving disease-modifying therapy for refractory cases", "C": "Observation, since early treatment does not affect long-term joint outcomes, an option that is sometimes discussed for patients with similar findings", "D": "Chronic oral corticosteroids as sole long-term therapy, a strategy that is occasionally favored in related clinical scenarios"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Early initiation of a disease-modifying antirheumatic drug, most often methotrexate, to prevent irreversible joint damage): Early initiation of disease-modifying antirheumatic drug therapy, with methotrexate as the anchor agent, is essential in rheumatoid arthritis because delayed treatment allows ongoing synovial inflammation to cause irreversible cartilage and bone erosion. NSAIDs and corticosteroids can help control symptoms but do not halt disease progression and are not substitutes for disease-modifying therapy."}, {"id": 435, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A patient with rheumatoid arthritis who has had an inadequate response to methotrexate is being considered for a tumor necrosis factor inhibitor. He has no known history of tuberculosis exposure or hepatitis, but has never previously been screened for either condition before starting immunosuppressive therapy. Which of the following should be performed before starting this therapy? He has never previously been screened for either condition before starting immunosuppressive therapy of any kind for his joint disease, and his rheumatologist orders baseline testing.", "options": {"A": "Baseline bone density scan only, with no infectious disease screening required, a choice occasionally made for patients presenting in a similar fashion", "B": "A trial of oral corticosteroids to assess symptomatic response before adding a biologic", "C": "Screening for latent tuberculosis and hepatitis B, since biologic therapy can reactivate latent infection", "D": "Colonoscopy to screen for inflammatory bowel disease, which remains a consideration in some analogous clinical presentations"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Screening for latent tuberculosis and hepatitis B, since biologic therapy can reactivate latent infection): Biologic agents that inhibit tumor necrosis factor or other inflammatory pathways can reactivate latent infections, most notably tuberculosis and hepatitis B. Screening for both is required before initiating these agents, and any evidence of latent infection should be addressed, often with concurrent antimicrobial or antiviral treatment, before or during biologic therapy."}, {"id": 436, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A 24-year-old man reports six months of low back and buttock pain that is worse in the morning and after prolonged rest, but improves with exercise and activity throughout the day. He has no history of trauma. Imaging demonstrates sacroiliitis. He also reports that his symptoms are accompanied by intermittent eye redness and pain, and he has a first-degree relative with a similar chronic back condition. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis? He has a first-degree relative with a similar chronic back condition diagnosed in early adulthood, and his physician suspects an associated seronegative spondyloarthropathy.", "options": {"A": "Back strain", "B": "Lumbar osteoarthritis", "C": "Fibromyalgia", "D": "Ankylosing spondylitis"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ankylosing spondylitis): Inflammatory back pain that improves with exercise and worsens with rest, occurring in a young patient with sacroiliac joint involvement, is characteristic of ankylosing spondylitis, one of the spondyloarthropathies strongly associated with HLA-B27. This pattern, improvement with movement, is the reverse of typical mechanical low back pain, which tends to worsen with activity."}, {"id": 437, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A 45-year-old man with a history of plaque psoriasis develops swelling of an entire toe that gives it a uniformly swollen, sausage-like appearance, along with new nail pitting. He also reports new-onset pain and swelling of several fingers and toes over the past month, in addition to his longstanding plaques over the elbows and scalp. Which of the following terms best describes this finding, and with which condition is it most closely associated? His longstanding plaques over the elbows and scalp have been present for years, and his dermatologist refers him to rheumatology for further joint evaluation.", "options": {"A": "Boutonniere deformity, associated with rheumatoid arthritis", "B": "Swan neck deformity, associated with systemic lupus erythematosus", "C": "Heberden nodes, associated with osteoarthritis", "D": "Dactylitis, associated with psoriatic arthritis"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Dactylitis, associated with psoriatic arthritis): Dactylitis, diffuse swelling of an entire digit giving a sausage-like appearance, is a distinguishing feature of psoriatic arthritis. Combined with a history of psoriasis and nail changes such as pitting, this presentation strongly supports a diagnosis of psoriatic arthritis rather than another inflammatory arthropathy."}, {"id": 438, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A 27-year-old man develops conjunctivitis, urethritis, and asymmetric oligoarthritis of the knee and ankle three weeks after an episode of chlamydial urethritis. He has no history of joint disease prior to this episode, and his vital signs show a low-grade temperature of 37.8 degrees Celsius with normal blood pressure. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis? He has no other systemic complaints, and his physician suspects a reactive process triggered by his recent infection rather than a primary septic process.", "options": {"A": "Rheumatoid arthritis", "B": "Reactive arthritis", "C": "Gonococcal septic arthritis", "D": "Systemic lupus erythematosus"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Reactive arthritis): The triad of conjunctivitis, urethritis, and asymmetric arthritis occurring after a genitourinary or gastrointestinal infection is classic for reactive arthritis, one of the seronegative spondyloarthropathies. Unlike septic arthritis, the joint itself is sterile, since reactive arthritis is an immune-mediated response triggered by a preceding infection rather than direct joint infection."}, {"id": 439, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A clinician is trying to distinguish osteoarthritis from rheumatoid arthritis based on the pattern of morning stiffness reported by two different patients. Both patients otherwise have normal vital signs and no systemic symptoms, and the clinician is reviewing their histories side by side before ordering further testing. Which of the following statements correctly contrasts the two conditions? The clinician is reviewing their histories side by side before ordering further testing to confirm the underlying diagnosis in each case, focusing specifically on the stiffness pattern each patient reports.", "options": {"A": "Both conditions produce morning stiffness of similar duration, so this feature cannot help distinguish them", "B": "Osteoarthritis stiffness is typically brief, under thirty minutes, while rheumatoid arthritis stiffness typically lasts over an hour and improves with activity", "C": "Osteoarthritis stiffness lasts longer than rheumatoid arthritis stiffness in nearly all cases, which is sometimes seen in patients with a similar history", "D": "Morning stiffness only occurs in rheumatoid arthritis and is absent in osteoarthritis, a step that some clinicians take in comparable situations"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Osteoarthritis stiffness is typically brief, under thirty minutes, while rheumatoid arthritis stiffness typically lasts over an hour and improves with activity): Morning stiffness duration is a genuinely useful distinguishing feature: osteoarthritis produces brief stiffness, usually resolving within thirty minutes, that follows a mechanical pattern worsening with activity, while rheumatoid arthritis produces prolonged stiffness lasting more than an hour that improves as the patient becomes more active, reflecting its inflammatory rather than mechanical basis."}, {"id": 440, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A 55-year-old man with a history of osteoarthritis presents with an acutely hot, swollen, and exquisitely tender knee along with fever. He has no prior history of gout or rheumatoid arthritis. His temperature is 38.6 degrees Celsius, heart rate is 110, and the joint is markedly warm and erythematous with severe pain on any attempted range of motion. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step regardless of the eventual diagnosis? The joint is markedly warm and erythematous with severe pain on any attempted range of motion, raising broad diagnostic concern that requires urgent evaluation regardless of his underlying joint history.", "options": {"A": "Assume this represents a simple osteoarthritis flare and treat symptomatically with acetaminophen alone, without further diagnostic workup at this visit", "B": "Start empiric oral antibiotics without performing joint aspiration first, an approach sometimes used when clinical suspicion for infection is felt to be low", "C": "Obtain an MRI as the initial diagnostic test in place of joint aspiration, which some clinicians consider in patients with comparable presentations", "D": "Perform arthrocentesis with synovial fluid cell count, Gram stain, and culture to evaluate for septic arthritis before attributing the presentation to another cause"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Perform arthrocentesis with synovial fluid cell count, Gram stain, and culture to evaluate for septic arthritis before attributing the presentation to another cause): A hot, swollen, febrile joint should always prompt arthrocentesis with cell count, Gram stain, and culture to evaluate for septic arthritis, since this diagnosis can be rapidly destructive to the joint and mimics gout, pseudogout, or an inflammatory arthritis flare closely. Even when crystals are identified, septic arthritis must still be excluded by culture, since crystal disease and joint infection can coexist."}, {"id": 441, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A 26-year-old man with inflammatory low back pain and a strong suspicion for axial spondyloarthritis has normal plain radiographs of the sacroiliac joints. He has no other systemic symptoms, and his inflammatory markers, including C-reactive protein, are mildly elevated, supporting an inflammatory rather than purely mechanical process. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step to evaluate for early sacroiliitis? His inflammatory markers, including C-reactive protein, are mildly elevated, supporting an inflammatory rather than purely mechanical process affecting his lower back over recent months.", "options": {"A": "MRI of the sacroiliac joints, which can detect bone marrow edema before changes are visible on plain film", "B": "Repeat plain radiographs in one year, since MRI has no advantage over plain film in this setting", "C": "Bone scan, which is more sensitive than MRI for early sacroiliitis, an option that is sometimes discussed for patients with similar findings", "D": "No further imaging is indicated if plain films are normal, a strategy that is occasionally favored in related clinical scenarios"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (MRI of the sacroiliac joints, which can detect bone marrow edema before changes are visible on plain film): MRI of the sacroiliac joints can detect active inflammation, seen as bone marrow edema, well before the structural changes such as erosion or ankylosis that are required to see sacroiliitis on plain radiographs. This makes MRI the preferred imaging modality for detecting early axial spondyloarthritis in a patient with a normal or equivocal plain film."}, {"id": 442, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A 54-year-old man with a history of hypertension treated with hydrochlorothiazide awakens at 3 a.m. with sudden, severe pain, redness, and swelling of his right great toe. He reports similar but milder episodes in the past that resolved on their own. He denies any recent trauma, and on examination the joint is warm, erythematous, and exquisitely tender to even light touch or the weight of a bedsheet. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis? On examination the joint is warm, erythematous, and exquisitely tender to even light touch or the weight of a bedsheet resting on the toe overnight, prompting urgent evaluation.", "options": {"A": "Septic arthritis of the first metatarsophalangeal joint", "B": "Gout, presenting as podagra", "C": "Osteoarthritis flare", "D": "Reactive arthritis"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Gout, presenting as podagra): Sudden, severe monoarticular pain and inflammation of the first metatarsophalangeal joint, termed podagra, in a patient with a risk factor for hyperuricemia such as thiazide diuretic use, is the classic presentation of acute gout. Recurrent self-limited episodes in the past further support this diagnosis over a first presentation of septic arthritis."}, {"id": 443, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A patient with known gout is started on allopurinol for chronic urate-lowering therapy and develops an acute gout flare in his knee two weeks later. His serum uric acid level had been persistently elevated prior to starting therapy, and he had no acute flare in the two weeks immediately preceding initiation of the medication. Which of the following best explains why starting urate-lowering therapy can itself precipitate an acute attack? He had no acute flare in the two weeks immediately preceding initiation of the medication that was recently started, making this timing particularly notable to his physician.", "options": {"A": "Allopurinol directly stimulates monosodium urate crystal formation in the joint, a choice occasionally made for patients presenting in a similar fashion", "B": "A sudden change in serum urate concentration, in either direction, can destabilize existing crystal deposits and trigger an acute flare", "C": "Allopurinol is unlikely to be related to acute flares, and this presentation more likely suggests a new, unrelated diagnosis", "D": "This effect is generally more characteristic of febuxostat than of allopurinol, which remains a consideration in some analogous clinical presentations"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A sudden change in serum urate concentration, in either direction, can destabilize existing crystal deposits and trigger an acute flare): Acute gout flares can be triggered not only by rising uric acid levels but also by a rapid fall, as occurs when urate-lowering therapy is initiated. This is why urate-lowering agents are never started or adjusted during an acute attack and are typically covered with prophylactic low-dose colchicine or an NSAID for several months after initiation to prevent this reaction."}, {"id": 444, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A 68-year-old man with a history of hyperparathyroidism develops acute swelling, warmth, and pain in his knee without preceding trauma, and arthrocentesis reveals rhomboid-shaped, positively birefringent crystals. He also undergoes plain radiography of the affected knee as part of his workup. Which of the following radiographic findings would be most consistent with his underlying diagnosis? He also undergoes plain radiography of the affected knee as part of his workup, and his rheumatologist is reviewing the imaging alongside the synovial fluid analysis already obtained.", "options": {"A": "Linear calcification within the articular cartilage of the joint, known as chondrocalcinosis", "B": "Diffuse osteopenia with a normal joint space and no calcification, a step that some clinicians take in comparable situations", "C": "Punched-out lytic erosions with overhanging bony margins adjacent to the joint", "D": "Periarticular soft tissue swelling with marginal erosions and osteopenia"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Linear calcification within the articular cartilage of the joint, known as chondrocalcinosis): Chondrocalcinosis, calcification within the articular cartilage itself visible as a thin linear density paralleling the joint surface, is a supportive radiographic finding in calcium pyrophosphate deposition disease, or pseudogout. This condition is associated with hyperparathyroidism, hemochromatosis, hypothyroidism, and hypomagnesemia, and most often affects the knee and wrist in older adults.", "image": "question_images/q_444.png"}, {"id": 445, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A 60-year-old man with a history of recurrent podagra and untreated hyperuricemia presents again with an acutely swollen, hot, and exquisitely painful right knee that began abruptly overnight, in the setting of the shared case above. The knee is warm, erythematous, and exquisitely tender with a large effusion on examination, and he has no other joints involved. Joint aspiration is performed for diagnostic purposes. Which of the following synovial fluid findings would confirm the most likely diagnosis? Joint aspiration is performed for diagnostic purposes, and the laboratory is asked to specifically comment on crystal shape and birefringence pattern under polarized light.", "options": {"A": "Pseudogout, confirmed by rhomboid, positively birefringent crystals, which is sometimes seen in patients with a similar history", "B": "Gout, confirmed by needle-shaped, negatively birefringent crystals under polarized light microscopy", "C": "Septic arthritis, confirmed by a positive Gram stain alone without any crystal analysis", "D": "Osteoarthritis flare, which does not require joint aspiration for diagnosis"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Gout, confirmed by needle-shaped, negatively birefringent crystals under polarized light microscopy): Definitive diagnosis of gout requires arthrocentesis demonstrating needle-shaped, negatively birefringent monosodium urate crystals under polarized light microscopy. Synovial fluid should also be sent for Gram stain and culture in every case, since septic arthritis can closely mimic an acute gout flare and the two conditions can even coexist in the same joint.", "case_id": "case_msk_gout", "case_text": "A 60-year-old man with a history of recurrent podagra and untreated hyperuricemia presents to the emergency department with an acutely swollen, hot, and exquisitely painful right knee that began abruptly overnight. He denies fever, recent trauma, or antibiotic use. Vital signs are within normal limits. Arthrocentesis of the knee is performed for diagnostic and therapeutic purposes.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 446, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "Synovial fluid analysis in this patient confirms the diagnosis of acute gout, and Gram stain and culture are pending but so far negative. His creatinine and complete blood count are within normal limits, and he has no known drug allergies that would limit his treatment options. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate management for this acute flare? He has no known drug allergies that would limit his treatment options for this acute presentation of joint pain, and the treating physician is selecting initial therapy.", "options": {"A": "Immediate initiation of high-dose allopurinol to rapidly lower serum uric acid, which some clinicians consider in patients with comparable presentations", "B": "Initiation of febuxostat as first-line therapy for the acute attack, an approach sometimes used in similar clinical situations", "C": "Observation alone, since acute gout flares do not benefit from pharmacologic treatment", "D": "NSAIDs, colchicine, or corticosteroids, chosen based on renal function and comorbidities, without starting or adjusting urate-lowering therapy"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (NSAIDs, colchicine, or corticosteroids, chosen based on renal function and comorbidities, without starting or adjusting urate-lowering therapy): Acute gout flares are treated with NSAIDs, colchicine, or corticosteroids, with the choice guided by the patient's renal function and comorbidities. Urate-lowering therapy such as allopurinol or febuxostat should never be started or adjusted during an acute attack, since a change in serum urate concentration at this time can prolong or worsen the flare.", "case_id": "case_msk_gout", "case_text": "A 60-year-old man with a history of recurrent podagra and untreated hyperuricemia presents to the emergency department with an acutely swollen, hot, and exquisitely painful right knee that began abruptly overnight. He denies fever, recent trauma, or antibiotic use. Vital signs are within normal limits. Arthrocentesis of the knee is performed for diagnostic and therapeutic purposes.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 447, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "Three months later, after resolution of the acute flare, this patient has now had three gout attacks within the past year and is found to have a subcutaneous tophus over his elbow. He has no history of kidney stones or significant renal impairment, and his most recent serum uric acid level remains elevated despite dietary modification. Which of the following is the most appropriate long-term management approach? His most recent serum uric acid level remains elevated despite dietary modification over the past several months, and his physician is now discussing long-term prevention strategies.", "options": {"A": "Continue as-needed NSAIDs only, reserving urate-lowering therapy for patients with kidney disease alone, an option that is sometimes discussed for patients with similar findings", "B": "Start allopurinol immediately during the next acute flare to shorten its duration, a strategy that is occasionally favored in related clinical scenarios", "C": "Recommend surgical excision of the tophus as the only definitive treatment, without addressing serum urate", "D": "Begin urate-lowering therapy, most often allopurinol, once the acute flare has resolved, typically covered with several months of low-dose colchicine or an NSAID"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Begin urate-lowering therapy, most often allopurinol, once the acute flare has resolved, typically covered with several months of low-dose colchicine or an NSAID): Recurrent gout attacks and the presence of tophi are indications for chronic urate-lowering therapy, most often with allopurinol, a xanthine oxidase inhibitor. This should be started only after an acute flare has fully resolved, and low-dose colchicine or an NSAID is typically continued for several months afterward to prevent a flare triggered by the change in serum urate levels that urate-lowering therapy itself causes.", "case_id": "case_msk_gout", "case_text": "A 60-year-old man with a history of recurrent podagra and untreated hyperuricemia presents to the emergency department with an acutely swollen, hot, and exquisitely painful right knee that began abruptly overnight. He denies fever, recent trauma, or antibiotic use. Vital signs are within normal limits. Arthrocentesis of the knee is performed for diagnostic and therapeutic purposes.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 449, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A patient with gout and a history of a prior myocardial infarction is being considered for febuxostat after developing an allergic reaction to allopurinol. He has well-controlled hypertension and hyperlipidemia in addition to his cardiac history, and his most recent uric acid level remains above goal despite dietary changes. Which of the following should be discussed with the patient before starting this medication? His most recent uric acid level remains above goal despite dietary changes made several months ago, and he is now asking about alternative medication options given his prior reaction.", "options": {"A": "Febuxostat has no cardiovascular risk and is preferred over allopurinol in all patients with heart disease", "B": "Febuxostat cannot be used in any patient with a history of gout flares, a choice occasionally made for patients presenting in a similar fashion", "C": "Febuxostat carries a boxed warning for increased cardiovascular mortality in patients with established cardiovascular disease", "D": "Febuxostat requires the same renal dose adjustment as colchicine, which remains a consideration in some analogous clinical presentations"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Febuxostat carries a boxed warning for increased cardiovascular mortality in patients with established cardiovascular disease): Febuxostat is an alternative xanthine oxidase inhibitor for patients who cannot tolerate allopurinol, but it carries a boxed warning for increased cardiovascular mortality in patients with pre-existing cardiovascular disease. This risk should be weighed carefully and discussed with the patient, particularly given his history of myocardial infarction, before starting therapy."}, {"id": 450, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A patient with a classic presentation of podagra is found to have a serum uric acid level within the normal range during the acute attack. He has classic podagra with warmth, erythema, and severe tenderness of the joint, and arthrocentesis is planned to confirm the diagnosis given this unexpected laboratory finding. Which of the following best explains this finding? Arthrocentesis is planned to confirm the diagnosis given this unexpected laboratory finding noted on his chart, since his physician wants to avoid relying on the serum level alone.", "options": {"A": "A normal uric acid level during an acute attack often lowers suspicion for gout as the diagnosis", "B": "This finding indicates the patient instead has pseudogout rather than gout, a step that some clinicians take in comparable situations", "C": "Serum uric acid is an unreliable marker during an acute attack and can be normal or even fall during a flare", "D": "Serum uric acid typically rises acutely during a gout flare and this result suggests a laboratory error"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Serum uric acid is an unreliable marker during an acute attack and can be normal or even fall during a flare): Serum uric acid level is an unreliable marker of an acute gout flare, since it can be normal or can even decrease during an attack due to urate deposition into inflamed tissue and cytokine-mediated renal excretion changes. Diagnosis of an acute flare should rely on the clinical presentation and, when needed, synovial fluid crystal analysis rather than the serum uric acid level alone."}, {"id": 452, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A rheumatology fellow is asked to summarize the key laboratory distinction between gout and pseudogout on synovial fluid analysis under polarized light microscopy. Both crystal types are of similar overall size on microscopy, so morphology and the direction of birefringence under a red compensator filter are the key distinguishing features used in the laboratory. Which of the following is the correct distinction? The direction of birefringence under a red compensator filter are the key distinguishing features used in the laboratory to tell them apart, since both crystal types can appear grossly similar without polarized light.", "options": {"A": "Gout crystals are needle-shaped and negatively birefringent, while pseudogout crystals are rhomboid and positively birefringent", "B": "Both conditions produce identical needle-shaped, negatively birefringent crystals and can only be distinguished by clinical history", "C": "Gout crystals are rhomboid and positively birefringent, while pseudogout crystals are needle-shaped and negatively birefringent", "D": "Neither condition can be reliably distinguished by crystal morphology under polarized light"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Gout crystals are needle-shaped and negatively birefringent, while pseudogout crystals are rhomboid and positively birefringent): The key distinguishing feature between these two crystal arthropathies is that monosodium urate crystals in gout are needle-shaped and negatively birefringent, appearing yellow when parallel to the axis of the red compensator, while calcium pyrophosphate crystals in pseudogout are rhomboid-shaped and positively birefringent, appearing blue in the same orientation."}, {"id": 454, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A 68-year-old woman undergoes DEXA scanning and is found to have a T-score of negative 2.8 at the femoral neck. She has no history of prior fragility fracture, and she is otherwise healthy aside from being three years postmenopausal, with no chronic corticosteroid use. Which of the following is the correct interpretation of this result? She is otherwise healthy aside from being three years postmenopausal, with no chronic corticosteroid use or other secondary risk factors identified on her intake history.", "options": {"A": "This result meets the World Health Organization criteria for osteoporosis, since it is at or below negative 2.5", "B": "This result is consistent with osteopenia, not osteoporosis, an approach sometimes used in similar clinical situations", "C": "This result is within the normal range for a woman of her age, which some clinicians consider in patients with comparable presentations", "D": "T-scores below negative 2.0 have no established clinical significance"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (This result meets the World Health Organization criteria for osteoporosis, since it is at or below negative 2.5): The World Health Organization defines osteoporosis as a T-score of negative 2.5 or lower at the hip or spine. A T-score of negative 2.8 therefore meets diagnostic criteria for osteoporosis, distinct from osteopenia, which is defined by a T-score between negative 1.0 and negative 2.5."}, {"id": 455, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A 58-year-old woman undergoes DEXA scanning and is found to have a T-score of negative 1.8 at the lumbar spine. She has no prior fractures and takes no medications known to affect bone density, and she is now asking what this result means for her fracture risk. Which of the following best classifies this result? She is now asking what this result means for her fracture risk going forward, and whether medication is indicated at this stage of bone loss.", "options": {"A": "Osteoporosis, since any T-score below negative 1.0 meets this definition", "B": "Normal bone density, since the T-score is above negative 2.0", "C": "Severe osteoporosis with an established fragility fracture", "D": "Osteopenia, since the T-score falls between negative 1.0 and negative 2.5"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Osteopenia, since the T-score falls between negative 1.0 and negative 2.5): A T-score between negative 1.0 and negative 2.5 is classified as osteopenia, a category of reduced bone density that is not yet severe enough to meet the World Health Organization threshold for osteoporosis, which requires a T-score of negative 2.5 or lower. Osteopenia still warrants attention to modifiable risk factors and calcium and vitamin D intake."}, {"id": 457, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A patient who has been on oral bisphosphonate therapy for osteoporosis for over seven years asks about long-term risks of continued use. He has tolerated the medication well without gastrointestinal side effects, and his most recent DEXA scan shows meaningful improvement in bone density compared with baseline. Which of the following is an appropriate response? His most recent DEXA scan shows meaningful improvement in bone density compared with his baseline scan five years earlier, and he asks whether he can safely stop the medication now.", "options": {"A": "Long-term bisphosphonate use is generally thought to carry relatively few risks, and continuing without further reassessment is a reasonable approach for many patients.", "B": "Long-term use carries a small but real risk of atypical femoral fractures and osteonecrosis of the jaw", "C": "Bisphosphonates are often recommended to be stopped after about one year, an approach that would apply fairly broadly across differing levels of fracture risk.", "D": "Long-term use is thought to reduce the ongoing importance of maintaining adequate calcium and vitamin D intake."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Long-term use carries a small but real risk of atypical femoral fractures and osteonecrosis of the jaw): While bisphosphonates reduce fracture risk overall, long-term use carries a small but real risk of atypical femoral fractures and osteonecrosis of the jaw. Many patients are considered for a drug holiday after several years of therapy, with the decision individualized based on ongoing fracture risk, though calcium and vitamin D intake should continue regardless."}, {"id": 459, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A 55-year-old man with new onset low back pain also reports unintentional weight loss over the past two months and has a history of prostate cancer. He has no fever, saddle anesthesia, or bowel or bladder dysfunction, and straight leg raise testing is negative, but his oncologist is concerned about the combination of findings present. Which of the following features in this presentation is a red flag warranting further evaluation rather than presumed mechanical back pain? His oncologist is concerned about the combination of findings present in this patient, given his underlying malignancy history, and orders further evaluation despite the reassuring examination findings.", "options": {"A": "His age alone, since younger patients rarely have serious back pain", "B": "The combination of a cancer history and unexplained weight loss alone", "C": "The absence of radiation of pain down the leg", "D": "The fact that his pain is lumbar rather than thoracic"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The combination of a cancer history and unexplained weight loss alone): Red flag features for low back pain that warrant further evaluation, often with imaging, include age over fifty with new onset pain, a history of cancer, unexplained weight loss, fever, intravenous drug use, prolonged corticosteroid use, significant trauma, and progressive neurologic deficit. This patient's combination of a cancer history and unexplained weight loss, together with new onset pain after age fifty, raises concern for a possible metastatic or other serious underlying cause even without leg radiation or neurologic deficit."}, {"id": 463, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A 38-year-old man reports low back pain radiating down the posterior aspect of his left leg to below the knee. On examination, raising the affected leg while it is kept straight reproduces this radiating pain when the leg is elevated to approximately forty-five degrees. He has no history of trauma, fever, or bowel or bladder dysfunction, and his straight leg raise test is being performed as part of the standard examination. Which of the following best describes this examination finding and its usual underlying cause?", "options": {"A": "A negative straight leg raise test, most consistent with pure mechanical back strain", "B": "A positive Trendelenburg sign, indicating hip abductor weakness, which is sometimes seen in patients with a similar history", "C": "A positive straight leg raise test, supporting radicular pain most commonly from a herniated disc at the L4-L5 or L5-S1 level", "D": "A positive FABER test, indicating sacroiliac joint pathology, a step that some clinicians take in comparable situations"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A positive straight leg raise test, supporting radicular pain most commonly from a herniated disc at the L4-L5 or L5-S1 level): A straight leg raise test that reproduces radicular pain radiating below the knee when the leg is elevated between thirty and seventy degrees is considered positive and supports lumbar nerve root compression, most commonly from a herniated disc at the L4-L5 or L5-S1 level. This finding helps distinguish radicular sciatica from nonspecific mechanical low back pain."}, {"id": 464, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A patient with suspected L5 radiculopathy is examined for a specific motor finding that would support this level of nerve root involvement, distinct from an S1 radiculopathy. Both patterns are being reviewed together as part of a teaching exercise on localizing lumbar nerve root compression based on motor and reflex findings alone. Which of the following findings would be most consistent with an L5 radiculopathy rather than S1? Both patterns are being reviewed together as part of a teaching exercise on localizing lumbar nerve root compression based on motor and reflex findings alone, without relying on advanced imaging.", "options": {"A": "Weakness of great toe and foot dorsiflexion", "B": "Diminished or absent ankle reflex", "C": "Weakness of knee extension, an approach sometimes used in similar clinical situations", "D": "Diminished patellar reflex, which some clinicians consider in patients with comparable presentations"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Weakness of great toe and foot dorsiflexion): Weakness of great toe and foot dorsiflexion is characteristic of an L5 radiculopathy, while a diminished or absent ankle reflex is more characteristic of an S1 radiculopathy, since the ankle reflex is mediated primarily through the S1 nerve root. Recognizing these distinct motor and reflex patterns helps localize the level of nerve root compression on examination."}, {"id": 465, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A 52-year-old man with poorly controlled diabetes presents with two days of a warm, tender, poorly demarcated area of erythema and swelling over his lower leg, with a small area of tinea pedis noted between his toes as a likely portal of entry. He is afebrile and appears nontoxic. His temperature is 37.4 degrees Celsius, and his blood glucose on arrival is 210 mg/dL, though he has no chills, rigors, or other systemic symptoms. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Necrotizing fasciitis", "B": "DVT", "C": "Contact dermatitis", "D": "Cellulitis"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Cellulitis): Poorly demarcated erythema, warmth, tenderness, and edema, often with an identifiable portal of entry such as tinea pedis, are characteristic of cellulitis, a bacterial infection of the dermis and subcutaneous tissue most often caused by Streptococcus pyogenes or Staphylococcus aureus. The absence of systemic toxicity or findings such as bullae or crepitus makes necrotizing fasciitis unlikely at this stage."}, {"id": 466, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A patient with uncomplicated cellulitis of the lower leg has no purulent drainage, no abscess on examination, and no specific risk factors for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus. He has no history of MRSA infection, intravenous drug use, or recent hospitalization, and his vital signs are stable aside from mild localized warmth. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial antibiotic approach? His vital signs are stable aside from mild localized warmth over the affected area of skin, and his physician is selecting an appropriate initial oral antibiotic regimen.", "options": {"A": "No antibiotics are needed, since cellulitis typically resolves without treatment", "B": "Intravenous vancomycin as an initial treatment option for this presentation, sometimes considered when clinical severity is difficult to fully assess", "C": "An oral antibiotic targeting streptococcal species, without empiric MRSA coverage in the absence of purulence or specific risk factors", "D": "Topical mupirocin applied to the affected area alone, a strategy that is occasionally favored in related clinical scenarios"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (An oral antibiotic targeting streptococcal species, without empiric MRSA coverage in the absence of purulence or specific risk factors): Uncomplicated cellulitis without purulent drainage or abscess is most often streptococcal in origin, so an oral antibiotic targeting streptococci is appropriate first-line therapy. Coverage for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus is added when specific risk factors are present, such as a history of MRSA infection, purulent drainage, or an abscess, none of which are present in this patient."}, {"id": 468, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "On examination of a psoriatic plaque, a clinician gently removes an adherent scale and notes several pinpoint areas of bleeding from the underlying skin. The surrounding plaque has the typical well-demarcated, silvery-scaled appearance described earlier, and the clinician is teaching a medical student about characteristic examination findings in this condition. Which of the following terms describes this finding, and what does it reflect? The clinician is teaching a medical student about characteristic examination findings in this chronic inflammatory skin condition, using this specific finding as a teaching point during rounds.", "options": {"A": "The Nikolsky sign, reflecting epidermal detachment from the basement membrane", "B": "The Koebner phenomenon, reflecting new lesions at sites of skin trauma", "C": "The Darier sign, reflecting mast cell degranulation with local urtication", "D": "The Auspitz sign, reflecting the thinned epidermis over elongated dermal papillae"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The Auspitz sign, reflecting the thinned epidermis over elongated dermal papillae): The Auspitz sign, pinpoint bleeding when a psoriatic scale is removed, reflects the thinned suprapapillary epidermis overlying elongated, tortuous dermal papillae characteristic of psoriatic plaques. It is a classic, though not always elicited, clinical finding that supports the diagnosis of psoriasis."}, {"id": 469, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A 44-year-old woman with a longstanding history of plaque psoriasis develops new pitting and separation of several fingernails from their nail beds. She has no current joint pain, swelling, or morning stiffness, though she is concerned after reading that skin and nail findings can sometimes be linked to joint disease. Which of the following statements about this finding is most accurate? She is concerned after reading that skin and nail findings can sometimes be linked to joint disease in patients like her, and asks her dermatologist whether this warrants further evaluation.", "options": {"A": "Nail changes in psoriasis carry little meaningful association with the later development of joint disease", "B": "These nail findings most likely indicate a separate fungal infection rather than a manifestation of her underlying psoriasis", "C": "Nail pitting and onycholysis are common in psoriasis and can precede the development of psoriatic arthritis", "D": "Nail involvement is typically described in guttate psoriasis and is uncommon in the plaque subtype she has"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Nail pitting and onycholysis are common in psoriasis and can precede the development of psoriatic arthritis): Nail changes including pitting, onycholysis, and oil-drop discoloration are common in psoriasis and can precede the development of joint involvement, since a meaningful proportion of patients with psoriasis eventually develop psoriatic arthritis. Recognizing nail involvement can therefore prompt closer monitoring for emerging joint symptoms such as dactylitis or enthesitis."}, {"id": 470, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A 6-year-old boy with a personal history of asthma and a family history of allergic rhinitis presents with intensely pruritic, erythematous, scaly patches in the antecubital and popliteal fossae that have waxed and waned over the past several years. His mother reports that the rash has waxed and waned since infancy, worsening with dry winter weather and improving somewhat with regular use of emollients. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis? His rash has waxed and waned since infancy, worsening with dry winter weather and improving somewhat with regular use of emollients recommended by his pediatrician over the years.", "options": {"A": "Contact dermatitis", "B": "Tinea corporis", "C": "Psoriasis vulgaris", "D": "Atopic dermatitis"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Atopic dermatitis): A chronic, relapsing, intensely pruritic rash favoring the flexural creases in an older child, occurring in the setting of a personal or family history of asthma or allergic rhinitis, the atopic triad, is characteristic of atopic dermatitis. In infants the distribution is typically different, favoring the face and extensor surfaces rather than the flexural creases."}, {"id": 472, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A patient presenting with apparent cellulitis is noted to have pain markedly out of proportion to the visible skin findings, along with rapidly progressive erythema, several hemorrhagic bullae, and palpable crepitus on examination of the affected limb. His temperature is 39.1 degrees Celsius, heart rate is 124, and blood pressure is 92/58, and the surrounding skin has developed several dark, dusky patches over the past several hours. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step? The surrounding skin has developed several dark, dusky patches over the past several hours since his last assessment, and the covering physician is reassessing the situation urgently.", "options": {"A": "Emergent surgical consultation for possible necrotizing fasciitis, since debridement is both diagnostic and therapeutic", "B": "Outpatient oral antibiotics with close follow-up in 48 hours, a strategy that is occasionally favored in related clinical scenarios", "C": "Observation with serial examinations over the next 24 hours before further action", "D": "Topical antibiotic ointment applied to the affected area, which some clinicians consider in patients with comparable presentations"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Emergent surgical consultation for possible necrotizing fasciitis, since debridement is both diagnostic and therapeutic): Pain out of proportion to visible findings, rapid progression, bullae, skin necrosis, and crepitus from gas-forming organisms, along with systemic toxicity, are warning signs of necrotizing fasciitis. Any of these findings in a patient who otherwise appears to have simple cellulitis should prompt emergent surgical consultation, since surgical debridement is both diagnostic and therapeutic, and delay dramatically increases mortality."}, {"id": 473, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A child with longstanding atopic dermatitis develops new areas of honey-colored crusting and small pustules within his usual eczematous plaques, along with worsening redness. His mother reports he has been scratching the affected areas more than usual over the past several days, and he has had no fever or other systemic symptoms. Which of the following is the most likely explanation for this change? He has had no fever or other systemic symptoms during this time, though his mother is concerned about the new appearance of the affected skin areas.", "options": {"A": "Progression of atopic dermatitis itself, unrelated to any infectious process", "B": "Secondary bacterial infection, most often with Staphylococcus aureus, given the impaired skin barrier and frequent excoriation", "C": "A new allergic contact dermatitis unrelated to his underlying eczema, which remains a consideration in some analogous clinical presentations", "D": "An adverse reaction to his topical corticosteroid, an option that is sometimes discussed for patients with similar findings"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Secondary bacterial infection, most often with Staphylococcus aureus, given the impaired skin barrier and frequent excoriation): Honey-colored crusting and pustules developing within eczematous plaques suggest secondary bacterial infection, most often with Staphylococcus aureus, which occurs commonly in atopic dermatitis given the impaired skin barrier and frequent scratching and excoriation. This finding often warrants a course of antibiotic therapy in addition to continued management of the underlying atopic dermatitis."}, {"id": 474, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A patient presents with a tender, fluctuant, well-circumscribed area of swelling with a small central area of purulent drainage on the thigh, surrounded by mild erythema. His temperature is 37.6 degrees Celsius, and he reports the area has become increasingly painful over the past two days after a minor scrape while gardening. Which of the following is the most appropriate management in addition to any indicated antibiotics? He reports the area has become increasingly painful over the past two days after a minor scrape sustained while gardening in his yard, prompting this visit for evaluation.", "options": {"A": "Incision and drainage of the fluctuant, purulent collection", "B": "Oral antibiotics alone, without any procedural intervention", "C": "Warm compresses alone, with reassessment in one week", "D": "Intravenous antibiotics without any drainage procedure"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Incision and drainage of the fluctuant, purulent collection): A fluctuant, purulent collection is consistent with a cutaneous abscess, and incision and drainage is the essential component of management, since antibiotics alone are often insufficient to resolve a walled-off purulent collection. Antibiotics may be added in addition to drainage depending on the size of the abscess, surrounding cellulitis, and patient risk factors, but drainage itself should not be omitted."}, {"id": 475, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A 56-year-old man with poorly controlled diabetes presents with one day of warm, tender, poorly demarcated erythema over his lower leg following a minor abrasion sustained while gardening, as part of the case described above. His temperature is 37.2 degrees Celsius and his blood glucose is 240 mg/dL on arrival, and he has no bullae, crepitus, or pain out of proportion to examination findings at this initial visit. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis at this initial presentation?", "options": {"A": "Cellulitis", "B": "Necrotizing fasciitis", "C": "Deep venous thrombosis", "D": "Contact dermatitis"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Cellulitis): This presentation, poorly demarcated erythema, warmth, and tenderness following a portal of entry such as a minor abrasion, without bullae, crepitus, or pain out of proportion to examination findings, is consistent with uncomplicated cellulitis. Diabetes is a recognized risk factor for cellulitis and warrants close monitoring for progression given impaired wound healing and immune response.", "case_id": "case_msk_cellulitis", "case_text": "A 56-year-old man with poorly controlled diabetes presents with one day of warmth, tenderness, and redness over his lower leg following a minor abrasion sustained while gardening. He is afebrile and appears nontoxic on initial evaluation, and oral antibiotics are started for presumed cellulitis. He returns to the emergency department twelve hours later with worsening symptoms.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 476, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "Twelve hours after starting oral antibiotics, the patient returns with rapidly worsening pain that is now markedly out of proportion to the visible skin findings, along with new hemorrhagic bullae and palpable crepitus over the affected leg. He appears ill with a heart rate of 128 and blood pressure of 88/54. His temperature has risen to 39.4 degrees Celsius, and the skin surrounding the original area of erythema has developed a dusky, mottled discoloration not present on his prior visit. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Emergent surgical consultation for debridement, since this presentation is now concerning for necrotizing fasciitis", "B": "Continue the current oral antibiotic regimen and reassess in 24 hours, a choice occasionally made for patients presenting in a similar fashion", "C": "Switch to a different oral antibiotic with broader coverage and continue outpatient management", "D": "Discharge home with close primary care follow-up given his stable vital signs on initial presentation"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Emergent surgical consultation for debridement, since this presentation is now concerning for necrotizing fasciitis): The development of pain out of proportion to examination findings, hemorrhagic bullae, crepitus, and systemic toxicity with hypotension and tachycardia represents progression to a presentation concerning for necrotizing fasciitis. This requires emergent surgical consultation, since surgical debridement is both diagnostic and therapeutic and delay significantly increases mortality in this condition.", "case_id": "case_msk_cellulitis", "case_text": "A 56-year-old man with poorly controlled diabetes presents with one day of warmth, tenderness, and redness over his lower leg following a minor abrasion sustained while gardening. He is afebrile and appears nontoxic on initial evaluation, and oral antibiotics are started for presumed cellulitis. He returns to the emergency department twelve hours later with worsening symptoms.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 477, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "The patient is taken emergently to the operating room, where gray, necrotic fascia with minimal bleeding and a thin, foul-smelling exudate are found extending well beyond the visible skin changes. Intraoperative cultures are sent, and the surgical team plans to reassess the wound in the operating room again within twenty-four hours given the extent of tissue involvement discovered. Which of the following is the most appropriate definitive treatment at this point? The surgical team plans to reassess the wound in the operating room again within twenty-four hours given the extent of tissue involvement discovered during the initial procedure performed emergently.", "options": {"A": "Continued intravenous antibiotics alone, without surgical intervention", "B": "Discharge with a PICC line for home antibiotics", "C": "Hyperbaric oxygen therapy as the sole definitive treatment", "D": "Aggressive surgical debridement of all necrotic tissue"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Aggressive surgical debridement of all necrotic tissue): The intraoperative findings of necrotic fascia extending beyond the visible skin changes confirm necrotizing fasciitis. Definitive treatment is aggressive surgical debridement of all necrotic tissue, often requiring multiple return trips to the operating room, with broad-spectrum antibiotics serving as an important adjunct rather than a substitute for surgery, since antibiotics alone cannot penetrate poorly perfused necrotic tissue.", "case_id": "case_msk_cellulitis", "case_text": "A 56-year-old man with poorly controlled diabetes presents with one day of warmth, tenderness, and redness over his lower leg following a minor abrasion sustained while gardening. He is afebrile and appears nontoxic on initial evaluation, and oral antibiotics are started for presumed cellulitis. He returns to the emergency department twelve hours later with worsening symptoms.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 478, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A 70-year-old man with a history of significant lifetime sun exposure presents with a slowly enlarging, pearly, translucent papule on his nose with visible small blood vessels coursing over its rolled border. There is no history of rapid growth or bleeding. He has no history of prior skin cancer, and the lesion has been present and slowly enlarging for approximately eighteen months without any associated pain or tenderness. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis? The lesion has been present and slowly enlarging for approximately eighteen months without any associated pain, tenderness, or bleeding noted by the patient during this time.", "options": {"A": "Cutaneous malignant melanoma", "B": "Basal cell carcinoma", "C": "Seborrheic keratosis lesion", "D": "Squamous cell carcinoma"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Basal cell carcinoma): A pearly, translucent papule with rolled, telangiectatic borders, sometimes with central ulceration, arising in a sun-exposed area is the classic description of basal cell carcinoma, the most common skin cancer. It typically grows slowly and rarely metastasizes, though it can cause significant local tissue destruction if left untreated."}, {"id": 479, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A 66-year-old farmer with years of outdoor sun exposure has a rough, scaly, erythematous patch on his forearm that has recently become a firm, hyperkeratotic plaque with associated ulceration. He has no history of immunosuppression, and the lesion has changed noticeably over the past two months, prompting him to finally seek evaluation after years of sun exposure. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis, and from what type of precursor lesion did it likely arise? The lesion has changed noticeably over the past two months, prompting him to finally seek evaluation after years of chronic outdoor sun exposure without regular dermatologic follow-up.", "options": {"A": "Basal cell carcinoma, arising from a seborrheic keratosis", "B": "Melanoma, arising from a dysplastic nevus", "C": "Squamous cell carcinoma, arising from an actinic keratosis", "D": "Kaposi sarcoma, arising from chronic lymphedema"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Squamous cell carcinoma, arising from an actinic keratosis): Squamous cell carcinoma often develops from a precursor lesion called actinic keratosis, a rough, scaly, erythematous patch on chronically sun-exposed skin. Progression to a firm, hyperkeratotic, ulcerated plaque, as described here, is consistent with this evolution, and squamous cell carcinoma carries a real, though still modest, risk of metastasis compared with basal cell carcinoma."}, {"id": 483, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A 45-year-old man sustains a burn to his forearm from a splash of hot cooking oil. On examination the area is pale and mottled in appearance with notably decreased sensation to light touch compared with surrounding uninjured skin. He reports the area is far less painful than he would expect given its appearance, and capillary refill in the affected skin is notably sluggish compared with adjacent unburned skin. Which of the following burn classifications is most consistent with these findings?", "options": {"A": "Superficial burn, involving only the epidermis, an approach sometimes used in similar clinical situations", "B": "Superficial partial-thickness burn, with brisk capillary refill and intact sensation", "C": "Deep partial-thickness burn, extending into the reticular dermis with destruction of many nerve endings", "D": "Full-thickness burn, with a leathery, charred appearance, which some clinicians consider in patients with comparable presentations"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Deep partial-thickness burn, extending into the reticular dermis with destruction of many nerve endings): A pale, mottled appearance with decreased sensation reflects a deep partial-thickness burn, which extends further into the reticular dermis than a superficial partial-thickness burn and destroys more nerve endings, explaining the reduced sensation. These burns are more likely than superficial partial-thickness burns to require surgical grafting for optimal healing."}, {"id": 490, "domain": "musculoskeletal_system_and_skin", "question": "A 70-kilogram man sustains partial-thickness and full-thickness burns covering 40 percent of his total body surface area after being trapped briefly in a burning vehicle. His blood pressure is 108/68 and heart rate is 118, and the burn team is calculating his fluid resuscitation requirements according to standard protocol upon arrival to the burn unit. Using the Parkland formula, which of the following most closely approximates the total intravenous fluid volume to be administered over the first 24 hours, and how should it be distributed?", "options": {"A": "5,600 mL total, given over the first 8 hours as a lower estimate of his fluid needs", "B": "2,800 mL total, with half given over the first 24 hours and the remainder the next day", "C": "11,200 mL total, given as a single continuous infusion at a constant rate over 24 hours", "D": "11,200 mL total, with half given over the first 8 hours and the remainder over the following 16 hours"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (11,200 mL total, with half given over the first 8 hours and the remainder over the following 16 hours): The Parkland formula calculates total fluid over the first twenty-four hours as 4 mL times body weight in kilograms times percent total body surface area burned, which here is 4 times 70 times 40, equal to 11,200 mL. Half of this volume, 5,600 mL, is given in the first eight hours from the time of injury, with the remaining 5,600 mL given over the following sixteen hours, rather than as a constant rate across the full 24 hours."}, {"id": 491, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 34-year-old woman presents to her primary care physician with a 3-month history of feeling sad most days, sleeping 10 to 11 hours nightly yet still feeling tired, eating more than usual with a 6-lb weight gain, and losing interest in activities she used to enjoy. She denies suicidal ideation. She reports these symptoms have been present nearly every day for the past 3 months and are affecting her performance at work. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial pharmacologic treatment?", "options": {"A": "Sertraline", "B": "Alprazolam", "C": "Haloperidol", "D": "Lithium carbonate"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Sertraline): This patient meets criteria for major depressive disorder, with depressed mood, hypersomnia, hyperphagia, and anhedonia present for more than 2 weeks and causing functional impairment. SSRIs such as sertraline are first-line pharmacotherapy for moderate depression. Alprazolam treats anxiety, not depression, and carries dependence risk. Haloperidol is an antipsychotic with no role here. Lithium is a mood stabilizer used for bipolar disorder, not unipolar MDD."}, {"id": 494, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 52-year-old man with bipolar I disorder maintained on lithium presents with new-onset coarse tremor, ataxia, and confusion after starting ibuprofen for knee pain 1 week ago. His lithium level is 2.1 mEq/L (therapeutic range 0.6-1.2). He reports adequate oral intake and no vomiting or diarrhea preceding these symptoms. His most recent lithium level, checked 2 months ago before starting ibuprofen, was 0.9 mEq/L and he had no symptoms at that time. Which of the following best explains the mechanism of this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Lithium-induced nephrogenic diabetes insipidus", "B": "NSAID-induced hepatic enzyme induction increasing lithium metabolism", "C": "NSAID-induced reduction in renal lithium clearance", "D": "Ibuprofen displacing lithium from plasma protein binding"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (NSAID-induced reduction in renal lithium clearance): NSAIDs reduce renal prostaglandin synthesis, which decreases renal blood flow and glomerular filtration, thereby reducing lithium clearance and raising serum lithium to toxic levels. This produces the classic toxicity triad of coarse tremor, ataxia, and confusion. Lithium can cause nephrogenic diabetes insipidus over time, but that would not explain an acute toxic level after starting an NSAID. Lithium is renally cleared, not hepatically metabolized, so hepatic enzyme induction is irrelevant. Lithium has negligible plasma protein binding, so displacement is not a mechanism."}, {"id": 495, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 19-year-old college student presents with a 6-month history of excessive worry about school performance, finances, and her family's health that she finds difficult to control. She reports associated muscle tension, restlessness, fatigue, and difficulty falling asleep most nights. She has no history of panic attacks or discrete triggering situations. She reports these worries are present most days and are difficult to set aside even when she tries to distract herself. She denies any recent major life stressor, alcohol or drug use, and her physical examination and basic laboratory studies, including thyroid function, are unremarkable. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Generalized anxiety disorder", "B": "Panic disorder", "C": "Social anxiety disorder", "D": "Adjustment disorder with anxiety"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Generalized anxiety disorder): Excessive, difficult-to-control worry about multiple life domains for at least 6 months with associated restlessness, fatigue, muscle tension, and sleep disturbance defines GAD; this patient meets that 6-month threshold with the core GAD symptom cluster. Panic disorder requires recurrent unexpected panic attacks, which are absent here. Social anxiety disorder requires fear specific to social or performance situations, not generalized worry. Adjustment disorder requires a clear identifiable stressor within the preceding 3 months, which is not described."}, {"id": 496, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 26-year-old man describes recurrent, unexpected episodes over the past 2 months in which he suddenly feels his heart racing, becomes short of breath, sweats profusely, and feels an overwhelming sense of impending doom, peaking within about 5 minutes and resolving over the following 20 minutes. Between episodes he reports persistent worry about having another attack and has started avoiding the subway where his first episode occurred. He denies any cardiac history, and an electrocardiogram obtained during a prior episode in an urgent care visit showed sinus tachycardia with no ischemic changes. He has no other medical problems and takes no daily medications. Which of the following is the most appropriate first-line treatment?", "options": {"A": "Long-term daily alprazolam use for maintenance", "B": "Cognitive behavioral therapy and an SSRI", "C": "Haloperidol as needed during acute episodes", "D": "Propranolol monotherapy alone for his palpitations"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cognitive behavioral therapy and an SSRI): This vignette describes panic disorder, recurrent unexpected panic attacks with at least 1 month of anticipatory anxiety and avoidance behavior. First-line treatment is CBT combined with an SSRI or SNRI. Long-term daily benzodiazepine use is not first-line because of dependence and sedation risk, though a benzodiazepine may be used briefly for acute symptom relief. Haloperidol has no role in panic disorder, which is not a psychotic illness. Propranolol can help situational physical symptoms of anxiety but is not first-line monotherapy for panic disorder."}, {"id": 498, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 30-year-old combat veteran presents 8 months after returning from deployment with recurrent intrusive memories of a firefight, nightmares, avoidance of news coverage about the conflict, feelings of detachment from his family, and a heightened startle response to loud noises. Symptoms have persisted since he returned and impair his ability to work. He has been unable to return to his previous job and has become increasingly withdrawn from friends and family since returning from deployment. He denies current suicidal ideation but admits to occasional intrusive thoughts about the event that occur without warning. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Acute stress disorder", "B": "Posttraumatic stress disorder", "C": "Adjustment disorder with anxious mood", "D": "Generalized anxiety disorder"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Posttraumatic stress disorder): Exposure to a traumatic event followed by intrusion symptoms, avoidance, negative alterations in mood or cognition, and hyperarousal persisting beyond 1 month defines PTSD; this patient's symptoms have been present for 8 months. Acute stress disorder is diagnosed when the identical symptom picture lasts between 3 days and 1 month, which has been exceeded here. Adjustment disorder does not require the specific intrusion and hyperarousal symptom clusters tied to a life-threatening trauma. Generalized anxiety disorder does not require an inciting traumatic event or the intrusion/avoidance symptom pattern described."}, {"id": 499, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 21-year-old man avoids all public speaking, eating in front of others, and using public restrooms because he is intensely afraid of being embarrassed or judged, a pattern present since adolescence and causing him to decline promotions at work. He denies fear of open spaces or crowds themselves, only fear of scrutiny within them. He reports that in these situations he experiences a racing heart, blushing, and trembling, which further increases his fear of being noticed by others. He has never had a full panic attack outside of these specific social situations and denies fear of enclosed spaces or crowds themselves. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Social anxiety disorder", "B": "Agoraphobia without significant social component", "C": "Underlying avoidant personality disorder", "D": "An isolated specific phobia"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Social anxiety disorder): Marked, persistent fear of social or performance situations in which the person may be scrutinized or embarrassed, present for at least 6 months and causing functional impairment, defines social anxiety disorder. Agoraphobia centers on fear that escape would be difficult if panic-like symptoms occurred, not fear of judgment. Avoidant personality disorder is a pervasive, enduring pattern across the entire personality rather than a discrete anxiety disorder limited to social/performance contexts, though the two can be distinguished mainly by pervasiveness and are sometimes comorbid. Specific phobia involves fear of a single discrete object or situation, not the broad range of social contexts described here."}, {"id": 500, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 22-year-old man is brought to the emergency department by police after being found shouting at strangers on the street, convinced that a government satellite is broadcasting his thoughts. His family reports he has been increasingly withdrawn, has stopped bathing, and has been talking to himself for the past 4 months. He has a flat affect and disorganized speech on exam. He has no prior psychiatric history and no known substance use, according to his family, and urine toxicology in the emergency department is negative. His vital signs are within normal limits, and there is no evidence of an acute medical illness on initial workup. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Brief psychotic disorder", "B": "Schizophrenia", "C": "Schizoaffective disorder", "D": "Schizophreniform disorder"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Schizophreniform disorder): Delusions, disorganized speech, and negative symptoms (flat affect) present for 4 months meet the core symptom criteria for schizophrenia, but the diagnosis of schizophrenia requires continuous signs of disturbance for at least 6 months; between 1 and 6 months, the correct diagnosis is schizophreniform disorder. Brief psychotic disorder requires resolution within 1 month, which has already been exceeded. Schizophrenia requires 6 months of continuous disturbance, not yet met at 4 months. Schizoaffective disorder requires a concurrent major mood episode, which is not described."}, {"id": 503, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 26-year-old man with schizophrenia has failed adequate trials of both risperidone and olanzapine, each given for several months at therapeutic doses with poor symptom control and continued auditory hallucinations. Both trials were given for at least 8 weeks at maximum tolerated doses with documented adherence confirmed by his family and pharmacy refill records. He continues to experience distressing auditory hallucinations that significantly impair his ability to function independently. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": {"A": "Initiate clozapine with regular absolute neutrophil count monitoring", "B": "Increase olanzapine beyond the maximum recommended dose", "C": "Add haloperidol to the current failing regimen", "D": "Discontinue all antipsychotics and begin psychotherapy alone"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Initiate clozapine with regular absolute neutrophil count monitoring): Clozapine is indicated for treatment-resistant schizophrenia after failure of at least two adequate antipsychotic trials, and its use requires mandatory regular monitoring of absolute neutrophil count because of the risk of agranulocytosis. Exceeding the maximum recommended dose of a failed agent is unlikely to help and increases side-effect risk. Adding a second first-generation antipsychotic without addressing treatment resistance is not standard practice. Discontinuing all antipsychotics in a patient with ongoing hallucinations would risk clinical deterioration; psychotherapy alone is not sufficient for treatment-resistant schizophrenia."}, {"id": 504, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 29-year-old woman has a lifelong pattern of unstable, intense relationships that alternate between idealization and devaluation, chronic feelings of emptiness, a fluctuating self-image, and recurrent episodes of superficial wrist cutting when she feels her boyfriend might leave her. She has had this pattern since her late teens. She reports that her relationships often begin with idealizing new partners as \"perfect\" before quickly becoming convinced they are entirely uncaring, and she describes her self-image as changing dramatically depending on who she is with. This pattern has been consistent since age 17 and has led to two prior psychiatric hospitalizations after similar episodes of self-harm. Which of the following is the most effective evidence-based treatment for her core condition?", "options": {"A": "Clozapine", "B": "Dialectical behavior therapy", "C": "Exposure and response prevention", "D": "Lithium monotherapy"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Dialectical behavior therapy): The pattern of unstable relationships, identity disturbance, chronic emptiness, and recurrent self-harm in the context of fear of abandonment describes borderline personality disorder, for which dialectical behavior therapy, combining individual and group skills training in distress tolerance and emotion regulation, has the strongest evidence base. Clozapine is used for treatment-resistant schizophrenia, not personality disorder. Exposure and response prevention is the treatment for OCD, not borderline personality disorder. Lithium monotherapy is used for bipolar disorder and is not the primary treatment for this personality disorder."}, {"id": 507, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 52-year-old man with a long history of heavy daily alcohol use is hospitalized for pancreatitis. On hospital day 3, he becomes acutely confused, disoriented to place and time, and develops visual hallucinations of insects crawling on the walls, along with a heart rate of 140/min, blood pressure of 180/110 mm Hg, and temperature of 38.9C. His family reports he last had alcohol on hospital day 1, and nursing staff note he has been increasingly restless overnight with intermittent episodes of picking at his intravenous line as though removing insects. He is unable to state the correct date or location when asked. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Alcoholic hallucinosis", "B": "Delirium tremens", "C": "Wernicke encephalopathy", "D": "Simple alcohol withdrawal"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Delirium tremens): Confusion, disorientation, hallucinations, and severe autonomic instability occurring 48-96 hours after the last drink is the classic presentation of delirium tremens, the most severe form of alcohol withdrawal, which carries significant mortality if untreated and requires aggressive benzodiazepine dosing and supportive care. Alcoholic hallucinosis presents with hallucinations but a clear sensorium, without the global confusion and severe autonomic instability described here. Wernicke encephalopathy presents with the triad of confusion, ataxia, and ophthalmoplegia from thiamine deficiency, not primarily with hallucinations and this degree of autonomic instability. Simple alcohol withdrawal does not include confusion or hallucinations."}, {"id": 509, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 38-year-old woman who has taken alprazolam daily for 3 years for anxiety wants to stop the medication because she is planning a pregnancy. She has tapered other medications successfully in the past without difficulty and wants to avoid any withdrawal symptoms this time. She has no other psychiatric or medical comorbidities and takes no other daily medications. She reports her anxiety symptoms have been well controlled on this dose for the past 2 years without any breakthrough episodes, and she has no other psychiatric or medical comorbidities that would complicate a change in her regimen. Which of the following is the most appropriate approach to discontinuation?", "options": {"A": "Abrupt discontinuation is safe because benzodiazepines have no withdrawal syndrome", "B": "Immediate switch to disulfiram", "C": "Gradual taper over weeks to months", "D": "Abrupt discontinuation with as-needed naloxone available"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Gradual taper over weeks to months): Long-term benzodiazepine use can produce a withdrawal syndrome resembling alcohol withdrawal, including anxiety, tremor, autonomic instability, and seizure risk, so discontinuation should always be done through a gradual taper, sometimes with substitution of a longer-acting benzodiazepine, rather than abruptly. Abrupt discontinuation is unsafe and can precipitate seizures. Disulfiram is used for alcohol use disorder, not benzodiazepine discontinuation, and has no role here. Naloxone reverses opioid effects and has no role in benzodiazepine withdrawal."}, {"id": 510, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 24-year-old man presents to the emergency department 2 days after his last cocaine use with complaints of severe fatigue, increased appetite, vivid unpleasant dreams, and low mood, but no chest pain, agitation, or autonomic instability. He denies any chest pain, palpitations, or suicidal ideation, and his physical examination is unremarkable aside from mild diaphoresis. His vital signs are within normal limits, and he has no other acute medical complaints. His vital signs, including heart rate and blood pressure, are within normal limits aside from mild tachycardia, and he denies any chest pain, palpitations, or suicidal ideation on further questioning by the emergency physician. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?", "options": {"A": "Naloxone to reverse his symptoms", "B": "A gradual benzodiazepine taper regimen", "C": "Supportive care and monitoring", "D": "Disulfiram therapy to prevent further use"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Supportive care and monitoring): Stimulant withdrawal presents with dysphoria, fatigue, increased appetite, and vivid unpleasant dreams, which is generally managed supportively without a specific pharmacologic antidote, and this patient's vital signs and symptoms do not suggest a competing emergency. Naloxone is used for opioid overdose, which is not described. A benzodiazepine taper is used for benzodiazepine or alcohol withdrawal, not stimulant withdrawal. Disulfiram is used in alcohol use disorder and is irrelevant here."}, {"id": 513, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 23-year-old woman of normal body weight is noted to have parotid gland swelling, dental enamel erosion, and calluses on the dorsal aspect of her right hand's knuckles. Laboratory studies show potassium of 3.0 mEq/L, chloride of 88 mEq/L, and a mild metabolic alkalosis. She admits to eating large quantities of food in short periods followed by self-induced vomiting several times weekly for the past 6 months. She works as a dental hygienist, and a routine dental examination first prompted concern when her dentist noted the enamel changes. She reports feeling intense guilt and shame after these episodes but states she feels unable to stop the pattern despite wanting to. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Anorexia nervosa, binge-eating/purging type", "B": "Bulimia nervosa", "C": "Binge-eating disorder", "D": "Illness anxiety disorder"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Bulimia nervosa): Recurrent binge eating with compensatory self-induced vomiting at least weekly for 3 months, in a patient of normal body weight, with the classic findings of parotid swelling, dental erosion, knuckle calluses (Russell sign), and hypokalemic hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis from vomiting, defines bulimia nervosa. Anorexia nervosa requires a significantly low body weight, which is absent here. Binge-eating disorder lacks the compensatory purging behavior and its physical stigmata. Illness anxiety disorder involves preoccupation with having a serious illness, not an eating and purging pattern."}, {"id": 514, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 41-year-old man has seen his primary care physician monthly for 2 years with rotating complaints of headaches, abdominal pain, and joint aches. Extensive workup including imaging and laboratory testing has been repeatedly normal. He spends several hours daily researching his symptoms online and reports high anxiety that he has an undiagnosed serious illness, which he cannot set aside despite reassurance. His workup has included normal complete blood count, comprehensive metabolic panel, thyroid studies, abdominal ultrasound, and colonoscopy, all performed within the past year. He continues to schedule frequent appointments and reports that reassurance from his physician provides only brief relief before his worry returns. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Somatic symptom disorder", "B": "Malingering for an external incentive", "C": "Factitious disorder", "D": "Conversion disorder"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Somatic symptom disorder): Distressing somatic symptoms accompanied by excessive, disproportionate thoughts, anxiety, and time devoted to the symptoms for more than 6 months defines somatic symptom disorder; the symptoms need not be medically unexplained for this diagnosis. Malingering requires conscious fabrication for a clear external incentive such as disability payments, which is not described. Factitious disorder involves conscious falsification of symptoms to assume the sick role, not genuine symptom experience with excessive worry. Conversion disorder specifically involves neurological symptoms incompatible with recognized neurological disease, not the diffuse somatic complaints described here."}, {"id": 516, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 68-year-old man hospitalized for pneumonia develops acute confusion over the course of a single afternoon, fluctuating between agitation and drowsiness, with impaired attention noted on bedside testing. He was cognitively intact on admission 2 days earlier. His oxygen saturation, basic metabolic panel, and chest radiograph obtained on admission were unremarkable aside from findings consistent with his pneumonia. Nursing staff report he has been intermittently pulling at his intravenous line and does not recognize his hospital room as familiar despite having been admitted 2 days earlier. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial step in management?", "options": {"A": "Start haloperidol immediately for behavioral control", "B": "Begin donepezil for presumed new dementia", "C": "Identify and treat the underlying medical cause", "D": "Arrange transfer to inpatient psychiatry"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Identify and treat the underlying medical cause): Acute onset, fluctuating course, and impaired attention describe delirium, which is most often caused by an underlying medical condition (in this case, possibly worsening infection, hypoxia, or a metabolic derangement) or medication effect. The first step is always to identify and treat the underlying cause rather than reaching for symptomatic medication. Haloperidol may occasionally be used briefly for severe agitation that poses safety risk, but it is not the initial step and does not address the cause. Dementia has an insidious, progressive onset without the acute fluctuation described, so donepezil is not indicated. Transfer to inpatient psychiatry is inappropriate for a medically caused delirium, which should be managed on the medical service."}, {"id": 518, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 30-year-old man tells his outpatient therapist in detail about his plan to kill a specific coworker he believes has been sabotaging his career, including the method and timing he intends to use. He has attended weekly sessions for the past 6 months for work-related stress, and this is the first time he has expressed any violent ideation toward a specific individual. He appears calm while describing his plan and states he has already purchased the means to carry it out. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step for the therapist?", "options": {"A": "Maintain strict confidentiality and take no further action", "B": "Refer the patient to a support group and continue routine weekly therapy", "C": "Document the statement but wait until the next scheduled session to reassess", "D": "Warn the identified coworker and notify appropriate authorities"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Warn the identified coworker and notify appropriate authorities): A credible, specific threat toward an identifiable person creates a duty to warn or protect that person and to notify appropriate authorities, which overrides usual confidentiality protections under the Tarasoff principle. Maintaining strict confidentiality would leave an identified potential victim unprotected. Referral to a support group with routine follow-up does not address the imminent safety risk. Waiting until the next scheduled session is unsafe given the specificity and immediacy of the threat."}, {"id": 519, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 61-year-old woman with severe major depressive disorder and psychotic features (delusions that her internal organs are rotting) has failed two adequate trials of antidepressant and antipsychotic combination therapy and has stopped eating due to somatic delusions, resulting in significant weight loss. She has been hospitalized twice in the past year for similar episodes and has lost 18 lb over the past month because she believes eating will accelerate the decay she perceives in her organs. Her family reports she has become nearly mute and refuses all oral intake, including fluids, over the past 3 days. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": {"A": "A third antidepressant", "B": "Increased antipsychotic dosing", "C": "Electroconvulsive therapy", "D": "Discharge home"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Electroconvulsive therapy): ECT is indicated for severe or treatment-resistant depression, depression with psychotic features, and situations requiring rapid response such as a severely malnourished, medically compromised patient who is not eating due to delusions. It has a rapid onset of action compared to further medication trials. Adding a third antidepressant after two adequate failed trials with psychotic features is less effective and slower than ECT in this urgent scenario. Exceeding recommended antipsychotic dosing increases side-effect risk without proven added benefit. Discharge home is inappropriate given the medical urgency of ongoing food refusal and weight loss."}, {"id": 520, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 26-year-old man reports a lifelong pattern of lying, stealing from employers, and physically assaulting others without remorse, beginning with a history of animal cruelty, truancy, and fire-setting at age 12. He has been arrested multiple times as an adult. He has been unemployed for the past 2 years and relies on manipulating and deceiving family members for financial support, showing no evidence of guilt when confronted about his behavior. He was previously diagnosed with conduct disorder at age 13 following similar behaviors in adolescence. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Conduct disorder", "B": "Antisocial personality disorder", "C": "Narcissistic personality disorder", "D": "Borderline personality disorder"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Antisocial personality disorder): A pervasive pattern of disregard for and violation of the rights of others, beginning with conduct disorder before age 15 (here, animal cruelty, truancy, and fire-setting at age 12) and continuing into adulthood with lying, theft, and assault without remorse, defines antisocial personality disorder, which requires the individual to be at least 18 years old for the diagnosis. Conduct disorder is the childhood/adolescent precursor diagnosis and does not apply to this 26-year-old adult. Narcissistic personality disorder centers on grandiosity and need for admiration rather than rights violations and criminal behavior. Borderline personality disorder centers on unstable relationships and self-image rather than the persistent antisocial conduct described."}, {"id": 522, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "Based on the information above, which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Bulimia nervosa", "B": "Binge-eating disorder", "C": "Anorexia nervosa, restricting type", "D": "Major depressive disorder with weight loss"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Anorexia nervosa, restricting type): Restriction of energy intake leading to a significantly low body weight (BMI 15.3), an intense fear of weight gain despite being underweight, and disturbance in body image, without binge eating or purging behavior, defines anorexia nervosa, restricting type. Bulimia nervosa requires binge eating and compensatory behavior with typically normal body weight, neither of which is described. Binge-eating disorder involves binge episodes without the severe caloric restriction and low body weight seen here. While depression can cause weight loss, the specific fear of weight gain, body image disturbance, and degree of restriction point specifically to anorexia nervosa.", "case_id": "BHP-C1", "case_text": "A 19-year-old woman is brought to student health services by her roommate, who reports increasing concern over the past 2 months. The patient has lost 25 lb, now weighs 92 lb at a height of 5'5\" (BMI 15.3), and restricts her intake to under 600 calories daily. She runs 10 miles every day regardless of weather or injury and insists she is \"still too big.\" She has not menstruated in 4 months. Vital signs show a heart rate of 42/min, blood pressure of 82/54 mm Hg, and temperature of 35.6C.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 523, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "Which of the following findings on this patient's exam is most directly explained by her hypometabolic, starved physiologic state?", "options": {"A": "Bradycardia and hypotension", "B": "Parotid gland enlargement", "C": "Knuckle calluses from repeated gagging", "D": "Dental enamel erosion"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Bradycardia and hypotension): Severe caloric restriction produces a hypometabolic state with reduced cardiac workload, causing bradycardia and hypotension, both present in this patient. Parotid gland enlargement, knuckle calluses (Russell sign), and dental enamel erosion are physical stigmata of recurrent self-induced vomiting seen in bulimia nervosa or the purging subtype of anorexia, and none of these are described in this patient, who has the restricting subtype.", "case_id": "BHP-C1", "case_text": "A 19-year-old woman is brought to student health services by her roommate, who reports increasing concern over the past 2 months. The patient has lost 25 lb, now weighs 92 lb at a height of 5'5\" (BMI 15.3), and restricts her intake to under 600 calories daily. She runs 10 miles every day regardless of weather or injury and insists she is \"still too big.\" She has not menstruated in 4 months. Vital signs show a heart rate of 42/min, blood pressure of 82/54 mm Hg, and temperature of 35.6C.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 524, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "The patient is admitted for medical stabilization and nutritional rehabilitation is begun. Three days into hospitalization, she develops new muscle weakness, and laboratory studies reveal a phosphate level of 1.2 mg/dL (reference 2.5-4.5). Which of the following best explains this finding?", "options": {"A": "Acute renal failure resulting from severe dehydration", "B": "Primary hyperparathyroidism causing this hypophosphatemia directly", "C": "Refeeding syndrome from an insulin-driven intracellular electrolyte shift", "D": "Malabsorption from longstanding chronic laxative use"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Refeeding syndrome from an insulin-driven intracellular electrolyte shift): Reintroducing nutrition in a severely malnourished patient triggers an insulin surge that drives phosphate, potassium, and magnesium into cells, producing hypophosphatemia and associated muscle weakness, the hallmark of refeeding syndrome. This is why caloric advancement must be gradual with proactive electrolyte monitoring and repletion. Acute renal failure would present with elevated creatinine and typically hyperphosphatemia, not hypophosphatemia. Primary hyperparathyroidism causes hypercalcemia and hypophosphatemia through a different mechanism unrelated to the timing of refeeding. Laxative-induced malabsorption is not described in this vignette and does not fit the temporal relationship to refeeding.", "case_id": "BHP-C1", "case_text": "A 19-year-old woman is brought to student health services by her roommate, who reports increasing concern over the past 2 months. The patient has lost 25 lb, now weighs 92 lb at a height of 5'5\" (BMI 15.3), and restricts her intake to under 600 calories daily. She runs 10 miles every day regardless of weather or injury and insists she is \"still too big.\" She has not menstruated in 4 months. Vital signs show a heart rate of 42/min, blood pressure of 82/54 mm Hg, and temperature of 35.6C.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 525, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "Which of the following is the most appropriate strategy to reduce the risk of the complication described in the previous finding?", "options": {"A": "Administer a single large caloric bolus to correct the malnutrition rapidly", "B": "Advance caloric intake slowly with proactive electrolyte monitoring and repletion", "C": "Restrict all electrolyte supplementation until levels normalize spontaneously", "D": "Delay any nutritional support until she is discharged"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Advance caloric intake slowly with proactive electrolyte monitoring and repletion): Refeeding syndrome is prevented by advancing caloric intake gradually while proactively monitoring and repleting phosphate, potassium, and magnesium before and during refeeding, since the electrolyte shifts occur as an insulin response to reintroduced glucose. A single large caloric bolus is precisely the practice that precipitates refeeding syndrome and must be avoided. Withholding electrolyte supplementation would worsen the complication rather than prevent it. Nutritional support should not be delayed indefinitely, since this patient requires stabilization; rather, it should be introduced cautiously and gradually alongside electrolyte monitoring.", "case_id": "BHP-C1", "case_text": "A 19-year-old woman is brought to student health services by her roommate, who reports increasing concern over the past 2 months. The patient has lost 25 lb, now weighs 92 lb at a height of 5'5\" (BMI 15.3), and restricts her intake to under 600 calories daily. She runs 10 miles every day regardless of weather or injury and insists she is \"still too big.\" She has not menstruated in 4 months. Vital signs show a heart rate of 42/min, blood pressure of 82/54 mm Hg, and temperature of 35.6C.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 526, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "Which of the following is the most likely explanation for this new symptom?", "options": {"A": "Tardive dyskinesia", "B": "Worsening of his underlying psychosis", "C": "Restless legs syndrome, which usually occurs independent of antipsychotic exposure", "D": "Akathisia"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Akathisia): A subjective sense of inner restlessness and an inability to sit still, developing within days to weeks of antipsychotic use, describes akathisia, one of the extrapyramidal side effects associated with first-generation antipsychotics such as haloperidol. Tardive dyskinesia presents as involuntary repetitive movements, typically orofacial, after months to years of exposure, not a subjective urge to move after days. Worsening psychosis would present with hallucinations or delusions, not a specific motor restlessness. While restless legs syndrome exists independently, the clear temporal relationship to ongoing haloperidol use makes a medication-induced akathisia far more likely.", "case_id": "BHP-C2", "case_text": "A 47-year-old man with schizophrenia has been maintained on haloperidol for 8 years with reasonable symptom control but persistent mild residual auditory hallucinations. He returns to clinic today reporting a new symptom that started 3 days ago: an unpleasant, uncontrollable urge to move his legs and pace around the room, which he describes as an inability to sit still that is worse than his psychiatric symptoms ever were.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 527, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management for this new symptom?", "options": {"A": "Lower the antipsychotic dose or add a beta-blocker", "B": "Increase the haloperidol dose", "C": "Add an anticholinergic such as benztropine as the primary treatment", "D": "Switch immediately to clozapine"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Lower the antipsychotic dose or add a beta-blocker): Akathisia is managed by lowering the causative antipsychotic dose when feasible or adding a beta-blocker (or a benzodiazepine), both of which reduce the subjective motor restlessness. Increasing the haloperidol dose would worsen akathisia. Anticholinergics such as benztropine are the primary treatment for acute dystonia and parkinsonism, not the first choice for akathisia, though they are sometimes used adjunctively. Switching to clozapine is reserved for treatment-resistant psychosis after failure of at least two other agents and is not the appropriate response to a manageable side effect.", "case_id": "BHP-C2", "case_text": "A 47-year-old man with schizophrenia has been maintained on haloperidol for 8 years with reasonable symptom control but persistent mild residual auditory hallucinations. He returns to clinic today reporting a new symptom that started 3 days ago: an unpleasant, uncontrollable urge to move his legs and pace around the room, which he describes as an inability to sit still that is worse than his psychiatric symptoms ever were.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 528, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "Given this patient's now recognized vulnerability to extrapyramidal side effects, which of the following classes of antipsychotic would be most appropriate to consider for his ongoing residual symptoms going forward?", "options": {"A": "A second-generation (atypical) antipsychotic", "B": "Another first-generation antipsychotic at a higher dose", "C": "A benzodiazepine as monotherapy for psychosis", "D": "Discontinuation of all antipsychotic therapy"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A second-generation (atypical) antipsychotic): Atypical antipsychotics such as risperidone, olanzapine, quetiapine, or aripiprazole generally carry a lower risk of extrapyramidal symptoms such as akathisia and tardive dyskinesia compared with first-generation agents, making them a more appropriate consideration for a patient who has demonstrated sensitivity to these side effects, though they require metabolic monitoring. A second first-generation antipsychotic at a higher dose would increase, not decrease, extrapyramidal risk. Benzodiazepines are not effective monotherapy for psychotic symptoms. Discontinuing all antipsychotic therapy would risk relapse of his underlying schizophrenia and is not indicated by a manageable side effect.", "case_id": "BHP-C2", "case_text": "A 47-year-old man with schizophrenia has been maintained on haloperidol for 8 years with reasonable symptom control but persistent mild residual auditory hallucinations. He returns to clinic today reporting a new symptom that started 3 days ago: an unpleasant, uncontrollable urge to move his legs and pace around the room, which he describes as an inability to sit still that is worse than his psychiatric symptoms ever were.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 529, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 36-year-old woman describes a 3-week period of elevated mood, increased energy, and decreased need for sleep (sleeping only 4 hours nightly without fatigue), during which she took on three new work projects and felt highly productive and sociable. Her coworkers noticed she seemed \"unusually chatty\" but her performance reviews remained strong and she required no hospitalization. She has also had two prior episodes of depressed mood lasting several weeks each. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Bipolar I disorder", "B": "Cyclothymic disorder", "C": "Major depressive disorder with anxious distress", "D": "Bipolar II disorder"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Bipolar II disorder): At least 4 days of elevated mood with increased energy, decreased sleep need, and increased goal-directed activity, without marked impairment or need for hospitalization, describes a hypomanic episode; combined with a history of major depressive episodes, this meets criteria for bipolar II disorder. Bipolar I disorder requires a full manic episode with marked impairment or hospitalization, which is not described here (her performance and functioning remained intact). Cyclothymic disorder requires numerous periods of hypomanic and depressive symptoms that do not meet full episode criteria over at least 2 years, whereas this patient has met full criteria for discrete hypomanic and depressive episodes. Major depressive disorder alone would not account for the hypomanic episode described."}, {"id": 530, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 58-year-old man with bipolar I disorder is started on valproate for mood stabilization. He has no prior liver disease and his baseline laboratory studies before starting the medication were within normal limits. His psychiatrist plans to check relevant studies at baseline and periodically throughout treatment given the medication's known toxicity profile. His baseline complete blood count and metabolic panel were unremarkable prior to starting the medication, and his psychiatrist has scheduled follow-up laboratory studies at 1 month and then periodically thereafter given the medication's known toxicity profile. Which of the following is the most important laboratory parameter to monitor specifically because of this medication?", "options": {"A": "Thyroid-stimulating hormone", "B": "Serum creatinine only", "C": "Fasting lipid panel only", "D": "Liver function tests"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Liver function tests): Valproate carries a risk of hepatotoxicity, so liver function tests should be monitored at baseline and periodically during treatment. Thyroid-stimulating hormone and renal function are specifically monitored with lithium, not valproate, because lithium can cause hypothyroidism and nephrogenic effects. Fasting lipid panels are relevant to atypical antipsychotics because of metabolic side effects, not specifically to valproate's primary monitoring concern."}, {"id": 531, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 44-year-old woman is started on lamotrigine for bipolar depression. She has no prior psychiatric hospitalizations and this is her first mood stabilizer trial. Her psychiatrist plans to increase the dose incrementally over several weeks according to a standard titration protocol rather than reaching a full therapeutic dose immediately. Her psychiatrist explains that the medication must be increased gradually according to a specific schedule over several weeks rather than reaching the target therapeutic dose right away, given a known serious risk associated with faster escalation. Which of the following instructions is most important to reduce the risk of a serious dermatologic complication of this medication?", "options": {"A": "Take the medication on an empty stomach each morning to improve absorption", "B": "Avoid sun exposure as much as possible while taking this medication", "C": "Take the medication only at bedtime to help minimize daytime sedation", "D": "Titrate the dose slowly according to the recommended schedule"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Titrate the dose slowly according to the recommended schedule): Rapid dose escalation of lamotrigine increases the risk of Stevens-Johnson syndrome, a severe and potentially life-threatening skin reaction, so the medication must be titrated slowly following the recommended schedule. Taking the medication with or without food, sun avoidance, and timing of the dose do not meaningfully affect this specific risk."}, {"id": 532, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 25-year-old man with newly diagnosed bipolar I disorder is currently euthymic and mentions that his mother has been asking why he cannot \"just take an antidepressant\" like she does for her own depression. He has been clinically stable for the past 4 months on his current mood stabilizer regimen with no manic or depressive symptoms. His mother, who has no psychiatric history herself beyond her own depression, does not fully understand the difference between his condition and hers. Which of the following is the most appropriate response regarding antidepressant monotherapy in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Antidepressant monotherapy is preferred because it has fewer side effects than mood stabilizers", "B": "Antidepressants are contraindicated in all patients with any history of a mood disorder", "C": "Antidepressant monotherapy without a mood stabilizer can precipitate a manic or hypomanic episode", "D": "Antidepressant monotherapy is appropriate once he feels stable"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Antidepressant monotherapy without a mood stabilizer can precipitate a manic or hypomanic episode): In patients with bipolar disorder, using an antidepressant without a concurrent mood stabilizer or antipsychotic carries a recognized risk of triggering a manic switch, so antidepressants are generally used cautiously and in combination with mood-stabilizing therapy rather than as monotherapy. Antidepressant monotherapy is not preferred in bipolar disorder regardless of side-effect profile. Antidepressants are not universally contraindicated in all mood disorders; they are simply not used as unopposed monotherapy in bipolar disorder specifically. Being currently euthymic does not eliminate the risk of destabilization if an antidepressant is added without a mood stabilizer."}, {"id": 533, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 32-year-old man with a 10-year history of heavy daily alcohol use presents for elective inguinal hernia repair. He reports his last drink was 4 hours ago. He reports drinking approximately 12 standard drinks daily for the past several years and has never previously undergone a supervised detoxification. His surgical team is concerned about proceeding without addressing this risk given the elective nature of the procedure and the timing of his last drink. Which of the following is the most appropriate perioperative step to reduce his risk of alcohol withdrawal?", "options": {"A": "Proceed with surgery with no special precautions", "B": "Administer prophylactic benzodiazepines and monitor closely for withdrawal symptoms", "C": "Administer prophylactic naloxone before surgery", "D": "Delay all care until he independently detoxifies at home"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Administer prophylactic benzodiazepines and monitor closely for withdrawal symptoms): A patient with heavy, chronic daily alcohol use undergoing a procedure is at significant risk for alcohol withdrawal within hours of his last drink, so perioperative benzodiazepine prophylaxis with close monitoring for withdrawal symptoms is appropriate to prevent escalation to seizures or delirium tremens. Proceeding with no special precautions ignores a well-recognized and preventable risk. Naloxone is used for opioid overdose, not alcohol withdrawal prophylaxis. Delaying necessary surgical care and sending the patient home to detoxify independently is unsafe given his risk of severe withdrawal and does not address his surgical need."}, {"id": 534, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 27-year-old woman reports recurrent binge-eating episodes three times weekly for the past 4 months, during which she eats large amounts of food rapidly until uncomfortably full and feels a loss of control, followed by intense guilt. She denies any vomiting, laxative use, fasting, or excessive exercise afterward. Her BMI is 34. She reports these episodes typically occur alone at night when she feels stressed, and she does not engage in any behavior afterward to compensate for the amount eaten. She has tried multiple weight-loss diets in the past without sustained success and reports significant shame about her eating pattern. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Bulimia nervosa", "B": "Anorexia nervosa, binge-eating/purging type", "C": "Night eating syndrome", "D": "Binge-eating disorder"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Binge-eating disorder): Recurrent binge-eating episodes with a sense of loss of control and marked distress, occurring without any compensatory behavior such as vomiting, laxative misuse, fasting, or excessive exercise, defines binge-eating disorder, which is often associated with obesity as seen in this patient's BMI of 34. Bulimia nervosa requires recurrent compensatory behavior, which is explicitly absent here. Anorexia nervosa, binge-eating/purging type, requires a significantly low body weight, which contradicts this patient's elevated BMI. Night eating syndrome is characterized by eating predominantly after the evening meal or after waking at night, a distinct temporal pattern not described in this vignette."}, {"id": 535, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 30-year-old woman with a long history of factitious disorder is admitted after presenting with what is later discovered to be self-injected insulin causing recurrent hypoglycemic episodes, done without any apparent external incentive such as financial gain. Nursing staff discovered used insulin syringes hidden in her hospital room after several unexplained episodes of severe hypoglycemia during her admission for an unrelated complaint. She has no history of diabetes, and she denies any financial or occupational reason to want to appear ill. Which of the following features most distinguishes her condition from malingering?", "options": {"A": "The presence of true physical symptoms in her body rather than symptoms she simply fabricated", "B": "The involvement of a physician who unknowingly participates in administering the deceptive treatment", "C": "The absence of an external incentive and the motivation to assume the sick role", "D": "The chronicity of her presentation over time compared with a single isolated episode"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The absence of an external incentive and the motivation to assume the sick role): Factitious disorder involves conscious falsification or induction of symptoms (here, self-injected insulin) in the absence of an external incentive, with the underlying motivation being to assume the sick role itself. Malingering, in contrast, involves conscious symptom fabrication for a clear external incentive such as financial compensation, avoiding work, or obtaining a controlled substance. Both conditions can produce genuine physiologic derangement (as in this case, true hypoglycemia from injected insulin), so the presence of a true physical abnormality does not distinguish the two. Physician involvement and chronicity are not the defining distinguishing features between the two diagnoses."}, {"id": 536, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 50-year-old man with treatment-resistant schizophrenia is started on clozapine after failing risperidone and olanzapine. Two weeks into treatment, he develops a fever and sore throat, and a complete blood count reveals an absolute neutrophil count of 400/microL. He has otherwise tolerated the medication well over these 2 weeks with improvement in his auditory hallucinations. His psychiatrist had scheduled his first routine complete blood count for later this week as part of the standard monitoring protocol. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Continue clozapine and treat the fever symptomatically", "B": "Increase the clozapine dose to override the marrow suppression", "C": "Discontinue clozapine and evaluate for agranulocytosis", "D": "Switch to haloperidol without further workup"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Discontinue clozapine and evaluate for agranulocytosis): A markedly low absolute neutrophil count with fever in a patient on clozapine is concerning for clozapine-induced agranulocytosis, a serious and potentially life-threatening complication that requires immediate discontinuation of the drug and further evaluation and monitoring. Continuing clozapine risks progression of agranulocytosis and serious infection. Increasing the dose would worsen marrow suppression. Switching to haloperidol without addressing the acute hematologic emergency and infection risk first is not the appropriate immediate step; the agranulocytosis must be addressed and the patient stabilized before selecting a subsequent antipsychotic."}, {"id": 537, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 20-year-old man is evaluated for odd beliefs that he can influence the outcome of sporting events by wearing certain clothing, along with magical thinking, unusual perceptual experiences described as \"sensing presences\" that are not frank hallucinations, and notably eccentric speech and behavior. He has few close friends and experiences significant discomfort in social situations, though he has never had a frank psychotic episode. He works part time at a bookstore and describes himself as \"just different\" from his coworkers, though he denies any distress about his social isolation. His speech during the interview is notably tangential and includes unusual, idiosyncratic word choices that are difficult for the examiner to follow. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Schizotypal personality disorder", "B": "Schizoid personality disorder", "C": "Schizophrenia given his odd beliefs and behavior", "D": "Delusional disorder"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Schizotypal personality disorder): Cognitive or perceptual distortions (magical thinking, unusual perceptual experiences), eccentric behavior and speech, and acute discomfort with close relationships, without ever meeting full criteria for a frank psychotic episode, defines schizotypal personality disorder. Schizoid personality disorder involves detachment and restricted emotion without the odd perceptual and cognitive features described here. Schizophrenia requires an episode with true delusions, hallucinations, or disorganized speech/behavior meeting full symptom and duration criteria, which has not occurred in this patient. Delusional disorder requires a fixed, non-bizarre delusion without the broader eccentric personality pattern described."}, {"id": 539, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 34-year-old man with schizophrenia who has been stable on risperidone for 2 years is now noted at a routine visit to have gained 18 lb over the past year, along with a fasting glucose of 145 mg/dL and an LDL of 165 mg/dL. He reports no change in his diet or activity level over the past year and denies any new medications other than his psychiatric regimen. His psychiatrist reviews his chart and notes his baseline metabolic panel 1 year ago was entirely normal. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Discontinue all antipsychotic therapy immediately without first discussing alternative agents or the risk of psychiatric relapse with him.", "B": "Ignore these findings, reasoning that weight gain and glucose changes reflect unrelated lifestyle factors rather than his antipsychotic medication.", "C": "Add a stimulant medication specifically intended to counteract the weight gain associated with his current antipsychotic regimen.", "D": "Continue monitoring metabolic parameters and consider switching to an agent with a more favorable metabolic profile"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Continue monitoring metabolic parameters and consider switching to an agent with a more favorable metabolic profile): Atypical antipsychotics, including risperidone, are associated with metabolic side effects such as weight gain, hyperglycemia, and dyslipidemia, which require baseline and periodic monitoring; when significant metabolic changes develop, switching to an agent with a more favorable metabolic profile (such as aripiprazole) should be considered while managing the metabolic abnormalities directly. Abruptly discontinuing all antipsychotic therapy risks psychotic relapse and is not the appropriate first response to a manageable side effect. These metabolic changes are a well-documented antipsychotic side effect and should not be ignored. Adding a stimulant is not an appropriate or evidence-based approach to antipsychotic-induced weight gain and metabolic changes."}, {"id": 541, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 68-year-old woman with a 3-year history of progressive memory loss and word-finding difficulty has had a stable, gradually worsening course without fluctuation, and her attention remains intact on bedside testing during today's visit. Her family reports her personality and daily routines have remained largely consistent aside from the gradual cognitive decline, and she has had no recent illness, hospitalization, or new medication. She continues to live independently with some assistance from her daughter for finances and medication management. Which of the following best distinguishes her presentation from delirium?", "options": {"A": "The presence of hallucinations noted incidentally during today's bedside examination", "B": "Insidious onset and a stable, progressive course without fluctuating attention", "C": "An identifiable acute medical trigger accounting for her cognitive changes", "D": "Fluctuation in her level of consciousness observed throughout the day"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Insidious onset and a stable, progressive course without fluctuating attention): Dementia is characterized by an insidious onset and a generally stable, progressive decline in cognition, with attention typically preserved (at least in earlier stages), which distinguishes it from delirium, which has an acute onset, a fluctuating course, and prominent impairment of attention and awareness. Hallucinations can occur in both dementia and delirium and do not by themselves distinguish the two. An identifiable acute medical trigger is characteristic of delirium, not dementia, so its absence, not presence, supports this patient's diagnosis. Fluctuation in level of consciousness is a hallmark of delirium, not the stable course seen in this patient's dementia."}, {"id": 542, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 22-year-old woman presents to student health after a friend expressed concern about her eating habits. She weighs 118 lb at a height of 5'6\" (BMI 19), reports no fear of weight gain, and states she eats a normal, balanced diet with no binge eating, purging, or restriction. On further questioning, she has no eating-related symptoms at all and the visit was prompted by an unfounded concern from her friend. Her physical examination, including height, weight, and vital signs, is entirely unremarkable, and she denies any history of restrictive eating, binge eating, purging behavior, or excessive exercise. She has never expressed concern about her body shape or weight to anyone. Which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion?", "options": {"A": "She meets criteria for atypical anorexia nervosa", "B": "She meets criteria for binge-eating disorder", "C": "She meets criteria for avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder", "D": "She does not meet criteria for any eating disorder"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (She does not meet criteria for any eating disorder): This patient has a normal BMI, denies fear of weight gain, and has no binge eating, purging, restriction, or body image disturbance; there are no historical or clinical elements suggesting an eating disorder. Atypical anorexia nervosa requires significant weight loss and body image disturbance in the absence of low weight, neither of which is described. Binge-eating disorder requires recurrent binge episodes, which are denied. Avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder involves food avoidance based on sensory characteristics or lack of interest in eating without body image disturbance, which is also not described in this reassuring history."}, {"id": 544, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 41-year-old woman with major depressive disorder has been treated with sertraline for 8 weeks at an adequate dose without improvement in her symptoms. She reports taking the medication consistently as prescribed and has tolerated it without significant side effects, though her depressive symptoms, including low mood, anhedonia, and poor concentration, remain essentially unchanged from baseline. She has been engaged in weekly psychotherapy throughout this period as well. She has attended every scheduled psychotherapy session and reports no missed doses, confirmed by her pharmacy refill history, yet her depressive symptoms including low mood, anhedonia, poor sleep, and poor concentration remain essentially unchanged from her initial presentation 8 weeks ago. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Immediately refer for electroconvulsive therapy", "B": "Discontinue all treatment since antidepressants do not work for her", "C": "Add a second SSRI at full dose", "D": "Switch to a different antidepressant or augment therapy"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Switch to a different antidepressant or augment therapy): After an adequate trial (adequate dose for 4 to 8 weeks) without response, appropriate next steps include switching to a different antidepressant (potentially of a different class) or augmenting the current regimen (for example, with an atypical antipsychotic or another agent), along with reassessing psychotherapy. ECT is generally reserved for severe, treatment-resistant, or psychotic depression, or when rapid response is needed, not as the immediate next step after one failed trial. Discontinuing all treatment ignores other effective options that have not yet been tried. Combining two SSRIs at full dose is not standard practice and increases the risk of serotonin syndrome without established added benefit."}, {"id": 545, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 35-year-old man reports intrusive thoughts about contamination that cause him significant anxiety, leading him to wash his hands until they bleed, spending over 4 hours daily on this ritual. He recognizes the behavior is excessive but cannot stop. He reports these rituals significantly delay his ability to leave the house each morning and have caused conflict with his roommates, who are frustrated by the time he spends in the shared bathroom. He has been taking a maximally tolerated dose of his SSRI for the past 10 weeks with only mild improvement in his anxiety. Which of the following would be the most appropriate augmentation if an adequate SSRI trial alone has not produced sufficient improvement?", "options": {"A": "Add exposure and response prevention therapy", "B": "Add a benzodiazepine as the primary augmentation", "C": "Switch his current medication to bupropion", "D": "Add a stimulant to boost energy"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Add exposure and response prevention therapy): For OCD, the combination of an SSRI (often at a higher dose than used for depression) with cognitive behavioral therapy using exposure and response prevention is the most effective evidence-based approach, and adding ERP when medication alone is insufficient is standard practice. Benzodiazepines are not a primary or recommended augmentation strategy for OCD and do not address the underlying compulsive cycle. Bupropion has minimal serotonergic activity and is generally not effective for OCD. Stimulants have no established role in the treatment of OCD."}, {"id": 548, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 55-year-old man is evaluated for a 7-month history of holding a fixed belief that his neighbor is poisoning his water supply, despite no supporting evidence. Aside from this single fixed belief, his behavior is otherwise unremarkable, he continues to work as an accountant without difficulty, and he has no hallucinations, disorganized speech, or negative symptoms. He has installed a home water filtration system at significant personal expense and has reported his neighbor to local authorities twice, though investigations found no evidence to support his claims. Aside from this belief, his family and coworkers describe him as reliable, articulate, and entirely reasonable in every other aspect of his life. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Schizophrenia", "B": "Schizoaffective disorder", "C": "Delusional disorder", "D": "Brief psychotic disorder"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Delusional disorder): A single fixed, non-bizarre delusion (a plausible though false belief) persisting for at least 1 month, with otherwise preserved functioning and the absence of other psychotic symptoms such as hallucinations or disorganized speech, defines delusional disorder. Schizophrenia requires at least two of the five characteristic symptoms (delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, disorganized/catatonic behavior, negative symptoms) along with functional decline, which is not seen here given his intact work performance. Schizoaffective disorder requires a concurrent major mood episode, which is not described. Brief psychotic disorder resolves within 1 month, whereas this patient's belief has persisted for 7 months."}, {"id": 549, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 46-year-old woman with generalized anxiety disorder well controlled on escitalopram for 3 years wants to discontinue the medication because she feels better. She reports her anxiety symptoms, including excessive worry and muscle tension, have been fully controlled for the past 18 months without any breakthrough episodes. She has no other psychiatric comorbidities and wants to discuss the safest way to come off the medication with her psychiatrist. She reports her anxiety symptoms have been fully controlled without any breakthrough episodes for the past year and a half, and she has no other psychiatric or medical comorbidities that would complicate discontinuing her current medication regimen at this time. Which of the following is the most appropriate approach?", "options": {"A": "Gradual dose tapering to minimize discontinuation symptoms", "B": "Abrupt discontinuation is preferred to assess for symptom recurrence", "C": "Immediate switch to a benzodiazepine instead", "D": "Doubling the dose before stopping"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Gradual dose tapering to minimize discontinuation symptoms): SSRIs should generally be tapered gradually rather than stopped abruptly to reduce the risk of discontinuation symptoms such as dizziness, paresthesias, irritability, and flu-like symptoms, sometimes called SSRI discontinuation syndrome. Abrupt discontinuation increases the likelihood and severity of these symptoms. Switching to a benzodiazepine introduces dependence risk and is not an appropriate substitute for a controlled taper of an effective medication. Doubling the dose before stopping has no established benefit and is not standard practice."}, {"id": 551, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 19-year-old man is evaluated after his family reports 3 weeks of profound grief, social withdrawal, and disorganized behavior following the sudden death of his father, during which he also began hearing his father's voice calling his name and expressing the belief that his father is still alive and communicating with him daily. His symptoms began abruptly after the death and are expected to resolve. He has no prior psychiatric history according to his family, and this is his first identifiable episode of altered behavior and perception. His family reports he was functioning entirely normally prior to his father's sudden death 3 weeks ago and had no prior history of hallucinations or unusual beliefs. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Schizophrenia", "B": "Major depressive disorder with psychotic features", "C": "Schizoaffective disorder", "D": "Brief psychotic disorder"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Brief psychotic disorder): Psychotic symptoms (hallucinations and a fixed false belief) with sudden onset following an identifiable severe stressor, lasting less than 1 month with an expected full return to baseline functioning, defines brief psychotic disorder. Schizophrenia requires 6 months of continuous disturbance, far longer than the 3 weeks described. Major depressive disorder with psychotic features requires a full major depressive episode with psychotic features occurring specifically during the mood episode, which is not clearly established here as a full depressive syndrome versus an acute grief-triggered psychotic reaction. Schizoaffective disorder requires 2 weeks of psychosis in the absence of a mood episode plus a concurrent major mood episode, a more complex pattern not described in this acute presentation."}, {"id": 554, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 60-year-old man with major depressive disorder and a history of a myocardial infarction 2 months ago is being considered for antidepressant therapy. He has been adherent to his cardiac rehabilitation program and his cardiologist has cleared him for outpatient management of his mood symptoms, which include persistent low mood, anhedonia, and poor sleep since the cardiac event. His baseline electrocardiogram shows a normal QT interval and no conduction abnormalities. Which of the following classes of antidepressants would be most important to use cautiously given his cardiac history because of anticholinergic and cardiac conduction effects?", "options": {"A": "Tricyclic antidepressants", "B": "Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors", "C": "Bupropion", "D": "Buspirone"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Tricyclic antidepressants): Tricyclic antidepressants carry anticholinergic effects and can prolong cardiac conduction (QT prolongation, arrhythmia risk), making them relatively contraindicated or requiring particular caution in patients with recent cardiac events such as myocardial infarction. SSRIs are generally considered safer in cardiac patients and are often preferred in this population. Bupropion has minimal cardiac conduction effects compared with tricyclics, though it lowers seizure threshold. Buspirone is used for anxiety, not depression, and does not carry the same cardiac conduction risk profile as tricyclics."}, {"id": 556, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 29-year-old man with schizophrenia describes prominent negative symptoms including flat affect, poverty of speech, and social withdrawal, with only mild residual auditory hallucinations that do not distress him. He has been maintained on this regimen for the past year with good adherence, and his family reports his social withdrawal and lack of motivation have remained essentially unchanged despite adequate control of his hallucinations. His psychiatrist is considering adjusting his regimen specifically to address these residual symptoms. Which of the following medication classes is generally more effective for negative symptoms such as these compared to first-generation antipsychotics?", "options": {"A": "First-generation antipsychotics", "B": "Second-generation (atypical) antipsychotics", "C": "Benzodiazepines as maintenance therapy", "D": "Tricyclic antidepressants"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Second-generation (atypical) antipsychotics): Atypical antipsychotics are generally considered to have somewhat greater efficacy against negative symptoms (flat affect, avolition, alogia) compared with first-generation agents, which are primarily effective against positive symptoms such as hallucinations and delusions, making atypical agents a more appropriate choice when negative symptoms predominate. First-generation antipsychotics are less effective for negative symptoms. Benzodiazepines have no established role in treating negative symptoms of schizophrenia. Tricyclic antidepressants are not used to treat negative symptoms of schizophrenia."}, {"id": 559, "domain": "behavioral_health_and_psychiatry", "question": "A 31-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department by police after being found wandering a highway, convinced she is being pursued by federal agents. She has disorganized speech, and her family reports this began abruptly 2 weeks ago, shortly after she began using high-dose methamphetamine daily. Urine toxicology is positive for amphetamines. She has no prior psychiatric history and her family reports she was functioning normally at her job until she began this pattern of heavy stimulant use approximately 2 weeks ago. Her vital signs show tachycardia and mild hypertension, and she remains agitated but redirectable throughout the evaluation. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial step in determining the correct diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Establish a clear temporal relationship between substance use and psychotic symptoms, with symptoms expected to improve with abstinence", "B": "Diagnose schizophrenia based primarily on the psychotic symptoms observed today, without further exploring his recent pattern of substance use", "C": "Consider schizophreniform disorder as the likely diagnosis, without first exploring how his recent substance use may relate to these symptoms", "D": "Consider brief psychotic disorder as the primary diagnosis, without accounting for his recent heavy stimulant use as a possible contributing factor"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Establish a clear temporal relationship between substance use and psychotic symptoms, with symptoms expected to improve with abstinence): Given the clear temporal link between heavy methamphetamine use and the onset of psychotic symptoms, the most appropriate approach is to consider substance-induced psychotic disorder, confirming that symptoms developed during or shortly after intoxication and are expected to improve with sustained abstinence; if psychotic symptoms persist well beyond the expected period of intoxication and withdrawal, an independent primary psychotic disorder would need to be reconsidered. Diagnosing schizophrenia immediately ignores the substance-use context and the duration requirement for schizophrenia. Schizophreniform disorder similarly requires that symptoms not be attributable to a substance. Brief psychotic disorder is a diagnosis of exclusion once substance-induced and medical causes have been ruled out, not the immediate default given a clear substance trigger."}, {"id": 565, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 74-year-old woman with a history of atrophic gastritis presents with progressive fatigue, a sore tongue, and numbness and tingling in both feet that has been worsening over several months. On examination she has decreased vibration sense and proprioception in the lower extremities along with a positive Babinski sign. Laboratory studies show a macrocytic anemia with a mean corpuscular volume of 118 fL. Peripheral smear additionally reveals occasional macro-ovalocytes, and her serum methylmalonic acid level is pending at the time of this visit. Which of the following mechanisms best explains her combined hematologic and neurologic findings?", "options": {"A": "Folate deficiency causing impaired DNA synthesis in the marrow with secondary peripheral neuropathy", "B": "Chronic alcohol use causing direct bone marrow toxicity along with cerebellar degeneration over time", "C": "Iron deficiency causing impaired hemoglobin synthesis with secondary restless leg symptoms at night", "D": "Autoimmune destruction of gastric parietal cells causing vitamin B12 deficiency with subacute combined degeneration"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Autoimmune destruction of gastric parietal cells causing vitamin B12 deficiency with subacute combined degeneration): loss of parietal cells removes the source of intrinsic factor, producing pernicious anemia and vitamin B12 deficiency, which impairs both DNA synthesis in the marrow (macrocytic anemia) and myelin synthesis in the dorsal columns and corticospinal tracts (subacute combined degeneration), explaining both the hematologic and neurologic findings together. Option A is wrong because folate deficiency causes the identical megaloblastic blood picture but does not cause subacute combined degeneration or any neurologic disease. Option B is wrong because alcohol causes macrocytosis through a different, non-megaloblastic mechanism and does not classically produce dorsal column and corticospinal findings together. Option C is wrong because iron deficiency produces microcytic, not macrocytic, anemia and does not cause this neurologic syndrome."}, {"id": 567, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 58-year-old woman with stage 5 chronic kidney disease (end-stage renal disease) on a stable dialysis regimen has a hemoglobin of 9.2 g/dL. Her iron studies are normal, reticulocyte count is low, and peripheral smear shows normocytic red cells without significant abnormality. She has no history of prior kidney disease before this diagnosis, and a basic metabolic panel obtained at this visit is otherwise unremarkable aside from the anemia. Her blood pressure remains well controlled on her current antihypertensive regimen, and she has no history of proteinuria on prior evaluation. Which of the following best explains the pathophysiology of her anemia?", "options": {"A": "Increased peripheral destruction of red cells from uremic toxins", "B": "Chronic occult gastrointestinal blood loss related to uremic platelet dysfunction", "C": "Iron sequestration within macrophages driven by chronic inflammation", "D": "Reduced renal production of erythropoietin limiting marrow red cell output"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Reduced renal production of erythropoietin limiting marrow red cell output): the kidneys are the primary source of erythropoietin, and advanced chronic kidney disease reduces erythropoietin production, leading to a normocytic, hypoproliferative anemia with an inappropriately low reticulocyte count despite normal iron stores, exactly as described here. Option A is wrong because this patient's laboratory picture shows decreased production rather than the elevated reticulocyte count, bilirubin, and LDH expected with hemolysis. Option B is wrong because normal iron studies argue against ongoing blood loss as the primary driver of this anemia. Option C describes anemia of chronic disease, which can coexist with renal disease but does not best explain a picture with normal iron studies and no described inflammatory illness."}, {"id": 569, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 29-year-old woman who is 12 weeks pregnant reports fatigue. Her complete blood count shows a macrocytic anemia with a mean corpuscular volume of 108 fL and hypersegmented neutrophils on peripheral smear. She reports a diet poor in vegetables and admits to occasional heavy alcohol use before learning she was pregnant, though she has since stopped drinking. She has otherwise had an uncomplicated pregnancy to this point and denies any vaginal bleeding, abdominal pain, or fevers. Which nutrient deficiency is most likely responsible for this laboratory picture?", "options": {"A": "Folate deficiency from increased demand and poor dietary intake", "B": "Vitamin B12 deficiency from strict dietary restriction", "C": "Iron deficiency from expanding maternal blood volume", "D": "Vitamin B6 deficiency from pregnancy-related nausea and vomiting"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Folate deficiency from increased demand and poor dietary intake): pregnancy dramatically increases folate requirements to support rapid cell division, and a diet poor in folate-rich vegetables readily produces folate deficiency, which explains the megaloblastic picture of macrocytosis with hypersegmented neutrophils; her prior alcohol use further impairs folate absorption and storage. Option B is less likely without a description of a strict vegan diet, terminal ileal disease, or another specific risk factor for B12 deficiency, and B12 deficiency in pregnancy is considerably less common than folate deficiency. Option C is wrong because iron deficiency produces microcytic, not macrocytic, anemia. Option D is wrong because vitamin B6 deficiency does not cause megaloblastic anemia or hypersegmented neutrophils."}, {"id": 570, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 34-year-old woman presents with fever, confusion, and scattered petechiae. She has no history of recent illness or medication changes. The laboratory panel shown is obtained on admission. Peripheral smear reveals numerous schistocytes. Serum creatinine is mildly elevated at 1.6 mg/dL. Direct antiglobulin test is negative. She has no history of recent infection, and her family reports she was in her usual state of health until two days before this presentation. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Immune thrombocytopenic purpura", "B": "Disseminated intravascular coagulation from infection", "C": "Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura", "D": "Warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura): the combination of fever, neurologic changes, thrombocytopenia, microangiopathic hemolytic anemia with schistocytes, and renal involvement, together with a negative Coombs test and normal coagulation times, is classic for thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura, caused by a deficiency of the ADAMTS13 enzyme that normally cleaves large von Willebrand factor multimers. Option A is wrong because immune thrombocytopenic purpura causes isolated thrombocytopenia without hemolysis or schistocytes. Option B is wrong because DIC characteristically prolongs the PT and aPTT and consumes fibrinogen, which are not described here, and typically has an identifiable trigger such as sepsis. Option D is wrong because warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia has a positive direct Coombs test, which is negative in this patient.", "image": "question_images/q_570.png"}, {"id": 572, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 21-year-old man of West African descent develops acute fatigue, jaundice, and dark urine three days after starting trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole for a urinary tract infection. Peripheral smear reveals bite cells and Heinz bodies. He has no history of anemia, jaundice, or prior transfusion. His platelet count and coagulation studies obtained at this same visit are both within normal limits. Which of the following is the most likely underlying diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency", "B": "Hereditary spherocytosis", "C": "Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria", "D": "Cold agglutinin disease"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency): G6PD deficiency is an X-linked enzyme defect that impairs the red cell's ability to neutralize oxidative stress, so exposure to an oxidant drug such as a sulfonamide antibiotic precipitates acute hemolysis with bite cells (from macrophage removal of oxidized hemoglobin) and Heinz bodies (denatured hemoglobin precipitates), exactly as described in this patient. Option B is wrong because hereditary spherocytosis produces spherocytes rather than bite cells and Heinz bodies and is not typically drug-triggered. Option C is wrong because paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria is an acquired clonal disorder that is not triggered by a specific oxidant medication and does not produce bite cells. Option D is wrong because cold agglutinin disease is triggered by cold exposure and produces agglutinated red cells on smear, not bite cells."}, {"id": 573, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 9-year-old boy with sickle cell disease is found on a routine well-child visit to have Howell-Jolly bodies on peripheral smear. His parents ask why this finding is present. His platelet count and white blood cell count on this same panel are both within the normal reference range. Which of the following best explains this laboratory finding?", "options": {"A": "Chronic low-grade disseminated intravascular coagulation from recurrent vaso-occlusion", "B": "An inherited defect in the spectrin protein of the red cell membrane", "C": "Ongoing oxidative damage from an unrecognized enzyme deficiency", "D": "Functional asplenia from repeated splenic infarction due to sickling"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Functional asplenia from repeated splenic infarction due to sickling): repeated vaso-occlusive sickling episodes progressively infarct the spleen over early childhood, leading to functional asplenia; because the spleen normally removes nuclear remnants called Howell-Jolly bodies from circulating red cells, their appearance on smear is a direct marker of this autosplenectomy and also signals increased risk for infection with encapsulated organisms. Option A is wrong because Howell-Jolly bodies are a splenic function marker, not a coagulation finding. Option B describes hereditary spherocytosis, which is unrelated to sickle cell disease and to Howell-Jolly bodies specifically. Option C describes G6PD deficiency, which produces bite cells and Heinz bodies rather than Howell-Jolly bodies."}, {"id": 574, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 27-year-old man reports several months of intermittent dark-colored urine that is most noticeable in the morning, along with recurrent abdominal pain. Evaluation reveals a normocytic anemia with an elevated reticulocyte count, elevated LDH, and low haptoglobin. Abdominal imaging reveals thrombosis of the hepatic veins. He has never received a blood transfusion and reports no other new medications, herbal supplements, or recent infections before this presentation. Which of the following tests would most likely confirm the underlying diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Flow cytometry demonstrating absence of CD55 and CD59 on red cell surfaces", "B": "Osmotic fragility testing demonstrating increased red cell membrane fragility under stress", "C": "Hemoglobin electrophoresis demonstrating an abnormal hemoglobin variant consistent with a hemoglobinopathy", "D": "Direct antiglobulin testing demonstrating IgG on the red cell surface"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Flow cytometry demonstrating absence of CD55 and CD59 on red cell surfaces): the combination of episodic intravascular hemolysis with morning hemoglobinuria and unusual-site venous thrombosis, including hepatic vein (Budd-Chiari) thrombosis, is classic for paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria, an acquired clonal disorder from a PIGA gene mutation that removes the protective complement regulatory proteins CD55 and CD59, confirmed by flow cytometry. Option B describes the test for hereditary spherocytosis, which does not classically cause hepatic vein thrombosis. Option C describes the test for a hemoglobinopathy such as sickle cell disease, which would not explain this clinical picture. Option D describes the test for autoimmune hemolytic anemia, which is not associated with this pattern of unusual-site thrombosis."}, {"id": 575, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 38-year-old woman develops fatigue and dark urine two weeks after a documented Mycoplasma pneumoniae respiratory infection. Symptoms notably worsen when she goes outside in cold weather. Direct antiglobulin test is positive for complement (C3) but negative for IgG. Her symptoms have been present for about one week and notably improve somewhat whenever she stays indoors in a warm environment. She works outdoors during the winter months and reports that her symptoms are noticeably worse on particularly cold days. Which of the following best characterizes the mechanism of her hemolysis?", "options": {"A": "IgG antibody binding to red cells with extravascular splenic clearance", "B": "Mechanical red cell shearing across a damaged microvascular endothelium", "C": "IgM antibody binding at cold temperatures with complement-mediated intravascular hemolysis", "D": "An inherited membrane protein defect unmasked by a preceding viral illness"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (IgM antibody binding at cold temperatures with complement-mediated intravascular hemolysis): cold agglutinin disease is mediated by IgM antibodies, often triggered by Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection, that bind red cells preferentially in cooler peripheral tissues and fix complement, producing complement-mediated intravascular hemolysis that worsens with cold exposure, consistent with a positive C3 and negative IgG Coombs result. Option A describes warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia, which is IgG-mediated and would show a positive IgG Coombs test, not the C3-only pattern described here. Option B describes microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, which is not antibody-mediated and would have a negative Coombs test. Option D is incorrect because this presentation is antibody-mediated rather than an inherited structural defect."}, {"id": 577, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 41-year-old woman with systemic lupus erythematosus develops fatigue and jaundice. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 8.7 g/dL, elevated reticulocyte count, elevated indirect bilirubin, elevated LDH, and low haptoglobin. Peripheral smear shows spherocytes. Direct antiglobulin test is positive for IgG. Her vital signs today are within normal limits and physical examination reveals no lymphadenopathy, hepatomegaly, or splenomegaly. She has no known drug allergies and has not required any transfusions or hospitalizations in the past. Which of the following best explains this patient's hemolytic process?", "options": {"A": "Mechanical fragmentation of red cells across an inflamed microvasculature", "B": "IgG autoantibody binding red cell surface antigens with extravascular clearance", "C": "An inherited defect in ankyrin or spectrin proteins unmasked by lupus flare", "D": "Cold-dependent IgM antibody binding with complement fixation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (IgG autoantibody binding red cell surface antigens with extravascular clearance): systemic lupus erythematosus is a recognized cause of warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia, in which IgG autoantibodies coat the red cell surface and lead to partial phagocytic removal by splenic macrophages, producing spherocytes and a positive IgG-positive direct Coombs test, exactly as seen here. Option A describes microangiopathic hemolysis, which would show schistocytes and a negative Coombs test rather than the spherocytes and positive IgG result described. Option C describes hereditary spherocytosis, an inherited condition unrelated to autoimmune disease activity. Option D describes cold agglutinin disease, which is IgM- and complement-mediated rather than IgG-mediated."}, {"id": 578, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 6-year-old boy with hereditary spherocytosis undergoes splenectomy after recurrent severe hemolytic episodes. Vital signs reveal a temperature of 100.9 degrees Fahrenheit, heart rate of 108 beats per minute, and blood pressure of 92/58 mmHg. Which of the following statements about his expected postoperative course is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "His osmotic fragility test will show some improvement after splenectomy, though it typically remains somewhat abnormal long-term", "B": "His baseline hemoglobin electrophoresis pattern will likely shift in a lasting way after this splenectomy procedure", "C": "His red cell membrane defect will resolve because the spleen was the source of the abnormal protein", "D": "His hemolysis will substantially improve because the primary site of red cell destruction has been removed"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (His hemolysis will substantially improve because the primary site of red cell destruction has been removed): in hereditary spherocytosis, the underlying membrane protein defect (commonly affecting spectrin or ankyrin) itself is not corrected by splenectomy, but because the spleen is the principal site where abnormally shaped red cells are trapped and destroyed, removing it substantially reduces the rate of hemolysis and improves anemia, which is why splenectomy is the definitive treatment for severe disease. Option A is wrong because the underlying red cell fragility persists; splenectomy treats the consequence, not the membrane defect itself. Option B is wrong because hereditary spherocytosis is a membrane protein disorder, not a hemoglobinopathy, so electrophoresis is not the relevant test and would not change. Option C is wrong because the abnormal protein originates in the red cell membrane itself, not in the spleen."}, {"id": 579, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 30-year-old man with no prior medical history develops acute hemolytic anemia with hemoglobinuria two days after eating fava beans for the first time. Genetic testing later confirms a glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency. He has no history of recent infection or new medication exposure to otherwise explain his acute presentation. His hemoglobin at a routine visit just three months earlier was reportedly entirely within the normal range. Which of the following best explains why oxidative stress precipitates hemolysis in this condition?", "options": {"A": "G6PD deficiency directly destabilizes the red cell cytoskeleton under mechanical stress", "B": "G6PD deficiency impairs generation of NADPH, limiting the red cell's ability to neutralize oxidative damage", "C": "G6PD deficiency causes abnormal complement regulatory protein expression on the red cell surface", "D": "G6PD deficiency reduces intrinsic factor availability needed for red cell maturation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (G6PD deficiency impairs generation of NADPH, limiting the red cell's ability to neutralize oxidative damage): G6PD is the rate-limiting enzyme of the pentose phosphate pathway, which generates NADPH; NADPH is required to regenerate reduced glutathione, the red cell's main defense against oxidative stress, so a deficiency leaves red cells vulnerable to hemoglobin denaturation and hemolysis whenever an oxidative trigger such as fava beans, certain drugs, or infection is encountered. Option A describes the mechanism of hereditary spherocytosis, not G6PD deficiency. Option C describes the mechanism of paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria. Option D is unrelated to G6PD deficiency and instead relates to vitamin B12 absorption."}, {"id": 581, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 6-year-old boy presents with two weeks of fatigue, low-grade fevers, and diffuse bone pain. Examination reveals scattered petechiae and mild hepatosplenomegaly. Complete blood count shows pancytopenia with 60 percent lymphoblasts on differential. He has never required a blood transfusion, and his prior surgical history includes only an uncomplicated appendectomy in childhood. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Acute myeloid leukemia", "B": "Chronic myeloid leukemia", "C": "Hodgkin lymphoma", "D": "Acute lymphoblastic leukemia"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Acute lymphoblastic leukemia): acute lymphoblastic leukemia is the most common childhood malignancy and classically presents exactly as described here, with marrow replacement by lymphoblasts causing pancytopenia, bone pain from marrow expansion, and organomegaly from leukemic infiltration, all in a young child. Option A is wrong because AML is far less common in young children and typically presents with Auer rod-containing myeloblasts rather than lymphoblasts. Option B is wrong because CML is a disease of middle-aged adults with marked leukocytosis and a full spectrum of maturing myeloid cells, not blasts predominating in a child. Option C is wrong because Hodgkin lymphoma presents with painless lymphadenopathy rather than marrow replacement and pancytopenia."}, {"id": 582, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 58-year-old man presents with fatigue and early satiety. Examination reveals massive splenomegaly. Complete blood count shows a white blood cell count of 180,000/uL with a full spectrum of maturing granulocytic cells, from myeloblasts through mature neutrophils, and a low leukocyte alkaline phosphatase score. Cytogenetic analysis reveals a reciprocal translocation between chromosomes 9 and 22. He reports no fevers or recent infections, and his physical examination is otherwise unremarkable aside from the palpable spleen tip below the costal margin. Which of the following is the most appropriate targeted therapy for this condition?", "options": {"A": "A tyrosine kinase inhibitor targeting the BCR-ABL fusion protein", "B": "All-trans retinoic acid to promote promyelocyte differentiation", "C": "Rituximab targeting the CD20 antigen on malignant cells", "D": "High-dose corticosteroids to induce lymphoblast apoptosis"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A tyrosine kinase inhibitor targeting the BCR-ABL fusion protein): the t(9;22) Philadelphia chromosome produces the BCR-ABL fusion protein, a constitutively active tyrosine kinase that drives chronic myeloid leukemia, and imatinib and other BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase inhibitors are the targeted first-line therapy that directly addresses this molecular driver. Option B is the specific therapy for acute promyelocytic leukemia, an AML subtype unrelated to the Philadelphia chromosome. Option C targets CD20, a B-cell marker relevant to B-cell lymphomas and CLL, not the myeloid lineage described here. Option D is a component of ALL induction therapy, not the targeted approach for CML."}, {"id": 583, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 72-year-old man is found incidentally to have a white blood cell count of 45,000/uL on routine bloodwork. He is asymptomatic. Peripheral smear reveals a predominance of small, mature-appearing lymphocytes along with numerous smudge cells. Flow cytometry confirms a monoclonal population of CD5-positive B cells. He reports no fevers, rash, or recent infection, and physical examination reveals no lymphadenopathy at this visit. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia", "B": "Acute lymphoblastic leukemia", "C": "Hairy cell leukemia", "D": "Chronic myeloid leukemia"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Chronic lymphocytic leukemia): chronic lymphocytic leukemia is the most common leukemia in adults and classically presents as an indolent, often incidental finding of lymphocytosis with smudge cells on smear, reflecting fragile malignant lymphocytes damaged during slide preparation, along with a CD5-positive monoclonal B-cell population on flow cytometry. Option B is wrong because ALL involves lymphoblasts, an acute and typically symptomatic presentation, rather than mature small lymphocytes found incidentally. Option C is wrong because hairy cell leukemia shows cells with fine cytoplasmic projections and typically presents with pancytopenia and splenomegaly rather than this smear pattern. Option D is wrong because CML is a myeloid, not lymphoid, malignancy."}, {"id": 586, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 55-year-old woman presents with several months of progressive lower back pain and recent-onset confusion. Laboratory studies reveal a serum calcium of 12.4 mg/dL, creatinine of 2.1 mg/dL, and hemoglobin of 9.0 g/dL. Serum protein electrophoresis reveals a monoclonal spike, and urine studies reveal Bence Jones protein. Skeletal survey reveals multiple punched-out lytic lesions in the skull and pelvis. Which of the following best explains this patient's renal impairment?", "options": {"A": "Immune complex deposition along the glomerular basement membrane", "B": "Direct tubular toxicity from filtered free light chains and hypercalcemia", "C": "Thrombotic microangiopathy from complement dysregulation", "D": "Autoimmune destruction of the glomerular basement membrane by anti-GBM antibodies"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Direct tubular toxicity from filtered free light chains and hypercalcemia): in multiple myeloma, excess free light chains (Bence Jones protein) are filtered by the glomerulus and precipitate with Tamm-Horsfall protein within the renal tubules, causing direct tubular injury (myeloma cast nephropathy), and this injury is compounded by the hypercalcemia also present, together explaining her renal impairment. Option A describes a mechanism relevant to lupus nephritis or post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, not myeloma. Option C describes the mechanism of thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura or atypical hemolytic uremic syndrome, unrelated to this presentation. Option D describes Goodpasture syndrome, a distinct autoimmune process not associated with the monoclonal gammopathy described here."}, {"id": 588, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 61-year-old man with polycythemia vera confirmed by a JAK2 mutation asks about his risk of complications. Examination reveals scattered petechiae over the lower extremities without any associated lymphadenopathy, hepatomegaly, or splenomegaly on this visit. Which of the following complications is he at greatest risk for as a direct consequence of his elevated red cell mass?", "options": {"A": "Progressive marrow fibrosis with a leukoerythroblastic smear", "B": "Recurrent infections with catalase-positive organisms", "C": "Isolated marked thrombocytosis with paradoxical bleeding", "D": "Arterial and venous thrombosis from increased blood viscosity"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Arterial and venous thrombosis from increased blood viscosity): the hallmark risk in polycythemia vera is thrombosis, both arterial and venous, driven by the markedly increased red cell mass raising blood viscosity, which is why therapeutic phlebotomy to lower the hematocrit and low-dose aspirin form the backbone of management. Option A describes a possible late progression of myeloproliferative disease (myelofibrosis) rather than the most immediate risk from an elevated red cell mass itself. Option B describes chronic granulomatous disease, an unrelated immunodeficiency. Option C describes essential thrombocythemia, a distinct myeloproliferative neoplasm defined by platelet overproduction rather than red cell overproduction."}, {"id": 589, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 68-year-old woman presents with early satiety and progressive fatigue. Examination reveals massive splenomegaly. Peripheral smear reveals teardrop-shaped red cells along with immature myeloid precursors and nucleated red cells. Bone marrow biopsy reveals extensive fibrosis with a dry tap on aspiration attempt. She has no personal or family history of prior thrombosis and reports no tobacco use or oral contraceptive use. Her most recent complete blood count, obtained six weeks earlier during a routine visit, was entirely within normal limits. Which of the following best explains her peripheral smear and splenomegaly findings?", "options": {"A": "Marrow fibrosis forcing compensatory extramedullary hematopoiesis in the spleen and liver", "B": "Malignant plasma cell infiltration of the bone marrow with associated lytic lesions", "C": "Reactive lymphocytosis following a recent viral illness", "D": "Complement-mediated intravascular hemolysis from an acquired membrane defect"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Marrow fibrosis forcing compensatory extramedullary hematopoiesis in the spleen and liver): primary myelofibrosis replaces normal marrow with fibrous tissue, which both explains the dry tap on aspiration and forces the body to shift blood cell production to extramedullary sites such as the spleen and liver, producing massive splenomegaly along with a leukoerythroblastic smear containing teardrop cells, immature myeloid forms, and nucleated red cells. Option B describes multiple myeloma, which does not typically cause massive splenomegaly or a leukoerythroblastic smear. Option C would not produce marrow fibrosis or a dry tap and does not explain this degree of splenomegaly. Option D describes paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria, unrelated to this fibrotic marrow picture."}, {"id": 590, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 66-year-old man is incidentally found to have a platelet count of 950,000/uL on routine bloodwork. He is asymptomatic aside from occasional mild headaches. Bone marrow biopsy reveals increased numbers of large, mature-appearing megakaryocytes without significant fibrosis. JAK2 mutation testing is positive. He reports no gastrointestinal symptoms, and physical examination is otherwise unremarkable. Which of the following complications is this patient at greatest risk for?", "options": {"A": "Progressive renal failure from cast nephropathy related to a separate plasma cell disorder", "B": "Both thrombotic and, at very high platelet counts, paradoxical bleeding complications", "C": "Recurrent Neisseria infections resulting from an inherited terminal complement deficiency", "D": "Subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord from vitamin B12 deficiency"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Both thrombotic and, at very high platelet counts, paradoxical bleeding complications): essential thrombocythemia produces a markedly elevated platelet count with an associated increased risk of both thrombosis and, at very high counts, paradoxical bleeding from acquired von Willebrand factor dysfunction, which is why this dual risk profile is a defining and heavily tested feature of the condition. Option A describes a complication of multiple myeloma, unrelated to essential thrombocythemia. Option C describes a complement deficiency, unrelated to platelet overproduction. Option D describes vitamin B12 deficiency, an unrelated nutritional cause of macrocytic anemia."}, {"id": 592, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 45-year-old woman develops sudden hypotension and heavy vaginal bleeding immediately after a placental abruption during labor. The laboratory panel shown is obtained emergently. Which of the following best explains this coagulation picture?", "options": {"A": "An isolated inherited deficiency of factor VIII, unrelated to her current acute obstetric hemorrhage", "B": "Acquired von Willebrand disease developing secondary to her acute obstetric hemorrhage and abruption", "C": "Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome newly unmasked by the physiologic changes of her pregnancy", "D": "Disseminated intravascular coagulation triggered by tissue factor release from the abrupted placenta"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Disseminated intravascular coagulation triggered by tissue factor release from the abrupted placenta): placental abruption releases large amounts of tissue factor into the maternal circulation, triggering widespread activation of the coagulation cascade that consumes platelets and clotting factors while simultaneously activating fibrinolysis, producing the classic DIC picture of prolonged PT and aPTT, low fibrinogen, low platelets, and elevated D-dimer seen on this panel. Option A would show an isolated prolonged aPTT with a normal PT and normal platelet count and fibrinogen, not this global consumptive picture. Option B would not produce the combined prolongation of both PT and aPTT with low fibrinogen described here. Option C classically prolongs the aPTT alone in vitro and does not cause this acute consumptive bleeding picture.", "image": "question_images/q_592.png"}, {"id": 593, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 6-year-old boy is brought in for evaluation of a swollen, painful knee after a minor fall, the third such episode this year. Laboratory studies reveal a normal platelet count, normal PT, and markedly prolonged aPTT. Factor assay reveals a severe deficiency of factor VIII. Which of the following best describes the inheritance pattern of this condition?", "options": {"A": "Autosomal dominant", "B": "Autosomal recessive", "C": "Mitochondrial", "D": "X-linked recessive"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (X-linked recessive): hemophilia A, caused by factor VIII deficiency, is inherited in an X-linked recessive pattern, which explains why it almost always affects boys, who have only one X chromosome, while female carriers are typically asymptomatic, and it presents with deep bleeding into joints such as recurrent hemarthrosis after minor trauma, exactly as described here. Option A is wrong because hemophilia does not follow an autosomal dominant pattern; von Willebrand disease, a different bleeding disorder, is the one typically inherited in an autosomal dominant fashion. Option B is wrong because hemophilia is X-linked, not autosomal recessive. Option C is wrong because hemophilia involves a nuclear gene on the X chromosome, not mitochondrial DNA."}, {"id": 594, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 19-year-old woman reports lifelong easy bruising, frequent nosebleeds, and heavy menstrual periods since menarche. Her mother has a similar history. Laboratory studies show a normal platelet count, a mildly prolonged aPTT, and a normal PT. Her current hemoglobin is 6.8 g/dL, a substantial drop from her outpatient baseline of approximately 9.0 g/dL at her last visit. She appears fatigued and mildly tachycardic, with a heart rate of 118 beats per minute on repeat examination. Which of the following is the most likely underlying diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Hemophilia A", "B": "Immune thrombocytopenic purpura", "C": "Factor V Leiden mutation", "D": "Von Willebrand disease"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Von Willebrand disease): von Willebrand disease, the most common inherited bleeding disorder and typically autosomal dominant, presents with mucocutaneous bleeding such as epistaxis and menorrhagia because von Willebrand factor is required for platelet adhesion, and because it also stabilizes circulating factor VIII, deficiency can mildly prolong the aPTT while the platelet count and PT remain normal, exactly as described here. Option A is wrong because hemophilia A is X-linked and would essentially never present with an affected mother and daughter both symptomatic in this pattern, and it does not cause mucocutaneous bleeding. Option B is wrong because ITP causes a low platelet count, which is normal in this patient. Option C is wrong because Factor V Leiden causes a hypercoagulable, not a bleeding, phenotype."}, {"id": 595, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 5-year-old girl develops scattered petechiae and bruising one week after recovering from a viral upper respiratory infection. She otherwise feels well and has no organomegaly. Complete blood count shows an isolated platelet count of 18,000/uL with normal hemoglobin and white blood cell count. He has previously required hospital admission for a vaso-occlusive pain crisis but has never before developed acute chest syndrome. He has been maintained on hydroxyurea therapy for the past two years with reasonably good adherence reported by his family. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura", "B": "Immune thrombocytopenic purpura", "C": "Disseminated intravascular coagulation", "D": "Hemolytic uremic syndrome"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Immune thrombocytopenic purpura): immune thrombocytopenic purpura in children classically follows a viral illness by one to several weeks and presents with an isolated, often severe, drop in platelet count with an otherwise normal blood count and no organomegaly, and it is typically self-limited in this age group, sometimes managed with observation alone or corticosteroids or IVIG if bleeding is significant. Option A would also show hemolytic anemia with schistocytes and elevated LDH, not an isolated platelet drop. Option C would show abnormal PT and aPTT with low fibrinogen, not an isolated thrombocytopenia. Option D would show accompanying renal impairment and microangiopathic hemolysis, neither of which is described here."}, {"id": 596, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 33-year-old woman with systemic lupus erythematosus has a history of two prior first-trimester pregnancy losses and one prior deep vein thrombosis. Laboratory studies reveal a prolonged aPTT that fails to correct on a mixing study. Vital signs are within normal limits and physical examination is otherwise unremarkable. Which of the following best explains why her aPTT is prolonged despite her clinical history of clotting rather than bleeding?", "options": {"A": "Consumption of clotting factors from ongoing subclinical disseminated intravascular coagulation", "B": "An acquired deficiency of von Willebrand factor related to lupus activity", "C": "A coexisting inherited factor VIII deficiency unmasked by her autoimmune disease", "D": "Antiphospholipid antibodies interfering with the phospholipid reagent used in the aPTT assay in vitro"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Antiphospholipid antibodies interfering with the phospholipid reagent used in the aPTT assay in vitro): antiphospholipid syndrome, often seen alongside systemic lupus erythematosus and presenting with recurrent thrombosis and pregnancy loss exactly as described, paradoxically prolongs the aPTT in the laboratory because the antiphospholipid antibodies interfere with the phospholipid reagent used in the assay itself, even though the patient is clinically prothrombotic rather than at risk for bleeding, which is confirmed by a mixing study that fails to correct. Option A would not produce a stable, chronic aPTT prolongation with a normal bleeding history and would instead be an acute consumptive process. Option B does not explain a thrombotic clinical history. Option C would present with a bleeding, not a thrombotic and pregnancy-loss, history."}, {"id": 599, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 50-year-old man undergoing evaluation for recurrent venous thromboembolism is found to have low levels of a vitamin K-dependent protein that normally inactivates factors Va and VIIIa. He otherwise feels well and has no history of any prior bleeding episodes or abnormal laboratory findings before today. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Factor V Leiden mutation", "B": "Antithrombin III deficiency", "C": "Protein C deficiency", "D": "Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Protein C deficiency): protein C is a vitamin K-dependent anticoagulant protein that, once activated, cleaves and inactivates factors Va and VIIIa to limit clot propagation, so a deficiency of protein C removes this natural brake on coagulation and predisposes to recurrent venous thromboembolism, matching the mechanism described here. Option A describes a mutation that makes factor Va resistant to inactivation by protein C, which is a different mechanism than a deficiency of protein C itself. Option B describes a deficiency of a different natural anticoagulant that inhibits thrombin and factor Xa directly rather than inactivating factors Va and VIIIa. Option D is an acquired autoimmune condition rather than a deficiency of a specific vitamin K-dependent protein."}, {"id": 600, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "She has no significant past medical history and takes no regular medications, and she has not traveled internationally within the past year. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in establishing a diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Empiric antibiotic therapy with reassessment in two weeks", "B": "Excisional lymph node biopsy for histopathologic examination", "C": "Fine needle aspiration alone for tissue sampling", "D": "Observation with repeat imaging in three months"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Excisional lymph node biopsy for histopathologic examination): a firm, rubbery, persistently enlarged lymph node accompanied by drenching night sweats, significant weight loss, and a mediastinal mass strongly suggests lymphoma, and an excisional biopsy is required to preserve lymph node architecture, which is essential for distinguishing Hodgkin from non-Hodgkin lymphoma and for identifying diagnostic cells. Option A is inappropriate given the constellation of systemic B symptoms and a mediastinal mass, which should not be attributed to an infectious process without tissue diagnosis. Option C is wrong because fine needle aspiration does not preserve the architecture needed to reliably diagnose lymphoma. Option D is inappropriate given her significant systemic symptoms, which warrant prompt diagnostic evaluation rather than watchful waiting.", "case_id": "BL2-600", "case_text": "A 29-year-old woman presents with a two-month history of painless swelling in her left axilla along with drenching night sweats and an unintentional 12-pound weight loss. She reports intermittent low-grade fevers. Examination reveals a firm, rubbery, nontender 4 cm axillary lymph node. Chest imaging reveals a mediastinal mass. There is no hepatosplenomegaly.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 601, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "Excisional biopsy in this patient reveals large binucleate cells with prominent eosinophilic nucleoli surrounded by a mixed inflammatory infiltrate of eosinophils, lymphocytes, and histiocytes. Physical examination at this visit reveals no additional palpable lymphadenopathy beyond the axillary node, and her vital signs remain within normal limits. She has completed four cycles of combination chemotherapy to date and reports tolerating treatment reasonably well overall. Which of the following diagnoses is most consistent with this histologic finding together with her clinical presentation?", "options": {"A": "Hodgkin lymphoma", "B": "Burkitt lymphoma", "C": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia", "D": "Follicular lymphoma"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Hodgkin lymphoma): the binucleate, owl-eye Reed-Sternberg cell within a mixed inflammatory background is the diagnostic hallmark of Hodgkin lymphoma, and her presentation with painless lymphadenopathy, B symptoms (fevers, night sweats, weight loss), and a mediastinal mass in a young adult is a classic clinical picture for this disease, which frequently has a bimodal age distribution favoring young adults and older adults. Option B would show a starry sky pattern of intermediate-sized lymphoid cells rather than Reed-Sternberg cells. Option C would occur in a patient with a preceding history of CLL, not a de novo presentation in a young woman. Option D would show a nodular, follicular growth pattern of small cleaved cells rather than Reed-Sternberg cells.", "case_id": "BL2-600", "case_text": "A 29-year-old woman presents with a two-month history of painless swelling in her left axilla along with drenching night sweats and an unintentional 12-pound weight loss. She reports intermittent low-grade fevers. Examination reveals a firm, rubbery, nontender 4 cm axillary lymph node. Chest imaging reveals a mediastinal mass. There is no hepatosplenomegaly.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 602, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "Staging workup in this patient with newly diagnosed Hodgkin lymphoma reveals disease confined to the axillary and mediastinal lymph node regions above the diaphragm, without any extranodal involvement, splenic involvement, or B symptoms documented on formal staging evaluation despite her earlier reported fevers and night sweats now having resolved. Her B symptoms have since resolved with initiation of chemotherapy, and she reports tolerating treatment reasonably well so far. Which of the following staging systems is used to describe the anatomic extent of her disease?", "options": {"A": "The Ann Arbor staging system", "B": "The International Prognostic Index", "C": "The Rai staging system", "D": "The International Staging System for multiple myeloma"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The Ann Arbor staging system): the Ann Arbor staging system, based on the number and location of involved lymph node regions relative to the diaphragm and the presence or absence of extranodal extension and B symptoms, is the standard system used to stage both Hodgkin and non-Hodgkin lymphoma and directly guides treatment intensity and prognosis. Option B is a prognostic scoring tool used for non-Hodgkin lymphoma risk stratification rather than an anatomic staging system. Option C is the staging system used for chronic lymphocytic leukemia, an unrelated disease. Option D is the staging system used specifically for multiple myeloma, not lymphoma.", "case_id": "BL2-600", "case_text": "A 29-year-old woman presents with a two-month history of painless swelling in her left axilla along with drenching night sweats and an unintentional 12-pound weight loss. She reports intermittent low-grade fevers. Examination reveals a firm, rubbery, nontender 4 cm axillary lymph node. Chest imaging reveals a mediastinal mass. There is no hepatosplenomegaly.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 603, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 72-year-old man with stable coronary artery disease and no active bleeding has a hemoglobin of 7.4 g/dL found on routine preoperative labs before an elective hip replacement. He is asymptomatic at rest. Which of the following is the most appropriate transfusion threshold to apply in deciding whether to transfuse packed red blood cells preoperatively?", "options": {"A": "Transfuse mainly if hemoglobin falls below 5 g/dL, largely independent of cardiac history", "B": "Transfuse using a restrictive threshold around 8 g/dL given his cardiovascular disease", "C": "Transfuse to a target hemoglobin of 12 g/dL to maximize surgical safety margin", "D": "Avoid transfusion for now until overt bleeding or hemodynamic instability develops"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Transfuse using a restrictive threshold around 8 g/dL given his cardiovascular disease): patients with active cardiovascular disease are generally given a somewhat higher transfusion threshold, around 8 g/dL, than the roughly 7 g/dL threshold used in otherwise healthy stable patients, since these patients have less physiologic reserve to compensate for reduced oxygen-carrying capacity. Option A sets the threshold far too low and does not account for his cardiovascular risk. Option C targets an unnecessarily high hemoglobin level that exposes the patient to needless transfusion risk without added benefit. Option D fails to account for his elevated-risk threshold and could allow clinically significant anemia to persist unnecessarily before surgery."}, {"id": 604, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 55-year-old woman develops fever and chills fifteen minutes into a transfusion of packed red blood cells. Her blood pressure and oxygen saturation remain stable, and there is no hemoglobinuria or flank pain. Repeat type and crossmatch confirms no clerical error and no evidence of hemolysis. She has otherwise been healthy with no chronic medical conditions and takes no regular medications. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Acute hemolytic transfusion reaction", "B": "Febrile nonhemolytic transfusion reaction", "C": "Transfusion-related acute lung injury", "D": "Anaphylactic transfusion reaction"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Febrile nonhemolytic transfusion reaction): febrile nonhemolytic transfusion reaction is the most common transfusion reaction and results from cytokines accumulated in stored donor blood products, producing fever and chills without hemodynamic instability, hemolysis, or a clerical mismatch, exactly as described here, and it is prevented in future transfusions by using leukoreduced blood products. Option A would show evidence of hemolysis such as hemoglobinuria, flank pain, and hypotension, none of which are present here, and would typically reflect a clerical ABO mismatch. Option C presents with acute hypoxemia and pulmonary infiltrates rather than isolated fever with stable oxygen saturation. Option D presents with hypotension, urticaria, or respiratory compromise rather than isolated fever and chills."}, {"id": 606, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 68-year-old woman with congestive heart failure develops new hypertension, jugular venous distension, and bibasilar crackles four hours after receiving two units of packed red blood cells for chronic anemia. Chest imaging reveals new pulmonary edema. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Transfusion-related acute lung injury from donor antibody-mediated neutrophil activation", "B": "Transfusion-associated circulatory overload from excessive volume infused too quickly", "C": "Acute hemolytic transfusion reaction from ABO incompatibility", "D": "Anaphylactic transfusion reaction related to IgA deficiency"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Transfusion-associated circulatory overload from excessive volume infused too quickly): transfusion-associated circulatory overload results from infusing blood products faster or in greater volume than a patient's cardiovascular system, especially one already compromised by heart failure, can accommodate, producing hypertension and signs of volume overload such as elevated jugular venous pressure and pulmonary edema, in contrast to the hypotension typically seen with TRALI. Option A classically presents with hypotension rather than hypertension and occurs from an immune mechanism rather than simple volume overload. Option C would show hemolysis markers, fever, and hypotension rather than isolated volume overload. Option D would present with urticaria, wheezing, or hypotension rather than isolated pulmonary edema and hypertension."}, {"id": 607, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 30-year-old man receives a unit of packed red blood cells intended for a different patient due to a clerical labeling error. Within ten minutes he develops fever, severe flank pain, hypotension, and dark urine. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate step in his management?", "options": {"A": "Slow the infusion rate and administer acetaminophen for symptomatic relief", "B": "Administer intravenous corticosteroids and continue the transfusion at a reduced rate", "C": "Stop the transfusion immediately and begin aggressive intravenous fluid resuscitation", "D": "Switch to a different unit of blood and resume transfusion once symptoms improve"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop the transfusion immediately and begin aggressive intravenous fluid resuscitation): this presentation represents an acute hemolytic transfusion reaction from ABO incompatibility caused by a clerical error, and the immediate priority is to stop the transfusion right away to prevent further hemolysis, followed by aggressive fluid resuscitation to protect renal function and support blood pressure. Option A is dangerously inadequate because continuing to infuse incompatible blood, even slowly, continues the hemolytic process. Option B is wrong because the transfusion must be stopped entirely, not merely slowed, and corticosteroids are not the priority intervention here. Option D is dangerous because resuming transfusion once symptoms improve does not address the underlying incompatibility and risks further hemolysis."}, {"id": 610, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 47-year-old man with a warfarin-associated intracranial hemorrhage requires rapid reversal of his anticoagulation. Which of the following blood products provides the fastest and most complete correction of his coagulopathy?", "options": {"A": "Cryoprecipitate transfusion alone", "B": "Packed red blood cells", "C": "Four-factor prothrombin complex concentrate", "D": "Platelet transfusion alone"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Four-factor prothrombin complex concentrate): four-factor prothrombin complex concentrate contains concentrated vitamin K-dependent clotting factors and reverses warfarin-associated coagulopathy far more rapidly and completely than fresh frozen plasma, making it the preferred agent for emergent reversal in life-threatening bleeding such as an intracranial hemorrhage. Option A replaces fibrinogen and factor VIII and is not the appropriate agent for reversing warfarin's effect on the vitamin K-dependent factors. Option B corrects anemia and does not address the underlying coagulopathy at all. Option D addresses platelet-related bleeding disorders, not a vitamin K-dependent factor deficiency from warfarin."}, {"id": 614, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 3-year-old boy with hemophilia A is brought to the emergency department with an acutely swollen, painful right knee after tripping while running. His parents report no family history of a similar bleeding disorder in any other relative. He has met all expected developmental milestones to date and has never previously undergone any surgical procedure. His parents are both in good health with no known bleeding tendencies of their own. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate treatment for this acute hemarthrosis?", "options": {"A": "Oral aspirin for pain control alone", "B": "Prompt intravenous factor VIII replacement", "C": "Fresh frozen plasma as first-line therapy", "D": "Observation alone without any factor replacement"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Prompt intravenous factor VIII replacement): acute hemarthrosis in a patient with hemophilia A should be treated promptly with intravenous factor VIII replacement to stop ongoing bleeding into the joint and to prevent the chronic joint damage that results from repeated, undertreated bleeding episodes. Option A is dangerous because aspirin impairs platelet function and can worsen bleeding in a patient who already has a clotting factor deficiency. Option C is not the preferred replacement product for isolated factor VIII deficiency, since factor concentrate provides much more specific and concentrated correction. Option D risks progressive joint damage from repeated undertreated bleeding, which is a well known long-term complication of hemophilia when factor replacement is delayed or withheld."}, {"id": 615, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "He also has progressive dyspnea on exertion, no known drug allergies, and has not previously been evaluated or treated for any HIV-related illness. Which of the following is the most likely cause of his pulmonary findings?", "options": {"A": "Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia", "B": "Bacterial pneumonia", "C": "Mycobacterium avium infection", "D": "Pulmonary Kaposi sarcoma"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia): a CD4 count below 200 cells per microliter defines a substantially increased risk for Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia, which classically presents with progressive dyspnea, a dry cough, and diffuse bilateral ground-glass opacities on imaging, exactly as described in this patient, and this combination is one of the most heavily tested opportunistic infections in HIV medicine. Option B typically presents more acutely with focal consolidation and productive cough rather than this subacute, diffuse ground-glass pattern. Option C typically occurs at CD4 counts below 50 and usually presents with disseminated symptoms such as fevers, weight loss, and diarrhea rather than this isolated pulmonary picture. Option D would present with characteristic cutaneous or endobronchial violaceous lesions rather than diffuse ground-glass infiltrates alone.", "case_id": "BL3-615", "case_text": "A 38-year-old man with a history of intravenous drug use presents with two months of progressive weight loss, night sweats, and a dry cough. He has not seen a physician in over five years. Fourth-generation HIV antigen and antibody testing returns positive, and a subsequent CD4 count is 90 cells/microliter. Chest imaging reveals diffuse bilateral ground-glass opacities.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 616, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "He has tolerated trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole in the past without any adverse reaction or rash of any kind. His most recent CD4 count, obtained at the same visit as his diagnosis, remains at 90 cells per microliter. He lives with a supportive family member who assists with his medication adherence. Given this patient's CD4 count of 90 cells per microliter, which of the following opportunistic infection prophylaxis regimens should be initiated at this time, in addition to prophylaxis already indicated for his current CD4 level?", "options": {"A": "Prophylaxis against cytomegalovirus retinitis only, since his CD4 count is above the relevant threshold for other agents", "B": "No additional prophylaxis is indicated until his CD4 count falls below 50 cells per microliter", "C": "Prophylaxis against Mycobacterium avium complex only, since Pneumocystis prophylaxis is not indicated until treatment response is confirmed", "D": "Prophylaxis against both Pneumocystis jirovecii and Toxoplasma gondii, given his CD4 count below 200 and below 100"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Prophylaxis against both Pneumocystis jirovecii and Toxoplasma gondii, given his CD4 count below 200 and below 100): a CD4 count below 200 warrants Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia prophylaxis and a CD4 count below 100 additionally warrants Toxoplasma gondii prophylaxis, so at 90 cells per microliter both should be started, typically with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, which conveniently covers both organisms. Option A is wrong because cytomegalovirus prophylaxis is not routinely indicated at this CD4 level in the way Pneumocystis and Toxoplasma prophylaxis are. Option B is wrong because both Pneumocystis and Toxoplasma prophylaxis are already indicated at his current CD4 count, not only below 50. Option C incorrectly omits Pneumocystis prophylaxis, which is clearly indicated at this CD4 level, and Mycobacterium avium complex prophylaxis is not yet indicated until the count falls below 50.", "case_id": "BL3-615", "case_text": "A 38-year-old man with a history of intravenous drug use presents with two months of progressive weight loss, night sweats, and a dry cough. He has not seen a physician in over five years. Fourth-generation HIV antigen and antibody testing returns positive, and a subsequent CD4 count is 90 cells/microliter. Chest imaging reveals diffuse bilateral ground-glass opacities.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 617, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "This patient is started on antiretroviral therapy along with appropriate opportunistic infection prophylaxis. He has no other chronic medical conditions and takes no other prescription medications at this time. He denies any known sulfa allergy and has never previously required hospitalization for an opportunistic infection. He has reliable access to follow-up care at an outpatient infectious disease clinic. Which of the following statements regarding the timing of starting antiretroviral therapy in a newly diagnosed patient with HIV is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Antiretroviral therapy should be delayed until the CD4 count rises above 200 with prophylaxis alone", "B": "Antiretroviral therapy should be delayed until any active opportunistic infection is fully treated and resolved", "C": "Antiretroviral therapy should be reserved for patients with a CD4 count below 500", "D": "Antiretroviral therapy should be started promptly regardless of CD4 count, since early treatment reduces disease progression and transmission"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Antiretroviral therapy should be started promptly regardless of CD4 count, since early treatment reduces disease progression and transmission): current guidelines recommend starting antiretroviral therapy in essentially all patients diagnosed with HIV as soon as possible, regardless of CD4 count, since early initiation reduces the risk of disease progression, opportunistic infection, and onward transmission of the virus to others. Option A incorrectly suggests waiting for CD4 recovery through prophylaxis alone before treating the underlying infection, which is not the current standard of care. Option B is generally not required, since antiretroviral therapy is often started concurrently with treatment of an active opportunistic infection rather than delayed until full resolution. Option C incorrectly restricts therapy to only those with more advanced disease, contrary to current universal treatment recommendations.", "case_id": "BL3-615", "case_text": "A 38-year-old man with a history of intravenous drug use presents with two months of progressive weight loss, night sweats, and a dry cough. He has not seen a physician in over five years. Fourth-generation HIV antigen and antibody testing returns positive, and a subsequent CD4 count is 90 cells/microliter. Chest imaging reveals diffuse bilateral ground-glass opacities.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 621, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 4-year-old boy has a history of recurrent pneumonias, lymphadenitis, and a hepatic abscess, all caused by Staphylococcus aureus and Aspergillus species. The laboratory panel shown, including a dihydrorhodamine flow cytometry assay, is obtained. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Severe combined immunodeficiency", "B": "X-linked agammaglobulinemia", "C": "Chronic granulomatous disease", "D": "Terminal complement deficiency"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Chronic granulomatous disease): chronic granulomatous disease results from a defective NADPH oxidase complex that impairs the neutrophil oxidative burst, leaving phagocytes unable to kill catalase-positive organisms such as Staphylococcus aureus and Aspergillus, and it is confirmed with an abnormal dihydrorhodamine or nitroblue tetrazolium test showing reduced oxidative burst activity, consistent with this child's recurrent infections and the panel shown. Option A would present with much broader, more severe, and earlier-onset infections including viral and fungal opportunists beyond just catalase-positive organisms. Option B would present with recurrent sinopulmonary infections from encapsulated bacteria and low immunoglobulin levels, not this catalase-positive organism pattern. Option D would predispose specifically to recurrent Neisseria infections rather than Staphylococcus and Aspergillus.", "image": "question_images/q_621.png"}, {"id": 622, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 24-year-old woman develops facial swelling, diffuse urticaria, wheezing, and hypotension within two minutes of eating shellfish at a restaurant. She denies any prior episodes of severe allergic reaction and has no other known food or drug allergies. She reports having eaten shellfish on prior occasions without any adverse reaction until this most recent exposure. She was otherwise in her usual state of good health prior to this meal. Which of the following immune mechanisms best explains her reaction?", "options": {"A": "Mast cell and basophil degranulation triggered by IgE bound to the cell surface", "B": "Deposition of antigen-antibody immune complexes within small blood vessels", "C": "Direct antibody binding to cell surface antigens causing cellular destruction", "D": "Sensitized T lymphocytes releasing cytokines one to several days after exposure"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Mast cell and basophil degranulation triggered by IgE bound to the cell surface): this presentation is a classic type I hypersensitivity reaction, in which pre-formed IgE antibodies bound to mast cells and basophils bind the food allergen and trigger rapid degranulation, releasing histamine and other mediators that cause the immediate urticaria, angioedema, bronchospasm, and hypotension of anaphylaxis. Option B describes type III hypersensitivity, which produces a delayed, immune complex-mediated reaction rather than this immediate presentation. Option C describes type II hypersensitivity, a distinct antibody-mediated cytotoxic mechanism not responsible for anaphylaxis. Option D describes type IV hypersensitivity, which is delayed by one to several days and mediated by T cells rather than IgE, the opposite timeline from what is described here."}, {"id": 623, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 33-year-old man presents with hemoptysis and progressive renal failure. Renal biopsy reveals linear deposition of antibody along the glomerular basement membrane, and serum testing confirms circulating antibodies directed against type IV collagen. He reports no fevers, chills, or a recent upper respiratory infection preceding this presentation. His renal function and urinalysis obtained on this same visit are both within normal limits at presentation. He lives with both parents and has no known exposure to industrial chemicals. Which type of hypersensitivity reaction best describes this disease process?", "options": {"A": "Type I hypersensitivity", "B": "Type IV hypersensitivity", "C": "Type II hypersensitivity", "D": "Type III hypersensitivity"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Type II hypersensitivity): Goodpasture syndrome is a type II hypersensitivity reaction in which antibodies bind directly to a fixed tissue antigen, in this case type IV collagen within the glomerular and alveolar basement membranes, producing the characteristic linear immunofluorescence pattern and causing simultaneous pulmonary hemorrhage and glomerulonephritis. Option A describes an IgE-mediated immediate reaction, an entirely different mechanism from direct antibody binding to a fixed tissue antigen. Option B describes a delayed, T cell-mediated reaction without antibody deposition, which does not match the linear antibody staining described here. Option D describes immune complex deposition, which produces a granular rather than linear immunofluorescence pattern."}, {"id": 624, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 28-year-old man develops fever, diffuse urticarial rash, joint pain, and lymphadenopathy about ten days after receiving an antivenom derived from horse serum for a snake bite. He reports no prior reaction to horse-derived products and had never previously received antivenom therapy of any kind. He required emergent evaluation for the snake bite itself two days prior to the onset of these new symptoms. He was treated at an outside facility before transfer to this hospital. Which of the following best describes the underlying mechanism of this reaction?", "options": {"A": "IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation occurring within minutes of exposure", "B": "Deposition of antigen-antibody immune complexes in tissues, activating complement", "C": "Direct antibody-mediated destruction of circulating platelets", "D": "Delayed T cell-mediated cytokine release without antibody involvement"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Deposition of antigen-antibody immune complexes in tissues, activating complement): serum sickness is the prototypical type III hypersensitivity reaction, occurring roughly one to two weeks after exposure to a foreign protein such as horse serum-derived antivenom, once enough antibody has formed to create immune complexes with the circulating antigen; these complexes deposit in tissues such as joints and skin and activate complement, producing fever, urticaria, arthralgias, and lymphadenopathy. Option A describes an immediate reaction occurring within minutes, not the delayed one to two week timeline described here. Option C describes a type II mechanism targeting a specific cell type, not the multisystem immune complex process described. Option D describes a type IV reaction, which does not involve antibody or immune complex formation at all."}, {"id": 625, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 30-year-old woman develops an intensely pruritic, erythematous rash in a linear pattern on her wrist two days after wearing a new metal watch band. Patch testing confirms a positive reaction to nickel. She denies any prior reaction to jewelry or other metal-containing objects before this most recent exposure. She has otherwise been in good health and takes no regular prescription medications aside from an oral contraceptive. She works as an administrative assistant and has no significant occupational exposures. Which of the following best describes the immune mechanism underlying this reaction?", "options": {"A": "Pre-formed IgE antibody triggering mast cell degranulation within minutes of first exposure", "B": "Immune complex deposition within the dermal microvasculature triggering local complement activation", "C": "Sensitized T lymphocytes releasing cytokines that recruit inflammatory cells over one to several days", "D": "Direct antibody binding to keratinocyte surface antigens causing cell lysis and blistering"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Sensitized T lymphocytes releasing cytokines that recruit inflammatory cells over one to several days): allergic contact dermatitis, as with nickel exposure, is a type IV delayed hypersensitivity reaction in which T lymphocytes previously sensitized to the allergen are reactivated upon re-exposure and recruit additional inflammatory cells over one to several days, producing the characteristic delayed, localized, pruritic dermatitis pattern confirmed by patch testing. Option A describes an immediate IgE-mediated reaction, which would occur within minutes rather than days and would not be confirmed by patch testing. Option B describes a type III mechanism, which does not match this cell-mediated, delayed presentation. Option D describes a type II mechanism involving direct antibody-mediated cell destruction, which is not how contact dermatitis develops."}, {"id": 626, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "An 8-month-old boy has had three episodes of otitis media and one episode of pneumococcal pneumonia since birth, all after his maternally transferred antibodies would be expected to have waned. Laboratory studies reveal markedly low levels of all immunoglobulin classes and absent circulating B cells, with normal T cell numbers and function. His growth and development have otherwise been age-appropriate, and there is no family history of a similar immunodeficiency. His parents report that he has otherwise fed and grown well since birth aside from these recurrent infectious episodes. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "DiGeorge syndrome", "B": "Chronic granulomatous disease", "C": "Leukocyte adhesion deficiency", "D": "X-linked agammaglobulinemia"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (X-linked agammaglobulinemia): X-linked agammaglobulinemia results from a mutation in Bruton tyrosine kinase that blocks B cell maturation, leading to absent circulating B cells and markedly low levels of all immunoglobulin classes, with normal T cell function, and it classically presents with recurrent infections by encapsulated organisms once maternal antibody protection wanes around six months of age, exactly as described in this infant. Option A involves a T cell, not a B cell, defect and would show abnormal rather than normal T cell numbers and function. Option B involves a neutrophil oxidative burst defect and would not cause absent B cells or low immunoglobulins. Option C involves a neutrophil adhesion and migration defect, unrelated to B cell development or immunoglobulin levels."}, {"id": 628, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 58-year-old woman with a fifteen-year history of rheumatoid arthritis presents with recurrent bacterial infections. Examination reveals splenomegaly, and laboratory studies reveal a persistently low absolute neutrophil count. Her rheumatoid factor is strongly positive, and she has required two hospitalizations for infection over the past year. Her joint examination reveals symmetric synovitis of the small joints of both hands in addition to the described findings. She has been followed by rheumatology for the past several years for her arthritis. Which of the following terms best describes this clinical syndrome?", "options": {"A": "Sjogren syndrome", "B": "Felty syndrome", "C": "Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome", "D": "Evans syndrome"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Felty syndrome): Felty syndrome describes the triad of rheumatoid arthritis, splenomegaly, and neutropenia, and it predisposes affected patients to recurrent bacterial infections due to the associated neutropenia, representing an important hematologic complication of long-standing rheumatoid arthritis to recognize on the exam. Option A describes an autoimmune exocrine gland disorder causing dry eyes and dry mouth, unrelated to neutropenia and splenomegaly. Option C describes a distinct hypercoagulable autoimmune condition, not a triad involving neutropenia and splenomegaly in a rheumatoid arthritis patient. Option D describes the combination of autoimmune hemolytic anemia and immune thrombocytopenia together, a different hematologic syndrome unrelated to rheumatoid arthritis specifically."}, {"id": 629, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 34-year-old woman presents with recurrent sinopulmonary infections that began in her twenties, unlike the infantile onset typical of most primary antibody deficiencies. Laboratory studies reveal low levels of IgG and IgA with a normal or low IgM, and a poor antibody response to vaccination. B cell numbers are normal. She has no personal or family history of a separately diagnosed primary immunodeficiency prior to this evaluation. Her chest imaging obtained at this visit reveals no evidence of bronchiectasis or acute infiltrate at this time. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Common variable immunodeficiency", "B": "X-linked agammaglobulinemia", "C": "Selective IgA deficiency", "D": "Severe combined immunodeficiency"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Common variable immunodeficiency): common variable immunodeficiency is characterized by low immunoglobulin levels and impaired antibody responses to vaccination despite normal circulating B cell numbers, and unlike most primary antibody deficiencies it classically presents later, often in the second or third decade of life, with recurrent sinopulmonary infections, matching this patient's presentation closely. Option B would present in infancy with absent, not normal, circulating B cell numbers. Option C would show an isolated low IgA level with normal IgG and IgM, not the broader pattern described here, and often causes milder or no symptoms. Option D presents in early infancy with severe, broad-spectrum, often opportunistic infections rather than this later-onset, milder pattern."}, {"id": 630, "domain": "blood_lymphoreticular_and_immune_systems", "question": "A 30-year-old woman with systemic lupus erythematosus and known antiphospholipid antibodies presents with fatigue and easy bruising. Laboratory studies reveal hemoglobin 8.5 g/dL with a positive direct Coombs test, platelets 42,000/uL, and a prolonged aPTT that fails to correct on mixing study. She has been adherent with her prescribed hydroxychloroquine and low-dose prednisone throughout this presentation. Her most recent outpatient laboratory panel, obtained two weeks prior, showed a hemoglobin of 11.2 g/dL and a normal platelet count. Which of the following best summarizes the combined pathophysiology underlying her presentation?", "options": {"A": "A single unifying inherited coagulation factor deficiency explains all three laboratory abnormalities", "B": "Bone marrow infiltration by a hematologic malignancy explains both the anemia and thrombocytopenia", "C": "Disseminated intravascular coagulation from an undiagnosed infection explains this entire laboratory picture", "D": "Overlapping autoimmune mechanisms, including antibody-mediated hemolysis, immune thrombocytopenia, and antiphospholipid antibodies, together explain her findings"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Overlapping autoimmune mechanisms, including antibody-mediated hemolysis, immune thrombocytopenia, and antiphospholipid antibodies, together explain her findings): systemic lupus erythematosus frequently produces overlapping autoimmune hematologic manifestations, including warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia from red cell-directed autoantibodies, immune thrombocytopenia from platelet-directed autoantibodies, and a prolonged aPTT from antiphospholipid antibodies interfering with the assay in vitro, and recognizing that all three findings can arise from one underlying autoimmune disease is the key teaching point tying this domain together. Option A does not explain a positive Coombs test or antiphospholipid-associated aPTT prolongation, both of which reflect immune rather than inherited factor mechanisms. Option B is not supported by the vignette, which describes findings entirely explained by known autoimmune disease rather than any evidence of marrow infiltration. Option C is less likely given the chronic autoimmune disease context and lack of any described acute infectious trigger."}, {"id": 631, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 52-year-old man comes to the office with an 8-month history of burning retrosternal discomfort that occurs after large meals and when he lies down at night. He has no dysphagia, no weight loss, no gastrointestinal bleeding, and no anemia. He has never taken any medication for these symptoms. Vital signs and physical examination are normal. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": {"A": "Empiric trial of a proton pump inhibitor with lifestyle modification", "B": "Upper endoscopy performed right away before starting any medication trial", "C": "24-hour esophageal pH monitoring performed while off all antireflux therapy", "D": "Barium esophagram obtained specifically to evaluate esophageal motility disorders"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Empiric trial of a proton pump inhibitor with lifestyle modification): a patient under 60 with typical reflux symptoms and no alarm features such as dysphagia, weight loss, or bleeding can be started on empiric acid suppression without prior endoscopy. Option B is incorrect because endoscopy is reserved for patients with alarm features or those who fail empiric therapy. Option C is not the initial step since pH monitoring is used when the diagnosis is unclear or before an antireflux procedure. Option D evaluates motility disorders rather than typical reflux disease and is not indicated here."}, {"id": 634, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 70-year-old woman reports 4 months of early satiety, a 6-kg unintentional weight loss, and mild epigastric discomfort. Laboratory studies show a hemoglobin of 9.8 g/dL with low ferritin. She has no history of NSAID use. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in evaluation?", "options": {"A": "Abdominal ultrasound to evaluate the gallbladder", "B": "Reassurance and iron supplementation alone", "C": "Upper endoscopy with biopsy of any suspicious lesion", "D": "Empiric proton pump inhibitor trial for 8 weeks before further testing"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Upper endoscopy with biopsy of any suspicious lesion): an older patient with new dyspepsia, weight loss, and iron deficiency anemia has alarm features that raise concern for gastric malignancy and warrants prompt endoscopic evaluation with biopsy. Option D delays diagnosis in a patient who already has alarm features requiring endoscopy rather than empiric therapy. Option A addresses biliary disease, which does not explain this constellation of findings. Option B fails to investigate the underlying cause of the anemia and weight loss, which could represent a treatable malignancy."}, {"id": 636, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 74-year-old man with hypertension presents with three episodes of painless bright red blood per rectum over the past day. He has no abdominal pain, fever, or prior similar episodes. Vital signs are stable. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Toxic megacolon", "B": "Diverticular bleeding", "C": "Acute mesenteric ischemia", "D": "Perforated peptic ulcer"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Diverticular bleeding): painless, brisk hematochezia in an older adult without abdominal pain is the classic presentation of bleeding from an eroded vessel at the neck of a colonic diverticulum. Option C typically presents with severe abdominal pain out of proportion to examination findings, not painless bleeding alone. Option D would present with sudden severe abdominal pain and signs of peritonitis rather than isolated rectal bleeding. Option A is a complication of severe colitis with abdominal distension and systemic toxicity, not an isolated bleeding episode."}, {"id": 637, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 63-year-old man presents with melena. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin 10.2 g/dL, blood urea nitrogen 42 mg/dL, and creatinine 0.9 mg/dL. Which of the following best explains the disproportionate elevation of the blood urea nitrogen relative to creatinine in this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Lower gastrointestinal bleeding preferentially elevates blood urea nitrogen in this setting", "B": "Hepatic synthetic dysfunction reduces creatinine production, falsely lowering the calculated ratio here", "C": "Digestion and absorption of blood protein from an upper gastrointestinal source raises nitrogen load", "D": "Acute kidney injury from volume depletion has selectively raised his creatinine clearance rate"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Digestion and absorption of blood protein from an upper gastrointestinal source raises nitrogen load): blood protein sitting in the small bowel from an upper GI bleed is digested and its nitrogen absorbed, raising blood urea nitrogen out of proportion to creatinine, a classic clue favoring an upper GI source. Option D describes the opposite mechanism from what is happening and would not explain this specific ratio pattern. Option A is incorrect since a disproportionate BUN rise favors an upper, not lower, source. Option B describes a mechanism unrelated to the classic BUN to creatinine ratio finding in GI bleeding.", "image": "question_images/q_637.png"}, {"id": 638, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 55-year-old woman with alcohol use disorder and known cirrhosis is found to have hemoglobin 6.8 g/dL after an episode of hematemesis. She is hemodynamically stable, has no chest pain, and has no history of coronary artery disease. Which of the following hemoglobin thresholds most appropriately guides the decision to transfuse red blood cells in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Transfuse only after hematocrit falls below 15 percent", "B": "Transfuse to maintain hemoglobin above approximately 7 g/dL", "C": "Transfuse to maintain hemoglobin above approximately 10 g/dL in patients with active cardiac disease", "D": "Avoid transfusion until hemoglobin falls below 5 g/dL"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Transfuse to maintain hemoglobin above approximately 7 g/dL): a restrictive transfusion strategy targeting a hemoglobin threshold near 7 g/dL is appropriate for a hemodynamically stable patient without active cardiac disease and has been shown to improve outcomes compared with a more liberal strategy. Option C describes a liberal threshold reserved for patients with active cardiac ischemia or ongoing uncontrolled bleeding, which does not apply here. Option D delays necessary transfusion in a patient who is already significantly anemic. Option A is not a standard transfusion trigger used in this clinical context.", "image": "question_images/q_638.png"}, {"id": 639, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 29-year-old man presents with a 4-month history of crampy periumbilical pain, nonbloody diarrhea, and a 5-kg weight loss. He also reports a perianal fistula that has drained intermittently. Colonoscopy shows skip areas of inflammation with normal mucosa in between, and biopsy reveals transmural inflammation with noncaseating granulomas. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Crohn disease", "B": "Ulcerative colitis", "C": "Irritable bowel syndrome", "D": "Ischemic colitis"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Crohn disease): skip lesions, transmural inflammation with granulomas, and perianal fistulizing disease are all classic features of Crohn disease, which can affect any segment of the gastrointestinal tract. Option B is characterized by continuous mucosal and submucosal inflammation beginning in the rectum, without granulomas or fistulas of this type. Option C is a functional disorder without mucosal inflammation or granulomas on biopsy. Option D typically presents acutely in older patients with vascular risk factors and shows a distinct histologic pattern of mucosal necrosis rather than granulomatous transmural disease."}, {"id": 641, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 41-year-old man with a 6-year history of ulcerative colitis is admitted with severe bloody diarrhea, fever, and abdominal distension. Abdominal radiograph shows colonic dilation to 7 cm with loss of haustral markings. He does not improve after 48 hours of intravenous corticosteroids. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Start an oral 5-aminosalicylate agent", "B": "Discharge home with outpatient gastroenterology follow-up", "C": "Urgent surgical evaluation for possible colectomy", "D": "Increase the corticosteroid dose and continue for another week"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Urgent surgical evaluation for possible colectomy): this presentation is toxic megacolon, and failure to improve after 48 to 72 hours of intravenous corticosteroids in this setting is an indication for urgent surgical evaluation given the risk of perforation. Option D prolongs medical therapy in a patient who has already failed an adequate steroid trial and who is at risk of a life-threatening complication. Option A is not effective for an acute severe flare and would not address the emergency at hand. Option B is unsafe given the risk of colonic perforation and sepsis in untreated toxic megacolon."}, {"id": 642, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 26-year-old man with Crohn disease involving the terminal ileum has been on mesalamine for 8 months with persistent diarrhea, abdominal pain, and a new perianal fistula. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": {"A": "Proceed directly to segmental bowel resection", "B": "Add a second 5-aminosalicylate agent for combination therapy", "C": "Transition to an anti-TNF biologic agent", "D": "Increase the mesalamine dose and reassess in 8 more months"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Transition to an anti-TNF biologic agent): 5-aminosalicylate agents have limited efficacy in Crohn disease, and a patient with ongoing symptoms and fistulizing disease despite mesalamine should be escalated to a more effective agent such as an anti-TNF biologic. Option D relies on a medication class known to be less effective for Crohn disease and delays appropriate escalation. Option A is not the first step for medically manageable fistulizing disease without evidence of an abscess or fixed stricture. Option B combines two agents from a class already known to be inadequate for this indication."}, {"id": 644, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 55-year-old woman is found to have chronic hepatitis C infection on screening, confirmed by a positive HCV RNA test. Liver biopsy shows minimal fibrosis and she is otherwise asymptomatic with normal liver enzymes. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?", "options": {"A": "Treat only if she develops symptoms of liver failure", "B": "Initiate direct acting antiviral therapy now", "C": "Defer treatment until she develops advanced fibrosis or cirrhosis", "D": "Defer treatment until liver enzymes become abnormal"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Initiate direct acting antiviral therapy now): current guidance recommends treatment for essentially all patients with chronic hepatitis C regardless of fibrosis stage, since eradication prevents progression and transmission even in patients with minimal liver damage. Option C unnecessarily delays a highly effective, well-tolerated treatment and allows continued risk of progression and transmission. Option D is incorrect because normal liver enzymes do not exclude significant infection or the benefit of treatment. Option A waits far too long, since treatment is most beneficial before advanced liver disease develops."}, {"id": 647, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 59-year-old woman with cirrhosis undergoes diagnostic paracentesis for new ascites. Serum albumin is 3.8 g/dL and ascitic fluid albumin is 1.2 g/dL. Which of the following best interprets this finding?", "options": {"A": "The gradient cannot be interpreted without a 24-hour urine collection", "B": "A serum-ascites albumin gradient of 2.6 g/dL indicates portal hypertension as the cause", "C": "A serum-ascites albumin gradient of 2.6 g/dL indicates peritoneal carcinomatosis, a condition that instead classically produces a gradient below 1.1 g/dL", "D": "A serum-ascites albumin gradient below 1.1 g/dL indicates cirrhosis as the cause"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A serum-ascites albumin gradient of 2.6 g/dL indicates portal hypertension as the cause): subtracting the ascitic fluid albumin from the serum albumin gives a gradient of 2.6 g/dL, and a gradient of 1.1 g/dL or greater indicates a portal hypertensive cause such as cirrhosis. Option C describes the wrong interpretation, since a gradient this high favors portal hypertension rather than a malignant or infectious low-gradient cause. Option D states an incorrect gradient value that does not match these numbers and also mismatches the association, since cirrhosis produces a high, not low, gradient. Option A is unnecessary since the gradient is calculated directly from paired serum and ascitic fluid albumin values."}, {"id": 648, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 66-year-old man with cirrhosis and ascites presents with abdominal pain and worsening confusion. Diagnostic paracentesis shows an ascitic fluid absolute neutrophil count of 420 cells/mm3. Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment?", "options": {"A": "Observation with repeat paracentesis in one week", "B": "Intravenous antibiotics with albumin infusion", "C": "Oral antibiotics alone without albumin", "D": "Large volume paracentesis alone without antibiotics"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Intravenous antibiotics with albumin infusion): an ascitic fluid neutrophil count of 250 cells/mm3 or higher confirms spontaneous bacterial peritonitis, which is treated with intravenous antibiotics, and albumin infusion is added in patients with significant liver or renal dysfunction to reduce the risk of hepatorenal syndrome. Option C uses an inadequate route and omits albumin, which provides additional benefit in appropriate patients. Option D fails to treat the underlying infection, which requires antibiotic therapy. Option A delays urgently needed treatment for a potentially life-threatening infection."}, {"id": 649, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 62-year-old man with cirrhosis is brought in by family for confusion and asterixis that developed over two days. He was recently started on a new opioid for back pain and has not had a bowel movement in four days. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial treatment?", "options": {"A": "High protein diet to correct malnutrition", "B": "Discontinue all bowel regimen medications immediately", "C": "Lactulose to reduce ammonia absorption", "D": "Intravenous mannitol to reduce cerebral edema"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Lactulose to reduce ammonia absorption): hepatic encephalopathy precipitated by constipation and opioid use is treated with lactulose, which acidifies the colon and traps ammonia as ammonium for excretion, along with addressing the precipitating factors. Option D treats cerebral edema from other causes, not the ammonia-mediated mechanism of hepatic encephalopathy. Option A is incorrect since a high protein diet would worsen ammonia production and encephalopathy in this setting. Option B is the opposite of appropriate management, since a bowel regimen such as lactulose should be started, not discontinued, given his constipation."}, {"id": 651, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 50-year-old woman with necrotizing pancreatitis, admitted 3 weeks ago, develops a new fever to 39.1 C and worsening leukocytosis. CT imaging shows gas within a peripancreatic fluid collection. She remains hemodynamically stable. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Delayed drainage of the collection once it is well organized, with antibiotics", "B": "Immediate open surgical debridement performed within the next hour, before allowing the collection time to organize", "C": "Observation alone without any antibiotics or drainage, despite the clinical signs suggesting infection has developed", "D": "Switch to oral antibiotics alone without any drainage procedure, even though the collection appears infected on imaging"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Delayed drainage of the collection once it is well organized, with antibiotics): infected pancreatic necrosis is suspected given gas within the collection and clinical deterioration, and in a stable patient, drainage is typically delayed until the collection has organized, paired with antibiotic therapy. Option B is not the preferred initial approach, since immediate aggressive surgery in an unorganized collection carries higher morbidity than a staged, delayed approach in a stable patient. Option C fails to address a likely infection requiring intervention. Option D is inadequate since drainage of an infected necrotic collection is typically required in addition to antibiotics."}, {"id": 652, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 39-year-old woman presents with sudden right upper quadrant pain after eating a fatty meal that resolved completely within two hours without any specific treatment. She has no fever and physical examination is unremarkable between episodes. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Acute pancreatitis", "B": "Biliary colic", "C": "Acute cholecystitis", "D": "Ascending cholangitis"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Biliary colic): transient right upper quadrant pain after a fatty meal that resolves completely, without fever or ongoing tenderness, is classic for biliary colic caused by a stone temporarily obstructing the cystic duct. Option C involves persistent inflammation with fever and a positive Murphy sign, not a transient, fully resolving episode. Option D presents with fever, jaundice, and pain together as part of the Charcot triad, which is not described here. Option A would present with epigastric pain radiating to the back along with elevated pancreatic enzymes, not a brief self-resolving episode."}, {"id": 653, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 45-year-old woman presents with right upper quadrant pain, fever to 38.6 C, and a positive Murphy sign. Ultrasound shows gallbladder wall thickening with pericholecystic fluid and a stone impacted in the neck of the gallbladder. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?", "options": {"A": "Observation alone since the stone may pass spontaneously", "B": "Intravenous antibiotics with cholecystectomy within 72 hours", "C": "Discharge home with oral antibiotics and outpatient follow-up", "D": "Emergent ERCP to remove the impacted stone"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Intravenous antibiotics with cholecystectomy within 72 hours): this presentation is acute cholecystitis, confirmed by ultrasound findings and a positive Murphy sign, and standard management is intravenous antibiotics followed by early cholecystectomy, ideally within 72 hours in a patient who can tolerate surgery. Option C is inappropriate given the systemic signs of infection that warrant inpatient management. Option D is used for common bile duct stones causing obstruction or cholangitis, not for a stone impacted in the gallbladder neck causing cholecystitis. Option A risks progression to gallbladder gangrene or perforation without definitive treatment."}, {"id": 654, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 71-year-old man presents with fever, jaundice, and right upper quadrant pain. Shortly after arrival he becomes hypotensive and confused. Laboratory studies show total bilirubin 8.9 mg/dL, alkaline phosphatase 410 U/L (reference range 44-147 U/L), and AST 88 U/L (reference range 10-40 U/L). Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Urgent biliary decompression, most often by ERCP, along with intravenous antibiotics", "B": "Outpatient oral antibiotics with follow-up in one week", "C": "Elective cholecystectomy scheduled for the following month", "D": "Observation with serial liver function tests over the next 48 hours"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Urgent biliary decompression, most often by ERCP, along with intravenous antibiotics): fever, jaundice, and right upper quadrant pain with new hypotension and confusion represent the Reynolds pentad of severe ascending cholangitis, a medical emergency requiring prompt biliary decompression and antibiotics. Option B is inadequate for a critically ill patient with evidence of sepsis and biliary obstruction. Option C delays urgently needed decompression and does not address the acute obstructing process directly. Option D risks clinical deterioration and death by delaying emergency intervention.", "image": "question_images/q_654.png"}, {"id": 658, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 76-year-old woman presents with left lower quadrant pain, low-grade fever, and a mild change in bowel habits over two days. CT imaging shows sigmoid diverticulitis with a 4 cm pericolic abscess. She is hemodynamically stable and tolerating oral intake. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?", "options": {"A": "Outpatient oral antibiotics alone without any further intervention at this time", "B": "Immediate colonoscopy to evaluate the abscess directly during the acute episode", "C": "Emergency sigmoid colectomy within the next few hours of admission", "D": "Hospitalization with intravenous antibiotics and percutaneous drainage of the abscess"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hospitalization with intravenous antibiotics and percutaneous drainage of the abscess): complicated diverticulitis with an abscess generally requires hospitalization, intravenous antibiotics, and percutaneous drainage when the abscess is large enough or fails to resolve with antibiotics alone. Option A is appropriate for uncomplicated diverticulitis without an abscess, not for this complicated presentation. Option B is contraindicated during an acute diverticulitis episode because of the risk of perforation. Option C is not the first-line approach for a stable patient with a drainable abscess, since drainage and antibiotics are typically tried first."}, {"id": 659, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 58-year-old man is treated successfully as an outpatient for a first episode of uncomplicated sigmoid diverticulitis. He asks when he should follow up for further evaluation. Which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation?", "options": {"A": "No further evaluation is needed after a single uncomplicated episode, even though malignancy has not yet been excluded by any imaging or endoscopic study", "B": "Immediate CT colonography while symptoms are still present", "C": "Colonoscopy in six to eight weeks after resolution of the acute episode", "D": "Colonoscopy during the acute episode before starting antibiotics"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Colonoscopy in six to eight weeks after resolution of the acute episode): colonoscopy is recommended after resolution of an episode of diverticulitis to exclude an underlying colorectal malignancy that could mimic or coexist with diverticular disease. Option D is unsafe because colonoscopy during active diverticulitis carries an increased risk of perforation. Option A misses the opportunity to exclude malignancy, which is standard practice after a diverticulitis episode. Option B is not appropriate during the acute phase for the same perforation risk concern as colonoscopy."}, {"id": 663, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 38-year-old man's father was diagnosed with colorectal cancer at age 52. The son is otherwise healthy and asymptomatic. Which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation for when he should begin colorectal cancer screening?", "options": {"A": "Begin screening at age 40, since that is earlier than ten years before his father's age at diagnosis", "B": "Begin screening at the standard average-risk age of 45", "C": "Begin screening only if he develops rectal bleeding", "D": "Begin screening at age 60 since his father's cancer was not early-onset, an approach that ignores the accepted definition of early-onset diagnosis under 60"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Begin screening at age 40, since that is earlier than ten years before his father's age at diagnosis): a first degree relative diagnosed with colorectal cancer, particularly before age 60, warrants earlier screening, generally beginning at age 40 or ten years before the relative's age at diagnosis, whichever is EARLIER (the smaller, younger age). Ten years before his father's diagnosis at 52 is 42, but 40 is earlier than 42, so screening should begin at 40. Option B applies to average risk individuals and does not account for his elevated familial risk. Option C waits for symptoms rather than screening proactively, which defeats the purpose of early detection. Option D delays screening well beyond what is appropriate given his family history."}, {"id": 664, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 24-year-old woman presents with a 5-month history of bloating, chronic diarrhea, and fatigue. Laboratory studies show a hemoglobin of 10.5 g/dL with low ferritin. She has no known IgA deficiency. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial test?", "options": {"A": "Stool ova and parasite examination alone", "B": "Colonoscopy with random biopsies throughout the colon", "C": "IgA tissue transglutaminase antibody with a total IgA level", "D": "Upper endoscopy with duodenal biopsy as the first test, which is generally performed after confirmatory serologic testing in most clinical protocols"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (IgA tissue transglutaminase antibody with a total IgA level): serologic testing with IgA tissue transglutaminase antibody, paired with a total IgA level to exclude selective IgA deficiency, is the preferred initial test for suspected celiac disease. Option D is used to confirm the diagnosis after positive serology rather than as the first-line test. Option A evaluates for parasitic infection, which is not suggested by this presentation and would not explain the iron deficiency pattern described. Option B evaluates the colon rather than the small intestine, which is the site of pathology in celiac disease.", "image": "question_images/q_664.png"}, {"id": 665, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 31-year-old woman has strongly positive celiac serology. She began a gluten-free diet three weeks ago on her own after reading about celiac disease online, and her symptoms have since improved. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step to confirm the diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Diagnose celiac disease based on symptom improvement alone, without confirming the diagnosis with any additional testing", "B": "Advise reintroduction of gluten before duodenal biopsy to avoid a false negative result", "C": "Proceed directly to duodenal biopsy while she remains gluten-free, accepting the risk of a falsely reassuring result", "D": "Repeat serology only, without any biopsy, to confirm the diagnosis with a second blood test"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Advise reintroduction of gluten before duodenal biopsy to avoid a false negative result): early gluten avoidance can normalize both serology and duodenal histology, so gluten should ideally be reintroduced before biopsy confirmation to avoid a falsely reassuring result. Option C risks a false negative biopsy since her villous changes may already be resolving on a gluten-free diet. Option D does not provide histologic confirmation, which remains part of the standard diagnostic approach. Option A is not sufficient for diagnosis, since symptom improvement alone does not confirm celiac disease and could reflect a placebo effect or an unrelated dietary change."}, {"id": 666, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 33-year-old woman with a 2-year history of Crohn disease affecting the terminal ileum develops worsening right lower quadrant pain, fever, and a palpable tender mass. CT imaging shows a 5 cm abscess adjacent to a segment of thickened, strictured ileum. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management?", "options": {"A": "Immediate segmental bowel resection without prior drainage", "B": "Increase her current 5-aminosalicylate dose alone", "C": "Start a biologic agent alone without addressing the abscess, an approach suited to luminal inflammatory activity rather than a drainable infected collection", "D": "Percutaneous drainage of the abscess with intravenous antibiotics"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Percutaneous drainage of the abscess with intravenous antibiotics): an intra-abdominal abscess complicating Crohn disease is generally managed initially with percutaneous drainage and antibiotics, reserving surgery for cases that fail this approach or have an inaccessible collection. Option A is more invasive than needed as a first step when percutaneous drainage is feasible. Option B is not effective for an abscess and Crohn disease in general responds poorly to 5-aminosalicylate agents. Option C is unsafe since starting immunosuppressive biologic therapy in the setting of an undrained abscess can worsen the infection."}, {"id": 670, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Crohn disease", "B": "Infectious colitis from Clostridioides difficile", "C": "Ischemic colitis", "D": "Ulcerative colitis"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ulcerative colitis): continuous mucosal and submucosal inflammation starting in the rectum and extending proximally without skip lesions, along with crypt abscesses and no granulomas, is the classic pattern of ulcerative colitis. Option A typically shows skip lesions, transmural inflammation, and granulomas, none of which are present in this biopsy description. Option B would show a distinct clinical history of antibiotic exposure and typically a different histologic and epidemiologic pattern rather than this continuous mucosal disease. Option C usually occurs in older patients with vascular risk factors and shows a different, often segmental, distribution correlating with watershed areas rather than a continuous rectosigmoid-to-transverse pattern.", "case_id": "GI-C2", "case_text": "A 27-year-old woman presents with a 5-month history of bloody diarrhea, tenesmus, and lower abdominal cramping. She has lost 3 kg. Colonoscopy shows continuous inflammation beginning in the rectum and extending to the mid-transverse colon, without any areas of normal intervening mucosa. Biopsy shows crypt abscesses confined to the mucosa and submucosa without granulomas.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 671, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "This patient is started on an oral 5-aminosalicylate agent for her moderate disease activity. Two months later she reports ongoing bloody diarrhea and cramping despite adherence to therapy. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Increase the current 5-aminosalicylate dose and reassess in six more months", "B": "Proceed directly to total colectomy", "C": "Switch to an antidiarrheal agent alone to control symptoms", "D": "Escalate therapy with corticosteroids for induction of remission"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Escalate therapy with corticosteroids for induction of remission): a patient with ongoing moderate to severe symptoms despite an adequate trial of 5-aminosalicylate therapy should be escalated to corticosteroids to induce remission, with maintenance therapy adjusted afterward. Option A prolongs a strategy that has already proven inadequate rather than escalating appropriately. Option B is reserved for fulminant disease, toxic megacolon, or medically refractory disease after appropriate medical therapy has been tried, not as the next step after a single failed agent. Option C does not address the underlying inflammation and can be unsafe in active colitis by masking disease severity.", "case_id": "GI-C2", "case_text": "A 27-year-old woman presents with a 5-month history of bloody diarrhea, tenesmus, and lower abdominal cramping. She has lost 3 kg. Colonoscopy shows continuous inflammation beginning in the rectum and extending to the mid-transverse colon, without any areas of normal intervening mucosa. Biopsy shows crypt abscesses confined to the mucosa and submucosa without granulomas.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 672, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "Several years later, the same patient returns for a follow-up visit. She asks about her long-term risk of colorectal cancer given her diagnosis and extensive colonic involvement. Which of the following best describes the recommended surveillance approach?", "options": {"A": "A single screening colonoscopy at age 45 identical to average-risk screening", "B": "Surveillance only if she develops new rectal bleeding", "C": "Surveillance colonoscopy with biopsies beginning approximately eight to ten years after diagnosis", "D": "No surveillance colonoscopy is needed as long as she remains in clinical remission"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Surveillance colonoscopy with biopsies beginning approximately eight to ten years after diagnosis): extensive ulcerative colitis carries an elevated colorectal cancer risk that rises with disease duration, so surveillance colonoscopy with biopsies is recommended beginning around eight to ten years after diagnosis regardless of symptomatic remission. Option D is incorrect because cancer risk relates to cumulative colonic inflammation over time, not current symptom control alone. Option A understates her risk, since her extensive disease requires an earlier and more frequent surveillance schedule than average-risk screening. Option B waits for a new symptom rather than proactively screening for dysplasia, which defeats the purpose of surveillance.", "case_id": "GI-C2", "case_text": "A 27-year-old woman presents with a 5-month history of bloody diarrhea, tenesmus, and lower abdominal cramping. She has lost 3 kg. Colonoscopy shows continuous inflammation beginning in the rectum and extending to the mid-transverse colon, without any areas of normal intervening mucosa. Biopsy shows crypt abscesses confined to the mucosa and submucosa without granulomas.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 673, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "During a subsequent surveillance colonoscopy, biopsies reveal high-grade dysplasia in a flat area of mucosa in the sigmoid colon that cannot be completely removed endoscopically. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?", "options": {"A": "Segmental sigmoid resection alone while preserving the rest of the colon", "B": "Total colectomy given unresectable high-grade dysplasia", "C": "Repeat colonoscopy in one year without further intervention", "D": "Increase corticosteroid dose to treat the dysplasia"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Total colectomy given unresectable high-grade dysplasia): high-grade dysplasia in flat, endoscopically unresectable mucosa in a patient with ulcerative colitis is an indication for total colectomy given the substantial risk of coexisting or progressing malignancy. Option C is inadequate surveillance for a finding this concerning and risks progression to invasive cancer. Option D does not treat dysplasia, which is a precancerous change rather than active inflammation responsive to steroids. Option A leaves the remaining colon, which carries the same underlying disease and elevated cancer risk, in place rather than achieving definitive cancer risk reduction, which total colectomy is uniquely able to provide in ulcerative colitis.", "case_id": "GI-C2", "case_text": "A 27-year-old woman presents with a 5-month history of bloody diarrhea, tenesmus, and lower abdominal cramping. She has lost 3 kg. Colonoscopy shows continuous inflammation beginning in the rectum and extending to the mid-transverse colon, without any areas of normal intervening mucosa. Biopsy shows crypt abscesses confined to the mucosa and submucosa without granulomas.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 674, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Alcoholic pancreatitis", "B": "Acute cholangitis", "C": "Perforated duodenal ulcer", "D": "Gallstone pancreatitis"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Gallstone pancreatitis): epigastric pain radiating to the back with a markedly elevated lipase in a patient with known gallstones and a normal bilirubin is consistent with gallstone-induced acute pancreatitis without concurrent biliary obstruction. Option A is a common alternative cause of pancreatitis but is not supported here, since the vignette specifies known gallstone disease rather than a history of alcohol use. Option B would typically show fever, jaundice, and an elevated bilirubin as part of the Charcot triad, none of which are present in this normal bilirubin, afebrile patient. Option C would present with sudden severe pain and peritoneal signs rather than an isolated markedly elevated lipase without free air or peritonitis described.", "image": "question_images/q_674.png", "case_id": "GI-C3", "case_text": "A 61-year-old woman with known gallstones presents with severe epigastric pain radiating to the back, nausea, and vomiting. She has no fever. Laboratory studies show lipase 620 U/L (reference range 10-140 U/L) and total bilirubin 1.0 mg/dL.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 675, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "The patient improves with intravenous fluids, pain control, and early enteral feeding. Three days later, she develops fever, right upper quadrant pain, and a rising bilirubin to 5.8 mg/dL with alkaline phosphatase elevation. Which of the following is the most likely explanation?", "options": {"A": "A new duodenal ulcer has developed, causing external biliary compression", "B": "Progressive pancreatic necrosis has developed without any biliary involvement", "C": "A gallstone has migrated into the common bile duct, causing obstruction and cholangitis", "D": "Recurrent pancreatitis alone has developed without any biliary obstruction"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A gallstone has migrated into the common bile duct, causing obstruction and cholangitis): new fever, right upper quadrant pain, and a rising bilirubin with a cholestatic pattern in a patient with known gallstones suggests a stone has passed into the common bile duct, causing obstruction and superimposed infection, that is, ascending cholangitis. Option D does not explain the new fever and cholestatic pattern of bilirubin and alkaline phosphatase elevation, which point toward a biliary rather than purely pancreatic process. Option A is not supported by the clinical history, which centers on known gallstone disease rather than peptic ulcer disease. Option B would not typically produce this specific cholestatic pattern with fever and rising bilirubin in the way that duct obstruction does.", "case_id": "GI-C3", "case_text": "A 61-year-old woman with known gallstones presents with severe epigastric pain radiating to the back, nausea, and vomiting. She has no fever. Laboratory studies show lipase 620 U/L (reference range 10-140 U/L) and total bilirubin 1.0 mg/dL.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 676, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in this patient's management now that ascending cholangitis is suspected?", "options": {"A": "Observation alone since her pancreatitis has already improved", "B": "Immediate open common bile duct exploration without prior antibiotics", "C": "Intravenous antibiotics with urgent ERCP for biliary decompression", "D": "Oral antibiotics with elective outpatient cholecystectomy in six weeks"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Intravenous antibiotics with urgent ERCP for biliary decompression): ascending cholangitis is a medical emergency requiring prompt intravenous antibiotics along with urgent biliary decompression, most often achieved through ERCP with stone extraction or stent placement. Option D is far too delayed and uses an inadequate route of antibiotics for a patient with an acute infected, obstructed biliary system. Option A fails to treat a new, potentially life-threatening infectious process that is unrelated to whether the earlier pancreatitis has resolved. Option B bypasses antibiotics, which should always be given promptly, and open surgical exploration is not the first-line approach when ERCP is available.", "case_id": "GI-C3", "case_text": "A 61-year-old woman with known gallstones presents with severe epigastric pain radiating to the back, nausea, and vomiting. She has no fever. Laboratory studies show lipase 620 U/L (reference range 10-140 U/L) and total bilirubin 1.0 mg/dL.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 679, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 60-year-old man undergoes upper endoscopy for dyspepsia and is found to have a 1.5 cm gastric ulcer on the lesser curvature. Biopsies are taken and he is started on proton pump inhibitor therapy. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "No further endoscopy is needed since biopsies were already taken", "B": "Repeat endoscopy only if symptoms recur after one year", "C": "Proceed to partial gastrectomy before the biopsy results are available, given the ulcer's size and location", "D": "Repeat upper endoscopy in six to eight weeks to confirm healing"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Repeat upper endoscopy in six to eight weeks to confirm healing): gastric ulcers require follow-up endoscopy after treatment to confirm healing and to exclude an underlying malignancy, since a malignant gastric ulcer can appear identical to a benign one at initial inspection. Option A is insufficient since biopsy alone at a single time point does not confirm healing or fully exclude malignancy if initial biopsies were nondiagnostic. Option B delays necessary confirmation of healing well beyond the recommended interval. Option C is far too aggressive without first confirming whether the biopsies show malignancy or whether the ulcer heals with medical therapy alone."}, {"id": 680, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 33-year-old man reports chronic heartburn for 3 years, well controlled with once-daily omeprazole, without dysphagia, weight loss, or bleeding. He asks whether he needs an upper endoscopy. Which of the following is the most accurate response?", "options": {"A": "Endoscopy should be performed now specifically to screen for gastric cancer", "B": "Endoscopy should be replaced by an annual barium esophagram instead", "C": "Endoscopy is not routinely required since he lacks alarm features and responds well to therapy", "D": "Endoscopy is generally recommended annually for patients using long-term proton pump inhibitor therapy, especially with symptom changes or added risk factors"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Endoscopy is not routinely required since he lacks alarm features and responds well to therapy): a patient with typical reflux symptoms that are well controlled on therapy and no alarm features such as dysphagia, weight loss, or bleeding does not require routine endoscopy. Option D is incorrect since long-term proton pump inhibitor use alone, without alarm features, is not an automatic indication for annual endoscopy. Option A is not appropriate since gastric cancer screening is not routinely recommended in this population without specific risk factors. Option B is not an equivalent or preferred substitute for endoscopy and is not indicated in this well-controlled patient at all."}, {"id": 683, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 50-year-old man with cirrhosis due to nonalcoholic steatohepatitis is found on screening ultrasound to have a 2.5 cm hepatic lesion. Alpha-fetoprotein is elevated. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Immediate percutaneous biopsy of the lesion as the first step", "B": "Repeat ultrasound alone in one year without further imaging", "C": "Reassurance since hepatic lesions in cirrhosis are rarely malignant", "D": "Multiphase contrast-enhanced CT or MRI of the liver"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Multiphase contrast-enhanced CT or MRI of the liver): a hepatic lesion detected in a cirrhotic patient with an elevated alpha-fetoprotein warrants multiphase cross-sectional imaging, which can often diagnose hepatocellular carcinoma noninvasively based on characteristic enhancement patterns. Option A is not the routine first step, since noninvasive imaging can often establish the diagnosis and biopsy carries a small risk of tumor seeding. Option B delays the evaluation of a lesion with concerning features in a high-risk patient. Option C is incorrect since cirrhotic patients have a substantially elevated risk of hepatocellular carcinoma, which is precisely why they undergo surveillance."}, {"id": 684, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 46-year-old woman with primary biliary cholangitis on ursodeoxycholic acid therapy asks about her expected disease course. Which of the following best describes the classic pattern of this disease?", "options": {"A": "Diffuse strictures of both intrahepatic and extrahepatic large bile ducts seen on imaging", "B": "An acute, self-limited inflammatory process that rarely recurs after the initial episode resolves", "C": "A condition confined exclusively to men over age 60 with no female predominance", "D": "Progressive destruction of small intrahepatic bile ducts leading to cholestasis and potential cirrhosis"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Progressive destruction of small intrahepatic bile ducts leading to cholestasis and potential cirrhosis): primary biliary cholangitis is an autoimmune condition causing progressive destruction of small intrahepatic bile ducts, leading to cholestasis and, if untreated or unresponsive to therapy, progression to cirrhosis. Option A describes primary sclerosing cholangitis, which involves large duct strictures rather than the small duct destruction seen in primary biliary cholangitis. Option B mischaracterizes this as an acute, self-limited process, when it is in fact a chronic, progressive condition. Option C is incorrect since primary biliary cholangitis occurs overwhelmingly in middle-aged women, not older men."}, {"id": 685, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 30-year-old man with ulcerative colitis develops pruritus, fatigue, and a cholestatic liver enzyme pattern. Magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography shows multifocal strictures alternating with dilated segments of both intrahepatic and extrahepatic bile ducts. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Primary sclerosing cholangitis", "B": "Primary biliary cholangitis", "C": "Autoimmune hepatitis", "D": "Choledocholithiasis from a single obstructing stone"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Primary sclerosing cholangitis): a beaded appearance of multifocal strictures and dilations affecting both intrahepatic and extrahepatic bile ducts in a patient with ulcerative colitis is the classic imaging and clinical presentation of primary sclerosing cholangitis. Option B involves small duct destruction rather than the large duct beading pattern described here and typically presents in middle-aged women rather than being strongly linked to ulcerative colitis. Option C presents with a hepatocellular, not cholestatic, injury pattern and does not produce this ductal beading on imaging. Option D would show a single, focal ductal obstruction rather than the multifocal beaded pattern described here."}, {"id": 686, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 42-year-old woman is found to have persistently elevated ALT and AST with positive antinuclear antibody and anti-smooth muscle antibody. Liver biopsy shows interface hepatitis with a lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial treatment?", "options": {"A": "Corticosteroids, often combined with azathioprine", "B": "Ursodeoxycholic acid as monotherapy", "C": "Direct acting antiviral therapy", "D": "Complete abstinence from alcohol alone"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Corticosteroids, often combined with azathioprine): this presentation, positive autoantibodies with interface hepatitis and a lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate on biopsy, is characteristic of autoimmune hepatitis, which is treated with immunosuppression, typically corticosteroids with or without azathioprine. Option B is the first-line treatment for primary biliary cholangitis, a different cholestatic autoimmune liver disease, not autoimmune hepatitis. Option C treats viral hepatitis, which is not suggested by this serologic and histologic picture. Option D would be appropriate for alcohol-related liver disease, which is not indicated by this clinical and biopsy pattern."}, {"id": 688, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 45-year-old man with obesity and type 2 diabetes is found to have mildly elevated ALT on routine labs. Ultrasound shows a hyperechoic liver consistent with steatosis. He drinks alcohol rarely. Viral hepatitis serologies are negative. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Acute viral hepatitis A", "B": "Wilson disease, an inherited copper disorder", "C": "Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease", "D": "Alcoholic hepatitis from heavy drinking"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease): hepatic steatosis on imaging with mild transaminase elevation in a patient with obesity and diabetes, minimal alcohol use, and negative viral serologies is characteristic of nonalcoholic fatty liver disease, closely linked to metabolic risk factors. Option D requires a significant alcohol use history, which this patient does not have. Option A would present acutely with more marked transaminase elevation and typically a positive serologic test, not an incidental steatotic finding. Option B typically presents in younger patients with neuropsychiatric symptoms and distinct copper metabolism abnormalities, not this metabolic risk factor profile."}, {"id": 690, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 55-year-old man with alcohol use disorder is admitted with jaundice, tender hepatomegaly, and fever. His Maddrey discriminant function score indicates severe disease. Which of the following is the most appropriate additional treatment beyond abstinence and supportive care?", "options": {"A": "Iron supplementation", "B": "Corticosteroid therapy", "C": "Antiviral therapy", "D": "Immediate liver transplantation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Corticosteroid therapy): patients with severe alcoholic hepatitis, as indicated by a high Maddrey discriminant function score, may benefit from corticosteroid therapy in addition to supportive care and abstinence. Option C treats viral hepatitis and has no role in alcoholic liver disease. Option D is not the immediate next step, since transplantation evaluation involves specific criteria and is not appropriate as an immediate universal response to a severe Maddrey score. Option A has no role in alcoholic hepatitis and could be harmful, since iron overload is not the underlying pathology in this condition."}, {"id": 691, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 48-year-old woman develops jaundice, coagulopathy, and altered mental status over 5 days after taking excessive acetaminophen for a headache. Which of the following laboratory abnormality patterns would be most expected in this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Isolated elevation of alkaline phosphatase with normal transaminases", "B": "Normal liver enzymes with isolated hyperbilirubinemia", "C": "Elevated lipase with normal transaminases and normal INR", "D": "Markedly elevated AST and ALT with a prolonged INR"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Markedly elevated AST and ALT with a prolonged INR): acetaminophen-induced acute liver failure produces marked hepatocellular injury with very high transaminases and impaired hepatic synthetic function reflected by a prolonged INR, along with encephalopathy in severe cases. Option A describes a cholestatic pattern, which is not the classic pattern of acute toxic hepatocellular injury from acetaminophen. Option B would not explain the encephalopathy and coagulopathy described, which reflect significant hepatocellular dysfunction, not isolated bilirubin elevation. Option C describes a pancreatic enzyme pattern unrelated to acetaminophen hepatotoxicity."}, {"id": 693, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 62-year-old man with a history of aortic stenosis develops recurrent episodes of painless hematochezia over several months. Colonoscopy identifies a vascular lesion in the cecum consistent with angiodysplasia. Which of the following best describes the association between his cardiac condition and this gastrointestinal finding?", "options": {"A": "Aortic stenosis directly causing chronic ischemic damage to the cecal mucosa over time", "B": "Angiodysplasia is a direct embolic complication arising from his underlying valvular disease", "C": "Heyde syndrome, in which aortic stenosis is associated with acquired von Willebrand factor deficiency promoting bleeding from angiodysplasia", "D": "A coincidental finding with no established biological relationship between these two separate conditions"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Heyde syndrome, in which aortic stenosis is associated with acquired von Willebrand factor deficiency promoting bleeding from angiodysplasia): the combination of aortic stenosis and bleeding angiodysplasia, known as Heyde syndrome, arises from shear stress across the stenotic valve degrading von Willebrand factor multimers, impairing hemostasis at fragile angiodysplastic lesions. Option D understates a well-recognized clinical association between these two conditions. Option A is not the correct mechanism, since the relationship is hematologic rather than a direct ischemic effect of the valve on the cecum. Option B mischaracterizes the mechanism, which relates to acquired coagulopathy rather than embolization."}, {"id": 694, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 70-year-old woman presents with hematochezia and hemodynamic instability. She has no prior colonoscopy findings on file. CT angiography localizes active extravasation in the right colon. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Interventional radiology embolization or urgent surgical evaluation depending on institutional resources", "B": "Elective outpatient colonoscopy scheduled for follow-up in about two weeks", "C": "Oral iron supplementation with outpatient observation over the following weeks", "D": "Barium enema to further characterize the lesion before any intervention"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Interventional radiology embolization or urgent surgical evaluation depending on institutional resources): a hemodynamically unstable patient with active extravasation localized on CT angiography requires prompt hemostatic intervention, typically through interventional radiology embolization, with surgery reserved for cases where this is not feasible or successful. Option B is unsafe given ongoing instability and active bleeding requiring urgent, not elective, management. Option C fails to address active, hemodynamically significant bleeding. Option D adds unnecessary delay and does not offer therapeutic intervention, unlike angiography with embolization capability."}, {"id": 695, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 3-year-old child with celiac disease confirmed by biopsy is started on a strict gluten-free diet. His parents ask how they will know the treatment is working. Which of the following is the most appropriate way to monitor response to therapy?", "options": {"A": "Clinical improvement along with a decline in celiac-specific antibody titers over time", "B": "Repeat duodenal biopsy periodically during follow-up, independent of his reported symptoms", "C": "There is limited reliable means available to monitor response to a gluten-free diet", "D": "Serum total IgA level, checked at one point without further antibody monitoring"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Clinical improvement along with a decline in celiac-specific antibody titers over time): response to a gluten-free diet in celiac disease is typically monitored through clinical improvement in symptoms along with a progressive decline in celiac-specific antibody titers, such as tissue transglutaminase antibody. Option B is more invasive and frequent than needed for routine monitoring in a child responding clinically and serologically. Option C is incorrect since serologic titers and symptom resolution provide a reliable, noninvasive way to follow response. Option D is used to interpret the validity of the initial antibody test rather than to monitor ongoing treatment response over time."}, {"id": 697, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 66-year-old man with a known abdominal aortic aneurysm repair 3 years ago presents with severe, poorly localized abdominal pain out of proportion to a benign-appearing abdominal examination, along with bloody diarrhea. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Irritable bowel syndrome", "B": "Ischemic colitis", "C": "Sigmoid diverticulitis", "D": "Acute appendicitis"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ischemic colitis): severe abdominal pain out of proportion to examination findings along with bloody diarrhea in a patient with vascular risk factors, including prior aortic surgery, is characteristic of ischemic colitis affecting watershed areas of the colon. Option C typically presents with more localized left lower quadrant pain and fever rather than pain out of proportion to examination with bloody diarrhea. Option D presents with right lower quadrant pain that localizes over time, not diffuse pain out of proportion to examination with bloody diarrhea. Option A does not cause pain out of proportion to examination or bloody diarrhea, since it is a functional rather than an ischemic or inflammatory process."}, {"id": 698, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 50-year-old man undergoes screening colonoscopy that identifies a 1.2 cm tubular adenoma with low-grade dysplasia, completely removed. Which of the following is the most appropriate follow-up interval for his next colonoscopy?", "options": {"A": "Annual colonoscopy for the rest of his life, more frequent than his risk level actually supports", "B": "Approximately seven to ten years, as with average-risk screening given a single small low-risk adenoma", "C": "Immediate repeat colonoscopy scheduled within one week of the original polypectomy procedure", "D": "No further colonoscopy is typically needed after a single polyp of this type is removed"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Approximately seven to ten years, as with average-risk screening given a single small low-risk adenoma): a single small tubular adenoma with low-grade dysplasia, completely removed, generally allows a return to an average-risk screening interval rather than more frequent surveillance. Option C is unnecessary and not indicated after a straightforward, completely removed low-risk polyp. Option D is incorrect since ongoing screening remains important given his adenoma history and continued colorectal cancer risk over time. Option A is more frequent than needed for a single low-risk polyp and does not reflect current risk-based surveillance recommendations."}, {"id": 699, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 68-year-old man with a strong family history consistent with Lynch syndrome, confirmed by genetic testing showing a pathogenic mismatch repair gene mutation, asks about his colorectal cancer screening plan. Which of the following best describes the appropriate approach?", "options": {"A": "Colonoscopy beginning at a substantially younger age than average risk, repeated at a shorter interval such as every one to two years", "B": "Standard average-risk screening beginning at age 45, with colonoscopy repeated only every ten years as typically recommended for average-risk adults.", "C": "Stool-based testing alone, without any colonoscopy, used as the sole screening approach given his confirmed genetic diagnosis of this syndrome.", "D": "Little additional screening would be needed, since his genetic testing has already confirmed his mismatch repair mutation status."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Colonoscopy beginning at a substantially younger age than average risk, repeated at a shorter interval such as every one to two years): patients with Lynch syndrome require earlier initiation of colonoscopy and more frequent surveillance intervals given their markedly elevated and accelerated colorectal cancer risk. Option B substantially understates his risk and screening needs given a hereditary cancer syndrome. Option C is inadequate since colonoscopy, not stool-based testing alone, is the recommended modality for this high-risk population given the need for polypectomy capability. Option D confuses genetic confirmation of risk with actual cancer surveillance, which remains essential precisely because the mutation confers ongoing risk."}, {"id": 700, "domain": "gastrointestinal_system", "question": "A 44-year-old woman with a history of familial adenomatous polyposis, confirmed genetically, is found on surveillance colonoscopy to have hundreds of colonic adenomas, several with high-grade dysplasia. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?", "options": {"A": "Prophylactic colectomy", "B": "Continued annual colonoscopy with polypectomy", "C": "A trial of 5-aminosalicylate therapy", "D": "Observation alone without further intervention"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Prophylactic colectomy): familial adenomatous polyposis with hundreds of adenomas and areas of high-grade dysplasia carries a near-certain lifetime risk of colorectal cancer if the colon is not removed, making prophylactic colectomy the standard recommended management. Option B is not feasible or safe given the sheer number of polyps and the presence of high-grade dysplasia, which substantially raises cancer risk if the colon is left in place. Option C has no established role in reducing the polyp burden of familial adenomatous polyposis to a degree that would avoid colectomy. Option D delays definitive treatment in a condition with an extremely high risk of progression to invasive cancer without surgical intervention."}, {"id": 701, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "Based on the pattern of weakness and language findings described in the case, which cerebral vessel is most likely occluded?", "options": {"A": "Left middle cerebral artery", "B": "Left anterior cerebral artery", "C": "Left posterior cerebral artery", "D": "Basilar artery"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Left middle cerebral artery): Expressive aphasia (a dominant hemisphere finding) together with contralateral face and arm weakness that is worse than leg weakness is the classic pattern of a middle cerebral artery occlusion, since that vessel supplies the lateral cortex where the face and arm are represented over a larger area than the leg. The anterior cerebral artery would cause leg weakness greater than arm weakness because it supplies the medial motor cortex, the posterior cerebral artery causes visual field loss rather than motor and language deficits, and a basilar artery occlusion causes bilateral or crossed brainstem signs rather than this isolated cortical pattern.", "case_id": "CASE-STROKE-1", "case_text": "A 68-year-old right-handed man is brought to the emergency department by his wife because 45 minutes ago he suddenly could not find the right words to answer her question and his right arm became weak. He has a history of hypertension and type 2 diabetes and takes lisinopril and metformin. On examination he is alert, blood pressure is 168/94 mmHg, and he has expressive aphasia with right facial droop and right arm weakness (strength 2/5) greater than right leg weakness (strength 4/5). There is no headache, neck stiffness, or vomiting.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 702, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "What is the most appropriate immediate next step in the management of this patient?", "options": {"A": "MRI of the brain with diffusion weighted imaging", "B": "Lumbar puncture", "C": "Carotid duplex ultrasound", "D": "Noncontrast head CT"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Noncontrast head CT): A rapid noncontrast head CT is the essential first study in any patient with acute focal neurologic deficits because it quickly excludes intracranial hemorrhage, which would be a contraindication to thrombolysis, and it can be obtained faster than an MRI. MRI is more sensitive for early ischemia but takes longer to obtain and is not the first test when a time-critical treatment decision is pending. Lumbar puncture and carotid duplex have no role in the first minutes of an acute stroke evaluation.", "case_id": "CASE-STROKE-1", "case_text": "A 68-year-old right-handed man is brought to the emergency department by his wife because 45 minutes ago he suddenly could not find the right words to answer her question and his right arm became weak. He has a history of hypertension and type 2 diabetes and takes lisinopril and metformin. On examination he is alert, blood pressure is 168/94 mmHg, and he has expressive aphasia with right facial droop and right arm weakness (strength 2/5) greater than right leg weakness (strength 4/5). There is no headache, neck stiffness, or vomiting.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 703, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "The head CT shows no hemorrhage and the patient's symptom onset was witnessed 50 minutes before arrival, with no contraindications identified on the thrombolysis checklist. Which treatment should be given now?", "options": {"A": "Intravenous alteplase or tenecteplase", "B": "Aspirin 325 mg only, with thrombolysis deferred", "C": "Immediate carotid endarterectomy", "D": "Therapeutic anticoagulation with heparin"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Intravenous alteplase or tenecteplase): This patient presents within 4.5 hours of clearly known onset with a disabling deficit, no hemorrhage on CT, and no contraindications, which is exactly the population that benefits from intravenous thrombolysis. Aspirin alone is used when thrombolysis is not given or after the thrombolysis window has passed, carotid endarterectomy is a secondary prevention procedure performed after the acute period once symptomatic stenosis is confirmed, and therapeutic heparin is not used in acute ischemic stroke because it increases hemorrhagic transformation risk without proven benefit.", "case_id": "CASE-STROKE-1", "case_text": "A 68-year-old right-handed man is brought to the emergency department by his wife because 45 minutes ago he suddenly could not find the right words to answer her question and his right arm became weak. He has a history of hypertension and type 2 diabetes and takes lisinopril and metformin. On examination he is alert, blood pressure is 168/94 mmHg, and he has expressive aphasia with right facial droop and right arm weakness (strength 2/5) greater than right leg weakness (strength 4/5). There is no headache, neck stiffness, or vomiting.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 704, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "The patient improves significantly with treatment. A carotid duplex ultrasound obtained during admission shows 75 percent stenosis of the left internal carotid artery. Which long term intervention is most appropriate for secondary stroke prevention in addition to antiplatelet therapy and a high intensity statin?", "options": {"A": "Warfarin anticoagulation", "B": "Bilateral carotid stenting", "C": "Left carotid endarterectomy", "D": "Repeat ultrasound in five years"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Left carotid endarterectomy): A symptomatic carotid stenosis of 70 percent or greater on the side corresponding to the patient's deficit is an indication for carotid endarterectomy, which reduces the risk of a future stroke more than medical therapy alone in this population. Warfarin has no proven benefit over antiplatelet therapy for large artery atherosclerotic disease, treating the asymptomatic contralateral vessel is not indicated based on the information given, and observation alone is inappropriate for a symptomatic, high grade stenosis.", "case_id": "CASE-STROKE-1", "case_text": "A 68-year-old right-handed man is brought to the emergency department by his wife because 45 minutes ago he suddenly could not find the right words to answer her question and his right arm became weak. He has a history of hypertension and type 2 diabetes and takes lisinopril and metformin. On examination he is alert, blood pressure is 168/94 mmHg, and he has expressive aphasia with right facial droop and right arm weakness (strength 2/5) greater than right leg weakness (strength 4/5). There is no headache, neck stiffness, or vomiting.", "case_size": 4}, {"id": 705, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 74-year-old woman with hypertension and hyperlipidemia develops sudden onset vertigo, double vision, and difficulty swallowing while gardening in her yard. Her husband calls emergency services and she arrives within 40 minutes. Vital signs show blood pressure 152/88 mmHg, heart rate 84/min, and normal oxygen saturation. On examination she has left facial numbness, a left-sided Horner syndrome with ptosis and miosis, ataxia of the left arm and leg on finger-to-nose and heel-to-shin testing, and diminished pain and temperature sensation on the right side of her body below the neck, with normal strength in all four limbs. Which structure is most likely infarcted?", "options": {"A": "Lateral medulla (left posterior inferior cerebellar artery territory)", "B": "Left middle cerebral artery territory, affecting cortical face and arm function", "C": "Right anterior cerebral artery territory", "D": "Bilateral occipital lobes"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Lateral medulla, left posterior inferior cerebellar artery territory): The combination of ipsilateral facial sensory loss, Horner syndrome, and ataxia with contralateral body pain and temperature loss is the classic crossed sensory pattern of lateral medullary (Wallenberg) syndrome from a posterior inferior cerebellar artery infarct. A middle cerebral artery stroke produces face and arm weakness on one side rather than this crossed brainstem pattern, an anterior cerebral artery infarct causes leg predominant weakness, and bilateral occipital infarcts cause cortical blindness rather than these findings."}, {"id": 706, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 61-year-old man with a history of atrial fibrillation not on anticoagulation because he stopped his warfarin two months ago after a fall develops sudden right-sided arm and leg weakness while eating breakfast. His wife notes his speech also became slurred. By the time paramedics arrive 30 minutes later, his weakness and speech have completely resolved and he insists he feels back to normal. In the emergency department his vital signs are stable, his neurologic exam is entirely normal, and a head CT shows no acute abnormality. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Discharge home with no further workup since symptoms resolved", "B": "Urgent evaluation including echocardiogram and initiation of anticoagulation", "C": "Six-month outpatient follow-up MRI", "D": "Immediate thrombolysis despite symptom resolution"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Urgent evaluation including echocardiogram and initiation of anticoagulation): Complete resolution of focal deficits with a normal scan defines a transient ischemic attack, which carries a high short-term risk of a completed stroke, especially with atrial fibrillation as an identified cardioembolic source, so urgent risk stratification and anticoagulation are indicated promptly. Discharging without workup ignores this high recurrence risk, waiting six months is far too long for a TIA workup, and thrombolysis is not given once symptoms have already fully resolved."}, {"id": 707, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 58-year-old man with poorly controlled hypertension, for which he frequently misses his medications, develops the sudden onset of severe headache, vomiting, and left-sided weakness while at rest watching television. His wife notes that over the next hour he becomes progressively more somnolent and harder to arouse. On arrival his blood pressure is 210/118 mmHg, he opens his eyes only to voice, and he has dense left hemiplegia. Noncontrast CT shows a hyperdense lesion in the right basal ganglia measuring 3 cm with surrounding edema and mild midline shift. Which of the following is the most important initial management priority?", "options": {"A": "Immediate lumbar puncture to confirm the diagnosis before any other intervention", "B": "Intravenous alteplase administered urgently for thrombolytic therapy", "C": "Careful blood pressure control and reversal of any anticoagulation", "D": "An oral aspirin loading dose given immediately"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Careful blood pressure control and reversal of any anticoagulation): This presentation and CT appearance are diagnostic of a hypertensive intracerebral hemorrhage, and the priorities are lowering blood pressure to reduce ongoing bleeding and hematoma expansion and reversing any anticoagulant, along with close neurologic monitoring for signs of increasing mass effect. Lumbar puncture is unnecessary once hemorrhage is already seen on CT and could be dangerous with mass effect present, and both thrombolysis and antiplatelet therapy are contraindicated because they would worsen the bleeding."}, {"id": 710, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 34-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after a high speed motor vehicle collision in which he was an unrestrained driver. He has obvious facial lacerations and a deformed left forearm. On assessment he opens his eyes only to painful stimuli, makes incomprehensible sounds when spoken to, and withdraws his limbs from painful stimulation but does not follow any commands. His blood pressure is 128/76 mmHg and his oxygen saturation is 96 percent on room air. What is his Glasgow Coma Scale score, and what does this indicate about airway management?", "options": {"A": "GCS 12; no airway intervention is needed", "B": "GCS 6; the airway is already considered adequately protected at this score", "C": "GCS 10; intubation is contraindicated", "D": "GCS 8; airway protection should be strongly considered"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (GCS 8; airway protection should be strongly considered): Eye opening to pain scores 2, incomprehensible sounds score 2, and withdrawal from pain scores 4, giving a total Glasgow Coma Scale of 8, which is the widely used threshold below which airway protection with intubation should be strongly considered because of the risk of losing the ability to protect the airway from aspiration. The other option totals are simply incorrect additions of the same findings, and none of them changes the clinical principle that a GCS this low warrants airway assessment."}, {"id": 711, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 24-year-old woman with no past medical history is brought to the emergency department after a witnessed generalized tonic-clonic seizure lasting about 2 minutes, her first ever, which occurred while she was at work. She takes no medications and denies alcohol or drug use, and there was no preceding head trauma, fever, or infectious symptoms. On arrival she is postictal but improving, her vital signs are normal, and her neurologic exam is nonfocal once she is fully alert. Her basic metabolic panel, calcium, magnesium, and glucose are all normal, and a brain MRI obtained during admission is also normal. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Start levetiracetam immediately and discharge her home from the emergency department", "B": "Start valproic acid immediately as first-line therapy without considering her reproductive potential", "C": "Obtain an EEG to help estimate recurrence risk before deciding on treatment", "D": "Perform a lumbar puncture as the next required test before any other decision"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Obtain an EEG to help estimate recurrence risk before deciding on treatment): After a first unprovoked seizure with a normal metabolic workup and a normal MRI, an EEG helps identify epileptiform abnormalities that would indicate a higher risk of recurrence and could change the decision about starting an antiepileptic drug, since medication is not routinely started after a single seizure with an otherwise low-risk workup. Starting an antiepileptic drug immediately is premature before this information is available, valproic acid should generally be avoided in women of childbearing potential, and lumbar puncture is not a routine part of the first-seizure workup absent signs of infection."}, {"id": 714, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 27-year-old woman with newly diagnosed generalized epilepsy is being counseled about antiepileptic drug options because she and her partner are planning to conceive within the next year. Her neurologist reviews several commonly used agents with her, discussing efficacy for her seizure type as well as reproductive safety considerations that should guide the choice of medication given her plans for pregnancy. Which antiepileptic drug is most appropriate to avoid, when reasonable alternatives exist, because of a high risk of neural tube defects and other fetal malformations?", "options": {"A": "Levetiracetam therapy", "B": "Valproic acid", "C": "Lamotrigine therapy", "D": "Carbamazepine therapy"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Valproic acid): Valproic acid carries the highest teratogenic risk among commonly used antiepileptic drugs, including neural tube defects and other major malformations, so it is generally avoided in women of childbearing potential when an alternative agent is reasonable. Levetiracetam and lamotrigine are generally considered to have a more favorable safety profile in pregnancy, and while carbamazepine also carries some teratogenic risk, it is not the agent most strongly associated with this specific concern among these choices."}, {"id": 715, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 22-year-old woman with a history of childhood sexual trauma and an anxiety disorder is admitted for recurrent episodes described by her family as generalized shaking. Video review of one event shows asynchronous, side-to-side limb movements, pelvic thrusting, and eyes held tightly closed with resistance to passive opening by the examining nurse, lasting several minutes and waxing and waning in intensity before gradually stopping. A prior continuous EEG recording captured during a similar event one year ago showed no epileptiform activity throughout the episode. Which of the following is most consistent with this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Generalized tonic-clonic seizure", "B": "Complex focal seizure of temporal lobe origin", "C": "Absence status epilepticus", "D": "Psychogenic nonepileptic seizure"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Psychogenic nonepileptic seizure): Asynchronous, side-to-side movements, pelvic thrusting, forced eye closure with resistance to opening, a waxing and waning course, and a normal EEG during a captured event are the classic features that distinguish psychogenic nonepileptic seizures from true epileptic seizures, which typically show synchronous, stereotyped movements and a corresponding electrographic correlate. A true generalized tonic-clonic seizure would show synchronous tonic then clonic activity with an EEG correlate, a complex focal seizure typically has an EEG correlate as well, and absence status would show continuous epileptiform discharges rather than a normal EEG."}, {"id": 716, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 55-year-old man with no prior seizure history presents after his family witnessed an episode of right hand jerking lasting about a minute that then progressed into a full body convulsion with loss of consciousness. Brain MRI reveals a small area of encephalomalacia in the left frontal lobe from an old head injury, and his neurologist is now selecting an antiepileptic drug, keeping in mind that his seizures began focally but sometimes secondarily generalize. Which class of drugs is effective for both focal and generalized seizure types and is a reasonable broad-spectrum first choice?", "options": {"A": "Ethosuximide, effective only for absence seizures", "B": "Levetiracetam, a broad-spectrum antiepileptic drug", "C": "A drug limited to absence seizures", "D": "A drug that tends to worsen focal seizures"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Levetiracetam): Levetiracetam, along with valproic acid, is considered a broad spectrum antiepileptic effective for both focal and generalized seizure types, making it a reasonable choice when the seizure type could evolve from focal to generalized, as described here. Ethosuximide is effective only for absence seizures and has no role in focal epilepsy, and the other two options describe properties that do not correspond to any standard first-line agent for this presentation."}, {"id": 720, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 45-year-old man with a long history of heavy alcohol use is brought to the emergency department after his roommate found him having a witnessed convulsion at home, two days after abruptly stopping alcohol following a hospitalization for an unrelated injury. His basic metabolic panel reveals a serum sodium of 116 mEq/L that has fallen rapidly over the preceding 24 hours according to prior outpatient labs. Before this seizure episode is simply labeled as new-onset unprovoked epilepsy, which of the following metabolic derangements should specifically be excluded as a well-recognized provoked cause?", "options": {"A": "Iron deficiency", "B": "Elevated cholesterol", "C": "Hyponatremia", "D": "Mild dehydration"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hyponatremia): Severe or rapidly developing hyponatremia is a classic metabolic cause of provoked seizures, along with hypoglycemia, hypocalcemia, uremia, and hepatic encephalopathy, all of which must be excluded before a seizure is classified as unprovoked epilepsy, since correcting the underlying abnormality is the primary treatment rather than starting a chronic antiepileptic drug. Mild iron deficiency anemia, isolated elevated LDL cholesterol, and mild dehydration alone are not recognized direct causes of acute provoked seizures."}, {"id": 723, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 30-year-old woman who works long hours as an accountant presents with a several-month history of bilateral head pressure most afternoons, which she describes as a tight cap or band squeezing around her whole head. She denies any nausea, vomiting, sensitivity to light or sound, and she notes that the pain does not worsen with routine physical activity such as walking or climbing stairs. She has not noticed any preceding visual changes and her neurologic exam is entirely normal. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Migraine without aura", "B": "Tension-type headache", "C": "Cluster headache", "D": "Idiopathic intracranial hypertension"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tension-type headache): Bilateral, band-like or pressure-type pain without nausea, photophobia, phonophobia, or activity-related worsening is the defining pattern of tension-type headache, the most common primary headache disorder overall. Migraine typically includes at least some of the associated features and is often unilateral, cluster headache is much more severe and briefer with autonomic features, and idiopathic intracranial hypertension usually presents with additional signs such as papilledema or visual disturbance."}, {"id": 726, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 45-year-old man with a long-standing history of episodic migraine without aura, well controlled on an as-needed triptan for the past several years, presents to urgent care with a headache he says feels different from his usual pattern. His physician is trying to decide which specific feature of a headache presentation, if it were present in this case, should most strongly prompt urgent neuroimaging rather than simply treating it as another episode of his known primary headache disorder. Which of the following headache red flag features fits that description?", "options": {"A": "A headache pattern that has been stable and unchanged for ten years", "B": "Mild bilateral pressure headache relieved by ibuprofen", "C": "Sudden thunderclap onset reaching maximum intensity within seconds", "D": "Headache only during examination periods in a college student"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Sudden thunderclap onset reaching maximum intensity within seconds): A thunderclap headache is a classic red flag requiring urgent evaluation for subarachnoid hemorrhage and other serious secondary causes, since its abrupt maximal intensity onset is fundamentally different from the gradual buildup typical of primary headache disorders. A long-standing stable pattern, a mild headache that responds to a simple analgesic, and a headache clearly tied to a recognizable stressor such as examinations are all reassuring features of a benign primary headache rather than red flags."}, {"id": 727, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 42-year-old man with a longstanding history of chronic migraine has been managing his headaches almost entirely on his own for the past several months using an over-the-counter combination analgesic containing caffeine and butalbital, which he now takes nearly every single day because his headaches seem to return as soon as each dose wears off. Despite this frequent use, or perhaps because of it, his headaches have become noticeably more frequent, more severe, and less responsive to the same medication that used to help him. Which of the following is the most likely explanation for this worsening pattern?", "options": {"A": "Progression of an underlying brain tumor", "B": "New onset cluster headache", "C": "Giant cell arteritis", "D": "Medication overuse headache"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Medication overuse headache): Frequent, near-daily use of acute headache medications, particularly combination analgesics and butalbital-containing products, is a well-recognized cause of medication overuse headache, in which the headache pattern worsens and becomes more refractory the more often the acute medication is used, and management requires carefully withdrawing the overused medication along with starting appropriate preventive therapy. A brain tumor and giant cell arteritis would typically show other systemic or focal findings, and a new cluster headache pattern would present with the distinctive unilateral autonomic features rather than a gradually worsening chronic migraine pattern."}, {"id": 729, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "An 82-year-old man with a history of mild baseline forgetfulness who otherwise lives independently is hospitalized for community-acquired pneumonia. On admission he was appropriately oriented and conversant with the nursing staff, but overnight on his second hospital day he becomes acutely confused, with a fluctuating level of attention throughout the following day such that he is difficult to engage in conversation at times and relatively clear at other moments, along with disorganized, rambling speech that was not present at admission two days earlier. Which of the following best describes this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Alzheimer's", "B": "FTD", "C": "Delirium", "D": "Normal aging"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Delirium): An acute onset over hours to days, a fluctuating course, and a core deficit in attention arising in the setting of an acute illness such as pneumonia are the defining features of delirium, which requires identification and treatment of the underlying medical cause. Alzheimer disease and frontotemporal dementia both have an insidious onset over months to years rather than an acute change, and this degree of new confusion and inattention is not part of normal aging."}, {"id": 731, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 70-year-old man is brought in by his family because of a several-month history of cognition that seems to vary dramatically from day to day, sometimes appearing quite sharp and other times markedly confused, along with well-formed visual hallucinations of small animals that he describes vividly and in detail. Around the same time his cognitive symptoms began, his family also noticed new rigidity, slowed movements, and a shuffling gait. Concerned about the hallucinations, his family gave him a low dose antipsychotic medication obtained from a relative, after which he became markedly more rigid, less responsive, and required emergency evaluation. Which diagnosis best explains this course?", "options": {"A": "Vascular dementia", "B": "Lewy body dementia", "C": "Alzheimer disease", "D": "Major depressive disorder"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Lewy body dementia): Fluctuating cognition, visual hallucinations, and parkinsonism arising around the same time as cognitive decline are the core features of Lewy body dementia, and patients with this disease have a well-documented severe, sometimes dangerous, sensitivity to antipsychotic medications, which explains his marked deterioration after receiving one. Vascular dementia typically shows a stepwise decline related to strokes, Alzheimer disease does not typically present with this early combination of hallucinations and parkinsonism, and major depressive disorder would not explain the parkinsonism or the antipsychotic sensitivity reaction."}, {"id": 732, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 58-year-old man is brought in by his wife because of a two-year history of increasingly inappropriate social behavior, including making crude comments to strangers in public and impulsively spending the family's savings on unnecessary purchases, along with disinhibition and blunted emotional responses that represent a marked change from his previously reserved personality. Despite these behavioral changes, when formally tested his memory for recent events, orientation, and recall of a short list of words all remain relatively intact. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Alzheimer disease", "B": "Delirium from an infection", "C": "Frontotemporal dementia", "D": "Normal pressure hydrocephalus"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Frontotemporal dementia): Early, prominent personality change and disinhibition with relatively preserved memory, occurring at a younger age than typical Alzheimer disease, is the classic presentation of frontotemporal dementia, which affects the frontal and temporal lobes responsible for behavior and social conduct before memory becomes significantly involved. Alzheimer disease characteristically presents with early memory loss rather than personality change, delirium would have an acute rather than two-year course, and normal pressure hydrocephalus presents with gait disturbance and incontinence rather than primarily personality change."}, {"id": 734, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 79-year-old man with known moderate Alzheimer disease, who has been stable at his baseline cognitive level for the past year according to his daughter, is brought to the emergency department because she noticed he became suddenly much more confused and agitated over the course of the last day, with new inattention that clearly fluctuates throughout the visit, at times answering questions appropriately and at other moments unable to follow simple commands. He has not had any recent medication changes according to his daughter. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Attribute the change to expected progression of Alzheimer disease and reassure the family", "B": "Start a new antipsychotic medication as first-line management", "C": "Search for an acute precipitating medical cause of delirium superimposed on his dementia", "D": "Increase his home dose of a cholinesterase inhibitor"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Search for an acute precipitating medical cause of delirium superimposed on his dementia): A sudden, acute worsening of cognition with fluctuating attention in a patient with baseline dementia should always prompt a search for a new acute medical cause, such as infection, medication effect, or a metabolic derangement, rather than being attributed to gradual progression of the underlying dementia, since dementia itself does not cause this kind of acute fluctuating change. Sedating medications should generally be avoided as first-line management of agitation in this setting, and adjusting his baseline dementia medication does not address an acute superimposed process."}, {"id": 735, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 69-year-old woman is being evaluated in a memory clinic for a one-year history of gradually progressive difficulty with word finding and short-term memory that has begun to interfere with her ability to manage her own finances and medications. Her physician orders a standard initial panel of laboratory and imaging studies before attributing her decline solely to a primary neurodegenerative dementia. As part of this initial dementia evaluation, which of the following tests is most appropriate to identify a potentially reversible contributor to her cognitive decline?", "options": {"A": "Genetic testing for the apolipoprotein E4 allele", "B": "Vitamin B12 level and thyroid function tests", "C": "Positron emission tomography amyloid imaging", "D": "Repeat neuropsychological testing in one year"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Vitamin B12 level and thyroid function tests): Vitamin B12 deficiency and thyroid dysfunction are well-recognized, treatable contributors to cognitive impairment, and screening for them along with basic metabolic studies and structural brain imaging is a standard part of the initial dementia workup to identify reversible causes before attributing decline solely to a neurodegenerative process. Apolipoprotein E4 genotyping and amyloid imaging are research or specialized tools rather than routine initial screening, and waiting a year to repeat testing delays identification of a potentially treatable cause."}, {"id": 736, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "An 84-year-old hospitalized patient recovering from hip surgery becomes agitated overnight and is repeatedly pulling at his intravenous lines and attempting to climb out of bed against staff instructions, with a level of alertness that fluctuates noticeably between periods of apparent clarity during the day and marked confusion and disorientation at night, a pattern his family says was not present before his surgery three days ago. The bedside nurse asks the covering physician for guidance on how to manage this behavior safely without resorting to prolonged physical restraints. Which of the following is the most appropriate first step in management?", "options": {"A": "Immediately start a scheduled benzodiazepine to sedate him", "B": "Physically restrain him for the remainder of the admission", "C": "Discharge him home to a more familiar environment for his agitation", "D": "Identify and treat any underlying precipitant while reorienting him and minimizing deliriogenic medications"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Identify and treat any underlying precipitant while reorienting him and minimizing deliriogenic medications): The cornerstone of delirium management is finding and correcting the underlying medical cause while implementing nonpharmacologic measures such as reorientation, promoting a normal sleep-wake cycle, and reducing exposure to deliriogenic drugs, particularly benzodiazepines and anticholinergic agents, which generally worsen delirium rather than help it. Scheduled benzodiazepines are not first-line therapy and typically worsen delirium, prolonged physical restraint without further evaluation is not appropriate management, and discharging a medically unstable, delirious patient is unsafe."}, {"id": 737, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 29-year-old woman with no significant past medical history develops painful vision loss in her right eye over the course of several days, describing the pain as worse with eye movement, and on examination she is found to have an afferent pupillary defect on the right along with decreased visual acuity in that eye. Her symptoms gradually improve over the following weeks with treatment. Six months later she develops new sensory changes and mild weakness in her left leg lasting about three weeks before again improving. MRI of the brain and spinal cord obtained at that time shows multiple periventricular white matter lesions that appear to be of varying age. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Multiple sclerosis", "B": "Guillain-Barre syndrome", "C": "Myasthenia gravis", "D": "Vitamin B12 deficiency"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Multiple sclerosis): Neurologic episodes separated in time and space, here optic neuritis followed months later by a separate sensory episode, together with multiple periventricular white matter lesions of different ages on MRI, is the classic pattern that defines multiple sclerosis. Guillain-Barre syndrome presents as an acute, monophasic ascending weakness rather than episodes separated over months, myasthenia gravis produces fatigable weakness without this pattern of white matter lesions, and vitamin B12 deficiency causes a subacute combined degeneration pattern rather than this relapsing, lesion-separated course."}, {"id": 741, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 32-year-old man with no significant past medical history develops symmetric weakness beginning in his legs that progresses upward to involve his arms over the course of three days, two weeks after an episode of diarrheal illness that he attributed to food poisoning at the time and did not seek care for. He reports tingling in his feet but no significant pain, and on examination his strength is reduced symmetrically in both legs and, to a lesser degree, in both arms, with deep tendon reflexes that are absent throughout all four limbs despite otherwise preserved sensation to light touch. Which of the following findings would be most expected on further workup?", "options": {"A": "Cerebrospinal fluid with markedly elevated neutrophils and a low glucose level, as seen in bacterial meningitis", "B": "MRI showing multiple periventricular white matter plaques consistent with a demyelinating process", "C": "A decremental response on repetitive nerve stimulation, consistent with a neuromuscular junction disorder", "D": "Cerebrospinal fluid with elevated protein and a normal white blood cell count"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Cerebrospinal fluid with elevated protein and a normal white blood cell count): This presentation of ascending, symmetric weakness with areflexia following a preceding gastrointestinal illness is classic for Guillain-Barre syndrome, and the characteristic cerebrospinal fluid finding is albuminocytologic dissociation, an elevated protein level with a normal white blood cell count. A neutrophilic pleocytosis with low glucose is seen in bacterial meningitis, periventricular white matter plaques are seen in multiple sclerosis, and a decremental response on repetitive nerve stimulation is characteristic of myasthenia gravis rather than Guillain-Barre syndrome."}, {"id": 742, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 35-year-old woman is admitted to the hospital three days after developing ascending, symmetric limb weakness that began in her feet and has progressed to involve her hands, in a pattern consistent with newly diagnosed Guillain-Barre syndrome following a recent respiratory illness. She is currently able to speak in full sentences and does not appear to be in acute distress, but her treating team is concerned about the trajectory of her weakness and wants to avoid an unplanned emergent intubation later in her hospital course. Which of the following should be measured serially to anticipate the need for mechanical ventilation before frank respiratory failure develops?", "options": {"A": "Serum creatine kinase measured serially throughout her hospital course", "B": "Erythrocyte sedimentation rate measured serially throughout her hospital course", "C": "Forced vital capacity and negative inspiratory force", "D": "Serum acetylcholine receptor antibody titer measured on repeated testing"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Forced vital capacity and negative inspiratory force): Because respiratory muscle weakness is the most dangerous complication of Guillain-Barre syndrome, serial bedside measurement of forced vital capacity and negative inspiratory force allows clinicians to anticipate the need for elective intubation before the patient develops overt respiratory failure. Creatine kinase and erythrocyte sedimentation rate do not predict respiratory decline in this disease, and acetylcholine receptor antibody titer is relevant to myasthenia gravis, not Guillain-Barre syndrome."}, {"id": 743, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 40-year-old man recently diagnosed with Guillain-Barre syndrome is being counseled by his neurology team about the treatment plan going forward. His wife, who works as a nurse on a different hospital unit, asks the team why they are not simply giving him a course of glucocorticoids, since she has seen corticosteroids used effectively for other neurologic conditions she has cared for, such as multiple sclerosis relapses. Which of the following treatments is considered ineffective and should not be used for Guillain-Barre syndrome despite being effective in other autoimmune neurologic conditions?", "options": {"A": "Intravenous immunoglobulin", "B": "Glucocorticoids", "C": "Plasmapheresis", "D": "Mechanical ventilation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Glucocorticoids): Glucocorticoids have been studied extensively in Guillain-Barre syndrome and have not been shown to improve outcomes, so they are not recommended despite being a mainstay of treatment in other autoimmune neurologic diseases such as multiple sclerosis relapses. Intravenous immunoglobulin and plasmapheresis are both proven effective therapies for Guillain-Barre syndrome, and mechanical ventilation remains an essential supportive measure when respiratory failure develops regardless of the specific immunotherapy chosen."}, {"id": 744, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 60-year-old man with a fifteen-year history of poorly controlled type 2 diabetes mellitus, with hemoglobin A1c values consistently above 9 percent despite multiple medication adjustments, reports several years of progressive numbness and tingling in both feet that has gradually ascended toward his ankles over time, without any weakness. On examination he has reduced sensation to monofilament testing over both feet and absent ankle reflexes bilaterally, with normal strength throughout and normal reflexes at the knees. Which pattern of neuropathy best describes this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Acute ascending demyelinating polyneuropathy", "B": "Fatigable weakness of ocular and bulbar muscles that most clearly worsens through the entire day", "C": "A single mononeuropathy affecting one nerve territory", "D": "Symmetric, distal, sensory-predominant polyneuropathy in a stocking-glove distribution"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Symmetric, distal, sensory-predominant polyneuropathy in a stocking-glove distribution): Diabetes mellitus is the most common cause of peripheral neuropathy in the outpatient setting, and it classically produces a gradually progressive, symmetric, distal, sensory-predominant polyneuropathy beginning in the feet and slowly ascending, described as a stocking-glove distribution. An acute ascending demyelinating polyneuropathy describes Guillain-Barre syndrome rather than a chronic diabetic process, fatigable weakness of ocular and bulbar muscles describes myasthenia gravis, and a single mononeuropathy would not explain this bilateral, symmetric pattern."}, {"id": 745, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 38-year-old man being treated for active pulmonary tuberculosis with a standard four-drug regimen including isoniazid returns to clinic after two months of therapy reporting new symmetric, painful burning sensations in both feet that have gradually worsened, along with mild numbness in a similar distribution. His treating physician reviews his medication list and realizes that a specific supplement that is typically co-prescribed with this regimen to prevent exactly this complication was inadvertently omitted when his prescriptions were filled. Which of the following should have been co-administered to prevent this complication?", "options": {"A": "Vitamin B12", "B": "Folic acid alone", "C": "Pyridoxine (vitamin B6)", "D": "Vitamin D"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Pyridoxine, vitamin B6): Isoniazid can cause a peripheral neuropathy by interfering with pyridoxine metabolism, so co-administration of pyridoxine is standard practice to prevent this complication in patients being treated for tuberculosis. Vitamin B12, folic acid, and vitamin D do not prevent isoniazid-associated neuropathy, since the mechanism specifically involves pyridoxine depletion rather than these other nutrients."}, {"id": 746, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 27-year-old woman with a six-month history of intermittent double vision and mild sensory changes in one arm is evaluated by a neurologist. On examination of extraocular movements, when she is asked to look to the right, her left eye fails to fully adduct past the midline while her right eye shows nystagmus as it abducts. When she looks to the left, the opposite pattern is observed. Convergence remains intact. Brain MRI obtained the same day reveals several periventricular white matter lesions. Which structure is most likely affected?", "options": {"A": "Optic nerve, causing monocular vision loss", "B": "Oculomotor nerve trunk, causing ptosis", "C": "Medial longitudinal fasciculus (internuclear ophthalmoplegia)", "D": "Neuromuscular junction, causing fatigable weakness"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Medial longitudinal fasciculus, internuclear ophthalmoplegia): Impaired adduction of one eye on attempted lateral gaze with nystagmus of the opposite abducting eye is the classic sign of internuclear ophthalmoplegia, caused by a lesion in the medial longitudinal fasciculus connecting the abducens and oculomotor nuclei, and it is a common and highly suggestive finding in multiple sclerosis, especially in a young patient with periventricular lesions. Isolated optic nerve involvement would cause monocular vision loss rather than this gaze abnormality, a full oculomotor nerve palsy would cause ptosis and a fixed, dilated pupil in addition to adduction weakness, and neuromuscular junction disease produces fatigable weakness rather than this specific brainstem sign."}, {"id": 747, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 45-year-old man with no significant past medical history is brought to the emergency department by his family because of three days of fever and progressively worsening confusion, culminating in an episode witnessed by his wife earlier that day in which his right hand and arm jerked rhythmically for about a minute while he stared blankly and did not respond to her voice. His family also notes a subtle personality change over the preceding two days, with uncharacteristic irritability. Brain MRI obtained emergently shows edema and abnormal signal change involving the temporal lobes bilaterally, worse on the left. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate treatment?", "options": {"A": "Empiric intravenous acyclovir", "B": "Oral fluconazole", "C": "Intravenous vancomycin alone", "D": "Watchful waiting pending PCR results"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Empiric intravenous acyclovir): Fever, altered mental status, seizures, and temporal lobe changes on MRI are highly suggestive of herpes simplex virus encephalitis, and because delayed treatment substantially worsens outcomes, empiric intravenous acyclovir should be started immediately once this diagnosis is suspected, without waiting for confirmatory cerebrospinal fluid polymerase chain reaction results. Fluconazole targets fungal infection rather than herpes encephalitis, vancomycin alone does not treat a viral process, and delaying treatment while awaiting results risks irreversible neurologic injury."}, {"id": 748, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate step in his management?", "options": {"A": "Wait for lumbar puncture results before giving any antibiotics", "B": "Obtain an outpatient MRI scheduled for the following week", "C": "Start oral amoxicillin and discharge with close follow-up", "D": "Draw blood cultures and start empiric intravenous antibiotics without delay"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Draw blood cultures and start empiric intravenous antibiotics without delay): Suspected bacterial meningitis is a true emergency, and empiric antibiotics should be started immediately after blood cultures are drawn if there will be any meaningful delay in obtaining cerebrospinal fluid, since delayed antibiotic therapy is directly associated with increased mortality. Waiting for lumbar puncture results before treating risks a fatal delay, outpatient imaging scheduled for the following week is entirely inappropriate for this acute presentation, and oral antibiotics with discharge would be dangerously inadequate for suspected bacterial meningitis.", "case_id": "CASE-MEN-1", "case_text": "A 24-year-old previously healthy man is brought to the emergency department with two hours of fever, severe headache, photophobia, and neck stiffness. He appears acutely ill and is somewhat confused.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 749, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "Cerebrospinal fluid analysis eventually returns showing a markedly elevated white blood cell count with neutrophil predominance, low glucose, and high protein. Which empiric antibiotic regimen should have been given, given his age?", "options": {"A": "A single dose of oral azithromycin given empirically for this presentation", "B": "Vancomycin plus ampicillin but with no third generation cephalosporin added", "C": "Acyclovir alone without any additional antibacterial coverage", "D": "Vancomycin plus a third generation cephalosporin such as ceftriaxone"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Vancomycin plus a third generation cephalosporin such as ceftriaxone): This cerebrospinal fluid profile, with a neutrophil-predominant pleocytosis, low glucose, and high protein, confirms bacterial meningitis, and standard empiric therapy in an adult of this age combines vancomycin (for resistant pneumococcus) with a third generation cephalosporin, reserving added ampicillin for patients at risk for Listeria (those under 1 month or over 50 years old, pregnant, or immunocompromised), which this 24-year-old does not describe. A single dose of oral azithromycin is grossly inadequate, omitting the cephalosporin would leave gaps in coverage, and acyclovir alone would not treat bacterial infection.", "case_id": "CASE-MEN-1", "case_text": "A 24-year-old previously healthy man is brought to the emergency department with two hours of fever, severe headache, photophobia, and neck stiffness. He appears acutely ill and is somewhat confused.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 750, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "Which adjunctive medication, given with or just before the first antibiotic dose, has been shown to reduce neurologic complications in this condition, particularly when caused by Streptococcus pneumoniae?", "options": {"A": "High dose oral prednisone started after antibiotics", "B": "Intravenous immunoglobulin therapy given before antibiotics", "C": "Dexamethasone given with the first antibiotic dose", "D": "Plasmapheresis or therapeutic plasma exchange started early"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Dexamethasone given with the first antibiotic dose): Adjunctive dexamethasone, given with or shortly before the first dose of antibiotics, has been shown to reduce neurologic complications and improve outcomes in bacterial meningitis, particularly pneumococcal meningitis, and its benefit is lost if it is delayed until after antibiotics have already been running for some time. Intravenous immunoglobulin and plasmapheresis have no established role in bacterial meningitis, and delaying the glucocorticoid until well after antibiotics begin loses the timing-dependent benefit that makes this intervention effective.", "case_id": "CASE-MEN-1", "case_text": "A 24-year-old previously healthy man is brought to the emergency department with two hours of fever, severe headache, photophobia, and neck stiffness. He appears acutely ill and is somewhat confused.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 751, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 20-year-old college student living in a dormitory presents with two days of fever, headache, and mild neck stiffness, along with several classmates reportedly having similar symptoms recently that resolved on their own within a week. He appears uncomfortable but is alert and oriented, and his neurologic exam is otherwise nonfocal. A lumbar puncture is performed as part of his evaluation. Which of the following cerebrospinal fluid patterns would be most expected in a patient with viral meningitis, in contrast to bacterial meningitis?", "options": {"A": "Marked neutrophilic pleocytosis with low glucose", "B": "Lymphocyte-predominant, modest pleocytosis with normal glucose", "C": "Grossly bloody fluid that does not clear between tubes", "D": "Markedly elevated protein with an otherwise normal white blood cell count"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Lymphocyte-predominant, modest pleocytosis with normal glucose): Viral meningitis typically produces a more modest, lymphocyte-predominant cerebrospinal fluid pleocytosis with a normal glucose level, in contrast to the marked neutrophilic pleocytosis and low glucose typical of bacterial meningitis. Grossly bloody fluid that fails to clear suggests subarachnoid hemorrhage rather than infection, and markedly elevated protein with a completely normal white blood cell count describes the albuminocytologic dissociation of Guillain-Barre syndrome rather than a meningeal infectious process."}, {"id": 754, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 65-year-old woman with no significant past medical history is watching a movie in a dark theater when she develops sudden severe pain in her left eye, blurred vision, and halos around the theater lights, along with nausea and vomiting that she initially attributes to something she ate. When she is brought to the emergency department an hour later, examination reveals that her left pupil is fixed in a mid-dilated position and poorly reactive to light, and the globe feels notably firm to gentle palpation compared with the other side. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate treatment?", "options": {"A": "Topical antibiotic drops applied alone without any attempt to lower intraocular pressure", "B": "Observation alone, since the episode occurred specifically in a darkened movie theater environment", "C": "Oral acetazolamide used alone, deliberately avoiding any additional topical pressure-lowering agents", "D": "Urgent lowering of intraocular pressure with topical and systemic agents followed by laser peripheral iridotomy"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Urgent lowering of intraocular pressure with topical and systemic agents followed by laser peripheral iridotomy): This presentation, precipitated by a dark environment with a fixed mid-dilated pupil, a firm globe, halos, and severe pain, is classic for acute angle closure glaucoma, an ophthalmologic emergency requiring urgent pressure-lowering treatment (such as a topical beta blocker, an alpha agonist, and acetazolamide) followed by laser peripheral iridotomy to prevent permanent vision loss. Topical antibiotics do not address elevated intraocular pressure, observation risks permanent blindness within hours, and acetazolamide alone without topical therapy is not the complete standard urgent regimen."}, {"id": 755, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 70-year-old man with well-controlled hypertension undergoes a routine eye examination as part of his annual physical, during which formal visual field testing incidentally reveals significant peripheral visual field loss in both eyes that he had not previously noticed or reported any symptoms related to. He denies any eye pain, redness, or recent change in central vision. Further examination reveals an elevated intraocular pressure in both eyes and cupping of the optic discs. Which of the following best describes his condition?", "options": {"A": "Chronic open angle glaucoma", "B": "Acute angle closure glaucoma", "C": "Bilateral retinal detachment", "D": "Acute anterior uveitis"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Chronic open angle glaucoma): Gradual, typically asymptomatic peripheral visual field loss discovered incidentally, with elevated intraocular pressure and no acute pain, is the classic presentation of chronic open angle glaucoma, managed with topical prostaglandin analogs or beta blockers to gradually lower intraocular pressure and preserve remaining vision. Acute angle closure glaucoma presents with sudden severe pain rather than a painless, gradual course, and retinal detachment and uveitis present with acute visual symptoms rather than this chronic, painless field loss."}, {"id": 756, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 55-year-old man with a history of severe high myopia requiring thick corrective lenses since childhood reports a sudden shower of new floaters in his right eye over the past day, along with flashes of light in his peripheral vision that he describes as brief lightning streaks, and now, over the past several hours, a gray curtain-like shadow that has begun spreading upward across the lower part of his visual field in that eye. He denies any eye pain or redness. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Urgent ophthalmology referral for likely retinal detachment", "B": "Reassurance that floaters are usually benign and generally require only routine follow-up", "C": "A course of oral glucocorticoids", "D": "Topical antibiotic eye drops"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Urgent ophthalmology referral for likely retinal detachment): A sudden shower of floaters, flashes of light, and a curtain-like visual field defect, especially in a patient with high myopia (a risk factor), is classic for retinal detachment, which is a surgical emergency because delay risks permanent vision loss, particularly if the macula becomes involved. While some floaters can be benign, this combination of symptoms should never simply be reassured away, and neither oral glucocorticoids nor topical antibiotics address a retinal detachment."}, {"id": 758, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 50-year-old man with no significant past medical history wakes up one morning with sudden hearing loss in his left ear along with mild ringing in that ear, and he denies any preceding ear pain, trauma, upper respiratory symptoms, or recent loud noise exposure. He initially assumes it will resolve on its own and waits three days before seeking care. On examination his ear canal and tympanic membrane appear entirely normal, and formal audiometry confirms a sensorineural pattern of hearing loss in the affected ear. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?", "options": {"A": "Reassurance that it should resolve untreated", "B": "Cerumen removal as the intervention", "C": "Prompt oral glucocorticoid therapy", "D": "Watchful waiting for six months first"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Prompt oral glucocorticoid therapy): Sudden sensorineural hearing loss is an otologic emergency because early treatment with oral glucocorticoids improves the chance of meaningful hearing recovery, so prompt evaluation and treatment should not be delayed. Sensorineural hearing loss does not reliably resolve on its own without treatment, cerumen removal addresses conductive rather than sensorineural hearing loss and would not be expected to help here, and waiting months before treating eliminates the window during which glucocorticoids are most likely to be beneficial."}, {"id": 759, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 44-year-old woman presents to her physician reporting several weeks of difficulty hearing conversations at work, more noticeably on one side, without any associated pain, drainage, or dizziness. As part of the bedside evaluation before ordering formal audiometry, tuning fork testing is performed. On Weber testing, performed by placing a vibrating tuning fork on the midline of her forehead, the sound is heard louder in her right ear, and on Rinne testing performed in that same right ear, bone conduction is perceived as louder than air conduction. Which of the following best describes this pattern?", "options": {"A": "Conductive hearing loss in the right ear", "B": "Sensorineural hearing loss in the right ear", "C": "Sensorineural hearing loss in the left ear", "D": "Normal hearing bilaterally"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Conductive hearing loss in the right ear): In conductive hearing loss, the Weber test lateralizes to the affected ear because bone conduction is relatively enhanced when the air conduction pathway is impaired, and the Rinne test in that ear shows bone conduction better than air conduction, which is the reverse of the normal pattern. Sensorineural hearing loss would instead cause the Weber test to lateralize away from the affected ear toward the better hearing ear, with air conduction remaining better than bone conduction on Rinne testing in both ears, so this pattern does not fit either sensorineural option, and it clearly is not normal hearing given the abnormal Rinne result."}, {"id": 761, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 68-year-old woman with no significant cardiovascular risk factors and no prior neurologic history reports brief episodes of intense spinning vertigo lasting less than a minute each, specifically and reliably triggered by rolling over in bed at night or by looking up to reach a high shelf in her kitchen, occurring several times over the past week and causing her significant anxiety about falling. She denies any associated hearing loss, tinnitus, headache, weakness, or other neurologic symptoms, and between episodes she feels entirely back to her normal baseline. Which maneuver is both diagnostic and therapeutic for this condition?", "options": {"A": "Weber and Rinne tuning fork tests performed at the bedside", "B": "Caloric testing as the sole diagnostic and therapeutic step for this patient", "C": "Valsalva maneuver performed alone without any additional positional testing", "D": "Dix-Hallpike maneuver for diagnosis and the Epley maneuver for treatment"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Dix-Hallpike maneuver for diagnosis and the Epley maneuver for treatment): Brief, intense, position-triggered vertigo without hearing loss is classic for benign paroxysmal positional vertigo, which is diagnosed with the Dix-Hallpike maneuver (provoking characteristic nystagmus that fatigues with repeated testing) and treated with the Epley repositioning maneuver, which repositions the displaced otoconia. Weber and Rinne tests assess hearing rather than positional vertigo, caloric testing evaluates vestibular function but is not itself therapeutic, and the Valsalva maneuver has no established diagnostic or therapeutic role in this condition."}, {"id": 762, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 60-year-old man presents to the emergency department with acute onset vertigo that began several hours ago and has been persistent and constant since then, rather than triggered by specific head movements. On examination his physician notes mild slurring of his speech, double vision on lateral gaze, and difficulty with rapid alternating hand movements on the left side. His nystagmus does not appear to fatigue or diminish with repeated positional testing. Which finding on examination would most suggest that this patient's acute vertigo has a central rather than a peripheral vestibular cause?", "options": {"A": "Nystagmus that clearly fatigues and diminishes with repeated positional testing over time", "B": "Symptoms triggered specifically by a change in head position and resolving within a minute", "C": "Additional brainstem signs such as dysarthria, diplopia, or limb ataxia, with nystagmus that does not fatigue", "D": "Complete absence of any nystagmus at any point observed throughout the entire examination"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Additional brainstem signs such as dysarthria, diplopia, or limb ataxia, with nystagmus that does not fatigue): Central causes of vertigo, such as a posterior circulation stroke, are suggested by accompanying brainstem signs and by nystagmus that is persistent rather than fatiguing with repeated testing, which helps distinguish a dangerous central cause from benign peripheral vertigo. A fatiguing nystagmus pattern and a brief, position-triggered course are instead features that support a benign peripheral cause such as benign paroxysmal positional vertigo, and a complete absence of nystagmus does not by itself indicate a central process."}, {"id": 764, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 67-year-old man with a known history of moderate carotid artery disease on the left side, followed by his vascular surgeon with serial ultrasounds, reports a strange episode earlier that day in which his vision in his left eye suddenly went dark, as though a curtain were coming down over that eye, lasting about 10 minutes before resolving completely on its own with no residual visual change. He denies any pain during the episode and his vision has been entirely normal since it resolved. Which of the following best explains this transient event?", "options": {"A": "Amaurosis fugax from transient retinal ischemia", "B": "Acute angle closure glaucoma with a firm globe", "C": "Anterior uveitis with anterior chamber inflammation", "D": "Meniere disease with episodic vertigo and hearing loss"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Amaurosis fugax from transient retinal ischemia): A transient, painless, curtain-like monocular vision loss that resolves completely is the classic description of amaurosis fugax, typically caused by a transient embolic or ischemic event affecting the retinal circulation, often arising from an ipsilateral carotid artery source, and it should prompt the same urgent stroke and carotid risk evaluation as a transient ischemic attack elsewhere in the brain. Acute angle closure glaucoma and anterior uveitis both present with a painful eye rather than this painless transient pattern, and Meniere disease causes vertigo and hearing symptoms rather than monocular vision loss."}, {"id": 766, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 72-year-old man presents to his physician with a two-week history of difficulty hearing out of his right ear that developed gradually. On otoscopic examination of the right ear canal, a large, dark, impacted cerumen plug is visualized completely occluding the view of the tympanic membrane, while the left ear canal and tympanic membrane appear entirely normal. He denies any vertigo, tinnitus, or ear pain. Which of the following historical or examination features would most support conductive rather than sensorineural hearing loss as the cause of his new difficulty hearing?", "options": {"A": "Sudden onset with no preceding ear symptoms and a normal-appearing ear canal", "B": "A visible impacted cerumen plug seen on otoscopic exam", "C": "Associated vertigo and tinnitus with a normal ear canal", "D": "A family history of a progressive sensorineural hearing disorder"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A visible impacted cerumen plug seen on otoscopic exam): Cerumen impaction is one of the most common causes of conductive hearing loss, and a visible obstructing plug on otoscopic exam directly supports a conductive mechanism, which is confirmed by a Weber test lateralizing to the affected ear and a Rinne test showing bone conduction better than air conduction in that ear. Sudden onset with a normal-appearing canal, associated vertigo and tinnitus, and a family history of a progressive sensorineural disorder are all features that point toward a sensorineural rather than conductive cause."}, {"id": 767, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 38-year-old woman presents with a two-year history of gradually progressive hearing loss in her right ear, which she first noticed while talking on the telephone and which has slowly worsened since then. She reports that her mother and maternal aunt both developed significant hearing loss in their thirties and forties as well. On examination her tympanic membrane appears entirely normal and there is no visible cerumen impaction or middle ear effusion, and tuning fork testing confirms a conductive pattern of hearing loss on the right. Which of the following is a common cause of conductive hearing loss in this setting, distinct from cerumen impaction?", "options": {"A": "Acoustic neuroma", "B": "Otosclerosis", "C": "Meniere disease", "D": "Presbycusis"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Otosclerosis): Otosclerosis is an inherited disorder of abnormal bone remodeling around the stapes footplate that causes progressive conductive hearing loss, often with a family history and a normal-appearing tympanic membrane, and it is a classic cause of conductive hearing loss distinct from cerumen impaction. Acoustic neuroma and presbycusis both typically cause sensorineural rather than conductive hearing loss, and Meniere disease presents with episodic vertigo and fluctuating hearing loss rather than this gradually progressive conductive pattern."}, {"id": 768, "domain": "nervous_system_and_special_senses", "question": "A 26-year-old man with a several-year history of occasional migraine headaches presents to his physician because he has recently developed a new and different pattern of headache that concerns him. He describes recurrent episodes of severe, strictly one-sided pain centered around his left eye lasting under an hour each, occurring several times a day for the past two weeks, during which the affected eye becomes noticeably red and begins tearing. He notes that between these clusters of frequent attacks, he can go months at a time without experiencing any headache at all, which is quite different from his usual migraine pattern. Which of the following best distinguishes this new headache type from his known migraine?", "options": {"A": "The presence of any new headache, since migraine typically does not recur in this clustered pattern", "B": "The much shorter attack duration, prominent ipsilateral autonomic features, and cyclical clustering pattern", "C": "The fact that cluster headache is typically described as unilateral", "D": "The presence of nausea, which is often considered more characteristic of cluster headache than migraine"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The much shorter attack duration, prominent ipsilateral autonomic features, and cyclical clustering pattern): Cluster headache is distinguished from migraine by its much shorter individual attack duration (minutes to a few hours versus 4 to 72 hours), prominent ipsilateral autonomic features such as tearing, conjunctival injection, and nasal congestion, and its characteristic pattern of clustering into periods of frequent attacks separated by long symptom-free intervals. Migraine can also recur in a pattern over time, migraine is frequently unilateral as well so this alone does not distinguish the two, and nausea is actually a prominent feature of migraine rather than being unique to cluster headache."}, {"id": 771, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 24-year-old woman with a history of intermittent wheezing since childhood presents to clinic complaining of nighttime cough and chest tightness three to four nights per week for the past two months, along with daytime symptoms most days. She uses an albuterol inhaler almost daily for relief. Spirometry in clinic shows an FEV1/FVC ratio of 68% that improves to 82% after bronchodilator administration. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in the long-term management of this patient's condition?", "options": {"A": "Continue albuterol as his only medication", "B": "Add a long-acting beta agonist as monotherapy", "C": "Begin a daily inhaled corticosteroid", "D": "Start daily oral prednisone for control"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Begin a daily inhaled corticosteroid): her symptom pattern of daily daytime symptoms and frequent nocturnal awakenings, along with a documented reversible obstructive pattern on spirometry, places her at a persistent asthma severity that requires a daily controller medication rather than reliance on a rescue inhaler alone. An inhaled corticosteroid reduces airway inflammation and is the preferred first controller for persistent asthma. A long-acting beta agonist should never be used without a concurrent inhaled corticosteroid because monotherapy increases the risk of severe exacerbation and death. Oral steroids and bronchial thermoplasty are reserved for more severe or refractory disease, not initial persistent asthma."}, {"id": 773, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 55-year-old man with COPD and a resting oxygen saturation of 86% on room air at two separate clinic visits, three weeks apart, is found to have a PaO2 of 54 mmHg on arterial blood gas while clinically stable. He has no evidence of cor pulmonale or peripheral edema. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": {"A": "Start empiric antibiotics for exacerbation", "B": "Increase his inhaled bronchodilator dose", "C": "Prescribe long-term supplemental home oxygen", "D": "Schedule pulmonary function testing in six months"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Prescribe long-term supplemental home oxygen): long-term home oxygen therapy improves survival in COPD patients with resting hypoxemia defined as a PaO2 at or below 55 mmHg, or an oxygen saturation at or below 88%, confirmed on stable outpatient testing. This patient meets that threshold on repeated measurement while clinically stable, so oxygen should be prescribed regardless of the absence of cor pulmonale. There is no indication of an acute infectious exacerbation requiring antibiotics. Simply adjusting bronchodilator dosing or deferring to repeat testing without addressing the hypoxemia would delay a mortality-reducing intervention, and nocturnal ventilation is not the first-line treatment for daytime resting hypoxemia in stable COPD."}, {"id": 775, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 32-year-old woman with known asthma is brought to the emergency department after two hours of worsening wheezing and dyspnea despite home albuterol use. On arrival she is anxious, tachypneic to 34 breaths per minute, and speaking in single words. An arterial blood gas is obtained and shown in the panel. Which of the following best describes her acid-base status and its clinical significance?", "options": {"A": "Acute respiratory acidosis indicating she is already in frank respiratory failure", "B": "Acute respiratory alkalosis from hyperventilation, consistent with an early severe exacerbation", "C": "Chronic compensated metabolic alkalosis unrelated to her asthma", "D": "Normal acid-base status that argues against a severe exacerbation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Acute respiratory alkalosis from hyperventilation, consistent with an early severe exacerbation): the blood gas shows an elevated pH with a low PaCO2 and a normal bicarbonate, consistent with acute respiratory alkalosis from tachypnea-driven hyperventilation, which is the expected early pattern in a severe asthma exacerbation as the patient works hard to compensate for airway obstruction. This is not yet respiratory acidosis, which would indicate a much later and more ominous stage of fatigue. There is no evidence of a chronic compensated process given the acute presentation and normal bicarbonate, and while a mild metabolic component can appear later, the primary driver here is respiratory alkalosis, not lactic acidosis.", "image": "question_images/q_775.png"}, {"id": 777, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 63-year-old man with COPD is admitted for an acute exacerbation with increased dyspnea, increased sputum volume, and new sputum purulence over the past three days. He is afebrile with a respiratory rate of 24 and oxygen saturation of 90% on 2 liters nasal cannula. Arterial blood gas shows a pH of 7.32 with a PaCO2 of 58 mmHg. He is alert and able to protect his airway. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management of his respiratory status?", "options": {"A": "Proceed immediately to endotracheal intubation", "B": "Initiate noninvasive positive pressure ventilation (BiPAP)", "C": "Observe without any change in respiratory support", "D": "Start high-flow nasal cannula alone without positive pressure"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Initiate noninvasive positive pressure ventilation (BiPAP)): this presentation reflects an acute COPD exacerbation complicated by acute-on-chronic respiratory acidosis, and in a patient who remains alert and able to protect the airway, noninvasive positive pressure ventilation is the first-line intervention because it reduces the work of breathing, improves alveolar ventilation, and often avoids the need for invasive intubation. Immediate intubation is not necessary when the patient can tolerate a noninvasive trial and has intact mental status. Simple observation would allow ongoing respiratory acidosis to worsen, and high-flow nasal cannula alone does not provide the positive pressure support needed to unload the respiratory muscles as effectively as BiPAP in this setting."}, {"id": 778, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 41-year-old nonsmoking woman presents with progressive dyspnea and is found to have panacinar emphysema predominantly affecting the lower lung zones on chest CT, a pattern distinct from the upper lobe predominant centriacinar emphysema typically seen with smoking. Her father died of liver cirrhosis at a young age. Which of the following is the most appropriate next diagnostic test?", "options": {"A": "Serum alpha-1 antitrypsin level", "B": "Sweat chloride testing for cystic fibrosis", "C": "Serum angiotensin converting enzyme level", "D": "Antinuclear antibody testing"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Serum alpha-1 antitrypsin level): lower lobe predominant panacinar emphysema in a young or nonsmoking patient, especially with a family history of early liver disease, is a classic presentation of alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, an inherited condition in which the abnormal protein accumulates in hepatocytes causing liver disease while its deficiency in the lungs leaves neutrophil elastase unopposed, destroying alveolar walls. A serum alpha-1 antitrypsin level is the appropriate initial test. Sweat chloride testing evaluates for cystic fibrosis, which presents differently with bronchiectasis, ACE level is used in sarcoidosis, and antinuclear antibody and tuberculin testing do not address this clinical picture."}, {"id": 780, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "Which of the following is the most appropriate initial pharmacologic management for this exacerbation?", "options": {"A": "Nebulized albuterol alone with no other medication changes", "B": "Systemic corticosteroids alone without any antibiotic or bronchodilator", "C": "A prophylactic antibiotic alone without corticosteroids or bronchodilators", "D": "Nebulized albuterol and ipratropium plus systemic corticosteroids and an antibiotic"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Nebulized albuterol and ipratropium plus systemic corticosteroids and an antibiotic): this represents an acute COPD exacerbation with the classic triad of increased dyspnea, increased sputum volume, and increased sputum purulence, all of which support adding an antibiotic to the standard bundle of short-acting bronchodilators (albuterol and ipratropium) and a short course of systemic corticosteroids. Bronchodilators or corticosteroids alone would omit a component of care that is indicated given the purulent sputum change. There is no indication for antifungal therapy in this typical bacterial exacerbation picture.", "case_id": "case_copd_1", "case_text": "A 68-year-old man with a 50 pack-year smoking history and known severe COPD presents to the emergency department with three days of worsening dyspnea, increased sputum production, and new yellow-green sputum. He is afebrile, respiratory rate 28, heart rate 110, and oxygen saturation 88% on room air. He is using accessory muscles but is alert and oriented, able to answer questions in short sentences.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 781, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "Despite initial bronchodilator and corticosteroid therapy, the same patient remains tachypneic with worsening dyspnea two hours later. An arterial blood gas is obtained and shown in the panel. Given these results and his intact mental status, which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Proceed directly to endotracheal intubation without a noninvasive trial", "B": "Initiate noninvasive positive pressure ventilation (BiPAP)", "C": "Discharge home with close outpatient follow-up", "D": "Continue current therapy with no escalation of respiratory support"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Initiate noninvasive positive pressure ventilation (BiPAP)): the blood gas demonstrates an acute respiratory acidosis with hypoxemia superimposed on a chronically elevated bicarbonate consistent with COPD, and because he remains alert and able to protect his airway, noninvasive positive pressure ventilation is the appropriate first-line escalation to reduce work of breathing before considering invasive ventilation. Jumping directly to intubation is not necessary when a noninvasive trial is appropriate and feasible. Discharging him or leaving his respiratory support unchanged would be unsafe given his worsening acidosis, and there is no clinical information here suggesting pulmonary embolism as the primary process.", "image": "question_images/q_781.png", "case_id": "case_copd_1", "case_text": "A 68-year-old man with a 50 pack-year smoking history and known severe COPD presents to the emergency department with three days of worsening dyspnea, increased sputum production, and new yellow-green sputum. He is afebrile, respiratory rate 28, heart rate 110, and oxygen saturation 88% on room air. He is using accessory muscles but is alert and oriented, able to answer questions in short sentences.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 782, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "The same patient is placed on BiPAP, but over the next 45 minutes he becomes increasingly somnolent, is difficult to arouse, and his respiratory effort visibly decreases despite the mask being properly fitted and pressures optimized. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": {"A": "Increase the expiratory positive airway pressure only and reassess in another hour", "B": "Switch to high-flow nasal cannula in place of BiPAP", "C": "Administer additional sedation to help him tolerate the mask", "D": "Proceed to endotracheal intubation and invasive mechanical ventilation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Proceed to endotracheal intubation and invasive mechanical ventilation): a declining level of consciousness and reduced respiratory effort on noninvasive ventilation are signs of failure of the noninvasive approach and impending respiratory arrest, which mandate prompt endotracheal intubation rather than further adjustment of noninvasive settings. Switching to high-flow nasal cannula would provide less ventilatory support, not more, and is inappropriate at this point. Administering sedation to a somnolent, fatiguing patient would be dangerous by further blunting respiratory drive, and observation on a general floor is unsafe given his trajectory toward respiratory failure.", "case_id": "case_copd_1", "case_text": "A 68-year-old man with a 50 pack-year smoking history and known severe COPD presents to the emergency department with three days of worsening dyspnea, increased sputum production, and new yellow-green sputum. He is afebrile, respiratory rate 28, heart rate 110, and oxygen saturation 88% on room air. He is using accessory muscles but is alert and oriented, able to answer questions in short sentences.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 783, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 71-year-old man with severe COPD on triple inhaler therapy (long-acting muscarinic antagonist, long-acting beta agonist, and inhaled corticosteroid) continues to have two to three exacerbations per year requiring oral corticosteroids. He has chronic bronchitis symptoms with daily sputum production. Which of the following additional therapies has been shown to reduce exacerbation frequency in patients with this phenotype?", "options": {"A": "Scheduled short-acting beta agonist four times daily", "B": "Daily oral corticosteroids as maintenance therapy", "C": "Daily low-dose azithromycin", "D": "Discontinuation of his inhaled corticosteroid"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Daily low-dose azithromycin): in patients with frequent COPD exacerbations and a chronic bronchitis phenotype who remain symptomatic despite optimized inhaler therapy, chronic low-dose macrolide therapy such as azithromycin has been shown in clinical trials to reduce exacerbation frequency, likely through anti-inflammatory and antimicrobial effects. Adding more scheduled short-acting beta agonist does not address exacerbation prevention. Chronic oral corticosteroids carry substantial long-term toxicity and are not recommended as routine maintenance therapy, and there is no indication to stop his inhaled corticosteroid or to treat empirically for tuberculosis based on chronic sputum production alone."}, {"id": 785, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 45-year-old previously healthy man presents with three days of fever, productive cough with rust-colored sputum, and right-sided pleuritic chest pain. Chest radiograph shows a right lower lobe consolidation. He has no comorbidities and takes no regular medications. Vital signs are stable and he is being managed as an outpatient. Which of the following is the most appropriate empiric antibiotic regimen?", "options": {"A": "Vancomycin plus piperacillin-tazobactam", "B": "A carbapenem with antipseudomonal activity", "C": "Amoxicillin or a macrolide", "D": "Isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Amoxicillin or a macrolide): this presentation is classic for lobar community-acquired pneumonia most likely caused by Streptococcus pneumoniae, and for a previously healthy outpatient without comorbidities, amoxicillin or a macrolide is the appropriate first-line empiric regimen per current outpatient CAP guidelines. Broad-spectrum agents with antipseudomonal or MRSA coverage such as vancomycin plus piperacillin-tazobactam or a carbapenem are reserved for patients with specific risk factors for resistant organisms or hospital-acquired infection, which this patient does not have. Antituberculous therapy and antifungal therapy are not indicated for this acute bacterial lobar pneumonia presentation."}, {"id": 787, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 62-year-old man develops fever, cough, and diarrhea three days after returning from a business trip that included a stay at a hotel with a recently serviced cooling tower. He is also noted to have a heart rate of 88 despite a temperature of 39.4 degrees Celsius, and his sodium level returns at 128 mEq/L. Chest radiograph shows a patchy infiltrate. Which of the following is the most appropriate diagnostic test to confirm the suspected diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Blood cultures alone", "B": "Urinary antigen test for Legionella", "C": "Sputum Gram stain alone", "D": "Rapid influenza antigen testing"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Urinary antigen test for Legionella): the combination of high fever with relative bradycardia, gastrointestinal symptoms, hyponatremia, and exposure to a potential aerosolized water source such as a cooling tower strongly suggests Legionella pneumonia, which is most efficiently diagnosed with a urinary antigen test. Legionella is a fastidious organism that does not grow well on standard blood culture media and Gram stain does not reliably identify it either, since it stains poorly with conventional methods. Influenza antigen testing and cold agglutinin titers, the latter more associated with Mycoplasma, would not be expected to confirm this specific clinical picture."}, {"id": 788, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 60-year-old man was recently diagnosed with Legionella pneumonia after a positive urinary antigen test obtained during a hospitalization for fever, cough, diarrhea, and confusion following a cruise ship vacation. He was initially started on a beta-lactam antibiotic in the emergency department before the diagnosis was confirmed, but his fevers persisted for another 48 hours despite adequate dosing. Once the urinary antigen result returns, the treatment team plans to change his antibiotic regimen. Which of the following antibiotic classes is most appropriate, and why does a standard beta-lactam alone fail against this organism?", "options": {"A": "A beta-lactam alone, because Legionella has an especially thick cell wall requiring only cell wall active therapy", "B": "A macrolide or fluoroquinolone, because Legionella is an intracellular organism not effectively treated by cell wall active agents", "C": "An aminoglycoside alone, because Legionella largely lacks bacterial ribosomes for this drug class to target", "D": "Vancomycin alone, because Legionella is thought to be a gram-positive organism requiring glycopeptide therapy specifically"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A macrolide or fluoroquinolone, because Legionella is an intracellular organism not effectively treated by cell wall active agents): Legionella is an intracellular pathogen, and beta-lactam antibiotics, which act on bacterial cell wall synthesis, do not achieve adequate intracellular concentrations to eradicate it, which is why macrolides or fluoroquinolones, which penetrate host cells effectively, are the treatment of choice. Legionella does not have an unusually thick cell wall requiring cell wall agents alone, it is not a gram-positive organism appropriately treated with vancomycin, it possesses ribosomes like other bacteria, and it is not a strict anaerobe requiring metronidazole."}, {"id": 791, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 29-year-old previously healthy woman presents with a one-week history of low-grade fever, dry cough, headache, and malaise. Her symptoms seem disproportionately mild given how unwell she initially felt. Chest radiograph shows a diffuse, patchy, interstitial infiltrate that appears more impressive than her clinical examination would suggest. Cold agglutinin testing is positive. Which of the following organisms is the most likely cause of her pneumonia?", "options": {"A": "Streptococcus pneumoniae", "B": "Staphylococcus aureus", "C": "Mycoplasma pneumoniae", "D": "Klebsiella pneumoniae"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Mycoplasma pneumoniae): this presentation, a gradual onset of dry cough and constitutional symptoms in a young, otherwise healthy patient with a chest radiograph that appears more severe than the clinical picture suggests, along with a positive cold agglutinin test, is classic for atypical pneumonia caused by Mycoplasma pneumoniae. Streptococcus pneumoniae typically causes a more acute lobar pneumonia with a productive cough and higher fever. Staphylococcus aureus and Klebsiella pneumoniae are more associated with severe, necrotizing, or postinfluenza pneumonia, and Pseudomonas aeruginosa is more typical of hospital-acquired or structural lung disease-associated pneumonia, none of which fit this mild atypical presentation."}, {"id": 792, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 68-year-old woman with hypertension and mild chronic kidney disease is evaluated for community-acquired pneumonia. She has fever, cough, and a new infiltrate on chest radiograph. She does not have confusion, her respiratory rate is 22, systolic blood pressure is 128 mmHg, and her BUN is within the normal range. She is 68 years old. Given her CURB-65 score, which of the following is the most appropriate initial management setting and regimen?", "options": {"A": "Intensive care unit admission recommended primarily on the basis of her age alone", "B": "Outpatient treatment with a respiratory fluoroquinolone or a beta-lactam plus macrolide given her comorbidities", "C": "Inpatient admission with intravenous vancomycin and meropenem as an initial broad-spectrum approach", "D": "No antibiotics started until sputum culture and sensitivity results become available"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Outpatient treatment with a respiratory fluoroquinolone or a beta-lactam plus macrolide given her comorbidities): her CURB-65 score is 1, from age 65 or older alone, since she has no confusion, a respiratory rate below 30, and a systolic blood pressure above 90, which supports outpatient management; however, because she has comorbidities including hypertension and chronic kidney disease, guidelines recommend a respiratory fluoroquinolone or a beta-lactam plus macrolide rather than amoxicillin monotherapy, which is reserved for previously healthy patients without comorbidities or recent antibiotic exposure. ICU admission and broad intravenous double coverage would be excessive for this low CURB-65 score, and withholding antibiotics pending culture results would inappropriately delay treatment of a likely bacterial pneumonia."}, {"id": 795, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 66-year-old man with alcohol use disorder is found down and unresponsive with vomitus around his mouth. He is intubated for airway protection. Two days later he develops fever and a foul-smelling productive cough, and chest radiograph reveals a cavitary lesion with an air-fluid level in the right lower lobe. Which additional antimicrobial coverage should be added to his regimen given this finding?", "options": {"A": "Antifungal coverage alone without any antibacterial therapy", "B": "Antiviral therapy targeting herpes simplex virus pneumonitis", "C": "No additional coverage, since standard aspiration pneumonia coverage already includes this", "D": "Anaerobic coverage such as clindamycin or a beta-lactam/beta-lactamase inhibitor combination"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Anaerobic coverage such as clindamycin or a beta-lactam/beta-lactamase inhibitor combination): a cavitary lung lesion with an air-fluid level and foul-smelling sputum following aspiration in a patient with impaired airway protection is characteristic of a lung abscess caused by oral anaerobic flora, which requires anaerobic antimicrobial coverage such as clindamycin or a beta-lactam combined with a beta-lactamase inhibitor in addition to standard therapy. Antifungal or antiviral therapy would not address this typical bacterial anaerobic process, and simply continuing standard aspiration coverage without specifically ensuring anaerobic activity, or starting empiric antituberculous therapy without supporting evidence such as chronic symptoms or risk factors, would not appropriately treat this abscess."}, {"id": 796, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 50-year-old man with well-controlled HIV on antiretroviral therapy and a CD4 count of 550 cells per microliter presents with typical symptoms and radiographic findings of community-acquired pneumonia. Which of the following statements about his empiric management is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "He should be treated according to standard community-acquired pneumonia guidelines given his preserved CD4 count", "B": "He should generally receive empiric Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia treatment given his underlying HIV status", "C": "He should receive empiric antituberculous therapy primarily because of his HIV status", "D": "He is unlikely to be a good candidate for outpatient treatment given his HIV status"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (He should be treated according to standard community-acquired pneumonia guidelines given his preserved CD4 count): with a CD4 count of 550 cells per microliter, well above the threshold of 200 cells per microliter associated with markedly increased risk for opportunistic infections such as Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia, this patient's presentation should be managed according to standard community-acquired pneumonia guidelines based on his clinical severity, similar to a patient without HIV. Empiric Pneumocystis or antituberculous therapy is not warranted based on HIV status alone without a suggestive CD4 count or clinical picture, and his HIV status alone does not preclude outpatient management if his CURB-65 score and clinical stability support it; CD4 count is directly relevant to risk stratification rather than irrelevant."}, {"id": 798, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 60-year-old man with active pancreatic cancer presents with acute dyspnea and pleuritic chest pain. His Wells score is calculated as high probability for pulmonary embolism given active malignancy, tachycardia, and no more likely alternative diagnosis. A D-dimer is drawn and returns mildly elevated. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Rely on the D-dimer result alone to exclude pulmonary embolism", "B": "Repeat the D-dimer test in one week before further action", "C": "Begin empiric antibiotics for presumed pneumonia without imaging", "D": "Proceed directly to CT pulmonary angiography regardless of the D-dimer result"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Proceed directly to CT pulmonary angiography regardless of the D-dimer result): in a patient with a high pretest probability for pulmonary embolism, a negative or even a mildly elevated D-dimer is not sufficiently reliable to exclude the diagnosis, because the test's negative predictive value is inadequate in this population, so he should proceed directly to CT pulmonary angiography regardless of the D-dimer result. Relying on the D-dimer alone in this high-probability setting could miss a true pulmonary embolism. Repeating the D-dimer in a week would dangerously delay diagnosis and treatment of a potentially life-threatening condition, and starting empiric antibiotics or discharging him without addressing the high suspicion for PE would be inappropriate given his risk profile."}, {"id": 804, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 62-year-old woman with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction presents with progressive dyspnea and is found to have a moderate right-sided pleural effusion. Thoracentesis reveals a pleural fluid protein to serum protein ratio of 0.4 and a pleural fluid LDH to serum LDH ratio of 0.5, with a pleural fluid LDH well below two-thirds the upper limit of normal serum LDH. Which of the following best classifies this effusion and the most appropriate initial management?", "options": {"A": "Exudative effusion; obtain immediate pleural biopsy for malignancy", "B": "Transudative effusion; treat the underlying heart failure", "C": "Exudative effusion; place a chest tube for suspected empyema", "D": "Transudative effusion; start empiric antituberculous therapy"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Transudative effusion; treat the underlying heart failure): none of Light's criteria are met since the pleural fluid to serum protein ratio is below 0.5, the pleural fluid to serum LDH ratio is below 0.6, and the pleural fluid LDH is below two-thirds the upper limit of normal serum LDH, classifying this as a transudative effusion, which typically reflects a systemic process such as heart failure and is managed by treating the underlying condition rather than pursuing invasive pleural procedures. Exudative-pattern interventions such as pleural biopsy or chest tube placement are not indicated for a transudate, and empiric antituberculous therapy would not be appropriate without supporting clinical or fluid findings suggestive of tuberculosis."}, {"id": 807, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 19-year-old tall, thin man with no significant past medical history presents with sudden onset of unilateral pleuritic chest pain and mild dyspnea while at rest. Chest radiograph shows a small apical pneumothorax occupying less than 20% of the hemithorax, with no mediastinal shift. He is otherwise comfortable and his oxygen saturation is 97% on room air. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?", "options": {"A": "Emergent needle decompression at the second intercostal space", "B": "Immediate chest tube thoracostomy performed here, based on his imaging and current presentation", "C": "Observation with supplemental oxygen and outpatient follow-up imaging", "D": "Bilateral chest tube placement as a precaution"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Observation with supplemental oxygen and outpatient follow-up imaging): a small, asymptomatic or minimally symptomatic primary spontaneous pneumothorax, typically from rupture of a subpleural bleb in a tall, thin young patient, without signs of tension physiology or significant respiratory compromise can be safely managed with observation, supplemental oxygen to help promote resorption of the pleural air, and outpatient follow-up imaging. Needle decompression and chest tube placement are reserved for larger, symptomatic, or tension pneumothoraces, not this small, stable presentation, and bilateral chest tube placement or emergency thoracotomy would be markedly excessive interventions for this scenario."}, {"id": 808, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 55-year-name man with severe emphysema is brought to the emergency department after a motor vehicle collision. He is in respiratory distress with absent breath sounds on the left, tracheal deviation to the right, distended neck veins, and a blood pressure of 80/50 mmHg. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate next step?", "options": {"A": "Obtain a chest radiograph first to confirm the diagnosis before any intervention", "B": "Immediate needle decompression at the second intercostal space, midclavicular line", "C": "Obtain a CT scan of the chest to characterize the pneumothorax before treatment", "D": "Administer intravenous fluids alone and reassess in thirty minutes"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Immediate needle decompression at the second intercostal space, midclavicular line): tension pneumothorax is a clinical diagnosis based on absent breath sounds, tracheal deviation away from the affected side, distended neck veins, and hypotension, and it requires immediate needle decompression at the second intercostal space, midclavicular line, without waiting for confirmatory imaging, since delaying treatment to obtain a chest radiograph, CT scan, or echocardiogram in a hemodynamically unstable patient with this clinical picture could be fatal. Intravenous fluids alone would not address the underlying mechanical problem of a tension pneumothorax obstructing venous return and cardiac output."}, {"id": 809, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 68-year-old man with severe COPD develops sudden worsening dyspnea and pleuritic chest pain. Chest radiograph reveals a moderate-sized pneumothorax with underlying emphysematous changes. Compared to a similarly sized primary spontaneous pneumothorax in a healthy young person, which of the following best describes the clinical significance of this presentation?", "options": {"A": "A clinical entity similar in some respects to primary spontaneous pneumothorax, typically managed with observation alone in most cases", "B": "A condition that rarely requires chest tube placement, since his COPD makes the pneumothorax less clinically significant overall", "C": "Secondary spontaneous pneumothorax in the setting of underlying lung disease, generally requiring more aggressive intervention due to reduced pulmonary reserve", "D": "A finding that often raises concern for malignancy and generally warrants further evaluation to exclude it"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Secondary spontaneous pneumothorax in the setting of underlying lung disease, generally requiring more aggressive intervention due to reduced pulmonary reserve): a pneumothorax occurring in a patient with underlying lung disease such as COPD is classified as a secondary spontaneous pneumothorax, and because these patients have substantially reduced pulmonary reserve, even a moderate pneumothorax is more likely to cause significant respiratory compromise and generally warrants more aggressive intervention such as chest tube placement compared with an equivalent-sized primary spontaneous pneumothorax in an otherwise healthy young person. It is not clinically identical to primary spontaneous pneumothorax and should not be managed the same way in all cases, it is not appropriate to reflexively attribute it to malignancy without further workup, and given his known severe COPD it is directly related to his underlying disease rather than coincidental."}, {"id": 810, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 40-year-old man undergoes ultrasound-guided thoracentesis for a large exudative pleural effusion found to be secondary to metastatic adenocarcinoma. He has recurrent, symptomatic effusions requiring thoracentesis every two to three weeks despite systemic therapy for his cancer. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step to reduce the frequency of repeated procedures?", "options": {"A": "Discontinuing all further pleural interventions since recurrence is expected", "B": "Switching primarily to oral diuretic therapy to control the effusion", "C": "Performing a single additional thoracentesis with no further planning", "D": "Pleurodesis or placement of an indwelling pleural catheter"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Pleurodesis or placement of an indwelling pleural catheter): for recurrent, symptomatic malignant pleural effusions, definitive management options such as chemical or mechanical pleurodesis, or placement of a tunneled indwelling pleural catheter, are appropriate to reduce the need for repeated thoracentesis procedures and improve quality of life. Simply discontinuing further intervention would leave his symptomatic effusions unaddressed, diuretics are not an effective treatment for a malignant exudative effusion, performing only a single additional thoracentesis without a longer-term plan does not address the recurrent nature of his effusions, and there is no indication here suggesting tuberculosis as the underlying cause."}, {"id": 811, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 52-year-old man with a history of hypertension undergoes evaluation for chronic dyspnea. Pulmonary function testing is performed and the results are shown in the panel. Which of the following patterns is demonstrated, and which category of lung disease is most consistent with these findings?", "options": {"A": "Obstructive pattern, most consistent with asthma or COPD", "B": "Restrictive pattern, most consistent with an interstitial lung process", "C": "Normal pulmonary function testing with no abnormality present", "D": "A mixed obstructive and restrictive pattern that cannot be further characterized"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Restrictive pattern, most consistent with an interstitial lung process): the findings show a reduced FEV1 and reduced FVC with a preserved or increased FEV1/FVC ratio, along with a reduced diffusing capacity for carbon monoxide, which together define a restrictive ventilatory pattern most consistent with an interstitial lung process rather than an obstructive pattern, which would instead show a reduced FEV1/FVC ratio. This is not a normal study, it does not represent a mixed pattern given the preserved ratio, and while pulmonary vascular disease can also reduce diffusing capacity, the combined reduction in both FEV1 and FVC with a preserved ratio points specifically toward a restrictive parenchymal process.", "image": "question_images/q_811.png"}, {"id": 812, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 33-year-old woman with no significant past medical history is found to have a small pneumothorax on a chest radiograph obtained for an unrelated reason. She reports no chest pain or dyspnea whatsoever and denies any prior similar episodes. Physical examination and vital signs are entirely normal. Which of the following best guides the initial management approach for this specific patient?", "options": {"A": "Clinical stability and symptom severity, more than the specific radiographic size, primarily determine whether observation is appropriate", "B": "The pneumothorax must usually be treated with chest tube placement independent of symptoms", "C": "No further imaging or follow-up is ever needed once a pneumothorax is identified incidentally", "D": "Needle decompression should be performed immediately independent of her stable presentation"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Clinical stability and symptom severity, more than the specific radiographic size, primarily determine whether observation is appropriate): in managing a spontaneous pneumothorax, clinical stability, symptom severity, and the presence or absence of tension physiology are the primary drivers of management decisions, and a small, asymptomatic pneumothorax in a hemodynamically stable patient can typically be observed with follow-up imaging rather than immediate invasive intervention. Reflexively placing a chest tube or performing needle decompression in every case regardless of symptoms would expose a stable patient to unnecessary procedural risk, and completely omitting follow-up imaging to confirm resolution would not be appropriate monitoring; bilateral chest tubes have no role in a unilateral, asymptomatic finding."}, {"id": 813, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 66-year-old man with a 45 pack-year smoking history presents with hyponatremia, and further workup reveals inappropriately concentrated urine with normal thyroid and adrenal function, consistent with syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion. Chest CT reveals a centrally located mass. Which type of lung cancer is most strongly associated with this paraneoplastic presentation?", "options": {"A": "Small cell lung cancer", "B": "Adenocarcinoma of the lung", "C": "Squamous cell lung carcinoma", "D": "Large cell lung carcinoma"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Small cell lung cancer): small cell lung cancer is the type most classically associated with paraneoplastic syndromes including syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion, along with ectopic ACTH production and Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome, reflecting its neuroendocrine origin and central location. Adenocarcinoma is the most common subtype overall and in nonsmokers but is not classically linked to SIADH. Squamous cell carcinoma is more associated with hypercalcemia from PTHrP secretion and cavitary lesions, and large cell carcinoma and bronchoalveolar carcinoma are not the classic associations for this specific paraneoplastic syndrome."}, {"id": 814, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 70-year-old man with a 55 pack-year smoking history is diagnosed with squamous cell carcinoma of the lung after presenting with hemoptysis and a centrally located mass on chest CT. During staging workup he is noted to have constipation, generalized weakness, and confusion, and laboratory testing reveals an elevated serum calcium level with an appropriately low, suppressed parathyroid hormone level. Renal function is mildly reduced and he is started on intravenous fluids. Which of the following mechanisms best explains his hypercalcemia?", "options": {"A": "Direct bony metastatic destruction as the exclusive mechanism in all cases", "B": "Ectopic secretion of antidiuretic hormone by the tumor", "C": "Ectopic secretion of adrenocorticotropic hormone by the tumor", "D": "Secretion of parathyroid hormone-related protein (PTHrP) by the tumor"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Secretion of parathyroid hormone-related protein (PTHrP) by the tumor): squamous cell lung carcinoma is classically associated with hypercalcemia mediated by tumor secretion of parathyroid hormone-related protein, which mimics the action of parathyroid hormone at the bone and kidney while appropriately suppressing endogenous parathyroid hormone secretion, explaining his low PTH level despite hypercalcemia. Direct bony destruction can contribute to hypercalcemia in some malignancies but is not the classic mechanism specifically linked to squamous cell lung cancer. Antidiuretic hormone and adrenocorticotropic hormone secretion cause hyponatremia and Cushing syndrome respectively, not hypercalcemia, and unregulated vitamin D activation by granulomas is characteristic of sarcoidosis rather than squamous cell lung cancer."}, {"id": 815, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 62-year-old woman presents with proximal muscle weakness that appears to improve with repeated muscle contraction on examination, along with autonomic symptoms including dry mouth. Chest CT reveals a central lung mass, and biopsy confirms small cell lung cancer. Which of the following best distinguishes her paraneoplastic neuromuscular syndrome from myasthenia gravis?", "options": {"A": "Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome improves with repeated muscle use, whereas myasthenia gravis worsens with repeated use", "B": "Both conditions improve equally with repeated use and cannot be distinguished clinically", "C": "Myasthenia gravis is caused by antibodies against presynaptic calcium channels, identical to her condition", "D": "Lambert-Eaton syndrome is usually associated with ocular symptoms as its most prominent feature, unlike myasthenia gravis"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome improves with repeated muscle use, whereas myasthenia gravis worsens with repeated use): Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome, caused by autoantibodies against presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels that impair acetylcholine release, characteristically shows improvement in strength with repeated muscle contraction as calcium accumulates presynaptically, along with autonomic symptoms such as dry mouth, which contrasts with myasthenia gravis, an autoantibody-mediated postsynaptic acetylcholine receptor disorder that characteristically worsens with repeated use due to receptor fatigue. The two conditions have a distinguishable clinical pattern rather than being indistinguishable, myasthenia gravis is not caused by presynaptic calcium channel antibodies, and prominent ocular symptoms are a more classic early feature of myasthenia gravis rather than Lambert-Eaton syndrome."}, {"id": 816, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 58-year-old woman with a 20 pack-year smoking history, quit ten years ago, is found to have a 2 cm peripheral nodule on a low-dose CT lung cancer screening study. Biopsy confirms non-small cell lung cancer, and complete staging workup including PET-CT and brain MRI shows no evidence of lymph node involvement or distant metastasis. Pulmonary function testing shows adequate predicted postoperative lung function for a lobectomy, and she has no other major comorbidities. Which of the following is the most appropriate primary treatment approach?", "options": {"A": "Chemotherapy and radiation as primary treatment", "B": "Immunotherapy alone as first-line treatment", "C": "Observation alone with repeat imaging", "D": "Surgical resection"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Surgical resection): for early-stage non-small cell lung cancer in a patient with adequate pulmonary reserve and no evidence of metastatic disease, surgical resection is the preferred primary treatment approach, offering the best chance for cure, unlike small cell lung cancer, which is rarely resectable at diagnosis due to early spread and is managed primarily with chemotherapy and radiation. Immunotherapy alone as first-line therapy is more typically used in advanced or metastatic disease based on molecular markers, and observation alone or palliative care referral without disease-directed treatment would be inappropriate for a potentially curable early-stage cancer in an operative candidate."}, {"id": 818, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 48-year-old man presents with severe left shoulder and arm pain radiating down the medial aspect of his arm, along with ptosis, miosis, and anhidrosis on the left side of his face. Chest CT reveals an apical lung mass invading the brachial plexus and sympathetic chain. Which of the following terms best describes this clinical presentation?", "options": {"A": "Pancoast tumor with associated Horner syndrome", "B": "Superior vena cava syndrome from mediastinal compression", "C": "Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome from paraneoplastic autoimmunity", "D": "Syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion from ectopic hormone release"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Pancoast tumor with associated Horner syndrome): an apical lung tumor invading the brachial plexus, causing shoulder and arm pain, and the sympathetic chain, causing ptosis, miosis, and anhidrosis (Horner syndrome), is the classic description of a Pancoast tumor. Superior vena cava syndrome instead presents with facial and upper extremity swelling and distended neck and chest wall veins from venous obstruction, and Lambert-Eaton syndrome, SIADH, and Cushing syndrome are paraneoplastic endocrine or neuromuscular syndromes that do not explain this localized structural invasion pattern with Horner syndrome."}, {"id": 819, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 62-year-old lifelong nonsmoker with no significant risk factors is incidentally found to have a 4 mm pulmonary nodule with smooth margins on a chest CT obtained for an unrelated reason. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": {"A": "No further follow-up imaging is needed given the low risk of malignancy for this size and appearance", "B": "Immediate PET scan and tissue biopsy performed independent of the nodule's small size and low risk features", "C": "Serial CT imaging obtained every three months for the next five years to monitor for growth", "D": "Empiric treatment for presumed early lung cancer initiated without any tissue confirmation of malignancy"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (No further follow-up imaging is needed given the low risk of malignancy for this size and appearance): a pulmonary nodule under 6 mm in a low-risk patient, such as this nonsmoker with smooth margins and no other risk factors, generally requires no further follow-up imaging given the very low probability of malignancy at this size. Proceeding immediately to PET imaging and biopsy, or to empiric treatment or surgical resection without tissue confirmation, would represent significant overtreatment for a nodule with such a low pretest probability of cancer, and routine serial imaging every three months for five years is not the recommended follow-up interval or duration for a nodule this small and low risk."}, {"id": 820, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis, and which additional criterion, beyond the timing and bilateral opacities already described, is required to confirm it?", "options": {"A": "Cardiogenic pulmonary edema; an elevated brain natriuretic peptide level is generally required to support this diagnosis", "B": "Community-acquired pneumonia; a single, well-defined lobar infiltrate on imaging is required to support this diagnosis", "C": "Acute respiratory distress syndrome; respiratory failure must not be fully explained by cardiac failure or fluid overload", "D": "Pulmonary embolism; a positive D-dimer result is required as part of confirming this diagnosis"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Acute respiratory distress syndrome; respiratory failure must not be fully explained by cardiac failure or fluid overload): the Berlin definition of ARDS requires onset within one week of a known clinical insult (here, sepsis), bilateral opacities not fully explained by effusion, lobar collapse, or nodules, and respiratory failure not fully explained by cardiac failure or fluid overload, which his normal echocardiogram and left ventricular function help support. Cardiogenic pulmonary edema would be expected to show evidence of volume overload or reduced cardiac function rather than the normal echocardiogram described, and elevated BNP is not the confirmatory criterion for ARDS. Community-acquired pneumonia typically presents with a single or focal infiltrate rather than the bilateral pattern described, pulmonary embolism is not excluded by these findings but a positive D-dimer is not part of the Berlin ARDS criteria, and this acute presentation over 18 hours does not fit chronic interstitial lung disease.", "case_id": "case_ards_1", "case_text": "A 45-year-old man is admitted to the intensive care unit with septic shock from a urinary source. Over the next 18 hours he develops worsening hypoxemia requiring intubation. Chest radiograph shows new bilateral pulmonary opacities, and echocardiography shows normal left ventricular function with no evidence of volume overload.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 821, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "The patient described above is now mechanically ventilated. His arterial blood gas on 100% inspired oxygen is shown in the panel. Based on his PaO2 to FiO2 ratio, which severity category of ARDS does he meet, and which specific ventilator strategy is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Mild ARDS (P/F ratio 200 to 300); a somewhat higher tidal volume strategy to help maximize oxygenation", "B": "Moderate ARDS (P/F ratio 100 to 200); comparatively unrestricted tidal volumes with no specific target set", "C": "Severe ARDS (P/F ratio below 100); low tidal volume ventilation at approximately 6 mL per kilogram of predicted body weight", "D": "Severe ARDS; comparatively large tidal volumes are generally preferred in this setting to reduce plateau pressure"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Severe ARDS (P/F ratio below 100); low tidal volume ventilation at approximately 6 mL per kilogram of predicted body weight): his PaO2 of 72 mmHg on an FiO2 of 1.0 yields a P/F ratio of 72, placing him in the severe ARDS category (P/F ratio below 100), and the cornerstone of ventilator management in ARDS across severities is a lung-protective strategy using low tidal volumes of approximately 6 mL per kilogram of predicted body weight, which minimizes ventilator-induced lung injury from overdistension of already compromised alveoli. High or unrestricted tidal volumes would increase the risk of further lung injury rather than reduce it, and large tidal volumes are specifically avoided rather than preferred in ARDS management; this blood gas, combined with his clinical scenario, is entirely consistent with severe ARDS rather than an alternative diagnosis.", "image": "question_images/q_821.png", "case_id": "case_ards_1", "case_text": "A 45-year-old man is admitted to the intensive care unit with septic shock from a urinary source. Over the next 18 hours he develops worsening hypoxemia requiring intubation. Chest radiograph shows new bilateral pulmonary opacities, and echocardiography shows normal left ventricular function with no evidence of volume overload.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 822, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "Despite low tidal volume ventilation and optimized PEEP, the same patient continues to have severe hypoxemia with a P/F ratio persistently below 100. Which of the following additional interventions has been shown to reduce mortality in patients with moderate to severe ARDS in this setting?", "options": {"A": "High tidal volume ventilation", "B": "Immediate discontinuation of all sedation", "C": "Aggressive intravenous fluid administration", "D": "Prone positioning"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Prone positioning): prone positioning has been shown to improve oxygenation and reduce mortality in patients with moderate to severe ARDS by improving ventilation-perfusion matching and recruiting previously dependent, poorly aerated lung regions. High tidal volume ventilation would worsen rather than help ventilator-induced lung injury in ARDS, discontinuing sedation for spontaneous breathing trials is not the appropriate next step in a patient with this degree of hypoxemia, aggressive fluid administration would worsen pulmonary edema in ARDS since conservative fluid management is instead preferred once hemodynamically stable, and targeting supranormal oxygen levels (permissive hyperoxia) is not an evidence-based strategy and can itself be harmful.", "case_id": "case_ards_1", "case_text": "A 45-year-old man is admitted to the intensive care unit with septic shock from a urinary source. Over the next 18 hours he develops worsening hypoxemia requiring intubation. Chest radiograph shows new bilateral pulmonary opacities, and echocardiography shows normal left ventricular function with no evidence of volume overload.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 823, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 68-year-old man with a 30 pack-year smoking history presents with progressive exertional dyspnea and dry cough over eighteen months. Examination reveals fine, bilateral, end-inspiratory crackles described as Velcro-like, along with digital clubbing. High resolution CT of the chest shows peripheral, basal-predominant reticular changes with honeycombing. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis", "B": "Sarcoidosis with hilar involvement", "C": "Hypersensitivity pneumonitis from antigen exposure", "D": "Silicosis from occupational dust exposure"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis): idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis is the most common interstitial lung disease, occurring predominantly in older men with a smoking history, and this presentation of progressive dyspnea, Velcro-like crackles, clubbing, and a usual interstitial pneumonia pattern with peripheral, basal-predominant reticular changes and honeycombing on high resolution CT is the classic description. Sarcoidosis more typically shows bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy rather than this fibrotic pattern, hypersensitivity pneumonitis is linked to a specific organic antigen exposure, silicosis shows upper lobe predominant nodules with eggshell calcification, and bronchiolitis obliterans organizing pneumonia typically presents with a more subacute course and patchy consolidative changes rather than this classic fibrotic pattern."}, {"id": 824, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 62-year-old man with a several-year history of progressive exertional dyspnea and dry cough is diagnosed with idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis after a multidisciplinary review of his high resolution chest CT confirms a usual interstitial pneumonia pattern with basal predominant honeycombing. His diffusing capacity has declined by 12 percent over the past year despite no clear infectious or environmental trigger. He and his family ask whether any medication can reverse the scarring already present in his lungs or whether treatment is otherwise futile. Which of the following statements about his management is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Antifibrotics can slow progression, but transplant is the only survival-extending option", "B": "Systemic corticosteroids alone reliably reverse the fibrosis and restore normal lung function", "C": "No treatment options exist, so only palliative symptom management should be offered", "D": "A prolonged course of antibiotics alone is considered curative for this condition"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Antifibrotics can slow progression, but transplant is the only survival-extending option): antifibrotic medications such as pirfenidone and nintedanib have been shown to slow the rate of decline in lung function in idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis, but they do not reverse existing fibrosis, and lung transplantation remains the only intervention demonstrated to substantially extend survival in patients with advanced disease. Systemic corticosteroids have not been shown to reliably reverse fibrosis in this condition and are not standard therapy, treatment options beyond palliative care do exist as described, antibiotics are not curative for this noninfectious fibrotic process, and bronchodilators alone have not been shown to halt the progression of pulmonary fibrosis."}, {"id": 825, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 45-year-old man who has worked for many years as a sandblaster presents with progressive dyspnea. Chest radiograph reveals upper lobe predominant nodular opacities with calcification of the rims of several hilar lymph nodes, described radiographically as eggshell calcification. Which of the following occupational lung diseases is most consistent with this presentation, and which additional infectious complication is he at increased risk for?", "options": {"A": "Asbestosis, with increased risk of mesothelioma", "B": "Coal worker's pneumoconiosis, with increased risk of invasive aspergillosis", "C": "Silicosis, with increased susceptibility to tuberculosis", "D": "Hypersensitivity pneumonitis, with increased risk of bacterial pneumonia"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Silicosis, with increased susceptibility to tuberculosis): silicosis, caused by inhalation of crystalline silica dust in occupations such as sandblasting, mining, and quarry work, characteristically produces upper lobe predominant nodular opacities with eggshell calcification of hilar lymph nodes, and it is well established to increase susceptibility to tuberculosis by impairing macrophage function. Asbestosis instead produces lower lobe predominant fibrosis with pleural plaques and increases risk of mesothelioma and bronchogenic carcinoma rather than being defined by this eggshell calcification pattern, coal worker's pneumoconiosis is not specifically linked to increased fungal infection risk, hypersensitivity pneumonitis relates to organic antigen exposure rather than mineral dust, and byssinosis is associated with cotton dust exposure rather than this radiographic pattern."}, {"id": 826, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 60-year-old man with a 25-year history of working in shipyard insulation installation presents with progressive dyspnea. Chest CT shows bilateral lower lobe predominant fibrosis along with bilateral pleural plaques. He continues to smoke one pack of cigarettes daily. Which of the following statements about his risk profile is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "His combined asbestos exposure and smoking history synergistically and dramatically increase his risk of bronchogenic carcinoma", "B": "His asbestos exposure alone, without smoking, would confer the same elevated cancer risk as combined exposure", "C": "Smoking has no additional effect on cancer risk in patients with asbestos exposure", "D": "His pleural plaques indicate an active malignant process requiring immediate biopsy"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (His combined asbestos exposure and smoking history synergistically and dramatically increase his risk of bronchogenic carcinoma): asbestos exposure and cigarette smoking act synergistically to dramatically increase the risk of bronchogenic carcinoma, meaning the combined risk is substantially greater than the additive risk from either exposure alone, which is an important reason to counsel him strongly on smoking cessation. Asbestos exposure alone without smoking still increases cancer risk but not to the same degree as combined exposure. Pleural plaques themselves are a marker of asbestos exposure and are typically benign findings rather than indicating active malignancy requiring immediate biopsy, and mesothelioma risk in a patient with a clear asbestos exposure history is directly related to that exposure rather than independent of it."}, {"id": 827, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 50-year-old dairy farmer presents with recurrent episodes of dyspnea, cough, and low-grade fever occurring several hours after working in the hay barn, with symptoms improving over the weekend when he is away from the farm. Chest CT shows patchy ground-glass opacities. Which of the following is the most appropriate management approach?", "options": {"A": "Long-term antituberculous therapy started here largely independent of his specific exposure history", "B": "Immediate lung transplantation considered here as a highly effective intervention for his condition", "C": "Antifibrotic therapy with pirfenidone started here as an initial first-line treatment approach", "D": "Avoidance of the causative antigen exposure, such as moldy hay"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Avoidance of the causative antigen exposure, such as moldy hay): this presentation, recurrent respiratory symptoms temporally related to hay exposure with improvement away from the farm, is classic for hypersensitivity pneumonitis, known in this context as farmer's lung, caused by an immune-mediated reaction to inhaled organic antigens such as those found in moldy hay, and the cornerstone of management is identification and avoidance of the causative antigen exposure. Antituberculous therapy, lung transplantation, and antifibrotic therapy are not appropriate initial treatments for this exposure-related hypersensitivity process, and while secondary bacterial infection can occasionally complicate respiratory illness, the primary driver of his recurrent symptoms described here is antigen exposure rather than bacterial pneumonia."}, {"id": 828, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 34-year-old woman presents with bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy found incidentally on chest radiograph, along with tender, erythematous nodules on her shins and new blurred vision with eye pain. Serum calcium is elevated, and serum angiotensin converting enzyme level is elevated. Lymph node biopsy shows noncaseating granulomas, and infectious workup including cultures and staining for acid-fast bacilli is negative. Which of the following diagnoses best explains this constellation of findings, including the mechanism of her hypercalcemia?", "options": {"A": "Tuberculosis, with hypercalcemia caused by direct bony destruction from disseminated mycobacterial invasion of the skeleton", "B": "Sarcoidosis, with hypercalcemia caused by unregulated conversion of vitamin D by macrophages within granulomas", "C": "Silicosis, with hypercalcemia caused by tumor-independent parathyroid hormone-related protein secretion from affected tissue", "D": "Hypersensitivity pneumonitis, with hypercalcemia caused by ectopic parathyroid hormone secretion from inflamed lung tissue"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Sarcoidosis, with hypercalcemia caused by unregulated conversion of vitamin D by macrophages within granulomas): this presentation of bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, erythema nodosum, uveitis (blurred vision and eye pain), hypercalcemia, an elevated angiotensin converting enzyme level, and noncaseating granulomas on biopsy with negative infectious workup is classic for sarcoidosis, a multisystem granulomatous disease in which macrophages within granulomas unregulatedly convert vitamin D to its active form, driving hypercalcemia. Tuberculosis would be expected to show caseating granulomas and a positive infectious workup rather than the negative studies described here, silicosis and hypersensitivity pneumonitis do not typically present with this extrapulmonary constellation, and idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis is not a granulomatous disease and does not explain the hypercalcemia or extrapulmonary findings.", "image": "question_images/q_828.png"}, {"id": 829, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 29-year-old woman is found to have bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy on a chest radiograph obtained during an evaluation for a minor unrelated complaint. She has no respiratory symptoms, no extrapulmonary findings, normal calcium, and normal pulmonary function testing. Biopsy confirms sarcoidosis with noncaseating granulomas after infectious causes are excluded. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management approach?", "options": {"A": "Observation with periodic monitoring, given her asymptomatic stage I presentation", "B": "Immediate high-dose systemic corticosteroids independent of symptom status", "C": "Antituberculous four-drug therapy as the definitive treatment", "D": "Immediate lung transplantation given the diagnosis of sarcoidosis"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Observation with periodic monitoring, given her asymptomatic stage I presentation): asymptomatic or minimally symptomatic sarcoidosis with stage I disease, characterized by isolated bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy without significant pulmonary infiltrates, extrapulmonary involvement, or functional impairment, is often managed with observation and periodic monitoring rather than immediate pharmacologic treatment, since many such cases remit spontaneously. Immediate systemic corticosteroids are reserved for more symptomatic or progressive disease. Antituberculous therapy is not indicated once infectious causes including tuberculosis have been excluded, and lung transplantation and antifibrotic therapy are not appropriate for this early, asymptomatic presentation of sarcoidosis."}, {"id": 834, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 48-year-old man is being treated for active pulmonary tuberculosis with a standard four-drug regimen. He develops burning paresthesias in his feet several weeks into therapy. Which of the following medications in his regimen is most likely responsible, and which co-administered agent could have prevented this complication?", "options": {"A": "Rifampin, which can be prevented with concurrent vitamin B12 supplementation", "B": "Isoniazid, which can be prevented with concurrent pyridoxine (vitamin B6) supplementation", "C": "Ethambutol, which can be prevented with concurrent thiamine supplementation", "D": "Pyrazinamide, which can be prevented with concurrent folate supplementation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Isoniazid, which can be prevented with concurrent pyridoxine (vitamin B6) supplementation): isoniazid can cause peripheral neuropathy by interfering with pyridoxine (vitamin B6) metabolism, and this complication can be prevented or treated with concurrent pyridoxine supplementation, which is why it is often co-administered with isoniazid therapy. Rifampin is more associated with orange discoloration of body fluids and cytochrome P450 induction rather than peripheral neuropathy prevented by B12, ethambutol is associated with optic neuritis and red-green color blindness rather than a neuropathy prevented by thiamine, and pyrazinamide is more associated with hepatotoxicity and hyperuricemia rather than a neuropathy addressed by folate; vitamin C supplementation does not address isoniazid-induced neuropathy."}, {"id": 836, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 60-year-old man with severe COPD is evaluated in clinic while clinically stable. His oxygen saturation is 90% on room air, and arterial blood gas testing performed on this visit and confirmed on a repeat visit two weeks later is shown in the panel, without evidence of cor pulmonale or polycythemia. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step regarding long-term oxygen therapy?", "options": {"A": "Prescribe long-term home oxygen immediately today, regardless of his current measured resting values", "B": "Defer any oxygen assessment for at least one year given his stable status", "C": "Begin oxygen therapy only during exercise and rarely at rest under any circumstance", "D": "Continue monitoring closely; oxygen therapy is not yet indicated since his values are still above threshold"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Continue monitoring closely; oxygen therapy is not yet indicated since his values are still above threshold): long-term home oxygen is indicated for a resting PaO2 at or below 55 mmHg or oxygen saturation at or below 88%, or a PaO2 at or below 59 mmHg with evidence of cor pulmonale or polycythemia, and his stable, repeated values here do not yet meet these thresholds, so ongoing monitoring for future qualification is appropriate rather than either immediate prescription or complete discontinuation of future reassessment. Prescribing oxygen now would not be supported by his current, above-threshold values, waiting a full year without reassessment could delay identification of a future qualifying change, restricting to exercise-only oxygen is a distinct consideration from this resting oxygen assessment, and permanently discontinuing future assessments is not appropriate given that COPD is a progressive disease.", "image": "question_images/q_836.png"}, {"id": 837, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 52-year-old man with obesity and a large neck circumference is evaluated for excessive daytime somnolence and loud snoring witnessed by his spouse, who also reports observing pauses in his breathing during sleep. Polysomnography reveals 42 apneic and hypopneic events per hour of sleep. Which of the following best classifies his condition and the most appropriate first-line treatment?", "options": {"A": "Severe obstructive sleep apnea; continuous positive airway pressure is first-line treatment", "B": "Mild obstructive sleep apnea; no treatment is needed at this severity", "C": "Moderate obstructive sleep apnea; oral appliance therapy is often considered as an alternative to CPAP at this severity", "D": "Severe obstructive sleep apnea; immediate surgical intervention is generally preferred over other treatment options"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Severe obstructive sleep apnea; continuous positive airway pressure is first-line treatment): an apnea-hypopnea index of 42 events per hour, well above the threshold of 30, classifies this as severe obstructive sleep apnea, and continuous positive airway pressure, which splints the upper airway open during sleep, is the first-line treatment for OSA across severities including severe disease. Mild OSA (AHI 5 to 15) would not apply here, and even in moderate disease CPAP rather than oral appliance therapy is generally preferred as first-line treatment. Immediate surgery is not the routine first-line approach and is reserved for specific anatomic obstruction or CPAP intolerance, and the clinical description of obesity, snoring, and witnessed apneas along with a clearly elevated AHI is consistent with obstructive rather than central sleep apnea, which would not be the primary consideration here."}, {"id": 838, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 70-year-old man with a 60 pack-year smoking history is found to have a 2.5 cm pulmonary nodule with spiculated margins in the right upper lobe on chest CT, along with mild growth compared to imaging performed fourteen months earlier. Which of the following best describes the appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "No further evaluation needed since growth occurring over more than a year is generally considered reassuring", "B": "Further evaluation with PET imaging and consideration of tissue biopsy given high-risk features", "C": "Reassurance and discharge from further pulmonary follow-up at this time", "D": "Repeat imaging in three more years given his age"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Further evaluation with PET imaging and consideration of tissue biopsy given high-risk features): a pulmonary nodule with spiculated margins, larger size, documented interval growth on serial imaging, and significant smoking history all represent high-risk features that warrant further evaluation with PET imaging and consideration of tissue biopsy to evaluate for malignancy, since findings such as irregular or spiculated margins and growth over time suggest malignancy rather than a benign process. Interval growth of a nodule is a concerning feature rather than a reassuring one, so simple reassurance, discharge from follow-up, or deferring further imaging for years would risk delaying diagnosis of a potentially malignant lesion, and there is no clinical information here supporting empiric antituberculous therapy as the appropriate next step."}, {"id": 839, "domain": "respiratory_system", "question": "A 65-year-old man with a history of heavy smoking undergoes chest CT for an unrelated indication, revealing a solitary pulmonary nodule with dense, central calcification in a pattern described as popcorn-like. Which of the following best characterizes the significance of this calcification pattern?", "options": {"A": "It suggests a benign process such as a hamartoma rather than malignancy", "B": "It is generally suggestive of malignancy and typically warrants biopsy along with other clinical features", "C": "It has little meaningful bearing on the likelihood of malignancy", "D": "It is a pattern seen mainly in active tuberculosis and only occasionally in benign tumors"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (It suggests a benign process such as a hamartoma rather than malignancy): a popcorn-like, dense, central pattern of calcification within a pulmonary nodule is a classic benign feature most often associated with a pulmonary hamartoma, and along with other benign patterns such as central or laminated calcification, this pattern argues against malignancy and generally supports a more conservative follow-up approach rather than immediate invasive intervention. This calcification pattern does not suggest malignancy requiring urgent biopsy, it is not irrelevant to malignancy risk assessment, it is not a pattern exclusive to tuberculosis, and it does not mandate immediate surgical resection without further imaging follow-up given its benign implications."}, {"id": 844, "domain": "multisystem_processes_and_disorders", "question": "Which of the following is the most likely source of this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Atelectasis from inadequate postoperative incentive spirometry", "B": "A catheter-associated urinary tract infection", "C": "Drug fever related to a postoperative antibiotic", "D": "An anastomotic leak with resulting intra-abdominal infection"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (An anastomotic leak with resulting intra-abdominal infection): Fever occurring on postoperative day 5 through 7 after a bowel resection, accompanied by hemodynamic instability, worsening abdominal pain, leukocytosis, and an elevated lactate, is highly concerning for an anastomotic leak with resulting peritonitis or intra-abdominal abscess. Atelectasis typically presents earlier (the first 24 to 48 hours) and would not explain this degree of hemodynamic compromise. A urinary source or drug fever would not typically produce worsening localized abdominal pain at the surgical site.", "case_id": "MULTI-C2", "case_text": "A 68-year-old woman underwent an open sigmoid colectomy for diverticular disease six days ago. Her postoperative course had been unremarkable until this morning, when she develops a temperature of 39.1 C, heart rate 118/min, blood pressure 92/58 mmHg, and respiratory rate 24/min. She reports increasing abdominal pain and the surgical incision appears mildly erythematous but without drainage. Laboratory studies show a white blood cell count of 19,000/mm3 and a lactate of 3.8 mmol/L.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 845, "domain": "multisystem_processes_and_disorders", "question": "Given the concern for an anastomotic leak in the patient described above, a repeat set of vital signs shows a temperature of 39.4 C, heart rate 126/min, blood pressure 86/52 mmHg, and respiratory rate 28/min. She appears mildly confused compared to her baseline mental status noted on morning rounds. Repeat point-of-care lactate is now 4.2 mmol/L, up from 3.8 mmol/L one hour earlier, and her urine output over the last two hours has been only 15 mL/hr. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate step before definitive imaging or surgical intervention?", "options": {"A": "Withhold antibiotics until a specific organism is identified by culture", "B": "Obtain a contrast enema to confirm the leak before any other intervention", "C": "Begin broad-spectrum empiric antibiotics and start intravenous fluid resuscitation", "D": "Start scheduled acetaminophen alone and reassess vital signs in six hours"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Begin broad-spectrum empiric antibiotics and start intravenous fluid resuscitation): This patient meets criteria for septic shock (suspected infection with hypotension and lactate above 2 mmol/L), so immediate management requires prompt broad-spectrum empiric antibiotics and aggressive fluid resuscitation, ideally within the first hour of recognition, followed by source identification and control. Waiting for a specific culture result before starting antibiotics would dangerously delay treatment in an unstable patient, and imaging can proceed in parallel with, not instead of, initial resuscitation.", "case_id": "MULTI-C2", "case_text": "A 68-year-old woman underwent an open sigmoid colectomy for diverticular disease six days ago. Her postoperative course had been unremarkable until this morning, when she develops a temperature of 39.1 C, heart rate 118/min, blood pressure 92/58 mmHg, and respiratory rate 24/min. She reports increasing abdominal pain and the surgical incision appears mildly erythematous but without drainage. Laboratory studies show a white blood cell count of 19,000/mm3 and a lactate of 3.8 mmol/L.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 846, "domain": "multisystem_processes_and_disorders", "question": "The patient in the prior vignette receives 30 mL/kg of balanced crystalloid and broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics targeting an intra-abdominal source. One hour later her blood pressure remains 78/50 mmHg despite the fluid bolus, her heart rate is 132/min, and central venous access has been established. She remains mildly confused, her extremities are warm with brisk capillary refill, and repeat lactate is 4.6 mmol/L. Her surgical team has been paged for likely operative source control once she is stabilized enough to tolerate transport. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Administer a second large fluid bolus and delay vasopressor therapy", "B": "Begin a dopamine infusion as the preferred first-line vasopressor", "C": "Begin a norepinephrine infusion to maintain adequate mean arterial pressure", "D": "Administer intravenous furosemide to reduce preload and improve perfusion"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Begin a norepinephrine infusion to maintain adequate mean arterial pressure): When hypotension persists despite adequate fluid resuscitation, the patient meets criteria for septic shock and requires vasopressor support to maintain a mean arterial pressure of at least 65 mmHg. Norepinephrine is the first-line vasopressor in septic shock because it provides balanced alpha and beta adrenergic support with a lower arrhythmia risk than dopamine, which is no longer preferred as first-line therapy. A diuretic would be inappropriate and dangerous in a hypotensive, likely volume-depleted patient.", "case_id": "MULTI-C2", "case_text": "A 68-year-old woman underwent an open sigmoid colectomy for diverticular disease six days ago. Her postoperative course had been unremarkable until this morning, when she develops a temperature of 39.1 C, heart rate 118/min, blood pressure 92/58 mmHg, and respiratory rate 24/min. She reports increasing abdominal pain and the surgical incision appears mildly erythematous but without drainage. Laboratory studies show a white blood cell count of 19,000/mm3 and a lactate of 3.8 mmol/L.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 850, "domain": "multisystem_processes_and_disorders", "question": "A 45-year-old farmworker is brought to the emergency department by coworkers after he was found collapsed in a field where organophosphate pesticides had been sprayed earlier that day. He is confused and unable to give a reliable history. On examination he has excessive salivation, lacrimation, diaphoresis, urinary incontinence, miosis, bradycardia at 48/min, diffuse fasciculations, and audible wheezing with increased bronchial secretions on auscultation. His coworkers report he was not wearing protective equipment while applying the pesticide. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial pharmacologic treatment?", "options": {"A": "Naloxone to reverse suspected concomitant opioid toxicity", "B": "Atropine to counteract excess muscarinic stimulation", "C": "Physostigmine to reverse anticholinergic blockade", "D": "Flumazenil to reverse suspected benzodiazepine coingestion"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Atropine to counteract excess muscarinic stimulation): This presentation, with excessive secretions, miosis, bradycardia, and fasciculations, is classic for organophosphate poisoning producing a cholinergic toxidrome from acetylcholinesterase inhibition. Atropine is the initial treatment to counteract the muscarinic effects (secretions, bradycardia), and pralidoxime is added to reactivate acetylcholinesterase before it becomes permanently bound. Naloxone, physostigmine, and flumazenil address entirely different toxidromes and would not address this cholinergic excess."}, {"id": 851, "domain": "multisystem_processes_and_disorders", "question": "A 32-year-old woman with a known diagnosis of systemic lupus erythematosus, previously well controlled on hydroxychloroquine, presents with two weeks of new bilateral lower extremity edema, new hypertension to 148/94 mmHg, and dark, tea-colored urine. She has also noticed increased fatigue and mild joint pain over the same period, but denies fever or new rash. Urinalysis reveals red blood cell casts and 3+ proteinuria, and a spot urine protein-to-creatinine ratio is markedly elevated. Which of the following serologic findings would most specifically support active lupus nephritis at this time?", "options": {"A": "A positive antinuclear antibody titer of only 1:80 on screening", "B": "An isolated elevation of the erythrocyte sedimentation rate alone", "C": "A positive rheumatoid factor with no other supporting findings", "D": "A rising anti-double-stranded DNA antibody titer with falling complement levels"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A rising anti-double-stranded DNA antibody titer with falling complement levels): Anti-double-stranded DNA antibody levels tend to rise and complement levels (C3 and C4) tend to fall during active lupus flares, particularly lupus nephritis, because complement is being consumed by immune complex deposition in the glomeruli. A low-titer antinuclear antibody is highly sensitive but not specific for SLE and does not track disease activity, an elevated erythrocyte sedimentation rate is nonspecific, and rheumatoid factor is more associated with rheumatoid arthritis and is not specific for lupus activity."}, {"id": 853, "domain": "multisystem_processes_and_disorders", "question": "A 58-year-old man with well-controlled hypertension is scheduled for an elective total knee arthroplasty for severe osteoarthritis. On preoperative evaluation he reports he can climb two flights of stairs and walk briskly on level ground without chest pain, shortness of breath, palpitations, or dizziness, and he regularly plays recreational tennis on weekends. He has no history of heart failure, myocardial infarction, cerebrovascular disease, insulin-dependent diabetes, or renal insufficiency, and his preoperative creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL. Which of the following best characterizes his need for further preoperative cardiac testing?", "options": {"A": "He would likely benefit from stress testing as part of his further evaluation.", "B": "He should undergo coronary angiography given his age", "C": "His functional capacity and low-risk profile make further cardiac testing unnecessary", "D": "He would likely benefit from a resting echocardiogram as part of his routine preoperative workup."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (His functional capacity and low-risk profile make further cardiac testing unnecessary): A patient who can achieve at least 4 metabolic equivalents of activity, such as climbing two flights of stairs without symptoms, and who has none of the major risk factors captured by the Revised Cardiac Risk Index, generally does not require further preoperative cardiac testing before moderate-risk surgery. Reflexively ordering stress testing, angiography, or echocardiography in a patient with good functional capacity and a low-risk profile adds cost and delay without meaningful benefit."}, {"id": 854, "domain": "multisystem_processes_and_disorders", "question": "A 63-year-old man is postoperative day 2 following an uncomplicated open cholecystectomy performed for symptomatic cholelithiasis. Overnight he develops a temperature of 38.4 C without chills or rigors. He reports mild incisional discomfort well controlled with scheduled acetaminophen, and he is ambulating well in the hallway with physical therapy. On examination he has clear breath sounds bilaterally, a clean, dry incision without erythema or drainage, no calf tenderness, and no urinary symptoms. Which of the following is the most likely explanation for his fever?", "options": {"A": "An early surgical site infection requiring immediate wound exploration", "B": "The normal postoperative inflammatory response and possible atelectasis", "C": "A deep venous thrombosis requiring anticoagulation", "D": "An anastomotic leak requiring emergent reoperation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The normal postoperative inflammatory response and possible atelectasis): Fever in the first 24 to 48 hours after surgery is most commonly attributed to the normal systemic inflammatory response to surgery itself, with atelectasis also traditionally implicated, though its direct causal role has been increasingly questioned. Surgical site infections typically present later (around day 5 or beyond), deep venous thrombosis is more typical after day 7, and this patient has no signs suggesting an anastomotic complication, which would also be far less expected after a cholecystectomy than after a bowel resection."}, {"id": 855, "domain": "multisystem_processes_and_disorders", "question": "A 40-year-old man with a history of depression presents to the emergency department after intentionally ingesting an unknown quantity of aspirin tablets approximately three hours earlier, following an argument with a family member. He reports tinnitus, nausea, and mild dizziness, and he appears anxious but is alert and oriented. His temperature is 37.8 C and respiratory rate is 28/min with deep breathing. Arterial blood gas obtained on room air shows a pH of 7.48, PaCO2 of 24 mmHg, and bicarbonate of 18 mEq/L. Which of the following best describes his acid-base status?", "options": {"A": "A pure respiratory alkalosis resulting from direct medullary respiratory center stimulation alone", "B": "A mixed picture with early respiratory alkalosis and evolving metabolic acidosis", "C": "A pure metabolic acidosis with an appropriate compensatory respiratory response here", "D": "A pure metabolic alkalosis resulting from vomiting-induced volume depletion in this patient"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A mixed picture with early respiratory alkalosis and evolving metabolic acidosis): Early salicylate toxicity classically produces a primary respiratory alkalosis from direct stimulation of the medullary respiratory center, evidenced by the elevated pH and low PaCO2. However, the bicarbonate is already low, more than expected compensation for a pure acute respiratory alkalosis would produce, indicating a concurrent evolving anion gap metabolic acidosis from salicylate uncoupling oxidative phosphorylation. This classic mixed picture is a high-yield salicylate toxicity finding."}, {"id": 856, "domain": "multisystem_processes_and_disorders", "question": "A 29-year-old woman with a known history of chronic back pain and prescription opioid use is found unresponsive on the floor of her apartment by a neighbor, who called emergency medical services. On arrival, paramedics note pinpoint pupils, a respiratory rate of 6/min with shallow breathing, an oxygen saturation of 82% on room air, and cool, clammy skin. Several empty pill bottles and drug paraphernalia are found nearby, suggesting an opioid overdose. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate treatment?", "options": {"A": "Intravenous or intranasal naloxone with attention to the risk of re-sedation", "B": "Flumazenil to reverse suspected sedative-hypnotic toxicity from a benzodiazepine overdose", "C": "Physostigmine to reverse suspected anticholinergic toxicity from an unknown ingestion", "D": "Fomepizole to block toxic alcohol metabolism in a suspected ingestion"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Intravenous or intranasal naloxone with attention to the risk of re-sedation): Miosis, respiratory depression, and altered mental status form the classic opioid toxidrome, and naloxone is the specific antidote. Because naloxone's duration of action is typically shorter than that of many opioids, repeat dosing or a continuous infusion may be necessary to prevent recurrent respiratory depression as the naloxone wears off. Flumazenil, physostigmine, and fomepizole are antidotes for unrelated toxidromes and would not reverse opioid-induced respiratory depression."}, {"id": 857, "domain": "multisystem_processes_and_disorders", "question": "A 22-year-old man is brought to the emergency department by friends after using cocaine at a party several hours earlier. He is agitated, combative, and repeatedly attempting to remove his intravenous line. He is diaphoretic, tachycardic at 130/min, hypertensive at 178/102 mmHg, and has dilated, reactive pupils. His temperature is 38.9 C and his skin is warm and wet to the touch. He reports mild chest tightness but denies shortness of breath. Which of the following medications should be avoided in his initial management?", "options": {"A": "Propranolol for heart rate and blood pressure control", "B": "Lorazepam for agitation and blood pressure control", "C": "Active cooling measures for possible hyperthermia", "D": "Intravenous fluids for volume repletion"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Propranolol for heart rate and blood pressure control): Beta blockers, including nonselective agents like propranolol, are generally avoided in cocaine toxicity because blocking beta-2 mediated vasodilation while leaving alpha-mediated vasoconstriction unopposed can worsen hypertension and coronary vasospasm. Benzodiazepines are first-line for the agitation, hypertension, and tachycardia of sympathomimetic toxicity, and supportive measures like cooling and fluids are appropriate adjuncts."}, {"id": 858, "domain": "multisystem_processes_and_disorders", "question": "A 55-year-old woman with stage 4 chronic kidney disease and known hyperkalemia risk misses two consecutive hemodialysis sessions due to transportation difficulties. She presents to the emergency department with generalized weakness and a sensation of skipped heartbeats. A stat basic metabolic panel shows a serum potassium of 7.2 mEq/L, and a bedside electrocardiogram shows peaked T waves, loss of the P wave, and a widened QRS complex compared to a prior electrocardiogram on file. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate step?", "options": {"A": "Administer a potassium-binding resin and reassess in six hours", "B": "Begin hemodialysis as the sole initial intervention", "C": "Administer intravenous insulin with glucose as the first action taken", "D": "Administer intravenous calcium gluconate to stabilize the cardiac membrane"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Administer intravenous calcium gluconate to stabilize the cardiac membrane): Hyperkalemia with electrocardiographic changes (peaked T waves progressing to QRS widening) represents an immediate cardiac emergency, and intravenous calcium should be given first to stabilize the cardiac membrane and reduce the risk of a fatal arrhythmia, even though it does not lower the serum potassium level itself. Measures to shift potassium intracellularly (insulin with glucose, beta agonists) and to remove potassium from the body (resins, diuretics, dialysis) follow but should not precede membrane stabilization when ECG changes are present."}, {"id": 859, "domain": "multisystem_processes_and_disorders", "question": "A 34-year-old man with a two-year history of adult-onset asthma requiring escalating inhaler therapy and chronic sinusitis presents with new numbness and weakness in his right foot, causing him to trip while walking. On examination he has a right foot drop with sensory loss over the dorsum of the foot. Laboratory studies reveal a peripheral eosinophil count of 2,400 cells/microliter, and nerve conduction studies confirm a mononeuritis multiplex pattern. Perinuclear antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies are positive. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Granulomatosis with polyangiitis vasculitis", "B": "Classic polyarteritis nodosa vasculitis", "C": "Isolated microscopic polyangiitis vasculitis", "D": "Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis): The combination of asthma, peripheral eosinophilia, sinus disease, and a vasculitic neuropathy (mononeuritis multiplex) with a positive perinuclear antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody strongly suggests eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis. Granulomatosis with polyangiitis typically involves the upper and lower respiratory tract with cytoplasmic antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies rather than prominent eosinophilia or asthma, microscopic polyangiitis lacks the granulomatous and asthmatic features, and polyarteritis nodosa is a medium-vessel vasculitis without ANCA association or asthma."}, {"id": 860, "domain": "multisystem_processes_and_disorders", "question": "A 60-year-old retired construction worker is being evaluated for fever of unknown origin after five weeks of documented temperatures above 38.3 C, night sweats, and an unintentional 6 kg weight loss. He denies recent travel, sick contacts, or new medications. An initial inpatient workup including three sets of blood cultures, a chest radiograph, urinalysis, and routine laboratory studies has been unrevealing, and empiric antibiotics have not improved his symptoms. Which of the following categories of disease should remain highest on the differential diagnosis given the epidemiology of this presentation?", "options": {"A": "An isolated viral upper respiratory tract infection", "B": "Infection, malignancy, and autoimmune or inflammatory disease", "C": "A simple uncomplicated urinary tract infection", "D": "Simple dehydration from poor oral fluid intake"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Infection, malignancy, and autoimmune or inflammatory disease): Fever of unknown origin, defined as a documented temperature above 38.3 C lasting more than three weeks without a diagnosis despite reasonable evaluation, has a differential traditionally organized into infections (such as tuberculosis, endocarditis, or occult abscess), malignancy (such as lymphoma), autoimmune or inflammatory conditions (such as adult-onset Still disease or vasculitis), and miscellaneous causes. Self-limited viral illnesses, uncomplicated urinary tract infections, and dehydration would not typically produce a persistent, unexplained fever of this duration."}, {"id": 861, "domain": "multisystem_processes_and_disorders", "question": "A 48-year-old man with a known bicuspid aortic valve, who underwent a dental extraction six weeks ago without antibiotic prophylaxis, presents with three weeks of progressive fatigue, low-grade fever, and unintentional weight loss. On cardiac auscultation he has a new diastolic murmur at the right upper sternal border. On examination of his extremities he has painless erythematous macules on his palms and soles, along with tender, violaceous nodules on his fingertip and toe pads. Funduscopic examination reveals a retinal hemorrhage with a pale center. Blood cultures grow Streptococcus viridans. Which of the following best describes the pathophysiology of the tender fingertip lesions?", "options": {"A": "Immune complex deposition producing painful nodules", "B": "Septic microemboli producing painless macular lesions", "C": "Direct bacterial invasion of the digital arteries", "D": "A paraneoplastic phenomenon that would be considered atypical in this clinical context"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Immune complex deposition producing painful nodules): The tender nodules on the finger and toe pads described are Osler nodes, which result from immune complex deposition rather than direct septic embolization. This distinguishes them from Janeway lesions, which are painless erythematous macules on the palms and soles caused by septic microemboli. Both findings occur in infective endocarditis, a classic multisystem disease with fever plus embolic and immunologic phenomena affecting distant organs."}, {"id": 862, "domain": "multisystem_processes_and_disorders", "question": "A 26-year-old previously healthy man is brought to the trauma bay by ambulance after his sedan was struck broadside by another vehicle at a moderate speed. He was restrained by a seatbelt and there was no airbag deployment. On arrival he is talking clearly in full sentences and answering questions appropriately, though he reports severe, diffuse abdominal pain and appears anxious. His blood pressure is 118/76 mmHg, heart rate is 98/min, and his abdomen is diffusely tender with voluntary guarding. According to the primary survey sequence, which of the following should be assessed and addressed first?", "options": {"A": "A focused abdominal examination looking for peritoneal signs first", "B": "Airway patency, which is already suggested by his clear speech", "C": "A complete secondary survey with a head-to-toe examination", "D": "Definitive imaging of the abdomen with computed tomography"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Airway patency, which is already suggested by his clear speech): The ATLS primary survey always begins with airway assessment, and a patient who is speaking clearly in full sentences has, by definition, a patent airway at that moment, though ongoing reassessment remains important. Even though his complaint is abdominal pain, the primary survey sequence (airway, breathing, circulation, disability, exposure) must be systematically confirmed before moving to a focused abdominal exam, secondary survey, or definitive imaging, which come later in the evaluation."}, {"id": 863, "domain": "multisystem_processes_and_disorders", "question": "A 50-year-old man is undergoing initial resuscitation in the trauma bay after sustaining a single gunshot wound to the left lower abdomen during an altercation. Prehospital estimated blood loss, based on his vital sign trend and visible external bleeding, is approximately 35 percent of his total blood volume. On arrival his blood pressure is 88/60 mmHg, heart rate is 128/min, respiratory rate is 26/min, and he is mildly confused but able to follow simple commands. Two large-bore intravenous lines have been placed. Which of the following classes of hemorrhagic shock best matches this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Class I hemorrhage", "B": "Class II hemorrhage", "C": "Class IV hemorrhage", "D": "Class III hemorrhage"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Class III hemorrhage): Class III hemorrhage, corresponding to roughly 30 to 40 percent blood volume loss, is characterized by overt hypotension, tachypnea, and early mental status changes, matching this presentation. Class I (up to 15 percent) shows minimal vital sign change, Class II (15 to 30 percent) shows tachycardia with a narrowing pulse pressure but typically preserved blood pressure, and Class IV (over 40 percent) shows more profound hypotension and significant mental status depression, representing an immediately life-threatening degree of hemorrhage."}, {"id": 864, "domain": "multisystem_processes_and_disorders", "question": "A 37-year-old woman with a history of depression is brought to the emergency department by her sister after being found confused and mumbling incoherently at home, with an open, mostly empty medication bottle nearby. She presents with dry, flushed skin, dilated pupils that react sluggishly to light, urinary retention requiring bladder catheterization, absent bowel sounds, agitation with picking motions at the air, and a temperature of 39.6 C. Her sister is unsure exactly which medication was ingested. Which of the following medications, if given, could worsen this patient's clinical course if she had also co-ingested a tricyclic antidepressant?", "options": {"A": "Intravenous fluids for supportive hydration", "B": "Active external cooling measures for hyperthermia", "C": "Physostigmine to reverse anticholinergic symptoms", "D": "Benzodiazepines for agitation control"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Physostigmine to reverse anticholinergic symptoms): This presentation describes the anticholinergic toxidrome. While physostigmine can reverse pure anticholinergic toxicity, it is generally avoided when tricyclic antidepressant co-ingestion is suspected or confirmed, because it can worsen cardiac conduction abnormalities and precipitate seizures or arrhythmias in that setting. Supportive fluids, cooling, and benzodiazepines for agitation remain appropriate regardless of a potential tricyclic co-ingestion."}, {"id": 865, "domain": "multisystem_processes_and_disorders", "question": "A 62-year-old woman is postoperative day 9 following an open reduction and internal fixation of a hip fracture sustained in a fall. Her recovery had been progressing with physical therapy until this afternoon, when she suddenly develops sharp, pleuritic chest pain, dyspnea, and a new low-grade fever of 38.0 C while sitting in a chair. On examination her respiratory rate is 24/min, oxygen saturation is 91% on room air, and her left calf is notably swollen, warm, and tender to palpation compared to the right. Which of the following is the most likely explanation for her new fever and symptoms?", "options": {"A": "A late surgical site infection at the fixation site", "B": "Atelectasis from poor postoperative pulmonary hygiene", "C": "Venous thromboembolism with pulmonary embolism", "D": "A drug fever from a recently started antibiotic"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Venous thromboembolism with pulmonary embolism): Fever occurring after postoperative day 7, combined with pleuritic chest pain, dyspnea, and a swollen tender calf, strongly suggests deep venous thrombosis with pulmonary embolism, which classically presents in this later postoperative window, particularly after orthopedic procedures involving prolonged immobility. Surgical site infection would more typically show localized wound findings, atelectasis presents earlier in the postoperative course, and there is no described new medication exposure to suggest drug fever."}, {"id": 869, "domain": "multisystem_processes_and_disorders", "question": "A 26-year-old man with a history of migraine prophylaxis on propranolol is brought to the emergency department by his roommate after he was found lethargic with an empty pill bottle nearby, roughly two hours after last being seen normal. He is bradycardic at 42/min, hypotensive at 78/46 mmHg, and his blood glucose is low-normal at 68 mg/dL. An electrocardiogram shows sinus bradycardia without conduction block. Which of the following treatments is most specific to reversing beta blocker toxicity?", "options": {"A": "High-dose insulin with glucose", "B": "Calcium gluconate infusion", "C": "Intravenous glucagon", "D": "Sodium bicarbonate infusion"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Intravenous glucagon): Glucagon is a relatively specific antidote for beta blocker toxicity because it increases intracellular cyclic AMP through a receptor pathway that bypasses the blocked beta-adrenergic receptor, improving heart rate and contractility. High-dose insulin with glucose (euglycemia therapy) and calcium are more specifically used for calcium channel blocker toxicity, and sodium bicarbonate is more relevant to sodium channel blockade toxicity such as tricyclic antidepressant overdose, though it can occasionally be used adjunctively in mixed overdoses."}, {"id": 870, "domain": "multisystem_processes_and_disorders", "question": "A 55-year-old man with poorly controlled type 1 diabetes mellitus who ran out of his insulin three days ago presents to the emergency department with confusion, deep and rapid (Kussmaul) breathing, diffuse abdominal pain, and a fruity odor on his breath. His serum glucose is 480 mg/dL. Arterial blood gas shows a pH of 7.18 with a bicarbonate of 8 mEq/L, and his anion gap is calculated as 28 mEq/L. Which of the following categories of causes should be considered first for this anion gap metabolic acidosis?", "options": {"A": "Gastrointestinal bicarbonate losses from diarrhea, producing a normal anion gap acidosis instead", "B": "Ketoacidosis, lactic acidosis, toxic alcohol ingestion, uremia, or salicylates", "C": "Renal tubular acidosis from impaired distal acid secretion", "D": "Excessive administration of normal saline, producing a dilutional non-anion gap acidosis instead"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ketoacidosis, lactic acidosis, toxic alcohol ingestion, uremia, or salicylates): An elevated anion gap metabolic acidosis has a differential that classically includes ketoacidosis (diabetic, alcoholic, or starvation), lactic acidosis, toxic alcohol ingestions (methanol, ethylene glycol), uremia, and salicylate toxicity, all of which generate unmeasured anions. Gastrointestinal bicarbonate losses and renal tubular acidosis instead classically produce a normal anion gap (hyperchloremic) metabolic acidosis, and excessive saline administration also typically produces a normal anion gap dilutional acidosis rather than a high anion gap picture."}, {"id": 873, "domain": "multisystem_processes_and_disorders", "question": "A 30-year-old man with no significant past medical history presents after a high-speed motor vehicle collision with diffuse abdominal tenderness, distension, hypotension at 82/54 mmHg, and tachycardia at 134/min. A FAST examination shows a large amount of free intraperitoneal fluid. He is taken emergently to the operating room, but remains hypotensive after 2 liters of crystalloid and initial packed red blood cell transfusion, and the surgical team activates the massive transfusion protocol. Which of the following transfusion strategies is most appropriate if massive transfusion is required?", "options": {"A": "Packed red blood cells given alone until hemoglobin normalizes", "B": "Crystalloid alone, reserving blood products for a hemoglobin below 5 g/dL", "C": "Fresh frozen plasma given alone to correct early coagulopathy", "D": "A balanced ratio of packed red blood cells, fresh frozen plasma, and platelets"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A balanced ratio of packed red blood cells, fresh frozen plasma, and platelets): Current trauma resuscitation practice favors a balanced transfusion strategy, using packed red blood cells, fresh frozen plasma, and platelets in roughly equal ratios, over crystalloid-heavy resuscitation or red cells alone. This approach better replaces the components actually being lost in hemorrhage and reduces dilutional coagulopathy, which worsens ongoing bleeding and contributes to the lethal triad of hypothermia, acidosis, and coagulopathy."}, {"id": 874, "domain": "multisystem_processes_and_disorders", "question": "A 25-year-old man is brought to the emergency department by friends who report he had been at an all-night party and reportedly used an unknown stimulant drug several hours earlier. He is agitated, restless, and speaking rapidly. His temperature is 38.6 C, heart rate is 122/min, and blood pressure is 168/98 mmHg, with dilated but reactive pupils. His skin is notably diaphoretic and clammy rather than dry, and he has fine tremor of both hands. Which of the following toxidromes best fits this presentation, and how does it differ from the anticholinergic toxidrome?", "options": {"A": "Cholinergic toxidrome, distinguished by bradycardia, pinpoint miosis, and excessive bodily secretions", "B": "Sympathomimetic toxidrome, distinguished from anticholinergic toxicity by diaphoresis rather than dry skin", "C": "Opioid toxidrome, distinguished by pinpoint pupils and significant respiratory depression over time", "D": "Sedative-hypnotic toxidrome, distinguished by marked drowsiness and progressive hypotension over time"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Sympathomimetic toxidrome, distinguished from anticholinergic toxicity by diaphoresis rather than dry skin): Sympathomimetic toxicity, from agents such as cocaine or amphetamines, produces tachycardia, hypertension, agitation, and mydriasis similar to the anticholinergic toxidrome, but the presence of diaphoresis (sweaty skin) rather than dry, flushed skin distinguishes sympathomimetic toxicity from anticholinergic toxicity. The other toxidromes listed feature entirely different vital sign patterns (bradycardia and miosis for cholinergic, respiratory depression and miosis for opioid, and drowsiness with hypotension for sedative-hypnotic toxicity) and do not match this presentation."}, {"id": 875, "domain": "multisystem_processes_and_disorders", "question": "A 58-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is admitted through the emergency department with three days of productive cough, fever, and worsening dyspnea. Chest radiograph confirms a right lower lobe infiltrate consistent with severe community-acquired pneumonia. Her respiratory rate is 26/min, systolic blood pressure is 96 mmHg, and she is mildly confused compared to baseline, meeting criteria for sepsis. Two sets of blood cultures are drawn at the bedside. Which of the following is the most appropriate timing for initiating empiric antibiotics relative to obtaining blood cultures?", "options": {"A": "Antibiotics should be delayed until blood culture results are finalized", "B": "Blood cultures should be obtained first, if this does not meaningfully delay prompt antibiotic administration", "C": "Antibiotics should be given at least six hours before drawing any cultures", "D": "Cultures should only be drawn after 48 hours of antibiotic therapy to assess treatment response"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Blood cultures should be obtained first, if this does not meaningfully delay prompt antibiotic administration): In suspected sepsis, blood cultures should ideally be obtained before starting antibiotics, since antibiotics can reduce culture sensitivity, but this should not meaningfully delay the prompt administration of broad-spectrum antibiotics, which is a time-sensitive intervention within the first hour of recognition. Waiting for final culture results, delaying antibiotics for hours, or drawing cultures only after prolonged antibiotic exposure would all inappropriately delay effective care in a septic patient."}, {"id": 878, "domain": "multisystem_processes_and_disorders", "question": "A 24-year-old woman presents with a one-year history of progressive fatigue, a persistent gritty sensation in both eyes, and dry mouth severe enough that she now keeps water at her bedside overnight and has developed several new dental caries. She also reports occasional parotid gland swelling. Schirmer testing confirms decreased tear production, and a labial salivary gland biopsy shows a focal lymphocytic infiltration pattern. Which of the following long-term complications should this patient be counseled about?", "options": {"A": "Increased risk of pulmonary fibrosis", "B": "Increased risk of scleroderma renal crisis", "C": "Increased risk of lymphoma", "D": "Increased risk of aortic aneurysm"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Increased risk of lymphoma): Sjogren syndrome, characterized by dry eyes and dry mouth from lymphocytic infiltration of exocrine glands, carries an increased long-term risk of lymphoma, which patients should be counseled about and monitored for. Pulmonary fibrosis is more classically associated with systemic sclerosis, renal crisis is specific to systemic sclerosis, and aortic aneurysm risk is more associated with large-vessel vasculitis or connective tissue disorders like Marfan syndrome, not Sjogren syndrome."}, {"id": 880, "domain": "multisystem_processes_and_disorders", "question": "A 52-year-old woman is postoperative day 1 following an emergent exploratory laparotomy for a perforated diverticular abscess with diffuse peritonitis. She remains intubated and sedated on mechanical ventilation in the surgical intensive care unit, with a nasogastric tube for decompression and an open abdomen managed with temporary closure. She is hemodynamically stable but her surgical drains continue to have moderate serosanguineous output. Her family asks about measures to reduce her risk of venous thromboembolism given her prolonged immobility. Which of the following is most appropriate?", "options": {"A": "Use mechanical prophylaxis plus low molecular weight heparin when her bleeding risk allows it", "B": "Hold off on thromboprophylaxis measures until she becomes fully ambulatory later in her recovery.", "C": "Rely solely on early ambulation efforts, without adding any mechanical device or pharmacologic medication for prevention.", "D": "Use only aspirin alone as the sole method of thromboprophylaxis in this postoperative setting."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Use mechanical prophylaxis plus low molecular weight heparin when her bleeding risk allows it): Postoperative venous thromboembolism prophylaxis typically combines mechanical measures (such as intermittent pneumatic compression devices) with pharmacologic prophylaxis, such as low molecular weight heparin, when the patient's bleeding risk allows it. Withholding all prophylaxis in an immobile postoperative patient increases venous thromboembolism risk, relying on ambulation alone is insufficient in a sedated, intubated patient, and aspirin alone is not the standard of care for surgical thromboprophylaxis in this setting."}, {"id": 882, "domain": "multisystem_processes_and_disorders", "question": "A 45-year-old man with a history of iron-deficiency anemia treated with over-the-counter iron supplements presents six hours after ingesting an unknown, but reportedly large, quantity of his iron tablets during a period of acute emotional distress. He has vomiting, diffuse abdominal pain, and one episode of hematemesis. His abdominal radiograph shows multiple radiopaque tablet fragments in the stomach, and his serum iron level is markedly elevated. Which of the following is the most appropriate antidote if significant iron toxicity is confirmed?", "options": {"A": "Deferoxamine", "B": "Fomepizole", "C": "Pralidoxime", "D": "Digoxin antibodies"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Deferoxamine): Deferoxamine is the chelating agent used for significant iron overdose, binding free iron to facilitate its excretion and reduce ongoing tissue toxicity. Fomepizole is used for toxic alcohol ingestions, pralidoxime is used alongside atropine for organophosphate poisoning, and digoxin-specific antibody fragments are used for digoxin toxicity, none of which apply to this iron ingestion."}, {"id": 884, "domain": "multisystem_processes_and_disorders", "question": "A 39-year-old man presents with fever, headache, and altered mental status after a recent upper respiratory infection. Over the following several hours in the emergency department he develops a rapidly spreading purpuric rash across his trunk and extremities, hypotension refractory to initial fluid boluses, and laboratory evidence of disseminated intravascular coagulation-like physiology, including a rapidly falling platelet count and prolonged coagulation times. Blood cultures ultimately grow Neisseria meningitidis. Given this fulminant presentation, which of the following describes the most appropriate immediate priority?", "options": {"A": "Delaying antibiotic administration until the lumbar puncture results eventually come back from the lab", "B": "Obtaining magnetic resonance imaging of the brain before any treatment", "C": "Immediate empiric antibiotics along with aggressive hemodynamic support, without delay for confirmatory testing", "D": "Observation alone, given what initially appeared to be his recent uncomplicated viral illness"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Immediate empiric antibiotics along with aggressive hemodynamic support, without delay for confirmatory testing): Fulminant meningococcemia can progress to septic shock within hours and carries high mortality, so empiric antibiotics and aggressive hemodynamic support must begin immediately based on clinical suspicion, without waiting for lumbar puncture or imaging results, which would dangerously delay life-saving treatment. This case illustrates how a multisystem infectious process can cause simultaneous neurologic, hematologic, and hemodynamic collapse, requiring immediate, parallel intervention rather than a sequential diagnostic-then-treatment approach."}, {"id": 885, "domain": "multisystem_processes_and_disorders", "question": "A 58-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes is five days post total abdominal hysterectomy for uterine fibroids. She develops fever to 39.3 C, a foul odor from the incision, and rapidly spreading skin discoloration extending well beyond the incision margins, with new bullae, crepitus on palpation, and severe pain out of proportion to the physical findings on exam. She appears acutely ill with tachycardia and mild confusion. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate management?", "options": {"A": "Oral antibiotics with outpatient wound care follow-up", "B": "Emergent surgical debridement along with broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics", "C": "Observation with serial wound checks over the next 48 hours", "D": "Topical antiseptic dressing changes alone"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Emergent surgical debridement along with broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics): Rapidly spreading skin changes with bullae, pain out of proportion to examination findings, and systemic toxicity in a surgical wound raise concern for necrotizing soft tissue infection, a surgical emergency requiring immediate operative debridement in addition to broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics. Oral antibiotics, simple observation, or topical dressing changes alone would allow rapidly progressive, life-threatening tissue destruction to continue unchecked."}, {"id": 886, "domain": "multisystem_processes_and_disorders", "question": "A 46-year-old man with no prior cardiac history undergoes an elective total hip arthroplasty under spinal anesthesia. On postoperative day 1 he develops sudden dyspnea, acute pulmonary edema on chest radiograph, hypotension, and cool, mottled extremities. Bedside echocardiography shows a new regional wall motion abnormality, and troponin is markedly elevated. His pulmonary capillary wedge pressure on invasive monitoring is markedly elevated and cardiac output is low. Which of the following types of shock best matches this hemodynamic profile?", "options": {"A": "Distributive shock from an undiagnosed infection", "B": "Hypovolemic shock from occult surgical bleeding", "C": "Cardiogenic shock from acute pump failure", "D": "Obstructive shock from a tension pneumothorax"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Cardiogenic shock from acute pump failure): A low cardiac output combined with elevated filling pressures, such as an elevated pulmonary capillary wedge pressure, reflects cardiogenic shock, in which the heart itself has failed as a pump, in this case likely from a perioperative myocardial event. Distributive shock would show low systemic vascular resistance rather than elevated filling pressures, hypovolemic shock would show low, not elevated, filling pressures, and obstructive shock from tension pneumothorax would typically present with additional findings such as absent breath sounds and tracheal deviation."}, {"id": 887, "domain": "multisystem_processes_and_disorders", "question": "A 29-year-old woman with systemic lupus erythematosus and a history of two prior first-trimester miscarriages is found on preoperative screening before an elective procedure to have a prolonged partial thromboplastin time that does not correct when her plasma is mixed 1:1 with normal plasma. Further workup reveals a positive lupus anticoagulant and moderate-titer anticardiolipin antibodies on two occasions twelve weeks apart. She reports no personal history of abnormal bleeding. Which of the following best explains this laboratory pattern despite her clinical risk of thrombosis rather than bleeding?", "options": {"A": "A true underlying factor deficiency causing both the laboratory abnormality and bleeding risk", "B": "Vitamin K deficiency from chronic malabsorption of multiple fat-soluble vitamins over time", "C": "An antiphospholipid antibody that interferes with phospholipid-dependent coagulation testing in vitro while promoting thrombosis in vivo", "D": "A simple laboratory error entirely unrelated to her underlying rheumatologic disease process"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (An antiphospholipid antibody that interferes with phospholipid-dependent coagulation testing in vitro while promoting thrombosis in vivo): The lupus anticoagulant is an antiphospholipid antibody that paradoxically prolongs phospholipid-dependent clotting assays in vitro, producing a prolonged partial thromboplastin time that fails to correct with mixing studies, while clinically promoting thrombosis rather than bleeding in vivo as part of antiphospholipid syndrome, which is associated with systemic lupus erythematosus and recurrent pregnancy loss. This pattern is distinct from a true factor deficiency, vitamin K deficiency, or a simple laboratory artifact, none of which would explain the combination of this specific antibody and the associated clinical thrombotic risk."}, {"id": 889, "domain": "multisystem_processes_and_disorders", "question": "A 34-year-old woman is undergoing preoperative risk assessment before an elective bariatric procedure for severe obesity. She has type 2 diabetes controlled with a basal-bolus insulin regimen, no history of ischemic heart disease, congestive heart failure, or cerebrovascular disease, and her most recent creatinine is 0.8 mg/dL. Her surgeon asks the preoperative medicine team to characterize her risk using a standard validated cardiac risk tool before proceeding with surgical planning. Which of the following best reflects her Revised Cardiac Risk Index profile?", "options": {"A": "She has one of the six risk factors captured by the index, insulin-dependent diabetes", "B": "She has zero of the six risk factors captured by this index", "C": "She has all six of the risk factors captured by this index", "D": "The Revised Cardiac Risk Index does not apply to bariatric surgery"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (She has one of the six risk factors captured by the index, insulin-dependent diabetes): The Revised Cardiac Risk Index includes high-risk surgery, ischemic heart disease, congestive heart failure, cerebrovascular disease, insulin-dependent diabetes, and a preoperative creatinine above 2 mg/dL. This patient has only one of those six factors, insulin-dependent diabetes, since she lacks the other listed comorbidities and has normal renal function; whether bariatric surgery itself qualifies as high-risk would need to be considered separately, but the index framework applies broadly across noncardiac surgeries."}, {"id": 890, "domain": "multisystem_processes_and_disorders", "question": "A 27-year-old man with no significant past medical history is brought to the emergency department after a high-speed fall from scaffolding at a construction site, landing on his left side. He is hypotensive at 78/50 mmHg, tachycardic at 138/min, and has a rigid, distended, diffusely tender abdomen with voluntary guarding. His breath sounds are equal bilaterally and his trachea is midline. Focused assessment with sonography for trauma shows a large amount of free fluid in the abdomen, and his hemoglobin has fallen from an initial 13.8 to 9.2 g/dL over 45 minutes despite 2 liters of crystalloid. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step after completion of the primary survey?", "options": {"A": "Emergent surgical exploration given hemodynamic instability with a positive FAST examination", "B": "Discharge home with outpatient surgical follow-up once vital signs normalize briefly", "C": "Elective computed tomography scan scheduled for the following morning", "D": "Observation alone with serial abdominal examinations over 24 hours"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Emergent surgical exploration given hemodynamic instability with a positive FAST examination): A hemodynamically unstable trauma patient with a positive FAST examination showing free intra-abdominal fluid should proceed to emergent surgical exploration rather than further diagnostic imaging, since the instability itself indicates ongoing significant hemorrhage requiring definitive surgical control. Discharge, elective imaging the next day, and observation alone would all inappropriately delay necessary intervention in a patient who is actively decompensating from presumed intra-abdominal hemorrhage."}, {"id": 894, "domain": "endocrine_system", "question": "A 58-year-old man with a 10-year history of type 2 diabetes controlled on metformin and a sulfonylurea presents with 3 days of fever and productive cough. He is diagnosed with community acquired pneumonia. His family notes he has become progressively more confused over the past day and has barely been drinking fluids. He has chronic kidney disease with a baseline creatinine of 1.6 mg/dL. On exam he is lethargic, with dry mucous membranes and poor skin turgor. Glucose is 780 mg/dL, bicarbonate is 20 mEq/L, arterial pH is 7.34, and serum ketones are negative. Calculated serum osmolality is 342 mOsm/kg. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial step in management?", "options": {"A": "Immediate initiation of a high-dose intravenous insulin infusion without prior fluid administration", "B": "Intravenous sodium bicarbonate to correct the mild acid-base abnormality before other therapy", "C": "Aggressive intravenous isotonic saline resuscitation before initiating an insulin infusion", "D": "Oral rehydration with sugar-free fluids to avoid further intravenous volume overload"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Aggressive intravenous isotonic saline resuscitation before initiating an insulin infusion): This presentation, marked hyperglycemia with near-normal pH, absent ketones, and markedly elevated osmolality in an elderly type 2 diabetic with an infectious trigger, is hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state. Patients with HHS are typically more profoundly volume depleted than those with DKA, so aggressive isotonic fluid resuscitation is the essential first step, with an insulin infusion added once fluids are underway. Bicarbonate is not indicated since the pH is near normal, and oral rehydration is inadequate for this degree of dehydration and altered mental status."}, {"id": 895, "domain": "endocrine_system", "question": "She presents to her primary care physician for a routine annual physical examination. She works a desk job, exercises occasionally on weekends, and reports no recent illnesses. Her blood pressure today is 122/78 mmHg and her heart rate is 76/min. She is otherwise feeling well and has no new complaints today. A 45-year-old woman is found on routine screening to have a fasting plasma glucose of 118 mg/dL on one occasion and 122 mg/dL on a repeat test two weeks later. She has no polyuria, polydipsia, or weight loss, and her body mass index is 24 kg/m2. She has no family history of diabetes. Which of the following best characterizes her current metabolic status?", "options": {"A": "Established type 2 diabetes mellitus based on two elevated fasting glucose measurements", "B": "Impaired fasting glucose, which does not yet meet diagnostic criteria for diabetes mellitus", "C": "Established type 1 diabetes mellitus given the absence of an identifiable family history", "D": "Diabetic ketoacidosis risk requiring immediate initiation of a basal-bolus insulin regimen"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Impaired fasting glucose, which does not yet meet diagnostic criteria for diabetes mellitus): A fasting plasma glucose between 100 and 125 mg/dL defines impaired fasting glucose, a prediabetic state, whereas diabetes requires a fasting glucose of 126 mg/dL or higher confirmed on two occasions, an A1c of 6.5 percent or higher, or other criteria described in this chapter. Her values fall short of the diagnostic threshold, so she has prediabetes rather than established diabetes, and there is no indication whatsoever for insulin therapy or concern for ketoacidosis at this stage."}, {"id": 896, "domain": "endocrine_system", "question": "He comes to clinic for a scheduled diabetes follow-up visit. He reports good adherence to his metformin and takes low-dose aspirin and a statin. He has no chest pain, shortness of breath, or leg swelling today, and his blood pressure is well controlled at 128/76 mmHg. He asks whether anything more can be done to protect his heart while also lowering his blood sugar. A 61-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and known coronary artery disease with a prior myocardial infarction has a hemoglobin A1c of 8.2 percent despite maximally tolerated metformin. His estimated GFR is 58 mL/min/1.73m2 and he has no history of heart failure. Which class of medication would provide the greatest additional benefit for his combined glycemic and cardiovascular risk profile?", "options": {"A": "A glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonist added to his current metformin regimen", "B": "A sulfonylurea added to his current metformin regimen for additional glucose lowering", "C": "A thiazolidinedione added to his current metformin regimen given his stable cardiac history", "D": "An alpha-glucosidase inhibitor added to his current metformin regimen to target postprandial glucose"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonist added to his current metformin regimen): In a patient with established atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease and inadequate control on metformin, both GLP-1 receptor agonists and SGLT2 inhibitors have demonstrated reductions in major adverse cardiovascular events independent of their glucose-lowering effect, making a GLP-1 agonist the preferred addition here. Sulfonylureas and alpha-glucosidase inhibitors lower glucose without this cardiovascular benefit, and thiazolidinediones can worsen fluid retention and are used cautiously in patients with cardiac disease."}, {"id": 897, "domain": "endocrine_system", "question": "She reports the symptoms have been gradually worsening over the past several months and now interfere with her sleep most nights. She denies any history of trauma, back pain, or weakness in her legs. Her vibration sense is diminished bilaterally at the great toes, and her ankle reflexes are difficult to elicit. A 67-year-old woman with longstanding type 2 diabetes reports several months of a burning, tingling sensation in both feet that is worse at night, along with decreased sensation to light touch and vibration in a stocking distribution on examination. Her monofilament testing is abnormal bilaterally. Her most recent hemoglobin A1c is 9.1 percent. Which of the following is the most appropriate first-line pharmacologic agent for her neuropathic pain?", "options": {"A": "A short course of oral prednisone to reduce presumed nerve inflammation", "B": "High-dose intravenous immunoglobulin targeting an underlying autoimmune neuropathic process", "C": "Naproxen, a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug, taken regularly for symptom control", "D": "Duloxetine, an agent with demonstrated efficacy for diabetic peripheral neuropathic pain"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Duloxetine, an agent with demonstrated efficacy for diabetic peripheral neuropathic pain): This patient has classic diabetic peripheral neuropathy, a symmetric, distal, sensory-predominant process related to chronic hyperglycemia. First-line pharmacologic options for painful diabetic neuropathy include gabapentin, pregabalin, and duloxetine, all of which have trial evidence supporting their use. Corticosteroids and intravenous immunoglobulin are not indicated since this is not an inflammatory or autoimmune neuropathy, and nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs are not effective for this type of neuropathic pain and carry renal risk in diabetic patients."}, {"id": 898, "domain": "endocrine_system", "question": "He works long hours and admits he rarely schedules preventive appointments unless something feels wrong. His blood pressure today is 134/82 mmHg and his hemoglobin A1c is 7.4 percent. He asks whether an eye examination is really necessary given that his vision currently feels completely normal to him. A 52-year-old man with type 2 diabetes for 8 years has never had an eye examination since his diagnosis. He reports no visual complaints. His most recent urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio was within normal limits three months ago, and his feet were unremarkable on examination today. Which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation regarding retinopathy screening for this patient?", "options": {"A": "Defer eye examination until he develops visual symptoms such as blurring or floaters", "B": "Order only a fasting glucose and hemoglobin A1c rather than an ophthalmologic examination", "C": "Refer for a dilated eye examination now, and continue annual screening thereafter regardless of symptoms", "D": "Screen for retinopathy only if his urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio becomes abnormal in the future"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Refer for a dilated eye examination now, and continue annual screening thereafter regardless of symptoms): Diabetic retinopathy can progress silently without symptoms until advanced, vision-threatening disease is present, so guidelines recommend a dilated eye examination at the time of type 2 diabetes diagnosis and annually thereafter regardless of visual symptoms or the status of other complications such as nephropathy. Waiting for symptoms or linking screening to the albumin-to-creatinine ratio would miss treatable early disease, and laboratory tests alone cannot substitute for direct examination of the retina."}, {"id": 899, "domain": "endocrine_system", "question": "She initially attributed her symptoms to being overworked and getting older, but her husband encouraged her to be evaluated after noticing she seemed unusually sluggish and was wearing sweaters even in a warm house. Her pulse today is 58/min and regular, and her skin feels dry and cool to the touch. A 34-year-old woman reports six weeks of fatigue, unintentional 8-pound weight gain, constipation, cold intolerance, and dry skin. On examination she has a diffusely enlarged, firm, nontender thyroid gland and delayed relaxation of her deep tendon reflexes. Laboratory studies are obtained. Which of the following autoantibodies is most likely to be positive in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Anti-thyroid peroxidase antibody, consistent with Hashimoto thyroiditis as the underlying cause", "B": "Thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulin, consistent with Graves disease as the underlying cause", "C": "Anti-glutamic acid decarboxylase antibody, consistent with autoimmune destruction of pancreatic islets", "D": "Anti-centromere antibody, consistent with a limited cutaneous autoimmune connective tissue disease"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Anti-thyroid peroxidase antibody, consistent with Hashimoto thyroiditis as the underlying cause): This vignette describes primary hypothyroidism, the most common cause of which in an iodine-sufficient country like the United States is Hashimoto thyroiditis, an autoimmune process associated with anti-thyroid peroxidase and anti-thyroglobulin antibodies and a firm, diffusely enlarged gland. Thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulin is associated with Graves disease, which causes hyperthyroidism rather than this hypothyroid picture, and anti-GAD and anti-centromere antibodies are unrelated to thyroid disease.", "image": "question_images/q_899.png"}, {"id": 900, "domain": "endocrine_system", "question": "She has otherwise tolerated the medication well over these eight months, with no prior episodes of illness requiring a call to the office. She denies any cough, nasal congestion, or known sick contacts, and reports feeling generally well aside from the throat discomfort and fever she is calling about today. A 41-year-old woman with Graves disease has been maintained on methimazole for 8 months with good symptomatic control. She calls the clinic reporting a sore throat and fever of 101.8 F that began this morning. She has no cough, congestion, or other localizing symptoms. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Reassure her that this is a typical viral illness and continue her current methimazole dose", "B": "Stop methimazole and obtain a complete blood count promptly to assess for agranulocytosis", "C": "Increase her methimazole dose to better control presumed thyrotoxicosis-related fever", "D": "Start empiric oral antibiotics for streptococcal pharyngitis without further evaluation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stop methimazole and obtain a complete blood count promptly to assess for agranulocytosis): Methimazole carries a risk of agranulocytosis, and patients are specifically counseled to seek immediate evaluation for fever or sore throat while on this medication, since these can be the only early signs of a dangerously low neutrophil count. The correct approach is to hold the medication and urgently check a complete blood count rather than assuming a benign viral illness, adjusting the dose for symptom control, or treating empirically without first excluding this serious drug effect."}, {"id": 902, "domain": "endocrine_system", "question": "He was previously healthy and had not seen a physician in over a year before this episode began. He denies any prior thyroid disease, recent travel, or sick contacts beyond the mild upper respiratory illness two weeks ago. His resting heart rate today is 108/min and he appears mildly anxious and diaphoretic. A 55-year-old man presents with several weeks of neck pain radiating to the jaw, low-grade fever, fatigue, and palpitations following an upper respiratory infection two weeks earlier. His thyroid gland is exquisitely tender to palpation. Laboratory testing shows a suppressed TSH and elevated free T4, and radioactive iodine uptake is markedly low. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Graves disease with superimposed viral pharyngitis causing the neck tenderness", "B": "Toxic multinodular goiter with an incidental unrelated upper respiratory infection", "C": "Subacute (de Quervain) thyroiditis with a transient thyrotoxic phase from release of stored hormone", "D": "Papillary thyroid carcinoma invading local soft tissue and causing referred jaw pain"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Subacute (de Quervain) thyroiditis with a transient thyrotoxic phase from release of stored hormone): A painful, tender thyroid gland following a viral illness, combined with a thyrotoxic biochemical picture but low radioactive iodine uptake, is classic for subacute (de Quervain) thyroiditis, in which inflammatory damage releases preformed hormone rather than reflecting new hormone synthesis. Graves disease and toxic multinodular goiter both show high, not low, uptake, and thyroid carcinoma does not typically present with an acutely tender gland or a thyrotoxic laboratory pattern."}, {"id": 905, "domain": "endocrine_system", "question": "The nodule is nontender and mobile on palpation, and he reports no difficulty swallowing, voice change, or neck pain. He has no personal history of head or neck radiation exposure, though he recalls a distant cousin who was treated for a thyroid cancer many years ago. A 47-year-old man has a 1.8 cm thyroid nodule found incidentally on a carotid ultrasound. TSH is normal. Ultrasound of the nodule shows microcalcifications, an irregular margin, and a height-to-width ratio greater than 1. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?", "options": {"A": "Fine needle aspiration biopsy of the nodule given its suspicious sonographic features", "B": "Radioactive iodine uptake scan to determine whether the nodule is hyperfunctioning", "C": "Reassurance and routine annual palpation alone, since the TSH is within normal limits", "D": "Immediate total thyroidectomy without prior tissue sampling given the imaging findings"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Fine needle aspiration biopsy of the nodule given its suspicious sonographic features): When TSH is normal or elevated, ultrasound features guide the next step, and this nodule shows several classic features of malignancy risk: microcalcifications, irregular margins, and an increased height-to-width ratio. Fine needle aspiration biopsy is the appropriate next step to obtain a tissue diagnosis. A radioactive iodine uptake scan is reserved for patients with a low TSH suggesting a hyperfunctioning nodule, and proceeding directly to surgery without a biopsy, or simply observing, would bypass necessary diagnostic information."}, {"id": 907, "domain": "endocrine_system", "question": "She initially assumed the changes in her body were simply related to stress at work and getting older, but her sister, a nurse, urged her to be evaluated after noticing the distinctive skin changes and how quickly her face seemed to have become fuller and rounder over the past several months. A 34-year-old woman presents with progressive central weight gain, new wide purple striae across her abdomen, easy bruising, and a rounded, plethoric face over the past year. She also reports new hypertension and irregular menses. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial screening test for her presentation?", "options": {"A": "A single random morning serum cortisol level drawn at any convenient time", "B": "A 24-hour urinary free cortisol collection or a late-night salivary cortisol level", "C": "A plasma ACTH level as the very first test before any cortisol measurement", "D": "An oral glucose tolerance test with growth hormone suppression testing"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A 24-hour urinary free cortisol collection or a late-night salivary cortisol level): This clinical picture of central obesity, wide striae, easy bruising, plethora, hypertension, and menstrual irregularity is classic for Cushing syndrome. Appropriate initial screening tests include a 24-hour urinary free cortisol, a late-night salivary cortisol, or an overnight low-dose dexamethasone suppression test, since cortisol has a diurnal pattern that makes a single random morning value unreliable for screening. A plasma ACTH level is used later to localize the source once excess cortisol is confirmed, and growth hormone suppression testing is used to evaluate acromegaly, not Cushing syndrome."}, {"id": 908, "domain": "endocrine_system", "question": "He reports increasing fatigue, unintentional weight loss, and mild proximal muscle weakness over the past two months, which he initially attributed to age and heavy smoking. He denies fever, cough productive of blood, or chest pain, though he did notice his ankles have become more swollen recently. A 60-year-old man with a 50-pack-year smoking history and a new lung mass is found to have hypokalemia, hypertension, and markedly elevated 24-hour urinary free cortisol. His plasma ACTH level is markedly elevated. Which of the following is the most likely underlying mechanism?", "options": {"A": "Ectopic ACTH secretion from a small cell lung carcinoma driving adrenal cortisol overproduction", "B": "A cortisol-secreting adrenal adenoma that has suppressed pituitary ACTH release", "C": "Primary adrenal insufficiency with compensatory but ineffective ACTH elevation", "D": "A pituitary corticotroph microadenoma unrelated to his newly discovered lung mass"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ectopic ACTH secretion from a small cell lung carcinoma driving adrenal cortisol overproduction): An elevated ACTH in the setting of confirmed hypercortisolism indicates ACTH-dependent disease, and in a heavy smoker with a new lung mass, hypokalemia, and markedly elevated cortisol, ectopic ACTH secretion from a tumor such as small cell lung carcinoma is the most likely mechanism, often producing more severe hypokalemia than pituitary Cushing disease. An adrenal adenoma would suppress rather than elevate ACTH, and adrenal insufficiency would present with low, not elevated, cortisol."}, {"id": 909, "domain": "endocrine_system", "question": "Which of the following is the most likely underlying diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Secondary adrenal insufficiency from longstanding exogenous corticosteroid use", "B": "Syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion from an unrelated cause", "C": "Primary adrenal insufficiency, most likely from autoimmune adrenalitis", "D": "Acute pyelonephritis with septic shock as the primary underlying process"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Primary adrenal insufficiency, most likely from autoimmune adrenalitis): The combination of hypotension, hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, hypoglycemia, weight loss, and hyperpigmentation strongly suggests primary adrenal insufficiency, and in a patient with an existing autoimmune condition such as autoimmune thyroiditis, autoimmune adrenalitis (Addison disease) is the most likely cause. Secondary adrenal insufficiency would not cause hyperkalemia or significant hyperpigmentation since aldosterone is preserved and ACTH is low rather than high. Isolated SIADH does not explain the hyperkalemia, hypotension, or skin findings, and nothing suggests an infectious source.", "case_id": "endo_case_addison", "case_text": "A 46-year-old woman with a history of autoimmune thyroiditis is brought to the emergency department after two days of worsening nausea, vomiting, and severe abdominal pain. Her husband reports she has felt increasingly fatigued and has lost 12 pounds over the past two months. On examination, blood pressure is 78/48 mmHg, pulse is 122/min, and she has noticeably darkened skin over her knuckles, elbows, and gum line. Laboratory studies show sodium 126 mEq/L, potassium 6.1 mEq/L, and glucose 62 mg/dL.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 910, "domain": "endocrine_system", "question": "Which of the following best explains the hyperpigmentation noted on examination?", "options": {"A": "Direct deposition of excess cortisol metabolites within the dermal layer of the skin", "B": "Chronic hyperglycemia leading to nonenzymatic glycation of dermal collagen fibers", "C": "A paraneoplastic process related to an undiagnosed underlying malignancy", "D": "Elevated ACTH and its precursor proopiomelanocortin, which shares a melanocyte-stimulating fragment"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Elevated ACTH and its precursor proopiomelanocortin, which shares a melanocyte-stimulating fragment): In primary adrenal insufficiency, loss of negative feedback from cortisol leads to markedly elevated ACTH, which is cleaved from the larger precursor proopiomelanocortin that also contains a melanocyte-stimulating hormone fragment, producing the characteristic hyperpigmentation seen at pressure points, skin creases, and mucous membranes. This mechanism is specific to primary, not secondary, adrenal insufficiency, since ACTH is low rather than elevated in the secondary form.", "case_id": "endo_case_addison", "case_text": "A 46-year-old woman with a history of autoimmune thyroiditis is brought to the emergency department after two days of worsening nausea, vomiting, and severe abdominal pain. Her husband reports she has felt increasingly fatigued and has lost 12 pounds over the past two months. On examination, blood pressure is 78/48 mmHg, pulse is 122/min, and she has noticeably darkened skin over her knuckles, elbows, and gum line. Laboratory studies show sodium 126 mEq/L, potassium 6.1 mEq/L, and glucose 62 mg/dL.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 911, "domain": "endocrine_system", "question": "Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate management step?", "options": {"A": "Intravenous hydrocortisone and aggressive isotonic fluid resuscitation without delay", "B": "Oral fludrocortisone alone, to be started once she is able to tolerate oral intake", "C": "Delay all hormone therapy until a cosyntropin stimulation test result is available", "D": "Intravenous insulin infusion to address the electrolyte abnormalities seen on her labs"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Intravenous hydrocortisone and aggressive isotonic fluid resuscitation without delay): This presentation represents an adrenal crisis, a medical emergency requiring immediate intravenous hydrocortisone and aggressive fluid resuscitation without waiting for confirmatory testing such as a cosyntropin stimulation test, since delay can be fatal. Oral fludrocortisone is not appropriate in this acute, unstable setting, and an insulin infusion is not indicated since her glucose is already low, not elevated, and insulin would worsen both the hypoglycemia and the hyperkalemia-related risk.", "case_id": "endo_case_addison", "case_text": "A 46-year-old woman with a history of autoimmune thyroiditis is brought to the emergency department after two days of worsening nausea, vomiting, and severe abdominal pain. Her husband reports she has felt increasingly fatigued and has lost 12 pounds over the past two months. On examination, blood pressure is 78/48 mmHg, pulse is 122/min, and she has noticeably darkened skin over her knuckles, elbows, and gum line. Laboratory studies show sodium 126 mEq/L, potassium 6.1 mEq/L, and glucose 62 mg/dL.", "case_size": 3}, {"id": 912, "domain": "endocrine_system", "question": "He has otherwise been in good health and takes no regular medications. He initially assumed the episodes were simply anxiety attacks related to a stressful new job, but he became concerned after one episode caused him to feel lightheaded and briefly see spots before his vision returned to normal. A 38-year-old man reports episodic severe headaches, palpitations, and profuse sweating that occur several times a week, each lasting about 20 minutes, sometimes triggered by exertion. During one episode captured in clinic, his blood pressure rises to 210/124 mmHg. Between episodes his blood pressure is only mildly elevated. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial diagnostic test?", "options": {"A": "A 24-hour urinary free cortisol collection to evaluate for Cushing syndrome", "B": "An oral glucose tolerance test with growth hormone suppression testing", "C": "Plasma free metanephrines or 24-hour urinary fractionated metanephrines", "D": "A cosyntropin stimulation test to evaluate for adrenal insufficiency"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Plasma free metanephrines or 24-hour urinary fractionated metanephrines): Episodic headache, palpitations, diaphoresis, and severe paroxysmal hypertension are classic for pheochromocytoma, and the appropriate initial biochemical test is plasma free metanephrines or 24-hour urinary fractionated metanephrines, both of which have high sensitivity for catecholamine-secreting tumors. Cortisol testing evaluates Cushing syndrome, growth hormone suppression testing evaluates acromegaly, and a cosyntropin stimulation test evaluates adrenal insufficiency, none of which fit this episodic hyperadrenergic presentation."}, {"id": 913, "domain": "endocrine_system", "question": "She initially assumed the missed periods were related to increased stress from a demanding new job and did not seek evaluation for several months. She denies headaches, visual changes, or significant weight change, and her physical examination, aside from the described findings, is otherwise unremarkable. A 27-year-old woman reports 8 months of amenorrhea and milky nipple discharge unrelated to pregnancy or breastfeeding. She takes no medications. A pregnancy test is negative. Her TSH is normal, and her prolactin level is markedly elevated at 180 ng/mL. Which of the following is the most appropriate first-line treatment if imaging confirms a pituitary microadenoma?", "options": {"A": "Immediate transsphenoidal surgical resection recommended as an initial approach for most microadenomas", "B": "A dopamine agonist such as cabergoline to lower prolactin and shrink the tumor", "C": "External beam radiation therapy directed at the pituitary gland as first-line therapy", "D": "High-dose levothyroxine to suppress thyrotropin-releasing hormone stimulation of prolactin"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A dopamine agonist such as cabergoline to lower prolactin and shrink the tumor): This patient has a prolactinoma, the most common functioning pituitary tumor, presenting with galactorrhea and hypogonadism from prolactin-mediated suppression of gonadotropin-releasing hormone. Her normal TSH argues against hypothyroidism as the cause of elevated prolactin. First-line treatment for a prolactinoma is a dopamine agonist such as cabergoline or bromocriptine, which shrinks the tumor and normalizes prolactin in most patients, reserving surgery for those who fail or cannot tolerate medical therapy, and radiation is not a first-line option."}, {"id": 916, "domain": "endocrine_system", "question": "Her pregnancy and delivery had otherwise been unremarkable until the hemorrhage occurred, which required an emergency blood transfusion and a brief stay in the intensive care unit. She had successfully breastfed a prior child without any difficulty and has no other chronic medical conditions. A 33-year-old woman had a massive postpartum hemorrhage requiring transfusion after delivery. Six weeks later she reports an inability to lactate, persistent fatigue, and failure to resume menstruation. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Primary ovarian insufficiency unrelated to her recent delivery and hemorrhage", "B": "Postpartum thyroiditis with an isolated transient hypothyroid phase", "C": "Lymphocytic hypophysitis occurring independently of her pregnancy history", "D": "Sheehan syndrome, causing postpartum pituitary necrosis from severe obstetric hemorrhage"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Sheehan syndrome, causing postpartum pituitary necrosis from severe obstetric hemorrhage): Failure to lactate, amenorrhea, and fatigue following severe postpartum hemorrhage are classic for Sheehan syndrome, in which the pituitary gland, physiologically enlarged during pregnancy, becomes particularly vulnerable to ischemic necrosis during an episode of severe hemorrhage and hypotension. Primary ovarian insufficiency and isolated postpartum thyroiditis would not explain the failure to lactate, and lymphocytic hypophysitis is a distinct autoimmune process rather than one directly tied to hemorrhagic shock."}, {"id": 918, "domain": "endocrine_system", "question": "She initially attributed the changes to simply needing new glasses and did not seek evaluation right away. She denies headache, nausea, or double vision, and the remainder of her neurologic examination, including extraocular movements and pupillary responses, is entirely normal. A 24-year-old woman with a known pituitary macroadenoma reports new, progressive loss of peripheral vision over several months. Formal visual field testing reveals loss of the outer (temporal) visual fields in both eyes, with central vision preserved. Which of the following structures is most likely being compressed by her tumor?", "options": {"A": "The optic tract on one side, posterior to the point where the chiasm forms", "B": "The optic chiasm, where fibers from the nasal retina of each eye cross", "C": "The oculomotor nerve, affecting extraocular movement rather than visual fields", "D": "The occipital cortex bilaterally, from a separate unrelated intracranial process"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (The optic chiasm, where fibers from the nasal retina of each eye cross): Bitemporal hemianopsia, loss of the outer visual field in both eyes with preserved central vision, is the classic finding when a pituitary mass compresses the optic chiasm from below, affecting the crossing nasal retinal fibers that carry temporal field information. A unilateral optic tract lesion would produce a homonymous, not bitemporal, field defect, oculomotor nerve compression affects eye movement rather than visual fields, and bilateral occipital cortex disease is not explained by a single midline pituitary mass."}, {"id": 919, "domain": "endocrine_system", "question": "She has no personal or family history of kidney stones, and a recent bone density scan showed mild osteopenia at the femoral neck. She takes a daily multivitamin but no calcium or vitamin D supplements, and her renal function on the same panel is within normal limits. A 63-year-old woman is found incidentally to have a calcium level of 11.4 mg/dL on a routine metabolic panel. She reports mild fatigue and constipation but denies bone pain or kidney stones. Repeat testing confirms hypercalcemia, and her intact PTH level is elevated at 96 pg/mL. Her phosphate is low-normal. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Malignancy-associated hypercalcemia from ectopic secretion of PTH-related peptide", "B": "Secondary hyperparathyroidism related to underlying chronic kidney disease", "C": "Vitamin D toxicity from excessive over-the-counter vitamin D supplementation", "D": "Primary hyperparathyroidism, most commonly due to a single parathyroid adenoma"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Primary hyperparathyroidism, most commonly due to a single parathyroid adenoma): An elevated PTH in the setting of confirmed hypercalcemia defines primary hyperparathyroidism, most commonly caused by a single parathyroid adenoma, and the low-normal phosphate fits this pattern since PTH increases renal phosphate excretion. Malignancy-associated hypercalcemia from PTH-related peptide would show an appropriately suppressed, not elevated, standard PTH level, and secondary hyperparathyroidism and vitamin D toxicity typically present with low or normal, rather than elevated, calcium at the time PTH rises.", "image": "question_images/q_919.png"}, {"id": 921, "domain": "endocrine_system", "question": "He reports fatigue and generalized itching but denies bone pain or fractures. He is on hemodialysis three times weekly, and his nephrologist is reviewing his most recent mineral metabolism panel during a routine dialysis clinic visit to reassess his bone and mineral disease management. A 58-year-old man with stage 4 chronic kidney disease is found to have a calcium level of 8.1 mg/dL, a phosphate level of 6.8 mg/dL, and an intact PTH level of 340 pg/mL. Which of the following best explains this laboratory pattern?", "options": {"A": "Primary hyperparathyroidism arising from an autonomously functioning parathyroid adenoma independent of his kidney disease", "B": "Malignancy-associated hypercalcemia from ectopic secretion of PTH-related peptide by an underlying tumor", "C": "Secondary hyperparathyroidism driven by chronic kidney disease impairing phosphate excretion and vitamin D activation", "D": "Isolated vitamin D deficiency occurring without any accompanying parathyroid hormone or phosphate abnormality"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Secondary hyperparathyroidism driven by chronic kidney disease impairing phosphate excretion and vitamin D activation): Chronic kidney disease impairs both phosphate excretion and renal activation of vitamin D, producing hyperphosphatemia and low calcium that together drive a sustained, appropriate rise in PTH, known as secondary hyperparathyroidism, which fits this patient's low calcium, high phosphate, and markedly elevated PTH. Primary hyperparathyroidism and malignancy-associated hypercalcemia would both produce elevated, not low, calcium, and isolated vitamin D deficiency alone would not produce phosphate retention of this degree in the absence of renal disease.", "image": "question_images/q_921.png"}, {"id": 922, "domain": "endocrine_system", "question": "She had been counseled before surgery about the possibility of this complication given the size and location of her goiter. Her surgical incision is healing well without signs of infection, and she otherwise feels stable aside from the described symptoms. A 39-year-old woman develops perioral numbness and cramping in both hands three days after total thyroidectomy for a large multinodular goiter. Tapping over her facial nerve produces twitching of her facial muscles, and inflating a blood pressure cuff above her systolic pressure for several minutes produces carpal spasm. Which of the following laboratory patterns is most consistent with her presentation?", "options": {"A": "Low calcium, low parathyroid hormone, and high phosphate", "B": "High calcium, high parathyroid hormone, and low phosphate", "C": "Low calcium, high parathyroid hormone, and high phosphate", "D": "High calcium, low parathyroid hormone, and normal phosphate"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Low calcium, low parathyroid hormone, and high phosphate): This patient has postsurgical hypoparathyroidism from inadvertent removal or devascularization of the parathyroid glands during thyroidectomy, demonstrating positive Chvostek and Trousseau signs from hypocalcemia. The expected laboratory pattern is low calcium, low PTH (since the source of PTH has been damaged), and high phosphate, since PTH normally increases renal phosphate excretion and this effect is lost. The other option patterns describe primary hyperparathyroidism or unrelated combinations that do not fit postsurgical hypoparathyroidism."}, {"id": 924, "domain": "endocrine_system", "question": "Her family reports she has become progressively more difficult to arouse over the past day. She has not been eating or drinking well and appears clinically dehydrated on examination, with dry mucous membranes and diminished skin turgor. A 55-year-old woman with primary hyperparathyroidism and a calcium level of 15.8 mg/dL presents with confusion and vomiting. Her family called emergency medical services after finding her unresponsive to normal conversation this morning. On arrival, paramedics noted a blood pressure of 96/58 mmHg and a heart rate of 118/min. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial step in management of her severe hypercalcemia?", "options": {"A": "Aggressive intravenous isotonic saline hydration as the first step in management", "B": "A single dose of oral calcitriol to further increase intestinal calcium absorption", "C": "Immediate parathyroidectomy without first correcting the acute metabolic derangement", "D": "Withholding all fluids until a nephrology consultation can be obtained"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Aggressive intravenous isotonic saline hydration as the first step in management): Severe symptomatic hypercalcemia is a medical emergency, and the first step in management is aggressive intravenous isotonic saline hydration, both to correct the dehydration typically present and to promote renal calcium excretion, often followed by additional therapies such as calcitonin or bisphosphonates. Calcitriol would worsen hypercalcemia by increasing intestinal calcium absorption, immediate surgery is not appropriate before the acute crisis is stabilized, and withholding fluids would delay essential emergency treatment."}, {"id": 925, "domain": "endocrine_system", "question": "He has no history of myocardial infarction, stroke, or peripheral arterial disease. He does not smoke and has well-controlled blood pressure on a single agent. He asks his physician directly whether starting a cholesterol medication would meaningfully lower his future risk of a heart attack. A 54-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and no history of cardiovascular disease has a fasting lipid panel obtained as part of routine care. His calculated 10-year atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease risk is 9 percent. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management step regarding statin therapy?", "options": {"A": "Defer any statin therapy since his LDL cholesterol is below 190 mg/dL", "B": "Initiate a high-intensity statin as though he already had established cardiovascular disease", "C": "Recommend fibrate therapy alone rather than a statin given his diabetes diagnosis", "D": "Initiate a moderate-intensity statin given his age, diabetes, and calculated cardiovascular risk"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Initiate a moderate-intensity statin given his age, diabetes, and calculated cardiovascular risk): Patients with diabetes between 40 and 75 years old are considered clear candidates for statin therapy regardless of a specific LDL threshold, and moderate-intensity statin therapy is the appropriate starting point for primary prevention in this group, with high-intensity statins generally reserved for those with established cardiovascular disease or very high LDL. Deferring therapy because LDL is below 190 ignores the diabetes-based indication, and fibrates are not first-line therapy for cardiovascular risk reduction in this context.", "image": "question_images/q_925.png"}, {"id": 927, "domain": "endocrine_system", "question": "He was brought to the emergency department by his wife after the pain failed to improve with over-the-counter antacids at home. He admits to drinking heavily most evenings and has not been checking his blood glucose regularly at home in recent weeks. A 46-year-old man with poorly controlled diabetes and heavy alcohol use presents with severe epigastric pain radiating to his back, nausea, and vomiting. His lipase is markedly elevated and his triglyceride level returns at 1,850 mg/dL. Which of the following is the most appropriate pharmacologic approach to his lipid abnormality in this setting?", "options": {"A": "A fibrate or other triglyceride-lowering therapy, since statins alone have limited effect at this level", "B": "A high-intensity statin alone, which is expected to substantially lower triglycerides of this magnitude", "C": "Cholestyramine, a bile acid sequestrant, as the preferred agent for severe hypertriglyceridemia", "D": "No specific lipid-lowering therapy, since his acute pancreatitis is unrelated to his triglyceride level"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A fibrate or other triglyceride-lowering therapy, since statins alone have limited effect at this level): Severe hypertriglyceridemia, generally above 500 to 1,000 mg/dL, carries a real risk of acute pancreatitis, which this patient appears to have developed. Fibrates and other triglyceride-lowering agents are used to treat severe hypertriglyceridemia independent of the LDL-focused statin decision, since statins have only a modest effect on triglycerides at this magnitude. Bile acid sequestrants can actually worsen hypertriglyceridemia and are not appropriate here, and this degree of hypertriglyceridemia is a well-recognized cause of acute pancreatitis rather than an unrelated finding."}, {"id": 929, "domain": "endocrine_system", "question": "He has been walking daily as part of his cardiac rehabilitation program and has otherwise felt well since his hospitalization. He is concerned that continuing the medication might cause more serious muscle damage and wonders whether he should simply stop taking it on his own. A 61-year-old man started on high-intensity atorvastatin two months ago for secondary prevention after a myocardial infarction now reports diffuse muscle aching in both thighs without weakness. His creatine kinase is mildly elevated at 1.5 times the upper limit of normal. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?", "options": {"A": "Discontinue his statin therapy for now, given the mild elevation in creatine kinase noted here", "B": "Continue the statin at the same or a reduced dose while monitoring symptoms and creatine kinase", "C": "Switch him instead to a fibrate as monotherapy for ongoing cardiovascular risk reduction going forward", "D": "Order an urgent electromyogram now to evaluate for a separate, unrelated primary myopathy"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Continue the statin at the same or a reduced dose while monitoring symptoms and creatine kinase): Mild statin-associated myalgia with only modestly elevated creatine kinase, in the absence of significant weakness or a markedly elevated creatine kinase suggesting rhabdomyolysis, is generally managed by continuing the statin at the same or a reduced dose, or trying an alternative statin, given the substantial secondary prevention benefit in a patient with recent myocardial infarction. Permanently stopping all statin therapy would remove a critical secondary prevention medication, a fibrate alone is not an adequate substitute for LDL-lowering secondary prevention, and an electromyogram is not the appropriate first step for this common, generally benign presentation."}, {"id": 931, "domain": "pregnancy_childbirth_and_the_puerperium", "question": "A 24-year-old woman, gravida 1 para 0, presents for her first prenatal visit at what she believes is 9 weeks gestation based on her last menstrual period. She reports that her cycles have always been irregular, ranging from 24 to 45 days, and she is not certain of the exact date of conception, though she recalls unprotected intercourse spanning several weeks around that time. A transvaginal ultrasound is performed, which measures the crown-rump length and estimates the gestational age at 11 weeks, a discrepancy of two weeks from her stated menstrual dates. Her vital signs are normal and there is no vaginal bleeding. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step regarding pregnancy dating for this patient?", "options": {"A": "Continue to use the last menstrual period date since patient-reported history should generally take priority", "B": "Redate the pregnancy to the ultrasound estimate since it is more accurate than uncertain menstrual dates", "C": "Average the last menstrual period date and the ultrasound date to obtain a compromise due date", "D": "Repeat the ultrasound in four weeks before assigning any estimated due date to this pregnancy"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Redate the pregnancy to the ultrasound estimate since it is more accurate than uncertain menstrual dates): a first-trimester crown-rump length measurement is the single most accurate method available for dating a pregnancy, more accurate than even a certain last menstrual period, and is especially preferred when cycles are irregular and the exact date of conception is unclear. When the ultrasound estimate differs from menstrual dating by more than about one week in the first trimester, the pregnancy should be redated to the ultrasound estimate. Option A is wrong because irregular cycles and uncertain timing of conception make menstrual dating unreliable here. Option C describes an arbitrary averaging method with no basis in obstetric dating standards. Option D unnecessarily delays establishing a due date that guides all subsequent screening and management timelines."}, {"id": 932, "domain": "pregnancy_childbirth_and_the_puerperium", "question": "A 38-year-old woman, gravida 2 para 1, presents at 10 weeks gestation for genetic counseling regarding aneuploidy screening options. She explains that she wants the single most sensitive noninvasive test currently available and states she is willing to accept that a positive result would still require a separate confirmatory diagnostic test before she would make any decisions about the pregnancy. Her medical history is unremarkable, her prior pregnancy was uncomplicated, and she has no known family history of chromosomal abnormalities. Dating by an early ultrasound performed today is consistent with her stated gestational age. Which of the following screening tests should be recommended first given her stated priorities and current gestational age?", "options": {"A": "Cell-free fetal DNA testing from a maternal blood sample drawn as early as 9 to 10 weeks", "B": "The quadruple screen measuring alpha-fetoprotein, hCG, estriol, and inhibin A at 16 weeks", "C": "Chorionic villus sampling performed directly at 11 to 13 weeks without prior noninvasive screening", "D": "A repeat transvaginal ultrasound for nuchal translucency alone without any serum analyte testing"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Cell-free fetal DNA testing from a maternal blood sample drawn as early as 9 to 10 weeks): cell-free fetal DNA testing is the most sensitive noninvasive screening method currently available for trisomy 21, 18, and 13, and can be drawn as early as 9 to 10 weeks gestation, matching both her stated priority for sensitivity and her current gestational age. It remains a screening test, so any positive result still requires diagnostic confirmation with chorionic villus sampling or amniocentesis, consistent with her stated willingness to accept that step. Option B describes a second-trimester test that is less sensitive and typically used when a patient presents too late for earlier screening. Option C is an invasive diagnostic procedure carrying a small procedural miscarriage risk and is not first-line before noninvasive screening. Option D alone, without serum analytes, is a less sensitive combined first-trimester approach than the test she has requested."}, {"id": 933, "domain": "pregnancy_childbirth_and_the_puerperium", "question": "A 32-year-old woman, gravida 3 para 2, has a positive rectovaginal culture for Group B Streptococcus obtained during a routine screening swab performed at her 37-week prenatal visit, which was documented clearly in her chart along with a note confirming she has no penicillin allergy. She is not in labor at that visit and is sent home with reassurance. Two weeks later she returns to the labor unit reporting regular painful contractions occurring every four minutes for the past three hours, and on examination her cervix is dilated to 4 cm with intact membranes and a reassuring fetal heart rate tracing. Which of the following management steps is most appropriate at this time given her earlier culture result?", "options": {"A": "Administer oral amoxicillin now and continue it throughout the remainder of active labor", "B": "Withhold antibiotics unless the newborn develops signs of sepsis after delivery occurs", "C": "Begin intravenous penicillin now and continue dosing at intervals throughout active labor", "D": "Delay any antibiotic administration until sometime after the placenta has been delivered."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Begin intravenous penicillin now and continue dosing at intervals throughout active labor): a positive Group B Streptococcus culture mandates intrapartum antibiotic prophylaxis once labor begins, and intravenous penicillin is the preferred first-line agent given her documented lack of allergy, continued at intervals through active labor to reduce vertical transmission and early-onset neonatal sepsis. Option A is wrong because oral dosing does not achieve adequate intrapartum drug levels for this indication. Option B is wrong because prophylaxis exists specifically to prevent neonatal sepsis before it can occur, not to treat it reactively afterward. Option D defeats the purpose of prophylaxis entirely, since protective drug levels must already be present in maternal and fetal circulation before delivery occurs."}, {"id": 934, "domain": "pregnancy_childbirth_and_the_puerperium", "question": "A 27-year-old woman, gravida 1 para 0, undergoes a quadruple screen at 16 weeks gestation that returns a markedly elevated maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein level more than twice the median for her gestational age, while the other three analytes, hCG, estriol, and inhibin A, are all within normal limits for her stated dates. Her dating was confirmed accurate by a first-trimester ultrasound performed at 12 weeks, and she reports no vaginal bleeding, no family history of neural tube defects, and normal fetal movement so far. She asks what this result means and what should happen next in her prenatal care. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in her evaluation?", "options": {"A": "Reassure the patient that an isolated elevated result requires no further evaluation or imaging", "B": "Proceed directly to amniocentesis for karyotype without any additional ultrasound evaluation first", "C": "Repeat the quadruple screen serum analytes in two weeks before considering any imaging study", "D": "Obtain a detailed anatomy ultrasound to evaluate for a neural tube or abdominal wall defect"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Obtain a detailed anatomy ultrasound to evaluate for a neural tube or abdominal wall defect): an isolated elevated maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein, once dating is confirmed accurate as it is here, should prompt a detailed anatomy ultrasound to look specifically for a neural tube defect or an abdominal wall defect such as gastroschisis or omphalocele before any invasive testing is considered. Option A is wrong because an elevated result of this magnitude carries real structural risk that ultrasound can often clarify directly and noninvasively. Option B skips the noninvasive imaging step that frequently explains the finding without any procedural risk to the pregnancy. Option C simply delays a structural evaluation that should occur promptly given the abnormal result already obtained."}, {"id": 935, "domain": "pregnancy_childbirth_and_the_puerperium", "question": "A 33-year-old woman, gravida 2 para 1, with a documented history of severe preeclampsia requiring delivery at 33 weeks in her prior pregnancy is now 14 weeks pregnant with her second child. She has no other chronic medical conditions, her body mass index is within the normal range, and she has not yet started any preventive medication this pregnancy. Her blood pressure today at this visit is 118/74 mm Hg and her urine protein on dipstick is negative. She asks whether anything can be done to lower her risk of developing preeclampsia again given what happened last time. Which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation at this visit?", "options": {"A": "Start low-dose aspirin now, since it was not yet started and she has one high-risk factor", "B": "Reassure her that a single prior episode of preeclampsia has little effect on her risk this pregnancy", "C": "Begin scheduled oral labetalol now even though her blood pressure today is within the normal range", "D": "Defer preventive therapy until she develops proteinuria or an elevated blood pressure herself"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Start low-dose aspirin now, since it was not yet started and she has one high-risk factor): a prior history of preeclampsia is a single high-risk factor that alone justifies starting low-dose aspirin between 12 and 28 weeks, ideally before 16 weeks, to reduce the risk of recurrent preeclampsia, and at 14 weeks she still falls within that recommended window. Option B is wrong because prior preeclampsia, particularly severe disease requiring early delivery, meaningfully raises recurrence risk in a subsequent pregnancy. Option C is inappropriate since scheduled antihypertensive therapy is not indicated for a patient with entirely normal blood pressure today. Option D misses the preventive window entirely, since aspirin's benefit depends on starting before disease onset rather than after warning signs appear."}, {"id": 936, "domain": "pregnancy_childbirth_and_the_puerperium", "question": "A 29-year-old woman, gravida 1 para 0, is found at a routine visit at 26 weeks gestation to have a blood pressure of 148/94 mm Hg, confirmed on a second reading four hours later at 146/92 mm Hg. She has no prior history of hypertension, and her blood pressure recorded at her first prenatal visit at 9 weeks was 112/70 mm Hg. Today's urine protein-to-creatinine ratio is 0.1, well below the threshold for significant proteinuria, and she denies headache, visual changes, or right upper quadrant pain. Laboratory studies including platelet count and liver enzymes are within normal limits. Which of the following diagnoses best fits this presentation?", "options": {"A": "Chronic hypertension, since the blood pressure elevation is sustained on repeated measurement at the time this reading was taken", "B": "Gestational hypertension, since it is new-onset after 20 weeks without proteinuria or severity features", "C": "Preeclampsia with severe features, based on the degree of blood pressure elevation alone", "D": "Eclampsia, since any new hypertension in pregnancy carries an equivalent risk of seizure"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Gestational hypertension, since it is new-onset after 20 weeks without proteinuria or severity features): gestational hypertension is defined as new-onset blood pressure of 140/90 mm Hg or higher after 20 weeks gestation, in a patient without prior hypertension documented earlier in pregnancy, and without proteinuria or other features of preeclampsia, which matches this presentation exactly given her normal early blood pressure and normal current labs. Option A is wrong because chronic hypertension requires elevated pressure documented before 20 weeks, which was clearly not the case at her 9-week visit. Option C requires either significant proteinuria or a defined severe feature, neither of which is present in her current evaluation. Option D is a seizure disorder superimposed on preeclampsia and does not apply here at all."}, {"id": 937, "domain": "pregnancy_childbirth_and_the_puerperium", "question": "A 31-year-old woman, gravida 1 para 0, at 33 weeks gestation with a two-week history of new hypertension and 2+ proteinuria on dipstick is admitted to the obstetric unit for further evaluation and observation. Her admission blood pressure is 168/112 mm Hg, and a repeat measurement four hours later remains elevated at 166/114 mm Hg. Laboratory studies drawn on admission show a platelet count of 88,000 per microliter, with normal liver transaminases and a normal serum creatinine. She reports no headache, no visual disturbance, and no right upper quadrant pain, and the fetal heart rate tracing is reassuring throughout observation. Which finding most clearly establishes that she has preeclampsia with severe features?", "options": {"A": "The presence of any proteinuria in a patient with new-onset hypertension after 20 weeks", "B": "Gestational age beyond 32 weeks in a patient with any degree of new hypertension present", "C": "The absence of headache or visual disturbance despite a diagnosis of preeclampsia being made", "D": "Severe-range blood pressure of 168/112 mm Hg together with a platelet count below 100,000"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Severe-range blood pressure of 168/112 mm Hg together with a platelet count below 100,000): severe features of preeclampsia include severe-range blood pressure, systolic 160 or higher or diastolic 110 or higher on two occasions at least four hours apart, and thrombocytopenia with a platelet count below 100,000 per microliter, both of which are clearly documented here on two separate readings and independently qualify as severe features. Option A describes the baseline definition of preeclampsia itself rather than a marker of severity. Option B is incorrect because gestational age alone, independent of blood pressure or laboratory findings, does not define disease severity. Option C is a distractor, since the absence of headache does not rule out severe disease once other severe criteria have already been met on laboratory testing."}, {"id": 938, "domain": "pregnancy_childbirth_and_the_puerperium", "question": "A 30-year-old woman with preeclampsia with severe features at 35 weeks gestation is started on an intravenous magnesium sulfate infusion for seizure prophylaxis after admission to the labor unit. Two hours into the infusion, the bedside nurse performs a routine neurologic check and reports that her patellar deep tendon reflexes are now completely absent bilaterally, though her respiratory rate remains normal at 16 breaths per minute, her oxygen saturation is 98% on room air, and she remains fully alert and oriented. Her urine output over the last hour has also been adequate. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in her management at this time?", "options": {"A": "Continue the infusion at the same rate since respiratory rate and mental status remain normal", "B": "Increase the magnesium infusion rate to ensure therapeutic seizure prophylaxis is being achieved", "C": "Stop the magnesium infusion and prepare to administer calcium gluconate if toxicity progresses", "D": "Switch immediately from magnesium sulfate to phenytoin for ongoing seizure prophylaxis instead"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stop the magnesium infusion and prepare to administer calcium gluconate if toxicity progresses): magnesium toxicity progresses in a predictable, ascending fashion, with loss of deep tendon reflexes occurring before respiratory depression and cardiac arrest at progressively higher serum magnesium levels, so the absent reflexes found on this examination are an early warning sign that should prompt stopping the infusion and preparing calcium gluconate, the antidote, even though her respiratory status and mental status remain normal for now. Option A dangerously continues an infusion despite a clear early sign of toxicity already documented. Option B would further worsen toxicity rather than improve seizure prophylaxis. Option D is unnecessary, since magnesium sulfate remains first-line therapy and simply needs to be held and reassessed rather than replaced with a different agent."}, {"id": 939, "domain": "pregnancy_childbirth_and_the_puerperium", "question": "A 34-year-old woman at 35 weeks gestation with preeclampsia with severe features has a blood pressure of 172/118 mm Hg confirmed on a repeat measurement obtained fifteen minutes after the first elevated reading. She has a peripheral intravenous line already in place from admission, reports no known drug allergies, and has no history of asthma or reactive airway disease. Her fetal heart rate tracing remains reassuring, and the obstetric team wants to lower her blood pressure promptly to reduce the risk of maternal stroke or other end-organ injury while further management is arranged. Which medication is most appropriate for acute treatment of this severe-range blood pressure right now?", "options": {"A": "Oral lisinopril given immediately to lower blood pressure into a safe range quickly", "B": "Intravenous labetalol given as a first-line agent for acute severe hypertension in pregnancy", "C": "Oral losartan started now with a plan to titrate the dose over the next several days", "D": "Intravenous furosemide given as the preferred first agent for this degree of hypertension"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Intravenous labetalol given as a first-line agent for acute severe hypertension in pregnancy): intravenous labetalol is one of the first-line agents, along with intravenous hydralazine and oral immediate-release nifedipine, for acute treatment of severe-range hypertension in pregnancy, and she has an intravenous line already available for its administration. Option A and option C are both incorrect because ACE inhibitors and angiotensin receptor blockers are contraindicated throughout pregnancy due to fetal renal toxicity, regardless of the intended route or titration schedule described. Option D is a diuretic rather than an antihypertensive and is not an appropriate first-line agent for acute severe hypertension in this clinical setting."}, {"id": 945, "domain": "pregnancy_childbirth_and_the_puerperium", "question": "A 28-year-old woman recently diagnosed with gestational diabetes at 27 weeks gestation has completed two full weeks of dietary modification with a registered dietitian and moderate daily exercise as recommended. She has been performing home glucose monitoring four times daily as instructed, and her logbook shows a fasting glucose consistently around 104 to 108 mg/dL each morning despite reporting good adherence to her prescribed meal plan and exercise routine. Her two-hour postprandial values, in contrast, have generally remained within the target range recommended by her obstetric provider. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in her management given this pattern?", "options": {"A": "Continue lifestyle measures alone for another month before considering any medication change", "B": "Add pharmacologic therapy, with insulin generally preferred as the first-line agent", "C": "Switch her diet plan to a higher carbohydrate composition to reduce fasting ketosis risk", "D": "Reassure her that a fasting glucose in this range remains within acceptable targets"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Add pharmacologic therapy, with insulin generally preferred as the first-line agent): when glycemic targets, generally a fasting glucose under 95 mg/dL, are not met despite adequate, well-documented lifestyle measures as in this case, pharmacologic therapy should be added, and insulin is generally preferred as first-line treatment because it does not cross the placenta in clinically significant amounts. Option A delays needed treatment when her fasting targets are clearly and consistently not being met over a full two-week trial. Option C is not an appropriate dietary strategy for improving glycemic control in gestational diabetes. Option D is incorrect since her fasting values consistently exceed the accepted target threshold for adequate control."}, {"id": 946, "domain": "pregnancy_childbirth_and_the_puerperium", "question": "A 35-year-old woman with well-documented gestational diabetes that was first diagnosed at 27 weeks gestation, after entirely normal glucose screening earlier in the pregnancy, is now at 34 weeks and being followed with twice-weekly antenatal testing for fetal growth and well-being. Her glucose control has been reasonably well managed with insulin since diagnosis. During a counseling visit, she specifically asks whether her baby is at increased risk for congenital heart defects because of her gestational diabetes diagnosis, since a close friend's baby was born with a cardiac anomaly related to diabetes in pregnancy. Which of the following statements is the most accurate response to her specific question?", "options": {"A": "Yes, gestational diabetes carries the same anomaly risk as pregestational diabetes with poor control", "B": "Yes, but only if her glucose remains poorly controlled throughout the third trimester of pregnancy", "C": "No, but her baby faces significantly increased risk of neural tube defects instead of cardiac ones", "D": "No, since gestational diabetes develops after the period of organogenesis is already complete"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (No, since gestational diabetes develops after the period of organogenesis is already complete): gestational diabetes typically develops or is first recognized in the late second trimester, well after the period of organogenesis is complete, so unlike pregestational diabetes with poor periconceptional control, it does not raise the risk of congenital anomalies such as cardiac defects or neural tube defects, and her documented normal earlier screening supports this timeline. Option A incorrectly equates gestational diabetes risk with the distinct risk profile of pregestational diabetes. Option B incorrectly ties anomaly risk to third-trimester control rather than to the timing of disease onset relative to organogenesis. Option C incorrectly attributes an anomaly risk that is specific to poorly controlled pregestational diabetes around conception."}, {"id": 947, "domain": "pregnancy_childbirth_and_the_puerperium", "question": "A 31-year-old woman with gestational diabetes that was managed successfully with diet and exercise alone throughout her pregnancy, never requiring pharmacologic therapy, delivers a healthy, appropriately grown infant vaginally at 39 weeks without complication. She returns for her scheduled postpartum visit six weeks after delivery, feeling well overall and breastfeeding successfully, and asks whether any additional testing related to her gestational diabetes diagnosis is recommended now that the pregnancy has ended and her glucose readings at home have returned to a normal range. Which of the following is the most appropriate recommended test to evaluate her glucose status at this visit?", "options": {"A": "A repeat 50 gram one-hour glucose challenge test identical to her prenatal screening test identical to the one used earlier in her prenatal course", "B": "No further glucose testing is needed since her gestational diabetes resolved at delivery", "C": "A 75 gram two-hour oral glucose tolerance test to confirm resolution of glucose intolerance", "D": "A random glucose level drawn at any time without regard to fasting status or timing"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A 75 gram two-hour oral glucose tolerance test to confirm resolution of glucose intolerance): all patients with gestational diabetes should undergo a 75 gram two-hour oral glucose tolerance test at 6 to 12 weeks postpartum to confirm resolution of glucose intolerance, since gestational diabetes substantially raises lifetime risk of developing type 2 diabetes and ongoing surveillance is warranted regardless of how well controlled the pregnancy itself was. Option A uses the wrong test entirely, since that screening protocol is designed for use during pregnancy rather than for postpartum evaluation. Option B incorrectly assumes permanent resolution without any confirmatory testing being performed. Option D is not sufficiently standardized or sensitive for this specific postpartum purpose compared with the recommended oral glucose tolerance test."}, {"id": 948, "domain": "pregnancy_childbirth_and_the_puerperium", "question": "A 29-year-old woman with a ten-year history of type 2 diabetes managed with metformin and a hemoglobin A1c of 9.8% at the time she discovers she is 6 weeks pregnant is referred urgently for preconception-style counseling now that the pregnancy is already underway. Her diabetes has been poorly controlled for the past year according to her primary care records, and she is counseled extensively about risks to the developing fetus given both her degree of hyperglycemia and the very early gestational age at which it is occurring. Which of the following complications is she at meaningfully increased risk for that would not apply equally to a patient who instead develops diabetes for the first time later in this same pregnancy?", "options": {"A": "Fetal macrosomia related to fetal hyperinsulinemia from maternal hyperglycemia exposure", "B": "Neonatal hypoglycemia occurring in the newborn period after umbilical cord clamping occurs", "C": "Polyhydramnios detected on routine third-trimester ultrasound assessment of amniotic fluid", "D": "Congenital cardiac and neural tube defects related to hyperglycemia during organogenesis"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Congenital cardiac and neural tube defects related to hyperglycemia during organogenesis): poor glycemic control around the time of conception and during early organogenesis, exactly as described in this patient with an elevated hemoglobin A1c at 6 weeks, raises the risk of congenital anomalies including cardiac defects and neural tube defects, a risk that gestational diabetes developing later in pregnancy does not share since organogenesis is already complete by the time it typically appears. Options A, B, and C describe complications that can occur with either pregestational or gestational diabetes when glycemic control is poor later in pregnancy, and none of them distinguish the two conditions the way early anomaly risk clearly does in this case."}, {"id": 949, "domain": "pregnancy_childbirth_and_the_puerperium", "question": "A 30-year-old woman, gravida 3 para 1, with a documented history of pelvic inflammatory disease treated five years ago and a prior right-sided salpingectomy performed for an ectopic pregnancy two years ago, presents to the clinic with six weeks of amenorrhea, new right-sided pelvic pain that began yesterday, and light vaginal spotting overnight. Her vital signs are stable and her abdominal examination reveals mild right lower quadrant tenderness without rebound or guarding. A urine pregnancy test performed in clinic is positive. The physician reviewing her chart wants to identify which single historical factor most strongly increases her risk for another ectopic pregnancy this cycle. Which of the following best answers that question?", "options": {"A": "Her prior history of tubal surgery and pelvic inflammatory disease affecting the fallopian tubes", "B": "Her current age of 30 years, which is considered an independent risk factor by itself", "C": "The presence of light vaginal spotting alone, apart from any other historical risk factor", "D": "A family history of ectopic pregnancy occurring in a first-degree relative many years ago"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Her prior history of tubal surgery and pelvic inflammatory disease affecting the fallopian tubes): prior tubal surgery and prior pelvic inflammatory disease are well-established risk factors for ectopic pregnancy because both processes can distort or scar the fallopian tube, impairing normal transport of the fertilized egg into the uterine cavity, and this patient has both risk factors clearly documented in her history. Option B is incorrect because age 30 alone is not considered a major independent risk factor for ectopic pregnancy in the way tubal disease is. Option C describes a presenting symptom rather than an underlying risk factor for developing the condition itself. Option D is a comparatively weak and nonspecific historical detail when set against her own well-documented tubal disease and prior ectopic pregnancy."}, {"id": 951, "domain": "pregnancy_childbirth_and_the_puerperium", "question": "A 27-year-old hemodynamically stable woman is diagnosed with an unruptured right adnexal ectopic pregnancy measuring 2.5 cm on transvaginal ultrasound, with no fetal cardiac activity identified and a quantitative beta-hCG of 3,200 mIU/mL. Her medical history is reviewed carefully and reveals no known liver or renal disease, no current breastfeeding since this is her first pregnancy, no peptic ulcer disease, and no known immunodeficiency. She strongly prefers to avoid surgery if a reasonable alternative exists and is counseled in detail about her treatment options for this diagnosis. Which of the following is the most appropriate management option to offer her at this time?", "options": {"A": "Emergency exploratory laparotomy, since surgery is generally the preferred approach for ectopic pregnancy", "B": "Expectant management alone with no medical or surgical treatment offered at this time", "C": "Immediate salpingectomy performed laparoscopically as the preferred approach in this clinical scenario", "D": "Methotrexate therapy, since she meets standard criteria for medical management of ectopic pregnancy"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Methotrexate therapy, since she meets standard criteria for medical management of ectopic pregnancy): methotrexate is appropriate for a hemodynamically stable patient with a confirmed, unruptured ectopic pregnancy who meets criteria including no fetal cardiac activity, hCG generally below about 5,000 mIU/mL, mass size under about 3.5 to 4 cm, and no contraindication such as hepatic or renal disease, breastfeeding, or peptic ulcer disease, all of which this patient clearly satisfies. Option A is unnecessarily invasive when medical management is appropriate, available, and preferred by the patient herself. Option B is inadequate, since an ectopic pregnancy still requires active treatment even when the patient is currently hemodynamically stable. Option C is more invasive than necessary given that she fully qualifies for medical therapy instead."}, {"id": 953, "domain": "pregnancy_childbirth_and_the_puerperium", "question": "A 28-year-old woman, gravida 2 para 1, at 9 weeks gestation by her reliable last menstrual period presents for a routine prenatal visit with no complaints of vaginal bleeding, cramping, or fluid leakage, and reports that early pregnancy symptoms such as nausea have actually improved over the past several days. A transvaginal ultrasound performed at this visit reveals a fetal pole measuring consistent with only 8 weeks gestation, with no fetal cardiac activity detected despite careful scanning, and the cervical os is closed and firm on bimanual examination. She is understandably distressed by this unexpected finding. Which term best describes this ultrasound and examination finding?", "options": {"A": "Threatened abortion, since the cervical os remains closed on her pelvic examination today", "B": "Incomplete abortion, since some portion of the pregnancy tissue is retained within the uterus", "C": "Missed abortion, since there is fetal demise without expulsion of tissue or cervical dilation", "D": "Inevitable abortion, since the cervical os would be expected to be open in this situation"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Missed abortion, since there is fetal demise without expulsion of tissue or cervical dilation): a missed abortion describes fetal demise without expulsion of tissue and without cervical dilation, often discovered incidentally exactly as in this asymptomatic patient with no cardiac activity detected and a closed, firm cervix on examination. Option A requires ongoing bleeding in the setting of a viable pregnancy, which does not match a demised fetus with no cardiac activity. Option B requires an open cervical os with some tissue already expelled, neither of which is described in this case. Option D requires an open cervical os as well, whereas this patient's cervix remains closed throughout her examination."}, {"id": 954, "domain": "pregnancy_childbirth_and_the_puerperium", "question": "A 34-year-old woman with regular, predictable menstrual cycles and no prior successful pregnancies has now had three consecutive first-trimester pregnancy losses over the past two years, each confirmed by ultrasound after an initial period of amenorrhea followed by documented loss of previously present fetal cardiac activity. She and her partner have both been otherwise healthy, and no prior evaluation has ever been performed despite these repeated losses. She is referred to a reproductive endocrinology clinic specifically to begin an evaluation for recurrent pregnancy loss before attempting conception again. Which of the following should be included as part of her initial recommended evaluation?", "options": {"A": "Testing for antiphospholipid syndrome along with evaluation of uterine anatomy and parental karyotype", "B": "Reassurance alone, since three losses in a row remains within the range of normal variation", "C": "Immediate recommendation for donor egg use without any further diagnostic evaluation performed before any further attempt at conception is made", "D": "A single repeat pregnancy attempt with no additional testing recommended before conception"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Testing for antiphospholipid syndrome along with evaluation of uterine anatomy and parental karyotype): recurrent pregnancy loss, generally defined as two or more consecutive clinical losses, prompts evaluation for antiphospholipid syndrome, uterine anatomic abnormalities, parental chromosomal translocations, and uncontrolled thyroid or diabetic disease, all of which are reasonable and standard components of a thorough workup for this patient given her history. Option B understates the clinical significance of three consecutive losses, a pattern that clearly warrants formal evaluation rather than reassurance alone. Option C is a premature recommendation made without first completing a standard diagnostic evaluation that might identify a treatable cause. Option D skips an evaluation that could identify a modifiable cause before she and her partner attempt another pregnancy."}, {"id": 959, "domain": "pregnancy_childbirth_and_the_puerperium", "question": "During active labor at 39 weeks gestation, continuous fetal heart rate monitoring shows a deceleration pattern with abrupt onset and abrupt offset that varies unpredictably in timing relative to uterine contractions, sometimes beginning well before a contraction starts and sometimes during its peak, occurring intermittently across the entire hour of monitoring reviewed by the resident physician. Baseline variability remains moderate between episodes, and the overall baseline rate stays within the normal range throughout. Which of the following mechanisms best explains this specific deceleration pattern observed on the tracing?", "options": {"A": "Fetal head compression occurring as the presenting part descends through the birth canal", "B": "Uteroplacental insufficiency reducing oxygen delivery to the fetus during each contraction", "C": "Maternal hypotension from epidural anesthesia reducing overall uterine blood flow supply", "D": "Umbilical cord compression producing an abrupt, variably timed deceleration pattern"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Umbilical cord compression producing an abrupt, variably timed deceleration pattern): variable decelerations have an abrupt onset and abrupt offset that are unrelated in timing to the uterine contraction, exactly as described here, and they reflect umbilical cord compression, making them the most common deceleration pattern seen during labor. Option A describes the mechanism of early decelerations, which mirror the contraction in a gradual, uniform pattern rather than the abrupt, variably timed pattern described. Option B describes the mechanism of late decelerations, which begin consistently after the contraction peaks rather than varying independently in timing as this pattern does. Option C could theoretically contribute to late decelerations but does not explain this specific abrupt, variably timed pattern seen on the tracing."}, {"id": 960, "domain": "pregnancy_childbirth_and_the_puerperium", "question": "A 22-year-old woman at 6 cm cervical dilation with ruptured membranes for the past four hours has had no further cervical change over that same four-hour period despite documented adequate uterine contractions confirmed by an intrauterine pressure catheter placed earlier in labor. The fetal heart rate tracing has remained reassuring, category I, throughout this entire period of observation, and the patient herself is tolerating labor well without excessive fatigue. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in her management given this labor pattern?", "options": {"A": "Proceed to cesarean delivery for probable cephalopelvic disproportion without further observation of her labor", "B": "Reassure her that this labor pattern is a normal variant and requires little additional intervention at this time", "C": "Continue labor and reassess, since adequate contractions with no progress for four hours defines arrest", "D": "Begin oxytocin augmentation immediately since her contractions are documented as inadequate"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Continue labor and reassess, since adequate contractions with no progress for four hours defines arrest): an arrest disorder in the active phase is generally defined as no cervical change for four hours with adequate contractions already confirmed by intrauterine pressure catheter, which this patient clearly meets, and the most common underlying cause is still addressed through continued observation and reassessment as management proceeds toward considering cesarean delivery only if arrest truly persists further, since cephalopelvic disproportion remains a diagnosis of exclusion. Option A moves to a diagnosis of exclusion prematurely without allowing further reassessment of this labor pattern. Option B dismisses a labor pattern that already meets formal criteria for arrest given the documented findings. Option D incorrectly assumes her contractions are inadequate when they have already been confirmed as adequate by pressure catheter."}, {"id": 961, "domain": "pregnancy_childbirth_and_the_puerperium", "question": "A 25-year-old woman, gravida 2 para 1, at 29 weeks gestation presents to the labor unit with regular painful contractions occurring every three minutes for the past two hours, and cervical examination on arrival shows dilation that has progressed from 1 cm to 3 cm over the two hours of observation since her arrival. Fetal heart rate tracing is reassuring throughout, her temperature is normal, white blood cell count is within normal limits, and there is no vaginal bleeding or evidence of ruptured membranes on sterile speculum examination. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate management for this patient given her gestational age and clinical picture?", "options": {"A": "Delivery now, since preterm labor at this gestational age is generally difficult to delay safely", "B": "Tocolysis to delay delivery 48 hours along with antenatal corticosteroids and magnesium sulfate", "C": "Discharge home on strict bed rest with no medication given at this gestational age", "D": "Prophylactic antibiotics alone with no tocolytic or corticosteroid therapy given at this time"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tocolysis to delay delivery 48 hours along with antenatal corticosteroids and magnesium sulfate): preterm labor with confirmed cervical change, exactly as documented in this patient over the observation period, is managed with tocolysis to delay delivery for 48 hours, allowing time for antenatal corticosteroids to promote fetal lung maturity and, since she is under 32 weeks gestation, magnesium sulfate for fetal neuroprotection as well. Option A is incorrect because tocolysis and corticosteroid administration are specifically intended to safely delay delivery when appropriate and no contraindication is present. Option C fails to offer proven interventions shown to improve neonatal outcomes at this early gestational age. Option D omits both tocolysis and corticosteroids, both of which are indicated here in the absence of infection or another contraindication."}, {"id": 963, "domain": "pregnancy_childbirth_and_the_puerperium", "question": "Immediately after delivery of the fetal head during an otherwise routine vaginal delivery at term, the fetal anterior shoulder becomes visibly impacted behind the maternal pubic symphysis, and gentle downward traction by the delivering physician fails to bring about delivery of the shoulder over the following several seconds. The fetal head has retracted slightly against the perineum, a finding sometimes described as the turtle sign, and the delivery team recognizes this as an obstetric emergency requiring immediate action. Which of the following is the most appropriate first-line maneuver to attempt at this point?", "options": {"A": "Firm fundal pressure applied by an assistant to help push the shoulder past the symphysis by the assisting provider at the bedside", "B": "Immediate cesarean delivery performed after the fetal head has already been delivered", "C": "The McRoberts maneuver, hyperflexing the maternal hips, combined with suprapubic pressure", "D": "Continued firm downward traction on the fetal head until the shoulder is delivered"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (The McRoberts maneuver, hyperflexing the maternal hips, combined with suprapubic pressure): the McRoberts maneuver, which hyperflexes the maternal hips sharply against the abdomen, combined with suprapubic pressure applied by an assistant, is the appropriate first-line approach to shoulder dystocia, and additional maneuvers such as delivery of the posterior arm are reserved for cases where this initial approach fails. Option A is specifically discouraged in this scenario, since fundal pressure actually worsens impaction of the shoulder behind the maternal symphysis. Option B is not feasible once the fetal head has already delivered, as described here. Option D risks excessive traction injury to the fetal brachial plexus and is not an appropriate management step for this obstetric emergency."}, {"id": 964, "domain": "pregnancy_childbirth_and_the_puerperium", "question": "A 33-year-old woman with a documented positive Group B Streptococcus culture obtained at her 36-week prenatal visit presents in spontaneous active labor at 39 weeks with cervical dilation of 5 cm and intact membranes. On careful review of her allergy history, she reports a penicillin allergy described only as a mild, nonpruritic rash that occurred many years ago in childhood, without any features suggestive of anaphylaxis, angioedema, or respiratory distress at that time. Her labor is progressing normally and the fetal heart rate tracing remains reassuring. Which of the following is the most appropriate approach to intrapartum antibiotic prophylaxis for this patient?", "options": {"A": "Withhold antibiotics, reasoning that a documented penicillin allergy would make prophylaxis unsafe for her in this situation.", "B": "Administer an appropriate antibiotic regimen for prophylaxis, since GBS status still requires treatment", "C": "Delay any antibiotic administration until after spontaneous rupture of membranes actually occurs", "D": "Administer prophylaxis only if she develops a fever during the remainder of her labor course"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Administer an appropriate antibiotic regimen for prophylaxis, since GBS status still requires treatment): intrapartum antibiotic prophylaxis remains indicated for a documented positive Group B Streptococcus culture regardless of current membrane status, and a reported low-risk penicillin allergy history such as hers, without features of anaphylaxis, does not eliminate the need for prophylaxis, since an appropriate alternative or modified regimen can be selected based on allergy severity. Option A incorrectly assumes that any reported penicillin allergy history precludes all antibiotic prophylaxis regardless of its severity. Option C incorrectly ties the timing of prophylaxis to membrane rupture rather than to the onset of active labor itself, which has already begun. Option D incorrectly reserves treatment for a reactive indication rather than the established preventive indication already present."}, {"id": 966, "domain": "pregnancy_childbirth_and_the_puerperium", "question": "A 29-year-old woman who delivered her first child four days ago reports to the visiting postpartum nurse that she has been feeling tearful at times, mildly anxious about caring for the baby, and generally emotionally up and down, crying during one feeding and laughing normally shortly afterward. Despite these mood swings, she continues to care for her infant appropriately, feeds and bonds with the baby without difficulty, sleeps reasonably well between nighttime feedings, and clearly denies any thoughts of harming herself or her infant when asked directly. Her partner confirms these observations independently. Which of the following best describes her presentation at this point in the postpartum period?", "options": {"A": "The baby blues, a common, self-limited condition expected to resolve within about two weeks", "B": "Postpartum depression, requiring immediate initiation of an SSRI for symptom control", "C": "Postpartum psychosis, requiring immediate psychiatric hospitalization given her presentation", "D": "Major depressive disorder unrelated to pregnancy that happens to have begun after delivery"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The baby blues, a common, self-limited condition expected to resolve within about two weeks): the baby blues affect the large majority of new mothers, begin within the first few days postpartum exactly as described here, and resolve spontaneously within about two weeks, characterized by mild mood lability and tearfulness without any impairment in her ability to function or care for her infant. Option B requires symptoms persisting beyond two weeks together with functional impairment, neither of which has been demonstrated in this early, still-mild presentation. Option C would require far more severe findings such as hallucinations, delusions, or disorganized behavior, none of which are present in her history or examination. Option D is not the most likely explanation for a presentation this early and this mild occurring so soon after delivery."}, {"id": 968, "domain": "pregnancy_childbirth_and_the_puerperium", "question": "A 26-year-old Rh-negative woman with a documented negative antibody screen earlier in pregnancy is now 28 weeks pregnant, and her partner, the biological father of the baby, is known to be Rh-positive. Her pregnancy has otherwise been entirely uncomplicated, with no episodes of vaginal bleeding, abdominal trauma, or any invasive procedure such as amniocentesis performed at any point so far. She presents today for her scheduled 28-week prenatal visit, and her obstetrician reviews her chart to confirm what preventive treatment, if any, should be offered at this specific visit. Which of the following is the most appropriate recommendation regarding anti-D immune globulin at this visit?", "options": {"A": "Withhold anti-D immune globulin for now since she has had no clearly identified sensitizing event this pregnancy", "B": "Administer anti-D immune globulin mainly after delivery once the newborn is confirmed Rh-positive", "C": "Administer anti-D immune globulin now at 28 weeks as routine antenatal prophylaxis", "D": "Check paternal Rh status first and give anti-D immune globulin mainly if the father is Rh-positive"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Administer anti-D immune globulin now at 28 weeks as routine antenatal prophylaxis): Rh-negative, antibody-negative patients routinely receive anti-D immune globulin at 28 weeks as standard antenatal prophylaxis, given again within 72 hours of delivery if the newborn is confirmed Rh-positive, regardless of whether an overt sensitizing event has occurred, because subclinical fetomaternal hemorrhage can happen even without any recognized clinical event. Option A incorrectly assumes prophylaxis is only needed after a recognized sensitizing event has already taken place. Option B omits the standard antenatal dose that is specifically given at 28 weeks regardless of eventual delivery timing. Option D is not the standard approach, since routine 28-week dosing is given without first requiring confirmation of paternal Rh status."}, {"id": 970, "domain": "pregnancy_childbirth_and_the_puerperium", "question": "A 27-year-old woman who delivered vaginally without any complication two weeks ago calls her obstetrician's office to ask when she should be seen next for a formal postpartum visit, since she was told at discharge from the hospital that some form of follow-up would be needed but does not remember the exact recommended timing. She reports feeling generally well overall, though she mentions some ongoing questions about resuming contraception and about breastfeeding difficulties she has been having at home. Which of the following best reflects the current recommended timing for her postpartum care given this history?", "options": {"A": "An initial contact within the first three weeks with a comprehensive visit completed by twelve weeks", "B": "A single comprehensive visit at six weeks postpartum, with no earlier contact typically recommended before then", "C": "No formal visit is needed unless she develops a specific complication or concern before then", "D": "A comprehensive visit at twelve weeks, with limited benefit expected from any earlier contact"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (An initial contact within the first three weeks with a comprehensive visit completed by twelve weeks): current recommendations favor an initial postpartum contact within the first three weeks after delivery, which would address her contraception and breastfeeding questions promptly, followed by a comprehensive visit completed by twelve weeks that covers mood, contraception planning, breastfeeding support, and resolution of any pregnancy complications. Option B reflects an older, single-visit model that has since been updated in favor of this earlier initial contact. Option C is inappropriate, since routine postpartum follow-up is recommended for all patients regardless of whether a specific complication has already developed. Option D similarly omits the benefit of earlier contact within the first three weeks that would address her current questions sooner."}, {"id": 973, "domain": "biostatistics_epidemiology_and_population_health", "question": "A screening program for a rare congenital metabolic disorder tests 1,000 newborns. The true prevalence of the disorder in this population is 5%, so 50 newborns truly have the disorder and 950 do not. Of the 50 affected newborns, 45 screen positive and 5 screen negative. Of the 950 unaffected newborns, 95 screen positive and 855 screen negative. What is the negative predictive value of this screening test?", "options": {"A": "90.0%, calculated as 45 true positives divided by the 50 newborns who truly have the disorder.", "B": "99.4%, calculated as 855 true negatives divided by the 860 newborns with a negative screen.", "C": "32.1%, calculated as 45 true positives divided by the 140 newborns with a positive screen.", "D": "90.0%, calculated as 855 true negatives divided by the 950 newborns who truly do not have the disorder."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (99.4%, calculated as 855 true negatives divided by the 860 newborns with a negative...): negative predictive value = true negatives / (true negatives + false negatives) = 855 / (855 + 5) = 855/860 = 0.994, or 99.4%. Option A restates the sensitivity, 45/(45+5), not the negative predictive value. Option C gives the positive predictive value, 45/(45+95), not the negative predictive value. Option D restates the specificity, 855/(855+95); it coincidentally also equals 90.0% here but is a different statistic from the negative predictive value."}, {"id": 976, "domain": "biostatistics_epidemiology_and_population_health", "question": "A 42-year-old woman participates in a mammography validation study. Among 400 women in the study cohort with disease status later confirmed by biopsy or 2-year follow-up, 100 women truly have breast cancer and 300 do not. Of the 100 with cancer, 80 have a positive mammogram and 20 have a negative mammogram. Of the 300 without cancer, 30 have a positive mammogram and 270 have a negative mammogram. What is the positive predictive value of mammography in this cohort?", "options": {"A": "80.0%, calculated as 80 true positives divided by the 100 women with confirmed cancer.", "B": "72.7%, calculated as 80 true positives divided by the 110 women with a positive mammogram.", "C": "90.0%, calculated as 270 true negatives divided by the 300 women without confirmed cancer.", "D": "93.1%, calculated as 270 true negatives divided by the 290 women with a negative mammogram."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (72.7%, calculated as 80 true positives divided by the 110 women with a positive mammogram.): positive predictive value = true positives / (true positives + false positives) = 80 / (80 + 30) = 80/110 = 0.727, or 72.7%. Option A gives the sensitivity, 80/(80+20), not the positive predictive value. Option C gives the specificity, 270/(270+30), not the positive predictive value. Option D gives the negative predictive value, 270/(270+20), not the positive predictive value."}, {"id": 977, "domain": "biostatistics_epidemiology_and_population_health", "question": "A clinician is deciding whether a new bedside ultrasound protocol for detecting pneumothorax is useful in a patient with a low-to-moderate pretest probability of pneumothorax. Prior validation studies report the protocol's sensitivity as 95% and its specificity as 80% for detecting pneumothorax compared with CT scan. Based on these values, what is the positive likelihood ratio of this ultrasound protocol?", "options": {"A": "0.95, equal to the sensitivity reported for the protocol.", "B": "0.06, calculated as 1 minus sensitivity divided by specificity.", "C": "4.75, calculated as sensitivity divided by 1 minus specificity.", "D": "1.19, calculated as sensitivity divided by specificity directly."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (4.75, calculated as sensitivity divided by 1 minus specificity.): the positive likelihood ratio (LR+) = sensitivity / (1 - specificity) = 0.95 / (1 - 0.80) = 0.95/0.20 = 4.75. An LR+ of 4.75 produces a moderate increase in the probability of disease when the test is positive. Option A simply restates the sensitivity rather than calculating the likelihood ratio. Option B is actually the negative likelihood ratio, (1 - 0.95)/0.80 = 0.05/0.80 = 0.0625, not the positive likelihood ratio. Option D divides sensitivity by specificity directly, which is not the formula for either likelihood ratio."}, {"id": 978, "domain": "biostatistics_epidemiology_and_population_health", "question": "A 26-year-old man arrives after a motor vehicle collision with mild chest wall tenderness but stable vital signs. The trauma team considers the same bedside ultrasound protocol for pneumothorax detection described above (sensitivity 95%, specificity 80%, compared against CT scan as the gold standard in prior validation studies). The ultrasound is performed at the bedside and the result is negative for pneumothorax. Based on the reported test characteristics of this protocol, what is the negative likelihood ratio associated with this negative ultrasound result?", "options": {"A": "4.75, calculated as sensitivity divided by 1 minus specificity.", "B": "0.20, equal to 1 minus the reported specificity.", "C": "1.19, calculated as specificity divided by sensitivity directly.", "D": "0.06, calculated as 1 minus sensitivity divided by specificity."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (0.06, calculated as 1 minus sensitivity divided by specificity.): the negative likelihood ratio (LR-) = (1 - sensitivity) / specificity = (1 - 0.95) / 0.80 = 0.05/0.80 = 0.0625, which rounds to approximately 0.06. An LR- this far below 0.1 produces a large, often conclusive decrease in the probability of disease when the test is negative. Option A is actually the positive likelihood ratio, not the negative likelihood ratio. Option B simply restates 1 minus specificity, which is not the LR- formula. Option C divides specificity by sensitivity directly, which is not a recognized likelihood ratio calculation."}, {"id": 981, "domain": "biostatistics_epidemiology_and_population_health", "question": "A case-control study investigates the association between occupational solvent exposure and bladder cancer. Investigators enroll 120 patients with confirmed bladder cancer (cases) and 200 disease-free patients (controls) matched for age and sex, then ask each participant about prior occupational solvent exposure. Among the 120 cases, 40 report solvent exposure and 80 do not. Among the 200 controls, 20 report solvent exposure and 180 do not. What is the odds ratio for the association between solvent exposure and bladder cancer in this study?", "options": {"A": "2.0, calculated as the ratio of exposed cases to exposed controls.", "B": "3.3, calculated as the simple ratio of exposure prevalence between the case group and the control group.", "C": "0.5, calculated as the inverse of the ratio of unexposed to exposed cases.", "D": "4.5, calculated as (exposed cases x unexposed controls) divided by (unexposed cases x exposed controls)."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (4.5, calculated as (exposed cases x unexposed controls) divided by (unexposed cases x...): odds ratio = (a x d) / (b x c), where a = exposed cases = 40, b = unexposed cases = 80, c = exposed controls = 20, d = unexposed controls = 180. OR = (40 x 180) / (80 x 20) = 7,200/1,600 = 4.5. The odds of solvent exposure are about 4.5 times higher among bladder cancer cases than among controls. Option A describes a simpler ratio of raw exposed counts that does not correspond to the cross-product odds ratio formula. Option B describes a simple prevalence ratio, not the correctly calculated cross-product odds ratio. Option C describes an unrelated inverse ratio that does not match the odds ratio formula."}, {"id": 982, "domain": "biostatistics_epidemiology_and_population_health", "question": "A 68-year-old woman with a history of ischemic stroke asks her neurologist whether a new antiplatelet agent is worth adding to her regimen for secondary stroke prevention. The neurologist cites a large randomized controlled trial in which 30% of patients assigned to placebo experienced a recurrent stroke within 2 years, compared with 20% of patients assigned to the new antiplatelet agent over the same follow-up period. To help the patient understand the practical magnitude of this benefit, the neurologist wants to express how many patients like her would need to take the new agent to prevent one additional recurrent stroke. Based on these trial results, what is the number needed to treat over 2 years?", "options": {"A": "3, calculated as the reciprocal of the relative risk reduction.", "B": "1.5, calculated as the control event rate divided by the experimental event rate.", "C": "10, calculated as the reciprocal of the absolute risk reduction.", "D": "3.3, calculated as the reciprocal of the control event rate alone."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (10, calculated as the reciprocal of the absolute risk reduction.): absolute risk reduction (ARR) = control event rate - experimental event rate = 0.30 - 0.20 = 0.10. Number needed to treat = 1/ARR = 1/0.10 = 10. Ten patients must be treated with the antiplatelet agent to prevent one additional recurrent stroke over 2 years, compared with placebo. Option A uses the relative, not the absolute, risk reduction, and does not give the correct number needed to treat. Option B computes an unrelated ratio of event rates rather than the reciprocal of the absolute risk reduction. Option D uses only the control event rate rather than the difference between the two groups."}, {"id": 983, "domain": "biostatistics_epidemiology_and_population_health", "question": "Continuing the discussion of the same antiplatelet trial described above, the patient also asks about the risks of the new medication, not just its benefit. The same randomized controlled trial reports that 5% of patients assigned to placebo experienced major bleeding over 2 years, compared with 25% of patients assigned to the new antiplatelet agent over the same follow-up period. The neurologist wants to translate this bleeding risk into a practical number for the patient to weigh against the earlier stroke-prevention benefit. Based on these same trial results, what is the number needed to harm for major bleeding with this antiplatelet agent?", "options": {"A": "5, calculated as the reciprocal of the absolute risk increase.", "B": "20, calculated as the reciprocal of the control event rate for bleeding.", "C": "10, calculated as the number needed to treat found in the earlier stroke calculation.", "D": "0.2, equal to the absolute risk increase itself."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (5, calculated as the reciprocal of the absolute risk increase.): absolute risk increase = treatment event rate - control event rate = 0.25 - 0.05 = 0.20. Number needed to harm = 1/(absolute risk increase) = 1/0.20 = 5. One in every 5 patients treated with the antiplatelet agent will experience major bleeding who would not have otherwise. Option B uses only the control bleeding rate rather than the difference between the two groups. Option C incorrectly reuses the number needed to treat from the stroke-prevention calculation, which is a different outcome with a different absolute risk difference. Option D restates the absolute risk increase itself rather than taking its reciprocal."}, {"id": 984, "domain": "biostatistics_epidemiology_and_population_health", "question": "A public health department monitors a rural county of 5,000 residents who are free of a reportable infectious disease at the start of the calendar year. Over the following 12 months, 100 new cases of the disease are diagnosed among these residents. Based on these numbers, what is the incidence of this disease in the county over the 1-year period?", "options": {"A": "5%, calculated as new cases divided by the number of cases diagnosed the prior year.", "B": "20%, calculated by multiplying the new cases by an arbitrary factor of 200 instead of using the correct denominator.", "C": "0.2%, calculated as new cases divided by 50,000, an incorrect denominator that is ten times too large for this population.", "D": "2%, or 20 new cases per 1,000 person-years, calculated as new cases divided by the population at risk."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (2%, or 20 new cases per 1,000 person-years, calculated as new cases divided by the...): incidence = new cases / population at risk during the period = 100/5,000 = 0.02, or 2%, equivalently expressed as 20 cases per 1,000 person-years. Option A describes an unrelated and undefined ratio that has no basis in the stated numbers. Option B misapplies an arbitrary multiplier rather than using the correct denominator of 5,000. Option C uses an incorrect denominator of 50,000, which is not the stated population at risk."}, {"id": 985, "domain": "biostatistics_epidemiology_and_population_health", "question": "A local health department is designing a diabetes prevention program and wants a current snapshot of disease burden in its metropolitan service area, rather than tracking new diagnoses over time. A cross-sectional health survey samples 4,000 adults living in the area and asks each participant whether they currently have physician-diagnosed type 2 diabetes, regardless of how long ago the diagnosis was made or how the disease has been managed since. The survey team identifies 250 adults among the sample who currently carry the diagnosis. Based on these numbers, what is the prevalence of type 2 diabetes in this sampled population, and what type of measure of disease frequency does this figure represent?", "options": {"A": "2.5%, calculated using an incorrect denominator of 10,000.", "B": "6.25%, calculated as existing cases divided by the total sampled population.", "C": "16.0 per 1,000 person-years, treating the result as an incidence rate.", "D": "25.0%, calculated using an incorrect denominator of 1,000."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (6.25%, calculated as existing cases divided by the total sampled population.): prevalence = existing cases at a point in time / total population sampled = 250/4,000 = 0.0625, or 6.25%. Because this is a single cross-sectional snapshot that counts all current cases regardless of when they were diagnosed, this is a prevalence, not an incidence. Option A uses an incorrect denominator of 10,000, which is not the stated sample size. Option C wrongly expresses this cross-sectional result as a person-time incidence rate, which is never appropriate for a single-point-in-time prevalence measurement. Option D uses an incorrect denominator of 1,000, which does not match the 4,000 adults actually sampled."}, {"id": 987, "domain": "biostatistics_epidemiology_and_population_health", "question": "A research team wants to study risk factors for a form of pediatric bone cancer that is diagnosed in approximately 1 in 50,000 children per year in their region. They plan to identify children recently diagnosed with the cancer through tumor registries, identify a comparable group of cancer-free children from the same clinics, and then interview parents in both groups about prior exposures, including parental occupation, maternal medication use during pregnancy, and residential proximity to industrial sites. Which study design does this describe, and why is it well suited to this research question?", "options": {"A": "A prospective cohort study, because it can directly calculate the incidence of this rare cancer over time.", "B": "A randomized controlled trial, because parents in this study are described as being randomly assigned to disclose their children's prior exposure histories to investigators.", "C": "A cross-sectional study, because exposure and outcome are both measured in the same interview at one point in time.", "D": "A case-control study, because it efficiently studies a rare disease by starting with affected and unaffected children and looking backward at exposures."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (A case-control study, because it efficiently studies a rare disease by starting with...): the investigators begin with the outcome (cancer diagnosis versus no diagnosis) and look backward for prior exposures, the defining structure of a case-control study, which is especially efficient for rare diseases like this one since it does not require following a large cohort forward for years to accumulate enough cases. Option A is incorrect because a prospective cohort study would follow exposed and unexposed children forward in time, which this design does not do, and would be extremely inefficient for a disease this rare. Option B is incorrect because there is no randomization of any exposure or intervention in this study. Option C is incorrect because although the interview happens at one time, the exposures asked about occurred well before the outcome, and this design is specifically structured around a known outcome, unlike a true cross-sectional study."}, {"id": 989, "domain": "biostatistics_epidemiology_and_population_health", "question": "A study examining the relationship between a chronic respiratory condition and gallbladder disease uses hospitalized patients as both cases (patients with the respiratory condition) and controls (patients admitted for unrelated reasons). The investigators find a strong association between the two conditions, but a critical reviewer points out that hospitalized patients are more likely than the general population to have multiple coexisting conditions that led to their admission in the first place, which could artificially inflate the observed association. What type of bias does this describe?", "options": {"A": "Recall bias, because hospitalized patients tend to remember their prior exposure history differently than people in the general population.", "B": "Lead-time bias, because hospitalization itself changes the apparent timing of when the disease is first detected.", "C": "Berkson bias, a form of selection bias from using hospitalized patients as controls, since their comorbidity patterns differ from the general population.", "D": "Confounding by indication, because the specific treatment given during hospitalization directly causes the observed gallbladder disease itself."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Berkson bias, a form of selection bias from using hospitalized patients as controls,...): using hospitalized controls introduces Berkson bias, since patients admitted to a hospital, by definition, tend to have more comorbid conditions and different exposure patterns than the general population, which can create or distort an apparent association between two conditions that are both associated with hospitalization itself. Option A describes a different bias entirely, involving differential memory of past exposures, which is not the issue described here. Option B applies to screening and early detection, not to this hospital-based selection problem. Option D describes confounding related to why a treatment was prescribed, which does not match this scenario about control selection."}, {"id": 990, "domain": "biostatistics_epidemiology_and_population_health", "question": "A case-control study asks mothers of children with a congenital limb malformation (cases) and mothers of healthy children (controls) to recall all medications and supplements taken during the first trimester of pregnancy, several years after delivery in both groups. Mothers of affected children report substantially more first-trimester medication use than mothers of unaffected children. The investigators are concerned that this difference may partly reflect how thoroughly each group of mothers searched their memory, rather than a true difference in exposure. What type of bias are the investigators describing?", "options": {"A": "Selection bias, since the two groups of mothers may have been selected into the study using systematically different criteria for enrollment overall", "B": "Recall bias, since mothers of an affected child are more likely to have searched their memory for possible causes than mothers of unaffected children.", "C": "Length-time bias, since children with the malformation were identified and enrolled over a different time window than the unaffected children in this study.", "D": "Observer bias, since the investigators recording the interview responses may have held different underlying expectations about each specific group of mothers"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Recall bias, since mothers of an affected child are more likely to have searched their...): this is the classic setup for recall bias, a problem specific to retrospective studies like this one, in which the group with the outcome of interest (an affected child) has been strongly motivated to search their memory for possible causes, leading to more complete or more prominent reporting of past exposures compared with controls, independent of any true difference in what actually happened during pregnancy. Option A describes a bias in how subjects entered the study, not in how they reported information after enrollment. Option C describes a bias specific to screening programs detecting disease of different growth rates, unrelated to this scenario. Option D would apply if the interviewers themselves, not the mothers, introduced the differential reporting, which is not what is described here."}, {"id": 991, "domain": "biostatistics_epidemiology_and_population_health", "question": "A medical student reviewing an observational database notices that adults who carry a disposable butane lighter in their pocket have a significantly higher rate of lung cancer than adults who do not carry one, with a relative risk well above 1.0 that persists across multiple statistical models the student runs. Puzzled, the student asks the attending physician whether lighter carrying should be added to the list of modifiable lung cancer risk factors discussed with patients. The attending explains that carrying a lighter almost certainly does not cause lung cancer directly through any biological mechanism. Which of the following best explains why this statistical association nonetheless appears so strong in the data?", "options": {"A": "Cigarette smoking is a confounder, since people who smoke are more likely to carry a lighter and smoking is an independent, well-established cause of lung cancer.", "B": "Lighter carrying is an effect modifier, meaning its relationship to lung cancer genuinely differs in strength across different strata of smoking status and duration.", "C": "This finding demonstrates reverse causation, since receiving a lung cancer diagnosis is what causes patients to begin carrying a lighter for symptom-related reasons", "D": "Lead-time bias explains the finding, since lighter carriers happen to be diagnosed with their lung cancer earlier in the disease course than nonsmokers are"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Cigarette smoking is a confounder, since people who smoke are more likely to carry a...): a confounder is a third variable that is independently associated with both the exposure being studied (lighter carrying) and the outcome (lung cancer), without being a step on the causal pathway between them. Smoking fits this definition perfectly here, since smokers both carry lighters more often and independently have a much higher risk of lung cancer, creating a spurious association between lighter carrying and cancer once smoking is not accounted for. Option B misapplies effect modification, which describes a real difference in the strength of an association across strata, not a spurious association created by a third factor. Option C proposes an implausible causal direction with no supporting mechanism. Option D misapplies a bias specific to screening and early detection, which does not fit this scenario."}, {"id": 992, "domain": "biostatistics_epidemiology_and_population_health", "question": "A randomized trial of a new lipid-lowering drug finds an overall relative risk reduction in cardiovascular events of 20%. When the investigators examine the results separately by age group, they find a 40% relative risk reduction in patients over age 65 but almost no benefit in patients under age 50, and this difference persists after adjusting for baseline cardiovascular risk factors. How should the investigators interpret this age-related difference in treatment effect?", "options": {"A": "Age is acting as a confounder and should be statistically adjusted away so that a single overall effect estimate can be reported.", "B": "This pattern indicates selection bias, since older and younger patients were likely selected into this randomized trial using different enrollment criteria.", "C": "This pattern indicates recall bias, since older patients may report their symptoms differently than younger patients.", "D": "Age is an effect modifier here, and the drug's effect should be reported separately for each age stratum rather than combined into one overall estimate."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Age is an effect modifier here, and the drug's effect should be reported separately...): when the true magnitude of an association or treatment effect genuinely differs across levels of a third variable, and that difference persists after adjustment for confounding, the third variable is an effect modifier rather than a confounder. The correct response to true effect modification is to report the effect separately within each stratum (here, by age group), since averaging it into one overall number would obscure a real, clinically important difference. Option A incorrectly treats a real biological difference in effect as if it were a nuisance variable to be adjusted away, which is only appropriate for confounding, not effect modification. Option B describes an unrelated bias that does not explain a differential treatment effect observed within a randomized trial. Option C describes an unrelated bias that does not explain a differential treatment effect observed within a randomized trial."}, {"id": 995, "domain": "biostatistics_epidemiology_and_population_health", "question": "A hospital pharmacy and therapeutics committee is reviewing a newly published randomized trial before deciding whether to add a new anticoagulant to the hospital formulary as an alternative to standard therapy for prevention of venous thromboembolism after major orthopedic surgery. The trial's primary results report a p-value of 0.04 for the difference in venous thromboembolism event rates between the two treatment groups, using a prespecified statistical significance threshold, or alpha, of 0.05 set before the trial began. One committee member asks the clinical pharmacist to explain precisely what this reported p-value of 0.04 actually means. Which of the following is the most accurate interpretation of this p-value?", "options": {"A": "There is a 4% probability that the null hypothesis of no true difference between treatments is actually correct, based on this single trial", "B": "The new anticoagulant reduces the risk of venous thromboembolism by exactly 4% compared with standard therapy in this population", "C": "There is a 96% probability that the observed difference between the two treatment groups reflects a true clinical benefit of the new anticoagulant agent overall", "D": "If there were truly no difference between the two treatments, a difference this large or larger would be observed by chance alone in 4% of similarly designed trials."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (If there were truly no difference between the two treatments, a difference this large...): a p-value is the probability of observing a result at least as extreme as the one obtained, assuming the null hypothesis is true, which is exactly what this option describes. Because 0.04 is below the prespecified alpha of 0.05, the trial would reject the null hypothesis and call the result statistically significant. Option A misinterprets the p-value as the probability that the null hypothesis itself is true, which is a common but incorrect interpretation. Option B confuses the p-value with a specific magnitude of clinical effect, which the p-value does not convey at all. Option C similarly misstates the p-value as a probability statement about the alternative hypothesis being true."}, {"id": 998, "domain": "biostatistics_epidemiology_and_population_health", "question": "A meta-analysis pools results from 15 published trials of an herbal supplement for anxiety and finds an overall significant benefit. A methodologist notes that at least 10 additional trials of the same supplement were registered in a clinical trials database but were never published, and that unpublished trials are more likely to have found no significant benefit. What effect would including these unpublished trials most likely have on the meta-analysis, and what is this phenomenon called?", "options": {"A": "Including the unpublished trials would likely strengthen the apparent benefit further; this phenomenon is called effect modification.", "B": "Including the unpublished trials would likely reduce or eliminate the apparent benefit; this phenomenon is called publication bias.", "C": "Including the unpublished trials would have no effect on the pooled estimate, since a forest plot only reflects the largest individual trial.", "D": "Including the unpublished trials would only affect the confidence interval width, not the direction of the point estimate; this phenomenon is called length-time bias."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Including the unpublished trials would likely reduce or eliminate the apparent...): publication bias occurs because studies with positive, statistically significant results are more likely to be submitted and accepted for publication than studies with null or negative results. When a meta-analysis pools only published trials, and a substantial number of unpublished, likely null trials exist, the pooled estimate is skewed toward showing a benefit that would shrink or disappear if the missing negative trials were included. Option A misapplies the term effect modification, which describes genuine variation in effect across subgroups, not a bias from selective publication. Option C misdescribes how a forest plot and meta-analysis actually work, since every included trial contributes to the pooled estimate weighted by its precision, not just the largest study. Option D misapplies length-time bias, a screening-specific concept unrelated to this scenario."}, {"id": 999, "domain": "biostatistics_epidemiology_and_population_health", "question": "A 58-year-old man with poorly controlled type 2 diabetes and hypertension has been hospitalized twice in the past 6 months for hyperglycemic complications. Chart review shows that his prescribed insulin regimen and antihypertensive medications are appropriate and well documented at each visit. During a follow-up visit, he reveals that he often skips meals and medication doses toward the end of each month because he runs out of money for both food and his medication copays. Which of the following is most likely to reduce this patient's risk of another hospitalization?", "options": {"A": "Switching to a more potent antihypertensive agent, since his blood pressure control is likely the primary driver of his hospitalizations.", "B": "Repeating diabetes self-management education focused primarily on insulin injection technique, since a technical error is the most likely explanation", "C": "Increasing the frequency of his follow-up visits without addressing his stated financial barriers, since more frequent monitoring by itself is often thought to help correct nonadherence over time", "D": "Referring him to sources of social support that address food insecurity and prescription cost, since these unaddressed social determinants of health are driving his nonadherence."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Referring him to sources of social support that address food insecurity and...): the vignette explicitly identifies economic instability, specifically food insecurity and inability to afford medication copays, as the direct cause of his skipped meals and doses. Addressing this social determinant of health directly, for example through referral to food assistance programs, patient assistance programs, or a social worker, is far more likely to prevent further hospitalizations than any purely medical intervention, since the medication regimen itself is already documented as appropriate. Option A incorrectly assumes a medical rather than a social cause for his poor control. Option B assumes a technical error that the history does not support. Option C increases monitoring without addressing the actual root cause the patient has already identified."}];
